/* 內建閱讀測驗文章庫（a14 起）：每篇 5 題、中文解析與逐段中文翻譯，由第二位老師盲解審核後加入。 */
window.READING_LIBRARY = [{"id": "a22", "title": "The Fifth Taste: How Umami Was Discovered", "title_zh": "第五種味道：鮮味的發現", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Food", "Science"], "passage": "For a long time, people in the West believed that the tongue could sense only four basic tastes: sweet, sour, salty and bitter. But think about a bowl of beef noodle soup, a ripe tomato or a piece of cheese. They all have a rich, satisfying flavor that does not belong to any of those groups. Today, scientists call this flavor umami, and it is known as the fifth basic taste.\n\nThe story of umami began in Japan in 1908. A chemistry professor named Kikunae Ikeda noticed that dashi, a Japanese soup stock made with kombu (a kind of dried seaweed), had a special flavor. After many experiments, he discovered that the flavor came from a substance called glutamate. He named the taste \"umami,\" from a Japanese word meaning \"delicious.\" A year later, a company began selling glutamate as a seasoning powder, now known as MSG.\n\nHowever, many scientists did not accept umami as a real taste for nearly a hundred years. Some believed it was only a mix of other tastes, such as salty and sweet. Things changed in the early 2000s, when researchers found receptors on the tongue that react to glutamate. In other words, the tongue has its own \"sensors\" for umami, just as it does for sweetness and bitterness. This finding finally gave umami its place beside the other four tastes.\n\nUmami is found naturally in many foods. Tomatoes, mushrooms, soy sauce, seaweed, dried fish and cheese all contain substances that create this taste. The amount often increases as food ripens, dries or ages. That is why a red, ripe tomato tastes richer than a green one, and why cooks add dried mushrooms or dried fish to soups. Even human breast milk is rich in glutamate, so most of us experience umami from our very first meals.\n\nWhy do humans enjoy this taste so much? Many scientists believe that umami tells us a food contains protein, which the body needs to build and repair muscles. Just as sweetness signals energy and bitterness often warns us of poison, umami helps guide us toward food that is good for us. So the next time you enjoy a bowl of hot soup, remember that your tongue is doing some clever chemistry.", "passage_zh": "長久以來，西方人認為舌頭只能感受到四種基本味道：甜、酸、鹹、苦。但想想一碗牛肉麵、一顆成熟的番茄或一片起司，它們都有一種濃郁又令人滿足的味道，並不屬於這幾種中的任何一種。今天，科學家稱這種味道為「鮮味」（umami），它被認為是第五種基本味道。\n\n鮮味的故事始於1908年的日本。一位名叫池田菊苗的化學教授注意到，日式高湯（dashi）——一種用昆布（一種乾燥的海帶）熬煮的湯底——有一種特別的味道。經過許多實驗後，他發現這種味道來自一種叫做麩胺酸（glutamate）的物質。他將這種味道命名為「umami」，取自日文中意思是「美味」的字。一年後，一家公司開始把麩胺酸做成調味粉販售，也就是現在所知道的味精（MSG）。\n\n然而，將近一百年的時間裡，許多科學家都不承認鮮味是一種真正的味道。有些人認為它只是其他味道（例如鹹和甜）的混合。到了2000年代初期，情況有了改變：研究人員在舌頭上發現了會對麩胺酸產生反應的受體。換句話說，舌頭有專門感應鮮味的「感應器」，就像它有感應甜味和苦味的感應器一樣。這項發現終於讓鮮味和其他四種味道並列。\n\n許多食物天然就含有鮮味。番茄、菇類、醬油、海藻、魚乾和起司都含有能產生這種味道的物質。食物在成熟、乾燥或熟成的過程中，這些物質的含量常常會增加。這就是為什麼紅熟的番茄吃起來比青番茄更濃郁，也是廚師會在湯裡加入乾香菇或魚乾的原因。就連人類的母乳也富含麩胺酸，所以大多數人從生命中最初的幾餐就開始體驗鮮味了。\n\n為什麼人類這麼喜歡這種味道呢？許多科學家相信，鮮味告訴我們這種食物含有蛋白質，而身體需要蛋白質來建造和修復肌肉。就像甜味代表能量、苦味常常警告我們有毒一樣，鮮味能引導我們找到對身體有益的食物。所以下次你享用一碗熱湯時，別忘了你的舌頭正在進行一場巧妙的化學作用。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How MSG is made in factories today", "The discovery of umami and why it is a basic taste", "Why Japanese food is healthier than Western food", "How to make soup stock with dried seaweed"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從西方傳統的四種味道談起，介紹池田菊苗發現鮮味的經過（第二段）、科學界後來如何證實鮮味是第五種基本味道（第三段），以及人類喜愛鮮味的原因（第五段）。(A) 味精只在第二段順帶提到，文中沒有說明工廠如何製造；(C) 文中沒有比較日式與西式飲食哪個較健康；(D) 高湯只是池田發現鮮味的起點，並非主旨。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Where did Kikunae Ikeda first notice the special flavor that he later called umami?", "options": ["In a piece of aged cheese", "In a ripe red tomato", "In a soup stock made with dried seaweed", "In human breast milk"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Kikunae Ikeda noticed that dashi, a Japanese soup stock made with kombu (a kind of dried seaweed), had a special flavor. 池田是從用昆布熬煮的日式高湯中注意到這種味道。(A)(B)(D) 起司、番茄、母乳都是第四段提到含有鮮味的食物，但不是池田最初注意到鮮味的地方。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did many scientists refuse to accept umami as a real taste for a long time?", "options": ["They thought it was only a mix of other tastes.", "They believed MSG was bad for people's health.", "Ikeda did not explain how he had found glutamate.", "No one outside Japan had ever tasted dashi."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Some believed it was only a mix of other tastes, such as salty and sweet. 有些科學家認為鮮味只是鹹、甜等味道的混合。(B) 文中沒有提到味精有害健康；(C) 文中說池田經過許多實驗找出麩胺酸，沒有說他不肯說明；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"ripens\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["is cut into small pieces", "is cooked in boiling water", "is kept in a cold refrigerator", "becomes fully grown and ready to eat"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The amount often increases as food ripens, dries or ages. 後面接著說 a red, ripe tomato tastes richer than a green one，可知 ripen 是指蔬果「成熟」，從青綠變成可以吃的狀態。(A)(B)(C) 切塊、煮、冷藏都和文中「紅熟的番茄比青番茄濃郁」的說明無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the last paragraph?", "options": ["Bitter foods are always safe to eat.", "Umami gives the body more energy than sugar does.", "Babies are not able to taste umami.", "People may enjoy umami because it points to food with protein."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說鮮味告訴我們 a food contains protein，並且 helps guide us toward food that is good for us，可推論人類喜愛鮮味，是因為它代表食物含有身體需要的蛋白質。(A) 文中說苦味常警告有毒，與「總是安全」相反；(B) 文中說代表能量的是甜味；(C) 第四段說人從最初幾餐（母乳）就體驗鮮味，與此相反。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a23", "title": "How Sweet Is Your Bubble Tea?", "title_zh": "你的珍珠奶茶有多甜？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Food"], "passage": "When Pei-shan's health teacher asked the class to keep a \"drink diary\" for one month, she thought it would be easy. She never drank soda, and she seldom ate candy. The only sweet thing in her day, she believed, was the large cup of brown sugar bubble milk tea she bought at the shop near her school almost every afternoon.\n\nThe diary told a different story. In class, the teacher explained that a large cup of full-sugar bubble tea may contain 50 grams of sugar or more. The chewy pearls add extra calories, too, because they are made from starch and are often soaked in sugar syrup. The World Health Organization suggests that people get less than 10 percent of their daily energy from added sugars. For an average adult, that is about 50 grams a day, and less than 25 grams is even better. \"So one drink can use up my sugar limit for the whole day,\" Pei-shan realized.\n\nShe did not want to give up her favorite drink, so she decided to make small changes instead. In Taiwan, most drink shops let customers choose how sweet their drinks are: full sugar, less sugar (about 70 percent), half sugar, light sugar (about 30 percent) or no sugar. In the second week, Pei-shan switched to half sugar. In the third week, she tried light sugar and ordered a medium cup instead of a large one. She also cut down from five drinks a week to three.\n\nAt first, the tea tasted strange, as if something was missing. But near the end of the month, something surprising happened. When a friend let her try a sip of a full-sugar drink, it tasted far too sweet. Her teacher explained that our sense of taste can slowly change. When we eat less sugar for a while, we often become more sensitive to sweetness, so a smaller amount feels like enough.\n\nIn her final report, Pei-shan wrote, \"I didn't have to quit bubble tea. I just had to pay attention to it.\" Her classmates were inspired, too. Several of them now check the sugar level before they order, and the whole class has agreed to try \"No-Sugar Fridays\" until the end of the semester.", "passage_zh": "健康教育老師要全班寫一個月的「飲料日記」時，佩珊以為這很簡單。她從來不喝汽水，也很少吃糖果。她認為自己一天當中唯一的甜食，就是幾乎每天下午在學校附近的店買的那一大杯黑糖珍珠奶茶。\n\n但日記呈現的卻是另一回事。老師在課堂上說明，一大杯全糖的珍珠奶茶可能含有50公克甚至更多的糖。有嚼勁的珍珠也會增加額外的熱量，因為它們是用澱粉做的，而且常常泡在糖漿裡。世界衛生組織建議，每天從添加糖攝取的熱量應少於總熱量的百分之十。對一般成人來說，大約是每天50公克，而少於25公克更好。「所以一杯飲料就可能把我一整天的糖分額度用光了。」佩珊恍然大悟。\n\n她不想放棄最愛的飲料，所以決定改做一些小小的改變。在台灣，大多數飲料店都讓顧客選擇飲料的甜度：全糖、少糖（約七分）、半糖、微糖（約三分）或無糖。第二週，佩珊改喝半糖。第三週，她試著喝微糖，並把大杯改成中杯。她也從每週喝五杯減少到三杯。\n\n一開始，茶喝起來怪怪的，好像少了什麼。但快到月底時，發生了一件令人驚訝的事。朋友讓她喝一口全糖飲料時，她覺得實在太甜了。老師解釋說，我們的味覺會慢慢改變。當我們有一段時間少吃糖，常會對甜味變得更敏感，所以比較少的糖就讓人覺得夠甜了。\n\n佩珊在期末報告中寫道：「我不必戒掉珍珠奶茶，只需要多留意它。」她的同學也受到啟發。現在有好幾位同學點飲料前會先確認甜度，全班也同意在這學期結束前試行「無糖星期五」。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How bubble tea was first invented in Taiwan", "Why students should never drink bubble tea", "How to make brown sugar pearls at home", "How a student cut down on sugar without giving up her favorite drink"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文敘述佩珊透過飲料日記發現珍奶含糖量很高，於是降低甜度、改喝中杯並減少杯數（第三段），最後在報告中寫下「不必戒掉，只需要多留意」（第五段）。(A)(C) 文中沒有提到珍奶的起源或珍珠的做法；(B) 佩珊並沒有戒掉珍奶，第五段 I didn't have to quit bubble tea 與「絕對不要喝」不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the World Health Organization, about how much added sugar should an average adult have in one day at most?", "options": ["10 grams", "25 grams", "50 grams", "70 grams"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：For an average adult, that is about 50 grams a day, and less than 25 grams is even better. 世界衛生組織建議的上限約為每天 50 公克。(B) 25 公克是「更理想」的目標，不是上限；(A) 文中的 10 是百分比（百分之十），不是公克數；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "If Pei-shan orders a drink with \"light sugar,\" how sweet will it be?", "options": ["About 30 percent of the full sugar level", "About 50 percent of the full sugar level", "About 70 percent of the full sugar level", "It will have no sugar at all."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：light sugar (about 30 percent)，微糖約為全糖的三成。(B) 是 half sugar（半糖）；(C) 是 less sugar（少糖）；(D) 是 no sugar（無糖）。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the full-sugar drink taste too sweet to Pei-shan near the end of the month?", "options": ["Her friend's drink shop used a different kind of sugar.", "Her sense of taste had changed after she ate less sugar.", "She had a cold, so she could not taste things well.", "The drink had twice as many pearls as usual."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：When we eat less sugar for a while, we often become more sensitive to sweetness. 佩珊減糖一段時間後，味覺對甜味變得更敏感，因此覺得全糖飲料太甜。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到糖的種類、感冒或珍珠的數量。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"sensitive\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["bored with something", "able to notice something easily", "afraid of something", "careless about something"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說少吃糖一段時間後會 become more sensitive to sweetness, so a smaller amount feels like enough，也就是一點點甜就能察覺，sensitive 意思是「敏感的、容易察覺的」。(A) 厭倦的、(C) 害怕的、(D) 不在乎的，都無法解釋為什麼少量的糖就讓人覺得夠甜。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a24", "title": "Why Chili Peppers Set Your Mouth on Fire", "title_zh": "辣椒為什麼讓嘴巴像著火？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Food"], "passage": "Bite into a hot chili pepper, and your mouth may feel as if it is on fire. Your face turns red, your eyes water and you may even start to sweat. Yet the pepper is not actually hot at all; it may have come straight out of the refrigerator. So what is really happening?\n\nThe answer is a chemical called capsaicin, found mainly in the white inner part of the pepper that holds the seeds. Capsaicin is not picked up by your taste buds. Instead, it attaches to pain sensors in your mouth, the same sensors that warn you when something is dangerously hot, like a cup of boiling tea. When capsaicin touches them, they send the message \"Burning!\" to your brain. Your brain believes it and tries to cool your body down, which is why you sweat. In other words, spiciness is not a taste like sweet or salty but a feeling of pain.\n\nIn 1912, an American pharmacist named Wilbur Scoville created a way to measure how hot peppers are. In his test, a pepper extract was mixed with more and more sugar water until a group of tasters could no longer feel any heat. The more water that was needed, the hotter the pepper. The Scoville scale is still used today. A sweet bell pepper scores zero, a jalapeño scores a few thousand, and the hottest peppers in the world score more than two million.\n\nIf you eat something too spicy, do not reach for a glass of cold water. Capsaicin mixes with oil but not with water, so water simply spreads it around your mouth. Milk works much better, because a protein in it called casein can hold on to capsaicin and wash it away. Rice and bread can also help by soaking up some of the heat.\n\nInterestingly, birds cannot feel capsaicin at all. They happily eat the hottest peppers and then spread the seeds far away in their droppings. Mammals, on the other hand, would crush the seeds with their teeth, but the burning feeling keeps most of them away. Many scientists believe this is why peppers developed capsaicin in the first place: it protects their seeds from the wrong animals and lets the right ones carry them to new places.", "passage_zh": "咬一口辣椒，你的嘴巴可能會覺得像著火一樣。你的臉會變紅、眼睛泛淚，甚至可能開始流汗。然而辣椒本身其實一點也不燙，它可能才剛從冰箱拿出來。那麼，到底發生了什麼事呢？\n\n答案是一種叫做辣椒素的化學物質，它主要存在於辣椒內部連著種子的白色部分。辣椒素不是由味蕾察覺的，而是附著在口中的痛覺感應器上——這些感應器原本是在東西燙得危險時（例如一杯滾燙的茶）向你發出警告的。當辣椒素碰到它們時，它們就會向大腦傳送「燒起來了！」的訊息。大腦信以為真，便設法讓身體降溫，這就是你會流汗的原因。換句話說，辣其實不是像甜或鹹那樣的味道，而是一種痛覺。\n\n1912年，一位名叫威爾伯・史高維爾的美國藥劑師發明了一種測量辣椒辣度的方法。在他的測試中，辣椒萃取物會加入越來越多的糖水稀釋，直到一群試吃者再也感覺不到任何辣味為止。需要的水越多，代表辣椒越辣。史高維爾辣度指標至今仍在使用。甜椒的分數是零，墨西哥辣椒（jalapeño）有幾千分，而世界上最辣的辣椒則超過兩百萬分。\n\n如果你吃到太辣的東西，別急著拿一杯冰水。辣椒素能溶於油卻不溶於水，所以喝水只會讓它在嘴裡擴散開來。牛奶的效果好得多，因為牛奶中一種叫做酪蛋白的蛋白質能抓住辣椒素，把它帶走。米飯和麵包也能吸收部分辣味，幫助緩解。\n\n有趣的是，鳥類完全感覺不到辣椒素。牠們能開心地吃下最辣的辣椒，再透過糞便把種子散播到遠方。另一方面，哺乳類動物會用牙齒把種子咬碎，但那種灼燒的感覺讓大部分哺乳類不敢靠近。許多科學家相信，這正是辣椒當初演化出辣椒素的原因：它能保護種子不被「不對的」動物吃掉，並讓「對的」動物把種子帶到新的地方。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this passage?", "options": ["To teach readers how to grow their own chili peppers", "To warn readers never to eat spicy food", "To compare cooking styles from different countries", "To explain why chili peppers feel hot and share some related facts"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明辣椒素如何讓人產生灼熱感（第二段）、辣度如何測量（第三段）、吃太辣時該怎麼辦（第四段），以及辣椒為何演化出辣椒素（第五段）。(A) 文中沒有教人種辣椒；(B) 文中只建議吃太辣時喝牛奶，並沒有叫人不要吃辣；(C) 文中沒有比較各國烹飪方式。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why do people sweat when they eat hot peppers?", "options": ["Their brains think the body is burning and try to cool it down.", "The peppers are usually served at a very high temperature.", "Their taste buds pick up capsaicin as a salty taste.", "They usually drink too much water during the meal."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：they send the message \"Burning!\" to your brain. Your brain believes it and tries to cool your body down, which is why you sweat. 大腦以為身體被燙到而想降溫，所以會流汗。(B) 第一段說辣椒本身並不燙；(C) 第二段說辣椒素不是由味蕾察覺，辣也不是像鹹那樣的味道；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the Scoville scale?", "options": ["It was created in the early 2000s.", "It measures how much sugar a pepper contains.", "A higher score means more water was needed to remove the heat.", "A jalapeño scores more than two million on it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：The more water that was needed, the hotter the pepper. 需要越多糖水稀釋才感覺不到辣，代表越辣、分數越高。(A) 它是在 1912 年發明的；(B) 糖水是用來稀釋的，不是測量含糖量；(D) 墨西哥辣椒只有幾千分，超過兩百萬分的是世界上最辣的辣椒。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Your friend has just eaten a very spicy dish and says his mouth is burning. Based on the passage, what is the best advice?", "options": ["Drink a large glass of ice water quickly.", "Have a glass of milk.", "Eat the white part of the pepper.", "Keep your mouth open until the heat is gone."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Milk works much better, because a protein in it called casein can hold on to capsaicin and wash it away. 牛奶中的酪蛋白能抓住辣椒素並把它帶走。(A) 文中說不要喝冷水，因為水只會讓辣椒素在口中擴散；(C) 第二段說辣椒素主要就在白色部分，吃了只會更辣；(D) 文中沒有提到這個方法。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about birds and chili peppers?", "options": ["Birds help pepper plants grow in new places.", "Birds stay away from peppers because of the burning feeling.", "Birds crush pepper seeds before they swallow them.", "Birds can only eat sweet bell peppers."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段說鳥類感覺不到辣椒素，會吃下辣椒並 spread the seeds far away in their droppings，最後一句也說辣椒素讓 the right ones carry them to new places，可推論鳥類幫助辣椒在新的地方生長。(B) 鳥類感覺不到灼熱感，不會因此避開；(C) 文中說用牙齒咬碎種子的是哺乳類；(D) 文中說鳥類能吃最辣的辣椒。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a25", "title": "A Second Chance for Ugly Vegetables", "title_zh": "給醜蔬果第二次機會", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Environment", "Food"], "passage": "On a farm in Yunlin, Mr. Huang pulls up hundreds of carrots every morning. Many are perfect: long, straight and bright orange. Others have two \"legs,\" are bent like a banana or are simply too small. These carrots taste exactly the same, but supermarkets often refuse to buy them because shoppers prefer vegetables that look perfect. In the past, Mr. Huang fed them to his neighbor's pigs or left them in the field to rot.\n\nThis problem is not unusual. According to the United Nations, about one third of all the food produced in the world is lost or wasted. Some is thrown away by families who buy too much, but a large amount never even leaves the farm. When food is wasted, the water, land and energy used to grow it are wasted, too. Rotting food also produces gases that make climate change worse.\n\nLast spring, a group of students from a nearby vocational high school decided to help. With their teacher, they visited five local farms and offered to sell the farmers' \"ugly\" fruit and vegetables at a weekend stall in front of the town's train station. They called it the \"Second Chance Market.\" Everything was about 30 percent cheaper than usual, and each box came with a card explaining why the food looked different.\n\nThe first few weekends were slow. Many shoppers picked up a twisted cucumber, laughed and put it back. So the students got creative. They cooked free samples, such as carrot soup and tomato salad, and posted funny photos online of \"the vegetable of the week,\" like a heart-shaped potato. Soon, people began coming just to see what odd shapes they would find. By the end of the summer, the stall had sold more than three tons of produce that would otherwise have been thrown away.\n\nMr. Huang says the market has changed the way he feels about his work. \"I used to feel sad when I saw good food lying in the field,\" he said. \"Now I know someone will enjoy it.\" The students have learned something, too: what matters most about food is not how it looks, but how it tastes and the hard work that goes into growing it.", "passage_zh": "在雲林的一座農場裡，黃先生每天早上都要拔起好幾百根紅蘿蔔。其中許多長得很完美：又長又直，顏色是鮮豔的橘色。其他的則長出兩隻「腳」、像香蕉一樣彎曲，或是太小。這些紅蘿蔔的味道完全一樣，但超市常常拒絕收購，因為顧客偏好外表完美的蔬菜。過去，黃先生會把它們拿去餵鄰居的豬，或是任由它們在田裡腐爛。\n\n這個問題並不少見。根據聯合國的資料，全世界生產的食物中約有三分之一在過程中損失或被浪費掉。有些是被買太多的家庭丟掉的，但還有大量的食物根本沒有離開過農場。食物被浪費時，用來種植它們的水、土地和能源也跟著浪費了。腐爛的食物還會產生讓氣候變遷更加惡化的氣體。\n\n去年春天，附近一所高職的一群學生決定伸出援手。他們和老師一起拜訪了五座當地農場，提議在週末到鎮上的火車站前擺攤，幫農民販售「醜」蔬果。他們把攤位取名為「第二次機會市集」。所有商品都比平常便宜約三成，而且每一箱都附上一張卡片，說明這些食物為什麼長得不一樣。\n\n最初幾個週末生意很冷清。許多顧客拿起一根扭曲的小黃瓜，笑一笑又放回去。於是學生們發揮了創意。他們煮了紅蘿蔔濃湯、番茄沙拉等免費試吃品，並在網路上張貼「本週蔬菜」的趣味照片，例如一顆心形的馬鈴薯。很快地，人們開始專程前來，只為了看看能找到什麼奇形怪狀的蔬果。到了夏天結束時，這個攤位已經賣出超過三公噸原本會被丟掉的農產品。\n\n黃先生說，這個市集改變了他對自己工作的感受。「以前看到好好的食物躺在田裡，我會很難過，」他說，「現在我知道會有人好好享用它們。」學生們也學到了一課：食物最重要的不是外表，而是它的味道，以及種植它所付出的辛勞。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a student market gives imperfect fruit and vegetables a second chance", "How farmers can grow straighter and bigger carrots", "Why supermarkets sell vegetables at low prices", "How climate change is damaging farms in Yunlin"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文從黃先生賣不出去的醜紅蘿蔔談起，說明食物浪費的問題，再介紹高職學生成立「第二次機會市集」販售醜蔬果的經過與成果（第三、四段）。(B) 文中沒有教人種出漂亮的紅蘿蔔；(C) 超市其實是拒收醜蔬果，打折販售的是學生的攤位；(D) 氣候變遷只在第二段說明食物浪費的影響時順帶一提。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did supermarkets often refuse to buy some of Mr. Huang's carrots?", "options": ["The carrots did not taste as good as the others.", "Shoppers prefer vegetables that look perfect.", "Mr. Huang asked for too high a price.", "The carrots had already started to rot."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：supermarkets often refuse to buy them because shoppers prefer vegetables that look perfect. 超市拒收是因為顧客偏好外表完美的蔬菜。(A) 文中說 These carrots taste exactly the same，味道完全一樣；(C) 文中未提到價格問題；(D) 腐爛是賣不出去之後被留在田裡的結果，不是拒收的原因。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the Second Chance Market?", "options": ["It was open every day in the school's parking lot.", "Its fruit and vegetables cost more than usual.", "Each box came with a card explaining why the food looked different.", "It sold only carrots from Mr. Huang's farm."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：each box came with a card explaining why the food looked different. (A) 市集是週末在火車站前擺攤，不是每天在學校；(B) 價格比平常便宜約三成；(D) 學生拜訪了五座農場，販售各種蔬果（如第四段的小黃瓜、番茄、馬鈴薯），不只有紅蘿蔔。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"odd\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["strange", "cheap", "fresh", "tiny"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：people began coming just to see what odd shapes they would find，前文提到扭曲的小黃瓜、心形的馬鈴薯等，可知 odd 指「奇怪的、不尋常的」，與 strange 意思最接近。(B) 便宜的、(C) 新鮮的、(D) 極小的，都不能用來形容這些讓人好奇而專程來看的形狀。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How does Mr. Huang most likely feel about the market now?", "options": ["Worried that it will hurt his business", "Angry that his carrots are sold so cheaply", "Not interested in what the students are doing", "Glad that his good food is no longer wasted"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段黃先生說：I used to feel sad when I saw good food lying in the field... Now I know someone will enjoy it. 他以前很難過，現在知道有人會享用這些食物，可知他感到欣慰、高興。(A)(B)(C) 擔心、生氣、不感興趣都與他說話的語氣不符。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a26", "title": "What's for Dinner in Space?", "title_zh": "太空中的晚餐吃什麼？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Food"], "passage": "Eating dinner seems simple on Earth. But imagine trying to eat in a place where your soup floats out of the bowl and your rice drifts around the room. That is the challenge astronauts face on the International Space Station (ISS), where everything is weightless. Over the years, scientists have found clever ways to feed people in space.\n\nThe first space meals, in the 1960s, were not very pleasant. Astronauts squeezed food such as applesauce out of metal tubes, like toothpaste, or ate small, dry cubes of food. Today the menu is much better. Astronauts on the ISS can choose from more than a hundred kinds of food, from scrambled eggs to chicken curry. Much of it is freeze-dried, which means the water has been removed so that it lasts a long time and weighs less. Before eating, astronauts simply add hot water to the package.\n\nSome ordinary foods cause special problems. Bread, for example, makes crumbs, and in space crumbs do not fall to the floor. They float around and could get into an astronaut's eyes or damage important equipment. For this reason, astronauts have eaten tortillas instead of bread since the 1980s. Salt and pepper are a problem for the same reason, so they come in liquid form: the salt is mixed with water, and the pepper is mixed with oil.\n\nAnother surprise is that food tastes weaker in space. In weightlessness, fluids in the body move up toward the head, so astronauts often feel as if they have a stuffy nose, just like when they have a cold. Because smell is an important part of flavor, food seems to lose some of its taste. This is why many astronauts start to love hot sauce and spicy dishes during their missions.\n\nIn the future, astronauts on long trips, such as a journey to Mars, will need to grow some of their own food. Scientists have already started testing this. In 2015, astronauts on the ISS ate red lettuce that they had grown on the station in a special plant box. It was only a small salad, but it was a big step toward farming in space.", "passage_zh": "在地球上，吃晚餐似乎是件簡單的事。但想像一下，在一個湯會從碗裡飄出來、米飯會在房間裡四處漂浮的地方吃飯會是什麼情形。這正是國際太空站（ISS）上的太空人面臨的挑戰，因為那裡的一切都處於失重狀態。多年來，科學家想出了許多巧妙的方法，讓人們在太空中也能吃飯。\n\n1960年代最早的太空餐並不怎麼令人愉快。太空人得像擠牙膏一樣，從金屬管裡擠出蘋果泥之類的食物，或是吃一小塊一小塊乾乾的方塊食物。如今的菜單好多了。國際太空站上的太空人可以從一百多種食物中挑選，從炒蛋到咖哩雞都有。其中很多是冷凍乾燥的，也就是食物中的水分已被去除，因此可以保存很久，重量也比較輕。吃之前，太空人只要在包裝裡加入熱水就可以了。\n\n有些普通的食物會造成特別的問題。例如麵包會產生碎屑，而在太空中，碎屑不會掉到地上，而是四處漂浮，可能會跑進太空人的眼睛或損壞重要的設備。因此，從1980年代起，太空人就改吃墨西哥薄餅而不是麵包。鹽和胡椒也有同樣的問題，所以它們是以液體的形式出現：鹽溶在水裡，胡椒則混在油裡。\n\n另一件令人意外的事是，食物在太空中吃起來味道比較淡。在失重狀態下，體內的液體會往頭部移動，所以太空人常覺得鼻子塞住，就像感冒時一樣。由於嗅覺是風味很重要的一部分，食物似乎就失去了一些味道。這就是為什麼許多太空人在執行任務期間會開始愛上辣醬和辛辣的菜餚。\n\n未來，進行長途旅行（例如前往火星）的太空人將需要自己種植一部分食物。科學家已經開始測試這件事。2015年，國際太空站上的太空人吃了他們在站上一個特殊植物箱裡種出來的紅萵苣。那雖然只是一小份沙拉，卻是邁向太空農業的一大步。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How astronauts eat in space and the problems they must solve", "How people can train to become astronauts", "Why bread is unhealthy for people on Earth", "What daily life will be like for people on Mars"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹太空食物從 1960 年代到現在的變化（第二段）、麵包碎屑與調味料的問題（第三段）、太空中食物變淡的原因（第四段），以及在太空種菜的嘗試（第五段）。(B) 文中沒有提到如何成為太空人；(C) 麵包的問題在於碎屑會漂浮，與健康無關；(D) 火星只在最後一段被當作長途旅行的例子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why do astronauts eat tortillas instead of bread?", "options": ["Tortillas are cheaper to send into space.", "Bread makes crumbs that float around and may cause damage.", "Bread tastes weaker than tortillas in space.", "Tortillas do not need any water before eating."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Bread, for example, makes crumbs... They float around and could get into an astronaut's eyes or damage important equipment. For this reason, astronauts have eaten tortillas instead of bread. 改吃薄餅是因為麵包屑會漂浮並造成危險。(A)(C)(D) 價格、味道、是否需要加水，文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"stuffy\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["running very fast", "full of nice smells", "blocked so that air cannot pass through easily", "too dry and cold"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說體液往頭部移動，太空人會覺得 a stuffy nose, just like when they have a cold，感冒時鼻子會「塞住、不通」，所以 stuffy 是「堵塞的、不通氣的」。(A) 跑得很快、(D) 太乾太冷，都與感冒鼻塞的情形不符；(B) 下一句說嗅覺因此變差、食物變淡，與「充滿香味」正好相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why do many astronauts start to like spicy food during their missions?", "options": ["Spicy food is the only kind of food on the ISS.", "Their sense of smell is weaker, so food seems to have less taste.", "Spicy food helps them sleep better in space.", "They have to eat it to fight off colds."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Because smell is an important part of flavor, food seems to lose some of its taste. This is why many astronauts start to love hot sauce and spicy dishes. 鼻塞讓嗅覺變弱，食物吃起來變淡，所以喜歡重口味的辣食。(A) 第二段說站上有一百多種食物；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 太空人只是「感覺像感冒」，並非真的需要對抗感冒。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned in the passage as a way of preparing food for space?", "options": ["Mixing salt with water", "Mixing pepper with oil", "Removing the water from food so that it lasts longer", "Baking fresh bread in a small oven"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段提到鹽溶在水裡 (A)、胡椒混在油裡 (B)；第二段提到冷凍乾燥是把食物中的水分去除以便長期保存 (C)。文中說麵包會產生碎屑，所以太空人改吃薄餅，完全沒有提到在太空烤麵包。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a27", "title": "Slow Down and Enjoy Your Lunch", "title_zh": "慢慢吃，好好享受午餐", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Food"], "passage": "The lunch bell rings, and many students rush to finish their lunch boxes in ten minutes so that they can play basketball, take a nap or review for a quiz. Eating fast may seem like a smart way to save time, but health experts say it can be bad for both your body and your enjoyment of food.\n\nThe main reason is that your stomach and your brain do not communicate right away. When you eat, your stomach stretches and your gut releases hormones, chemical messages that travel through the blood. These signals tell your brain that you are full, but they take about twenty minutes to have a strong effect. If you finish a meal in ten minutes, your brain has not received the message yet, so you may keep eating even though your body already has enough.\n\nOver time, this habit can cause problems. A study in Japan that followed nearly 60,000 people found that fast eaters were more likely to become overweight than slow eaters. Eating quickly can also lead to stomachaches, because large pieces of food that have not been chewed well are harder to digest. In addition, people who rush through meals often enjoy their food less, since they hardly notice how it tastes.\n\nWatching videos or scrolling through your phone while you eat can make things worse. When your attention is on a screen, you pay less attention to how much you have eaten. Several studies have shown that people who eat while distracted tend to eat more, not only during the meal but also later in the day.\n\nLuckily, slowing down is not difficult. Try putting down your chopsticks or spoon between bites, and chew each bite well before you swallow. Take a sip of water now and then, and chat with your friends instead of looking at your phone. If possible, give yourself at least twenty minutes for each meal. You may find that you feel full with less food, and that lunch tastes better than ever.", "passage_zh": "午餐鐘聲一響，許多學生就急著在十分鐘內吃完便當，好去打籃球、午睡或準備小考。吃得快看起來是節省時間的聰明方法，但健康專家表示，這對你的身體和享受食物的樂趣都可能有害。\n\n主要原因在於你的胃和大腦並不會立刻溝通。當你吃東西時，胃會撐開，腸道會釋放荷爾蒙，也就是透過血液傳遞的化學訊息。這些訊號會告訴大腦你已經飽了，但它們大約需要二十分鐘才會產生明顯的作用。如果你十分鐘就吃完一餐，大腦還沒收到訊息，所以即使身體已經吃夠了，你可能還會繼續吃。\n\n久而久之，這個習慣就會造成問題。日本一項追蹤將近六萬人的研究發現，吃飯快的人比吃飯慢的人更容易變得過重。吃得太快也可能導致胃痛，因為沒有好好咀嚼的大塊食物比較難消化。此外，匆忙吃飯的人往往比較無法享受食物，因為他們幾乎沒注意到食物的味道。\n\n邊吃飯邊看影片或滑手機會讓情況更糟。當你的注意力在螢幕上時，就比較不會注意自己吃了多少。好幾項研究顯示，邊吃東西邊分心的人往往會吃得比較多，不只是在那一餐，在當天稍後也是如此。\n\n幸好，放慢速度並不難。試著在每一口之間放下筷子或湯匙，每一口都充分咀嚼再吞下去。不時喝一小口水，並和朋友聊天，而不是看手機。可能的話，每一餐至少給自己二十分鐘。你可能會發現，吃比較少的食物就飽了，而且午餐比以往任何時候都更美味。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Students should skip lunch to have more time to study.", "Eating slowly is better for your health and helps you enjoy food more.", "Japanese people eat more slowly than people in other countries.", "Playing basketball right after lunch is dangerous."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文說明吃太快的壞處（大腦來不及接收飽足訊號、容易過重、胃痛、無法享受食物），並在第五段提供放慢吃飯速度的方法。(A) 文中沒有建議不吃午餐；(C) 第三段日本的研究是比較吃得快和吃得慢的人，不是比較各國；(D) 文中沒有說飯後打籃球危險。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why might people who eat fast eat too much?", "options": ["Their brains have not yet received the signal that they are full.", "Their lunch boxes usually contain too much food.", "The hormones in their gut make them feel hungrier.", "They drink too little water during their meals."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：If you finish a meal in ten minutes, your brain has not received the message yet, so you may keep eating. 飽足訊號約需二十分鐘才明顯，吃太快時大腦還沒收到。(C) 文中說荷爾蒙是告訴大腦「已經飽了」，與「更餓」相反；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"digest\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cook over a fire", "buy at a store", "break down in the body so that it can be used", "smell before eating"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：large pieces of food that have not been chewed well are harder to digest，前面說吃太快會胃痛，可知 digest 是指食物在體內被「消化」、分解。(A) 烹煮、(B) 購買、(D) 聞，都與「沒嚼好的大塊食物在胃裡造成不適」無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the study in Japan?", "options": ["It followed about 6,000 high school students.", "It found that slow eaters were more likely to be overweight.", "It found that eating quickly helped people lose weight.", "It found that fast eaters were more likely to become overweight."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：A study in Japan that followed nearly 60,000 people found that fast eaters were more likely to become overweight than slow eaters. (A) 研究對象將近六萬人，且文中沒有說是高中生；(B)(C) 都與研究結果相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which student is following the advice in the passage?", "options": ["Ben puts down his spoon between bites and chats with his classmates.", "Kevin watches cartoons on his phone while he eats.", "Amy finishes her lunch in eight minutes so that she can take a nap.", "Lily swallows big bites quickly to get to basketball practice."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段建議 putting down your chopsticks or spoon between bites，以及 chat with your friends instead of looking at your phone，Ben 的做法完全符合。(B) 邊吃邊看手機正是第四段提醒要避免的；(C) 八分鐘吃完不符合「每餐至少二十分鐘」；(D) 沒有好好咀嚼又吃得太快，也違反建議。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a28", "title": "The Cooker That Became Part of the Family", "title_zh": "像家人一樣的大同電鍋", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "In many Taiwanese kitchens, there is one machine that almost everyone recognizes: a round metal pot with a lid and a single switch on the front. It is the Tatung electric cooker, and for more than sixty years it has steamed rice, warmed up leftovers and cooked soup for millions of families. For many people, it is not just a kitchen tool but almost a member of the family.\n\nThe cooker was first sold in 1960 by Tatung, a Taiwanese company. To make it, Tatung worked with the Japanese company Toshiba, which had started selling the world's first automatic electric rice cooker in 1955. At that time, many families in Taiwan still cooked rice on stoves that burned wood or coal, which took time and careful watching. The new cooker made life much easier, because it could turn itself off when the food was ready.\n\nThe secret is a very simple design. The cooker has an outer pot and an inner pot. The cook puts the food in the inner pot, pours some water into the outer pot and presses the switch down. As the water boils, hot steam cooks the food. When all the water has turned into steam, the temperature inside rises quickly, and the switch pops up with a loud \"click,\" turning off the heat. In general, the more water you pour into the outer pot, the longer the food cooks.\n\nBecause of this design, the cooker can do much more than make rice. People use it to steam buns, dumplings and fish, to cook soups and to heat up yesterday's dinner. It has very few parts that can break, so it is not unusual for a family to use the same cooker for twenty or thirty years. Many Taiwanese students who go abroad to study even pack one in their suitcase so that they can cook familiar food in a new country.\n\nToday, there are many modern rice cookers with digital screens and dozens of programs. Still, the Tatung cooker remains popular. It now comes in many bright colors, and young designers have turned its shape into a symbol of Taiwanese daily life. For many older people, its \"click\" brings back warm memories of a mother or father calling, \"Dinner's ready!\"", "passage_zh": "在許多台灣家庭的廚房裡，有一台幾乎人人都認得的機器：一個附有鍋蓋、前面只有一個開關的圓形金屬鍋。它就是大同電鍋。六十多年來，它為數百萬個家庭蒸飯、加熱剩菜、煮湯。對很多人來說，它不只是廚房用具，幾乎就像家中的一分子。\n\n這款電鍋於1960年由台灣的大同公司首度推出。為了製造它，大同與日本的東芝公司合作；東芝在1955年開始販售世界上第一台自動電子鍋。當時，台灣許多家庭仍在燒木柴或煤炭的爐灶上煮飯，既費時又需要小心看顧。新的電鍋讓生活輕鬆了許多，因為食物煮好時它會自動關掉。\n\n它的祕密在於非常簡單的設計。電鍋有外鍋和內鍋。煮飯的人把食物放進內鍋，在外鍋倒一些水，再把開關按下去。水沸騰時，熱蒸氣就會把食物煮熟。當水全部變成蒸氣後，鍋內的溫度會迅速上升，開關就會「喀」的一聲跳起來，切斷加熱。一般來說，外鍋倒的水越多，食物煮的時間就越長。\n\n由於這樣的設計，電鍋能做的遠遠不只是煮飯。人們用它來蒸包子、水餃和魚，煮湯，以及加熱前一天的晚餐。它很少有會故障的零件，所以一個家庭用同一台電鍋用上二、三十年並不稀奇。許多出國留學的台灣學生甚至會把一台電鍋放進行李箱，好在新的國家煮熟悉的家鄉菜。\n\n如今市面上有許多附數位螢幕、有數十種功能的新式電子鍋。儘管如此，大同電鍋依然很受歡迎。它現在有許多鮮豔的顏色，年輕設計師也把它的造型變成台灣日常生活的象徵。對許多年長的人來說，它那「喀」的一聲，會勾起爸爸或媽媽呼喊「吃飯囉！」的溫暖回憶。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to cook perfect rice in any kind of pot", "Why Japanese kitchen products are better than Taiwanese ones", "The history, design and importance of a well-known Taiwanese cooker", "How to choose a modern rice cooker with a digital screen"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹大同電鍋的由來（第二段）、運作原理（第三段）、多種用途與耐用（第四段），以及它在台灣人生活和記憶中的地位（第一、五段）。(A) 文中沒有教人用各種鍋子煮飯；(B) 文中只說大同與東芝合作，沒有比較優劣；(D) 新式電子鍋只在第五段順帶提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, which of the following is true?", "options": ["Tatung invented the world's first automatic rice cooker.", "Tatung worked with a Japanese company to make its cooker.", "The Tatung cooker was first sold in 1955.", "Most Taiwanese families had electric stoves in 1960."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：To make it, Tatung worked with the Japanese company Toshiba. (A) 推出世界第一台自動電子鍋的是東芝，不是大同；(C) 1955 年是東芝開始販售電子鍋的年份，大同電鍋是 1960 年推出；(D) 文中說當時許多家庭仍用燒木柴或煤炭的爐灶煮飯。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What makes the switch on the cooker pop up?", "options": ["The inner pot becomes too full of rice.", "The cook presses the switch a second time.", "All the water in the outer pot has turned into steam.", "A timer rings after exactly twenty minutes."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：When all the water has turned into steam, the temperature inside rises quickly, and the switch pops up. 外鍋的水全部變成蒸氣、溫度上升，開關就會跳起。(A)(D) 文中未提及內鍋太滿或計時器；(B) 按下開關是開始煮的動作，不會讓開關跳起。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Mrs. Lin wants to cook some beef soup in her Tatung cooker for a longer time. Based on the passage, what should she do?", "options": ["Use a smaller inner pot.", "Press the switch down more quickly.", "Put less water in the outer pot.", "Pour more water into the outer pot."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段最後一句：the more water you pour into the outer pot, the longer the food cooks. 外鍋水越多，煮的時間越長。(C) 水較少反而煮得較短；(A)(B) 內鍋大小和按開關的速度，文中沒有說會影響烹煮時間。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 4, the word \"one\" in \"pack one in their suitcase\" refers to ______.", "options": ["a Tatung cooker", "a new country", "a Taiwanese student", "a dish of familiar food"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Many Taiwanese students who go abroad to study even pack one in their suitcase so that they can cook familiar food，能放進行李箱、又能用來煮家鄉菜的，就是本文介紹的大同電鍋。(B)(C) 國家和學生都不可能放進行李箱；(D) 熟悉的食物是用電鍋「煮」出來的，不是被裝進行李箱的東西。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a29", "title": "A Recipe Without Numbers", "title_zh": "沒有數字的食譜", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Life", "Food"], "passage": "One evening during last winter vacation, my mother had to work late, so my grandmother asked me to help her cook dinner. I agreed, mainly because I wanted to learn her three-cup chicken, which has been my favorite dish since I was little. I took out my phone, ready to type the recipe. \"How much soy sauce?\" I asked. Grandma just laughed. \"Enough,\" she said.\n\nThat was my first lesson: Grandma never measures anything. She learned to cook by watching her own mother, not by reading cookbooks. She told me that the dish got its name from the old way of making it, with one cup each of sesame oil, rice wine and soy sauce. But nobody uses three full cups today, she said, because the dish would be far too oily and salty. \"You cook with your eyes, your nose and your ears.\"\n\nI soon understood what she meant. First, she heated sesame oil in a clay pot and added thick slices of ginger. She did not use a timer. Instead, she waited until the edges of the ginger curled and turned light brown and the kitchen filled with a warm smell. Then she added the chicken and listened. \"Hear that loud sizzle? The pot is hot enough.\" When the sauce became thick and shiny, she threw in a handful of fresh basil, put the lid on and turned off the heat.\n\nOf course, I wanted to cook the dish without her help. So the next weekend, I measured each sauce before Grandma poured it in and wrote everything down in a notebook. A week later, I made three-cup chicken by myself for the whole family. It was good, but it was not quite Grandma's. The chicken was a little dry, because I had followed my notes exactly instead of watching the sauce.\n\nNow I understand that a recipe is only a starting point. Numbers help a beginner, but a good cook also pays attention to colors, smells and sounds. I still keep my notebook, and next to the numbers I have added Grandma's tips, such as \"Wait for the ginger to curl\" and \"Listen for the sizzle.\" Someday, I hope to teach this dish to my own grandchildren, with numbers and without them.", "passage_zh": "去年寒假的某個晚上，媽媽要加班到很晚，所以奶奶要我幫她煮晚餐。我答應了，主要是因為我想學她的三杯雞，那是我從小最愛的一道菜。我拿出手機，準備把食譜打下來。「醬油要放多少？」我問。奶奶只是笑了笑，說：「放夠就好。」\n\n這是我學到的第一課：奶奶從來不量任何東西。她是看著自己的媽媽做菜學會的，而不是看食譜學的。她告訴我，這道菜的名字來自傳統的做法：麻油、米酒和醬油各放一杯。但她說，現在沒有人真的會用滿滿三杯，因為那樣菜會太油又太鹹。「要用眼睛、鼻子和耳朵來煮菜。」\n\n我很快就明白她的意思了。首先，她在砂鍋裡把麻油燒熱，放進厚厚的薑片。她沒有用計時器，而是等到薑片的邊緣捲起、變成淡褐色，廚房裡充滿溫暖的香氣。接著她放入雞肉，仔細聆聽。「聽到那大聲的滋滋聲了嗎？鍋子夠熱了。」當醬汁變得濃稠又油亮時，她丟進一把新鮮的九層塔，蓋上鍋蓋，然後關火。\n\n當然，我希望自己不靠奶奶幫忙也能煮這道菜。所以下一個週末，在奶奶倒入每一種醬料之前，我都先量一量，並把所有東西都寫在筆記本裡。一週後，我自己為全家人做了三杯雞。味道不錯，但還是比不上奶奶做的。雞肉有點乾，因為我完全照著筆記做，而沒有留意醬汁的狀況。\n\n現在我明白，食譜只是一個起點。數字能幫助初學者，但好的廚師還會留意顏色、香氣和聲音。我仍然保留著那本筆記本，並在數字旁邊加上奶奶的訣竅，例如「等薑片捲起來」和「聽滋滋聲」。希望有一天，我能把這道菜教給我自己的孫子孫女——有數字的版本，也有沒有數字的版本。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main lesson the writer learned?", "options": ["Recipes are useless, so beginners should never use them.", "Good cooking needs careful attention, not just exact numbers.", "Grandparents should learn to use smartphones in the kitchen.", "Three-cup chicken must be made with three full cups of each sauce."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：a recipe is only a starting point. Numbers help a beginner, but a good cook also pays attention to colors, smells and sounds. 作者學到做菜除了數字，還要用感官細心觀察。(A) 作者說數字能幫助初學者，並仍保留筆記本，不是說食譜沒用；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第二段奶奶說現在沒有人真的用滿三杯。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Where does the name \"three-cup chicken\" come from?", "options": ["The dish was once served in three small cups.", "The chicken had to be cooked for three hours.", "In the old recipe, one cup each of three sauces was used.", "Three kinds of chicken were used to make the dish."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：the dish got its name from the old way of making it, with one cup each of sesame oil, rice wine and soy sauce. 名稱來自傳統做法中麻油、米酒、醬油各一杯。(A)(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How did Grandma know the pot was hot enough for the chicken?", "options": ["She listened for a loud sizzling sound.", "She set a timer on her phone.", "She touched the side of the clay pot.", "She checked the oil with a thermometer."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Then she added the chicken and listened. \"Hear that loud sizzle? The pot is hot enough.\" 奶奶是聽滋滋聲來判斷鍋子夠熱。(B) 文中說 She did not use a timer；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was the writer's three-cup chicken a little dry?", "options": ["The writer forgot to add the fresh basil.", "The writer used much more soy sauce than Grandma did.", "The writer's family did not like the taste of ginger.", "The writer followed the notes exactly instead of watching the sauce."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：The chicken was a little dry, because I had followed my notes exactly instead of watching the sauce. 雞肉偏乾是因為作者只照筆記上的數字做，沒有觀察醬汁的狀況。(A)(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "When Grandma answered \"Enough\" in paragraph 1, what did she most likely mean?", "options": ["She did not want to teach the writer how to cook.", "There was not enough soy sauce left in the kitchen.", "The right amount depends on the cook's judgment, not an exact number.", "The writer had asked too many questions that day."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段接著說 Grandma never measures anything，又說 You cook with your eyes, your nose and your ears，可知奶奶說「放夠就好」，是指份量要靠經驗和感官判斷，而不是固定的數字。(A) 奶奶後來仍一步步示範，並非不想教；(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到醬油不夠或問題太多。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a30", "title": "Pickleball: The Backyard Game That Brought a Family Together", "title_zh": "匹克球：讓一家人更親近的後院遊戲", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "Life"], "passage": "Last summer, sixteen-year-old Kai Lin noticed something unusual in his neighborhood park. Every morning, a group of retired people gathered on a small court and hit a plastic ball back and forth with flat paddles. One of them was his own grandmother. \"It's called pickleball,\" she told him. \"Come and try it. I bet you'll lose.\"\n\nPickleball was invented in 1965 on Bainbridge Island, near Seattle in the United States. Joel Pritchard and his friend Bill Bell came home from a game of golf and found their children bored. There was an old badminton court in Pritchard's backyard, but nobody could find a full set of rackets. So the fathers gave the kids table tennis paddles and a light plastic ball full of holes, and they lowered the net. People still argue about the strange name. Some say it came from the family dog, Pickles; others say it came from \"pickle boat,\" a rowing term for a boat filled with leftover rowers.\n\nThe game is easy to learn. The court is the same size as a doubles badminton court, much smaller than a tennis court, so players do not need to run very far. The ball is slow, and every serve must be hit underhand. There is one special rule: players may not hit the ball in the air while standing in the area right next to the net. This area is called the \"kitchen,\" and it stops tall, strong players from simply smashing every ball. As a result, good pickleball depends more on control and patience than on power.\n\nThese features help explain why the sport has spread so quickly. In the United States, it has been named the fastest-growing sport for several years in a row, and in 2022 it became the official state sport of Washington. Courts have also appeared in Taiwan, often in parks and school gyms.\n\nKai did lose his first game to his grandmother, 11 to 4. But he kept coming back. Now they play together twice a week, and he has noticed that the sport gives them more than exercise. \"At home, we don't always have much to talk about,\" he says. \"On the court, we never stop talking.\"", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，十六歲的林凱在住家附近的公園注意到一件不尋常的事。每天早上，一群退休人士會聚集在一座小球場上，用扁平的球拍把一顆塑膠球打來打去。其中一位竟然就是他自己的奶奶。「這叫做匹克球，」她告訴他，「來試試看吧。我敢說你會輸。」\n\n匹克球是在 1965 年於美國西雅圖附近的班布里奇島發明的。喬爾‧普里查德和他的朋友比爾‧貝爾打完高爾夫球回到家，發現孩子們很無聊。普里查德家的後院有一座舊的羽毛球場，但沒有人找得到一整套球拍。於是兩位父親給孩子們桌球拍和一顆布滿小洞的輕塑膠球，並把球網降低。至於這個奇怪的名字，人們到現在還在爭論。有人說它來自家裡的狗「Pickles」；也有人說它來自「pickle boat」，這是划船界的用語，指由其他船挑剩的划手所組成的船。\n\n這項運動很容易學。球場和羽毛球雙打場地一樣大，比網球場小得多，所以球員不需要跑很遠。球速很慢，而且每次發球都必須低手發球。它有一條特別的規則：球員站在緊鄰球網的區域時，不可以在球落地前於空中擊球。這個區域被稱為「廚房」，它能防止高大有力的球員一味地扣殺每一顆球。因此，打好匹克球靠的是控球和耐心，而不是力量。\n\n這些特點有助於說明這項運動為什麼擴展得這麼快。在美國，它已連續好幾年被評為成長最快的運動；2022 年，它更成為華盛頓州的官方州運動。台灣也出現了匹克球場，通常設在公園和學校體育館裡。\n\n林凱的第一場比賽確實以 4 比 11 輸給了奶奶。但他一次又一次回到球場。現在他們每週一起打兩次球，而他發現這項運動帶給他們的不只是運動而已。「在家裡，我們不一定有很多話可以聊，」他說，「但在球場上，我們從來沒停止過聊天。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How young players can easily beat older players at pickleball", "The history, rules and appeal of pickleball, seen through one family's experience", "Why tennis is losing players to newer sports in Taiwan", "A rowing team that invented a new sport near Seattle"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以林凱和奶奶打匹克球的經驗開頭與結尾，中間介紹匹克球的起源（第二段）、規則（第三段）和受歡迎的原因（第四段）。(A) 與第五段林凱第一場就輸給奶奶不符；(C) 文中沒有提到網球人口減少；(D) 匹克球是兩位父親為孩子發明的，「pickle boat」只是名字來源的說法之一，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did the fathers give the children table tennis paddles?", "options": ["They could not find a full set of badminton rackets.", "Table tennis paddles were cheaper than tennis rackets.", "The children were too young to play tennis.", "They wanted the children to play a game indoors."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：There was an old badminton court in Pritchard's backyard, but nobody could find a full set of rackets. So the fathers gave the kids table tennis paddles... 因為找不到整套羽毛球拍，才改用桌球拍。(B)(C)(D) 價錢、年紀、室內遊戲文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In pickleball, what is the \"kitchen\"?", "options": ["A place beside the court where players rest and eat snacks", "The area at the back of the court where every serve begins", "A small shop in the park that sells paddles and balls", "The area next to the net where players may not hit the ball in the air"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：players may not hit the ball in the air while standing in the area right next to the net. This area is called the \"kitchen\"。「廚房」指緊鄰球網、不可在球落地前截擊的區域，並非真的廚房或休息區。(A)(B)(C) 都是望文生義的錯誤解讀，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT true about pickleball according to the passage?", "options": ["Every serve must be hit underhand.", "Its court is larger than a tennis court.", "It was invented in 1965.", "It is the official state sport of Washington."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說球場和羽毛球雙打場地一樣大，much smaller than a tennis court（比網球場小得多），所以 (B)「比網球場大」與文意不符。(A) 見第三段 every serve must be hit underhand；(C) 見第二段 invented in 1965；(D) 見第四段 official state sport of Washington，三者皆正確，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does Kai mean when he says, \"On the court, we never stop talking\"?", "options": ["Pickleball games are too noisy for him to enjoy.", "His grandmother talks during games to make him lose.", "Playing pickleball has brought him closer to his grandmother.", "He would rather chat with his grandmother than play the game."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：林凱說在家不一定有話可聊，在球場上卻一直聊天，前一句也提到這項運動帶給他們 more than exercise。可推知打匹克球拉近了祖孫的距離。(A)(B) 文中沒有抱怨吵鬧或奶奶故意干擾；(D) 他每週和奶奶打兩次球，並非比較想聊天而不打球，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a31", "title": "Breaking: From Street Parties to the Olympic Stage", "title_zh": "霹靂舞：從街頭派對到奧運舞台", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "Arts"], "passage": "In August 2024, a new sport appeared at the Paris Olympics. There was no ball, no net and no finish line. Instead, two dancers took turns in the center of a stage while a DJ played music. They spun on their heads, froze in difficult positions and moved their feet so fast that it was hard to follow them. This was breaking, often called breakdancing, and it was making its Olympic debut.\n\nBreaking began in the Bronx, a part of New York City, in the 1970s. At neighborhood parties, DJs noticed that people danced hardest during the \"break,\" the short part of a song where the singing stops and only the drums play. A DJ known as Kool Herc began playing these breaks again and again so that the dancing could continue. The young people who danced to them became known as b-boys and b-girls. Many of them grew up in poor neighborhoods, and a dance battle gave them a way to win respect without fighting.\n\nAt the Olympics, breaking took the form of one-on-one battles. The dancers did not know which songs the DJ would play, so they had to make up many of their moves on the spot. A group of judges scored each round on technique, variety, musicality, originality and how cleanly the moves were performed. In other words, copying someone else's routine was not enough; the best breakers showed a personal style. Ami Yuasa of Japan won the first gold medal for women, and Phil Wizard of Canada won the men's event.\n\nNot everyone was happy to see breaking at the Olympics. Some fans felt that a street art should not be scored like gymnastics, and they worried that strict rules would take away its freedom. In fact, breaking will not be part of the 2028 Games in Los Angeles. Still, many young dancers say Paris gave them something important. For the first time, their parents and teachers saw that breaking needs years of training, strength and discipline.\n\nA dance that started at parties in one neighborhood has now traveled to stages around the world. Whether or not it returns to the Olympics, breaking will keep living wherever there is music and a little open floor.", "passage_zh": "2024 年 8 月，巴黎奧運出現了一項新運動。場上沒有球、沒有網子，也沒有終點線。取而代之的是，兩名舞者在 DJ 播放音樂時輪流站上舞台中央。他們用頭旋轉、以高難度的姿勢定格，雙腳移動得快到讓人幾乎看不清楚。這就是霹靂舞（breaking，常被稱為 breakdancing），而這是它第一次在奧運登場。\n\n霹靂舞在 1970 年代起源於紐約市的布朗克斯區。在社區派對上，DJ 們注意到，人們在歌曲的「break」（間奏）時跳得最起勁；那是歌曲中歌聲停止、只剩鼓聲的短短片段。一位名叫 Kool Herc 的 DJ 開始一再重複播放這些間奏，讓大家可以繼續跳舞。隨著這些間奏起舞的年輕人，後來被稱為 b-boy 和 b-girl。他們當中許多人在貧困的社區長大，而尬舞讓他們不必打架，也能贏得尊重。\n\n在奧運中，霹靂舞採用一對一對決的形式。舞者事先不知道 DJ 會播放哪些歌曲，所以許多動作必須當場即興發揮。一群評審針對技巧、動作多樣性、音樂性、原創性以及動作完成的俐落程度，為每一回合評分。換句話說，光是模仿別人的套路是不夠的；最好的舞者會展現出個人風格。日本的湯淺亞實贏得了史上第一面女子組金牌，加拿大的 Phil Wizard 則贏得男子組冠軍。\n\n並不是每個人都樂見霹靂舞進入奧運。有些舞迷認為，一種街頭藝術不應該像體操一樣被打分數，他們也擔心嚴格的規則會奪走它的自由。事實上，霹靂舞將不會出現在 2028 年的洛杉磯奧運。儘管如此，許多年輕舞者說，巴黎奧運帶給了他們一樣重要的東西。他們的父母和老師第一次看到，霹靂舞需要多年的訓練、力量和自律。\n\n一種從某個社區的派對中誕生的舞蹈，如今已走上世界各地的舞台。無論它是否會重返奧運，只要有音樂和一小塊空地，霹靂舞就會一直延續下去。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How judges are trained to score Olympic events", "Why breaking should take the place of gymnastics", "Where breaking came from and how it appeared at the Olympics", "The life and music of a famous New York DJ"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段介紹霹靂舞在 2024 巴黎奧運首次登場，第二段說明它在 1970 年代紐約布朗克斯區的起源，第三、四段描述奧運比賽方式與各方看法。(A) 評審只是第三段的細節；(B) 文中沒有主張取代體操；(D) Kool Herc 只在第二段被提到一次，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"on the spot\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["in one fixed place on the stage", "right at that moment, without planning", "after many weeks of careful practice", "under the brightest light"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The dancers did not know which songs the DJ would play, so they had to make up many of their moves on the spot. 舞者事先不知道音樂，只能「當場、臨時」編出動作，on the spot 意思是 right at that moment, without planning。(A) 是字面上的「在某個地點」，不合上下文；(C) 與「事先不知道音樂」矛盾；(D) 與文意無關，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what is the \"break\" in a song?", "options": ["The short part where the singing stops and only the drums play", "The loudest part, when the singer sings the chorus", "The silence between the end of one song and the start of another", "The first minute of a song, before the dancers begin"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：the \"break,\" the short part of a song where the singing stops and only the drums play。break 是歌聲停止、只剩鼓聲的短短片段。(B) 與「歌聲停止」相反；(C)(D) 文中未如此定義，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why were some fans unhappy about breaking becoming an Olympic sport?", "options": ["They thought the moves were too dangerous for young dancers.", "They felt the music played at the Olympics was too loud.", "They wanted the Olympics to spend more money on gymnastics.", "They worried that strict rules might take away its freedom."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Some fans felt that a street art should not be scored like gymnastics, and they worried that strict rules would take away its freedom. 舞迷擔心嚴格規則會奪走這種街頭藝術的自由。(A)(B)(C) 危險性、音樂太大聲、經費問題文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true according to the passage?", "options": ["Breaking will be one of the sports at the 2028 Los Angeles Games.", "Olympic breakers practiced with the DJ's songs before each battle.", "A dancer from Japan won the first Olympic gold medal for women.", "Breaking was first created by dancers in Paris in the 1970s."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：Ami Yuasa of Japan won the first gold medal for women. 日本選手湯淺亞實贏得第一面女子組金牌。(A) 第四段說 breaking will not be part of the 2028 Games；(B) 第三段說舞者不知道 DJ 會放哪些歌；(D) 第二段說霹靂舞起源於紐約布朗克斯區，不是巴黎，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a32", "title": "Hitting the Wall: The Marathon's 30-Kilometer Problem", "title_zh": "撞牆期：馬拉松三十公里的難關", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "Science"], "passage": "For the first three hours of her first marathon, Ya-ting felt wonderful. She passed other runners and waved at people cheering by the road. Then, at around the 32-kilometer mark, everything changed. Her legs felt as heavy as stone, her mind became foggy, and she suddenly wanted to cry. She had \"hit the wall,\" an experience that many marathon runners know too well.\n\nHitting the wall is not just in a runner's head; it has a clear scientific cause. Our muscles get most of their quick energy from glycogen, a form of sugar stored in the muscles and the liver. The body can store only a limited amount, often enough for roughly 30 kilometers of hard running. When glycogen runs low, the body has to depend more on fat. Fat contains plenty of energy, but the body turns it into fuel much more slowly. As a result, the runner is forced to slow down, sometimes to a walk. Because the brain also needs sugar, runners may feel confused, weak and emotional.\n\nThis is why the marathon can feel so cruel. The race is 42.195 kilometers long, so the wall usually appears with about ten kilometers still to go. Interestingly, this exact distance comes from the 1908 London Olympics, where the course was planned to start at Windsor Castle and finish in front of the royal family's seats in the stadium.\n\nThe good news is that the wall can usually be avoided. First, in the two or three days before a race, runners eat more rice, noodles and bread so that their bodies can store extra glycogen. Second, during the race, they take in sugar through sports drinks, bananas or energy gels before they feel tired. Third, they start at a steady pace. Running too fast in the early kilometers burns glycogen quickly and brings the wall closer. Finally, long training runs teach the body to use fat more efficiently.\n\nYa-ting walked and jogged the last ten kilometers and finished in just over five hours. Six months later, she ran her second marathon with a better plan, and this time she never met the wall at all. \"The wall is real,\" she says, \"but you can prepare for it.\"", "passage_zh": "在人生第一場馬拉松的前三個小時，雅婷感覺棒極了。她超越了其他跑者，也向路邊加油的民眾揮手。然而，大約跑到 32 公里處時，一切都變了。她的雙腿重得像石頭，腦袋變得昏昏沉沉，還突然很想哭。她「撞牆」了──這是許多馬拉松跑者都再熟悉不過的經驗。\n\n撞牆並不只是跑者的心理作用，它有明確的科學原因。我們的肌肉主要從肝醣獲得快速的能量；肝醣是一種儲存在肌肉和肝臟裡的醣類。身體能儲存的量有限，通常大約只夠以比賽強度跑 30 公里左右。當肝醣快要用完時，身體就必須更依賴脂肪。脂肪含有大量能量，但身體把它轉換成燃料的速度慢得多。因此，跑者會被迫放慢速度，有時甚至只能用走的。又因為大腦也需要醣類，跑者可能會感到思緒混亂、虛弱，情緒也變得激動。\n\n這就是為什麼馬拉松會讓人覺得如此殘酷。比賽全長 42.195 公里，所以撞牆通常出現在還剩大約十公里的時候。有趣的是，這個精確的距離來自 1908 年的倫敦奧運，當時的路線被規劃為從溫莎城堡出發，終點設在體育場內王室成員的座位前方。\n\n好消息是，撞牆通常是可以避免的。第一，在賽前兩三天，跑者會多吃米飯、麵條和麵包，好讓身體儲存額外的肝醣。第二，比賽途中，他們會在感到疲累之前，就先透過運動飲料、香蕉或能量膠補充醣分。第三，他們會以穩定的配速起跑。在前幾公里跑得太快，會讓肝醣很快耗盡，使撞牆提早到來。最後，長距離的訓練能讓身體更有效率地利用脂肪。\n\n雅婷在最後十公里邊走邊慢跑，以五個多小時完賽。六個月後，她帶著更好的計畫跑了第二場馬拉松，而這一次她完全沒有撞牆。「撞牆是真的，」她說，「但你可以事先做好準備。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the 1908 London Olympics changed the rules of running", "How Ya-ting trained to become a professional runner", "Why marathon runners hit the wall and how they can avoid it", "Why eating too much fat is bad for long-distance runners"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文以雅婷的經驗為例，第二段解釋撞牆的科學原因（肝醣不足），第四段說明避免撞牆的方法。(A) 1908 年倫敦奧運只是第三段提到馬拉松距離的由來；(B) 雅婷是一般跑者，文中沒說她要當職業選手；(D) 文中沒說吃脂肪不好，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why are runners forced to slow down when they hit the wall?", "options": ["Their bodies must depend more on fat, which is turned into energy slowly.", "Their muscles have used up all the water in their bodies.", "They have eaten too much rice and bread before the race.", "Their legs have been hurt by running on hard roads."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：When glycogen runs low, the body has to depend more on fat... the body turns it into fuel much more slowly. As a result, the runner is forced to slow down. 肝醣不足時，身體改靠轉換較慢的脂肪供能，所以速度被迫變慢。(B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 賽前多吃米飯麵包反而是第四段建議的預防方法，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer say that the marathon \"can feel so cruel\"?", "options": ["Because the first marathon was held only for the royal family.", "Because most marathons are held in very hot weather.", "Because few runners are able to finish their first marathon.", "Because runners often still have about ten kilometers left when their glycogen runs low."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：The race is 42.195 kilometers long, so the wall usually appears with about ten kilometers still to go. 肝醣約在 30 公里左右耗盡，但全程有 42 公里，撞牆後還要再跑大約十公里，所以說它殘酷。(A) 終點設在王室座位前，不代表只為王室舉辦；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT suggested in the passage as a way to avoid hitting the wall?", "options": ["Eating more rice, noodles and bread a few days before the race", "Running as fast as possible in the early kilometers to save time", "Taking in sugar from sports drinks or bananas during the race", "Doing long training runs before the race day"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段建議以穩定配速起跑，並指出 Running too fast in the early kilometers burns glycogen quickly and brings the wall closer（前幾公里跑太快會讓撞牆提早到來），所以 (B) 不是建議的方法。(A)(C)(D) 分別是第四段提到的賽前多吃澱粉、賽中補充醣分、長距離訓練，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"steady\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["even and not changing", "very fast and exciting", "slow and lazy", "difficult and painful"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段 Third, they start at a steady pace，下一句接著說一開始跑太快會讓撞牆提早到來，可知 steady pace 是「穩定、不忽快忽慢的配速」，意思接近 even and not changing。(B) 與「不要跑太快」相反；(C)(D) 不合文意，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a33", "title": "Is 10,000 Steps a Day Really the Magic Number?", "title_zh": "一天一萬步真的是神奇數字嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "Many smartwatches and phone apps give their users the same daily goal: 10,000 steps. When people reach it, the watch may shake gently or show fireworks on the screen. The number sounds so scientific that most people never question it. But where did it actually come from?\n\nThe answer is not a laboratory but an advertising campaign. In 1965, shortly after the Tokyo Olympics, a Japanese company began selling a simple step counter called the manpo-kei, which means \"10,000-step meter.\" Interest in health and exercise was growing in Japan at that time, and a round, easy-to-remember number helped sell the product. Some people also point out that the Japanese character for 10,000, 万, looks a little like a person walking. The goal soon spread around the world, even though no study had proved that it was the right number.\n\nIn recent years, scientists have finally tested the idea. In a 2019 study of more than 16,000 older women in the United States, those who walked about 4,400 steps a day were less likely to die during the following years than those who walked only 2,700. The benefits kept rising as the number of steps went up, but they leveled off at around 7,500 steps a day. Later research that combined many studies found similar results. For people over 60, the benefits stopped growing at 6,000 to 8,000 steps, while younger adults kept gaining up to about 8,000 to 10,000.\n\nDoes this mean 10,000 steps is a bad goal? Not at all. For young, healthy people, walking more is still good for the heart and the mind. However, the research sends an important message to people who hardly exercise: you do not need to reach a huge number to improve your health. Moving from 3,000 to 5,000 steps a day can already make a real difference.\n\nExperts also remind us that steps are not the only thing that counts. Walking fast enough to breathe a little harder, climbing stairs and playing sports all bring extra benefits. So if your watch says you have reached only 6,000 steps today, do not feel like a failure. The best number is the one you can keep up, day after day.", "passage_zh": "許多智慧手錶和手機 app 都為使用者設定了相同的每日目標：一萬步。當人們達成目標時，手錶可能會輕輕震動，或在螢幕上放出煙火。這個數字聽起來很科學，以至於大多數人從來沒有懷疑過它。但它究竟是從哪裡來的呢？\n\n答案不是來自實驗室，而是來自一場廣告宣傳。1965 年，東京奧運結束後不久，一家日本公司開始販售一種簡單的計步器，名叫「萬步計」，意思就是「一萬步的計數器」。當時日本人對健康和運動的興趣日漸提高，而一個整齊好記的整數有助於推銷這項產品。也有人指出，日文中代表一萬的「万」字，看起來有點像一個正在走路的人。這個目標很快就傳遍了全世界，儘管當時並沒有任何研究證明這是正確的數字。\n\n近年來，科學家終於開始檢驗這個說法。在 2019 年一項針對美國一萬六千多名年長女性的研究中，每天走大約 4,400 步的人，在接下來幾年中死亡的機率比每天只走 2,700 步的人來得低。步數增加時，好處也持續增加，但到了每天大約 7,500 步時，好處就不再上升。後來綜合多項研究的分析也發現了類似的結果：對 60 歲以上的人來說，好處在 6,000 到 8,000 步時就停止增加；較年輕的成年人則一直到大約 8,000 到 10,000 步都還能持續獲益。\n\n這是否表示一萬步是個不好的目標呢？完全不是。對年輕健康的人來說，多走路對心臟和心理仍然有益。不過，這些研究對平常很少運動的人傳達了一個重要的訊息：你不需要達到一個很大的數字，也能改善健康。從每天 3,000 步增加到 5,000 步，就已經能帶來真正的改變。\n\n專家也提醒我們，步數並不是唯一重要的事。走路快到讓呼吸稍微變喘、爬樓梯以及從事運動，都能帶來額外的好處。所以，如果你的手錶顯示你今天只走了 6,000 步，別覺得自己很失敗。最好的數字，是你能夠日復一日持續下去的那一個。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To advertise a new kind of smartwatch for walkers", "To prove that walking is harmful for older people", "To describe how Japan prepared for the 1964 Tokyo Olympics", "To explain where the 10,000-step goal came from and what research says about it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段說明一萬步的目標來自 1965 年日本計步器的廣告宣傳，第三到五段介紹科學研究的結果與建議。(A) 文中沒有推銷任何產品；(B) 研究顯示走路對年長者有益，與選項相反；(C) 東京奧運只是第二段的時間背景，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why was the number 10,000 chosen for the step counter?", "options": ["It was a round, easy-to-remember number that helped sell the product.", "A study had proved that it was the best number for health.", "Olympic athletes in Tokyo walked that many steps every day.", "Japanese doctors told everyone to walk that far each day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：a round, easy-to-remember number helped sell the product. 一萬是好記的整數，有助於產品銷售。(B) 與第二段最後一句 no study had proved that it was the right number 相反；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In the 2019 study, at about how many steps a day did the benefits stop increasing?", "options": ["2,700", "4,400", "7,500", "10,000"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：The benefits kept rising as the number of steps went up, but they leveled off at around 7,500 steps a day. 好處在每天約 7,500 步時就不再增加。(A) 2,700 和 (B) 4,400 是研究中拿來比較的步數；(D) 10,000 是廣告設定的目標，並非研究結果，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What would the writer most likely say to a person who walks only 3,000 steps a day?", "options": ["\"Walking is useless unless you reach 10,000 steps.\"", "\"Try adding a couple of thousand steps; it can already help.\"", "\"You should buy a smartwatch before you start walking.\"", "\"Walking slowly is healthier than walking fast.\""], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：Moving from 3,000 to 5,000 steps a day can already make a real difference. 作者認為每天多走兩千步左右就能帶來改變。(A) 與第四段 you do not need to reach a huge number 相反；(C) 文中未建議買手錶；(D) 第五段說走快一點、讓呼吸稍喘反而有額外好處，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"leveled off\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["stopped going up", "dropped suddenly", "started again", "became unfair"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 The benefits kept rising... but they leveled off at around 7,500 steps，「but」表示轉折：好處原本持續上升，到 7,500 步就「趨於平緩、不再增加」，同段下一句也用 stopped growing 換句話說，故 leveled off 意思接近 stopped going up。(B) 文中沒說好處突然下降；(C)(D) 不合文意，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a34", "title": "Winter Guests in Tainan: My First Black-faced Spoonbills", "title_zh": "臺南的冬季嬌客：我第一次看到黑面琵鷺", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature", "Life"], "passage": "When Pei-shan's biology teacher invited the class on a birdwatching trip, she almost said no. Getting up at five on a Saturday to look at birds did not sound like fun. But on a cold December morning, standing quietly beside a wetland in Qigu, Tainan, she saw a group of tall white birds in the shallow water. Each one had a long, flat black bill shaped like a spoon. \"Those are black-faced spoonbills,\" her teacher whispered. \"Some of them flew here all the way from Korea.\"\n\nThe black-faced spoonbill is an endangered bird. In the late 1980s, scientists could find only about 300 of them in the world. Their wetlands were being turned into farms, factories and roads, and the species seemed close to disappearing. Since then, countries in East Asia have worked together to protect them. Today there are more than 6,000, and every winter a large share of them come to Taiwan, especially to the coast of Tainan. They usually arrive around October and fly north again in spring.\n\nSpoonbills feed in an unusual way. Instead of looking for food with their eyes, they sweep their open bills from side to side under the water. When a small fish or shrimp touches the inside of the bill, it snaps shut. Because they feed by touch, they can find food in muddy water and even in the dark. In fact, they often feed at night and spend much of the day resting.\n\nThat morning changed how Pei-shan spends her weekends. She has since joined a local birdwatching club and learned some basic rules. Birdwatchers should wear clothes in dull colors, such as green or brown, and keep their voices low. They should stay on the paths and use binoculars instead of walking closer. Feeding wild birds or using a camera flash is not allowed, because it can frighten or harm them.\n\nBirdwatching, Pei-shan says, has taught her patience. You cannot order a bird to appear; you can only wait, look carefully and enjoy whatever comes. It has also changed how she sees wetlands, which she once thought were just empty, muddy land. \"Now when I look at a wetland,\" she says, \"I see a home.\"", "passage_zh": "當佩珊的生物老師邀請全班參加賞鳥活動時，她差點就拒絕了。星期六早上五點起床去看鳥，聽起來一點也不好玩。但在十二月一個寒冷的早晨，她靜靜地站在臺南七股的一片溼地旁，看到一群高大的白色鳥兒站在淺水中。每一隻都有又長又扁、形狀像湯匙的黑色鳥喙。「那些是黑面琵鷺，」老師小聲地說，「有些是大老遠從韓國飛過來的。」\n\n黑面琵鷺是一種瀕臨絕種的鳥類。在 1980 年代末期，科學家在全世界只找得到大約 300 隻。牠們的溼地被改建成農田、工廠和道路，這個物種似乎就快要消失了。從那時起，東亞各國便攜手合作保護牠們。如今牠們的數量已超過六千隻，而每年冬天都有很大一部分會來到臺灣，尤其是臺南沿海。牠們通常在十月左右抵達，到了春天再飛回北方。\n\n琵鷺覓食的方式很特別。牠們不是用眼睛找食物，而是把張開的喙放在水中左右掃動。當小魚或小蝦碰到喙的內側時，喙就會迅速閉合。因為牠們靠觸覺覓食，所以在混濁的水中、甚至在黑暗中都能找到食物。事實上，牠們經常在夜間覓食，白天則大多在休息。\n\n那個早晨改變了佩珊度過週末的方式。她後來加入了當地的賞鳥社團，並學到一些基本規則。賞鳥的人應該穿著綠色或咖啡色等暗色系的衣服，並且壓低音量。他們應該待在步道上，用望遠鏡觀察，而不是走近鳥兒。餵食野鳥或使用相機閃光燈是不被允許的，因為這樣可能會驚嚇或傷害牠們。\n\n佩珊說，賞鳥教會了她耐心。你無法命令一隻鳥出現；你只能等待、仔細觀察，然後享受眼前出現的一切。賞鳥也改變了她看待溼地的方式；她以前認為溼地只是空蕩蕩、泥濘的土地。「現在當我看著一片溼地，」她說，「我看到的是一個家。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a student became interested in birdwatching through the black-faced spoonbill", "Why students should not go on school trips early in the morning", "How farmers in Tainan turned wetlands into farms and factories", "Why Korea is the best place in the world to watch birds"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文從佩珊第一次在七股看到黑面琵鷺寫起，介紹這種鳥的處境與覓食方式，再說明她加入賞鳥社團、學會耐心。(B) 她雖不想早起，但最後愛上賞鳥；(C) 溼地被開發只是第二段的背景；(D) 韓國只是部分黑面琵鷺飛來的地方，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did the number of black-faced spoonbills fall so low in the 1980s?", "options": ["Too many people caught them for food.", "Winters in East Asia became much colder.", "Their wetlands were turned into farms, factories and roads.", "Birdwatchers often used camera flashes and frightened them."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Their wetlands were being turned into farms, factories and roads, and the species seemed close to disappearing. 溼地被開發成農田、工廠和道路，使牠們幾乎消失。(A)(B) 文中未提及；(D) 閃光燈是第四段的賞鳥規則，並非數量減少的原因，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why can spoonbills find food in muddy water?", "options": ["They have very sharp eyes that can see through mud.", "They feed by feeling for food with their bills.", "They wait for the water to become clear in the daytime.", "They follow other birds that can find fish easily."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Because they feed by touch, they can find food in muddy water and even in the dark. 牠們把喙放在水中左右掃動，靠觸覺感受小魚小蝦。(A) 第三段明說牠們 instead of looking for food with their eyes；(C) 牠們常在夜間覓食，白天多在休息；(D) 文中未提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would a careful birdwatcher do?", "options": ["Wear a bright red jacket so that friends can find him easily", "Throw some bread into the water to bring the birds closer", "Use a camera flash to take clearer photos of the birds", "Stay on the path and watch the birds through binoculars"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：They should stay on the paths and use binoculars instead of walking closer. 待在步道上用望遠鏡觀察才是正確做法。(A) 規則是穿暗色系衣服；(B) 不可餵食野鳥；(C) 不可使用閃光燈，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What does Pei-shan mean when she says, \"I see a home\"?", "options": ["She wants to build a house near the wetland.", "She now understands that wetlands are where many animals live.", "She thinks wetlands look like the village she grew up in.", "She feels more comfortable in a wetland than in her own home."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說她以前認為溼地只是 empty, muddy land（空蕩蕩、泥濘的土地），現在卻看到「一個家」，可推知她明白了溼地是黑面琵鷺等野生動物生活的地方。(A)(C)(D) 都把 home 誤解為人的住所，與上下文不符，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a35", "title": "Twenty Moves or Fewer: My Year with the Rubik's Cube", "title_zh": "二十步以內：我與魔術方塊的一年", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Life", "Science"], "passage": "I got my first Rubik's Cube as a birthday present when I was fifteen. For a week, I turned it in every direction. I could finish one side, but whenever I tried to fix the second side, the first one fell apart. At the end of the week, I put the cube in a drawer and decided that it was a toy for geniuses only.\n\nMonths later, I learned that even its inventor had trouble at first. Ernő Rubik, a Hungarian professor of architecture, created the cube in 1974 to help his students understand how objects move in three dimensions. After he mixed it up for the first time, it took him about a month to solve it. The cube went on sale around the world in 1980 and became one of the best-selling toys in history.\n\nWhat surprised me most was the math behind it. A standard cube can be mixed into more than 43 quintillion different positions. That is 43 followed by eighteen zeros. Yet in 2010, a team of researchers used powerful computers to prove that every position can be solved in twenty moves or fewer, and that some positions need the full twenty. Cube fans call this number \"God's Number.\"\n\nOf course, I am not a computer. I took the cube out of the drawer again and watched online videos about the \"beginner's method.\" Instead of solving the whole cube at once, you solve it layer by layer, from the bottom to the top. For each step, you learn a short, fixed series of moves called an algorithm. At first I wrote the algorithms on cards and carried them everywhere. After two weeks, I could solve the cube in four minutes. Then my hands started to remember the moves, and my time dropped below one minute.\n\nToday my best time is 38 seconds. That is slow compared with top \"speedcubers,\" who can finish in under five seconds at official competitions, but I am proud of it. More importantly, the cube taught me something about learning. When a problem looks impossible, it often helps to break it into smaller steps and practice each one until it feels easy.", "passage_zh": "我十五歲生日時收到了人生第一顆魔術方塊。整整一個星期，我把它往各個方向轉來轉去。我可以完成一面，但每當我想拼好第二面時，第一面就又被打亂了。那個星期結束時，我把方塊收進抽屜，認定它是只有天才才能玩的玩具。\n\n幾個月後，我才知道就連它的發明者一開始也碰到了困難。匈牙利建築學教授魯比克‧艾爾諾在 1974 年發明了這個方塊，目的是幫助學生理解物體在三度空間中如何移動。他第一次把方塊轉亂之後，花了大約一個月才把它還原。1980 年，魔術方塊開始在全世界販售，成為史上最暢銷的玩具之一。\n\n最讓我驚訝的是它背後的數學。一顆標準的魔術方塊可以被轉成超過 4,300 京種不同的排列，也就是 43 後面再加上十八個零。然而在 2010 年，一群研究人員利用強大的電腦證明了：任何一種排列都能在二十步以內還原，而且有些排列確實需要整整二十步。方塊迷把這個數字稱為「上帝之數」。\n\n當然，我不是電腦。我又把方塊從抽屜裡拿出來，觀看介紹「初學者解法」的網路影片。這個方法不是一次解開整顆方塊，而是從底部到頂部，一層一層地解。每一個步驟，你都要學會一組簡短、固定的轉動順序，這叫做「公式」（algorithm）。一開始，我把公式寫在卡片上，走到哪裡都帶著。兩個星期後，我能在四分鐘內把方塊還原。後來我的雙手開始記住這些動作，我的時間也降到了一分鐘以內。\n\n如今我的最佳紀錄是 38 秒。和頂尖的「速解玩家」相比，這樣的速度很慢，他們在正式比賽中可以在五秒內完成，但我仍然以此為傲。更重要的是，魔術方塊教會了我一些關於學習的道理。當一個問題看起來不可能解決時，把它拆成幾個較小的步驟，再把每一步練習到駕輕就熟，往往會很有幫助。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The life story of a Hungarian professor of architecture", "How computers are used to win speedcubing competitions", "How the writer learned to solve the Rubik's Cube and what it taught the writer", "Why the Rubik's Cube is too difficult for most teenagers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文從作者十五歲時放棄方塊寫起，接著介紹方塊的由來與數學，再描述作者如何用初學者解法學會還原，最後說明從中學到的學習方法。(A) 魯比克只出現在第二段；(B) 電腦是用來證明上帝之數，不是用來比賽；(D) 與作者最後成功解開的經驗相反，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Ernő Rubik create the cube?", "options": ["To help his students understand how objects move in three dimensions", "To sell a new toy to children around the world", "To test which of his students were geniuses", "To prove that any puzzle can be solved in twenty moves"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：created the cube in 1974 to help his students understand how objects move in three dimensions. 他發明方塊是為了幫助學生理解物體在三度空間中的移動。(B) 1980 年才在全世界販售，並非發明的目的；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 二十步是 2010 年研究人員證明的，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is \"God's Number\"?", "options": ["The total number of positions a cube can be mixed into", "The largest number of moves needed to solve any position of the cube", "The writer's best time for solving the cube", "The number of algorithms in the beginner's method"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：every position can be solved in twenty moves or fewer, and that some positions need the full twenty. Cube fans call this number \"God's Number.\" 任何排列最多只要二十步就能還原，而且有些排列正好需要二十步，所以二十就是還原任何排列「最多需要的步數」，也就是上帝之數。(A) 是 43 quintillion 種排列；(C) 作者的紀錄是 38 秒；(D) 文中沒有說初學者解法有幾組公式，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 4, the word \"algorithm\" refers to ______.", "options": ["a video that teaches beginners", "a card that the writer carried everywhere", "one layer of the cube", "a fixed series of moves for one step"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：For each step, you learn a short, fixed series of moves called an algorithm. algorithm 在這裡指解某一步時要用的一組固定轉動順序（公式）。(A) 影片是學習的管道；(B) 卡片是作者用來寫公式的工具；(C) layer 是方塊的「一層」，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What lesson does the writer most likely want readers to learn?", "options": ["Only people who are good at math can enjoy puzzles.", "Computers will always solve problems better than people.", "A hard problem becomes easier when you divide it into small steps and practice.", "It is better to give up early when something looks impossible."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段最後一句：When a problem looks impossible, it often helps to break it into smaller steps and practice each one until it feels easy. 作者想傳達：把難題拆成小步驟並反覆練習，就會變得容易。(A)(B) 文中沒有這樣的主張；(D) 作者先放棄後又重新學習並成功，與此相反，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a36", "title": "Thin Air on Jade Mountain", "title_zh": "玉山上的稀薄空氣", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Sports", "Travel"], "passage": "At 3,952 meters, Jade Mountain, or Yushan, is the highest mountain in Taiwan, even higher than Japan's Mount Fuji. Every year, thousands of hikers climb it to watch the sun rise over a sea of clouds. Last spring, seventeen-year-old Hsin-yi and her father were two of them. They had trained for months by climbing stairs and hiking smaller mountains near Taipei. Still, the mountain had a surprise for them.\n\nThe most popular route starts at Tataka, at about 2,600 meters. From there, hikers walk 8.5 kilometers to Paiyun Lodge, at 3,402 meters, and spend the night. At around two or three in the morning, they get up and climb the last 2.4 kilometers in the dark to reach the top before sunrise.\n\nOn the first afternoon, Hsin-yi felt fine. But that evening at the lodge, she developed a terrible headache. She lost her appetite and could not sleep. Her father recognized the signs at once: altitude sickness. As people climb higher, the air gets thinner, so each breath brings in less oxygen, and the body needs time to adjust. Above about 2,500 meters, some people, even young and fit ones, get headaches, feel sick to their stomachs or have trouble sleeping. Being strong does not protect you; what matters most is how fast you go up.\n\nMountain guides give simple advice. Walk slowly, even if you feel strong. Drink plenty of water and rest when you need to. Tell your group as soon as you feel unwell instead of hiding it. Most importantly, if the symptoms get worse (for example, if a headache does not go away after rest or the person becomes confused), the only real cure is to go down to a lower altitude. No summit is worth risking your life for.\n\nHsin-yi drank some warm water, rested and told the lodge staff how she felt. By two in the morning, her headache had disappeared, so she set off slowly toward the peak with her father. They reached the summit just as the sky turned orange. \"I almost gave up,\" she said later. \"But I learned that the mountain decides how fast you can go, not you.\"", "passage_zh": "玉山海拔 3,952 公尺，是台灣最高的山，甚至比日本的富士山還要高。每年都有成千上萬的登山客攀登玉山，為的是看太陽從雲海中升起。去年春天，十七歲的欣怡和她的爸爸也是其中兩位。他們花了好幾個月的時間，靠爬樓梯和攀登台北附近較小的山來鍛鍊體能。即使如此，這座山還是給了他們一個意外。\n\n最熱門的路線從海拔約 2,600 公尺的塔塔加出發。登山客從那裡步行 8.5 公里，到海拔 3,402 公尺的排雲山莊過夜。大約凌晨兩三點，他們起床，在黑暗中走完最後的 2.4 公里，趕在日出前登頂。\n\n第一天下午，欣怡感覺還好。但那天晚上在山莊裡，她開始劇烈頭痛。她沒有食慾，也睡不著。她的爸爸立刻認出這些徵兆：高山症。人爬得越高，空氣就越稀薄，每一次呼吸吸進的氧氣也越少，而身體需要時間適應。在大約 2,500 公尺以上，有些人──即使是年輕、體能好的人──會頭痛、反胃或難以入睡。身體強壯並不能保護你；最重要的是你上升的速度有多快。\n\n登山嚮導的建議很簡單。就算覺得自己體力很好，也要慢慢走。多喝水，需要時就休息。一感到不舒服就要告訴隊友，不要隱瞞。最重要的是，如果症狀變得更嚴重，例如休息後頭痛仍然沒有消失，或是意識變得混亂，唯一真正的解方就是下撤到較低的海拔。沒有任何一座山頂值得你拿生命去冒險。\n\n欣怡喝了一些溫水、休息，並告訴山莊工作人員她的狀況。到了凌晨兩點，她的頭痛已經消失了，於是她和爸爸一起慢慢地朝山頂出發。他們抵達山頂時，天空正好染成一片橘紅。「我差點就放棄了，」她事後說，「但我學到了：能走多快，是由山決定的，不是由你決定。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To describe a climb up Jade Mountain and explain how to deal with altitude sickness", "To compare Jade Mountain with Mount Fuji in Japan", "To advertise mountain-climbing classes near Taipei", "To explain why the sky turns orange at sunrise"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以欣怡和爸爸攀登玉山的經過為主軸，第三段說明高山症的原因與症狀，第四段提出預防與處理的建議。(B) 富士山只在第一段比較高度時提到；(C) 台北附近的山只是欣怡事前訓練的地點，文中沒有推銷課程；(D) 天空變橘紅只是第五段登頂時的描寫，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How far do hikers walk from Paiyun Lodge to the summit of Jade Mountain?", "options": ["8.5 kilometers", "3.4 kilometers", "2.4 kilometers", "10.9 kilometers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：they get up and climb the last 2.4 kilometers in the dark to reach the top. 從排雲山莊到山頂是最後的 2.4 公里。(A) 8.5 公里是塔塔加到排雲山莊的距離；(B) 3,402 是山莊的海拔高度（公尺），不是距離；(D) 10.9 公里是塔塔加到山頂的總距離（8.5＋2.4），故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is a sign of altitude sickness mentioned in the passage?", "options": ["A sore throat", "Pain in the knees", "A high fever", "A headache and a loss of appetite"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：she developed a terrible headache. She lost her appetite and could not sleep. Her father recognized the signs at once: altitude sickness. 頭痛和沒有食慾都是高山症的徵兆。(A)(B)(C) 喉嚨痛、膝蓋痛、發高燒文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "At Paiyun Lodge, a hiker's headache does not go away after a long rest, and he begins to feel confused. What should he do?", "options": ["Climb faster so that he can reach the summit sooner", "Go down to a lower altitude as soon as possible", "Keep quiet so that his group will not worry", "Drink coffee and continue toward the peak"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：if a headache does not go away after rest or the person becomes confused, the only real cure is to go down to a lower altitude. 症狀惡化時唯一的解方是下撤到較低海拔。(A)(D) 繼續往上只會更危險；(C) 第四段明說要 tell your group... instead of hiding it，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does Hsin-yi mean when she says, \"the mountain decides how fast you can go, not you\"?", "options": ["Hikers must follow the speed that guides set for every group.", "The trail on Jade Mountain is too narrow for hikers to walk quickly.", "Climbers should adjust their speed to how their bodies react to the altitude.", "Strong hikers should always walk in front of weaker ones."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段指出 Being strong does not protect you; what matters most is how fast you go up，欣怡雖然事前認真訓練仍得了高山症，必須休息、放慢腳步才能登頂。可推知她的意思是：上山的速度要配合身體對高海拔的反應，而不是照自己的計畫或體力。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a37", "title": "The Spinning Bell: Our School's Diabolo Team", "title_zh": "旋轉的鈴：我們學校的扯鈴隊", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Sports"], "passage": "Every afternoon at four, the basketball court behind Hsing-hua Senior High School fills with a low humming sound. It comes from twenty diabolos spinning fast on strings tied between pairs of sticks. The school's diabolo team is practicing for its biggest show of the year: the school's 60th anniversary celebration, in front of more than a thousand students, parents and guests.\n\nThe diabolo has been played in China for hundreds of years. Traditional ones were made of bamboo, with small holes cut in the sides. As the toy spun, air rushed through the holes and made a whistling sound, which is why it was often compared to a bell. In the early 1900s, a French engineer made a new version and gave it the name \"diabolo.\" It quickly became a craze in Europe, where adults and children played it in parks. Today most diabolos are made of plastic, which makes them lighter and stronger.\n\nHow does a diabolo stay balanced on a thin string? The secret is spinning. A fast-spinning object tends to keep pointing in the same direction, which is also why a spinning top can stand on its tip. That is why players keep pulling the string to make the diabolo spin faster. When it slows down, it begins to tilt, and the player must correct it by moving the sticks forward or backward.\n\nThe team's coach, Mr. Kao, says the hobby teaches more than balance. \"Every new trick means dropping the diabolo a hundred times,\" he says. \"Students learn that failing is part of learning.\" Team captain Yu-chen agrees. When she joined in her first year, she could barely keep the diabolo spinning for ten seconds. Now she can throw it ten meters into the air, turn around once and catch it on the string.\n\nThe night before the show, Yu-chen admits she is nervous. \"If I drop it in front of everyone, I'll want to disappear,\" she says with a laugh. Then she picks up her sticks again. After all, she has dropped the diabolo thousands of times, and each time, she has picked it up.", "passage_zh": "每天下午四點，興華高中後方的籃球場就會響起一陣低沉的嗡嗡聲。這聲音來自二十顆扯鈴，它們在兩根棍子之間的繩子上快速旋轉。學校的扯鈴隊正在為一年中最盛大的表演練習：學校的六十週年校慶，屆時台下將有超過一千名學生、家長和來賓。\n\n扯鈴在中國已經流傳了好幾百年。傳統的扯鈴是用竹子做的，側邊開了小孔。扯鈴旋轉時，空氣從小孔衝出，發出像哨子般的聲響，所以常被比喻成鈴鐺。二十世紀初，一位法國工程師做出了新版本，並把它命名為「diabolo」。它很快就在歐洲風靡一時，大人和小孩都在公園裡玩。如今大多數的扯鈴是塑膠製的，因此更輕也更堅固。\n\n扯鈴是怎麼在細細的繩子上保持平衡的呢？祕訣就在於旋轉。快速旋轉的物體會傾向維持指向同一個方向，這也是陀螺能用尖端立起來的原因。所以玩家會不斷拉動繩子，讓扯鈴轉得更快。當它轉速變慢時，就會開始傾斜，玩家必須把棍子往前或往後移動來修正。\n\n扯鈴隊的高教練說，這項嗜好教會學生的不只是平衡。「每學一個新招式，都要讓扯鈴掉下來上百次，」他說，「學生會學到，失敗是學習的一部分。」隊長宇辰也同意這一點。她高一剛入隊時，幾乎沒辦法讓扯鈴持續轉上十秒鐘。現在她能把扯鈴拋到十公尺高，原地轉一圈，再用繩子接住。\n\n表演前一晚，宇辰承認她很緊張。「如果我在大家面前掉了扯鈴，我會想找個地洞鑽進去，」她笑著說。接著，她又拿起了扯鈴棍。畢竟，她已經讓扯鈴掉下來好幾千次了，而每一次，她都把它撿了起來。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to build a diabolo out of bamboo at home", "Why French engineers changed traditional Chinese toys", "Why students should not perform in front of large crowds", "A school diabolo team, the history of the toy and how it works"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段介紹興華高中扯鈴隊準備校慶表演，第二段說明扯鈴的歷史，第三段解釋它保持平衡的原理，第四、五段描寫教練和隊長的練習與心情。(A) 文中沒有教人自製扯鈴；(B) 法國工程師只是第二段的一個細節；(C) 與文意無關，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did traditional diabolos make a whistling sound?", "options": ["They had small bells tied inside them.", "Air rushed through holes in their sides as they spun.", "Players blew into them while playing.", "Their strings were made of thin metal."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：As the toy spun, air rushed through the holes and made a whistling sound. 傳統竹製扯鈴側邊有小孔，旋轉時空氣從孔中衝出而發出聲音。(A) 「像鈴鐺」只是比喻，裡面並沒有鈴鐺；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what keeps a diabolo balanced on the string?", "options": ["Spinning fast", "Being made of plastic", "Using a very thin string", "Holding the sticks very still"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：How does a diabolo stay balanced on a thin string? The secret is spinning. 讓扯鈴保持平衡的祕訣是快速旋轉。(B) 塑膠材質只讓它更輕、更堅固（第二段）；(C) 繩子細正是平衡困難的原因；(D) 第三段說扯鈴傾斜時玩家要前後移動棍子修正，並非靜止不動，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Yu-chen?", "options": ["She has never dropped the diabolo during practice.", "She joined the team only a few weeks ago.", "She has improved greatly through a lot of practice.", "She is the coach of the school's diabolo team."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說宇辰剛入隊時幾乎無法讓扯鈴轉上十秒，現在卻能拋到十公尺高再接住；第五段也提到她已經掉過好幾千次扯鈴。可推知她靠大量練習進步很多。(A) 與「掉了好幾千次」相反；(B) 她是高一入隊，現在已是隊長；(D) 教練是高教練，宇辰是隊長，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"barely\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["easily", "hardly", "happily", "often"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 she could barely keep the diabolo spinning for ten seconds，這句描述她剛入隊時程度很差，與後面「現在能拋到十公尺高」形成對比，barely 意思是「幾乎不能、勉強」，與 hardly 最接近。(A) easily 意思相反；(C)(D) 不合文意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a38", "title": "Wishes in the Night Sky: The Sky Lanterns of Pingxi", "title_zh": "夜空中的心願：平溪天燈", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Environment"], "passage": "Every year on the night of the Lantern Festival, the fifteenth day of the first month of the lunar calendar, thousands of glowing lanterns rise slowly into the dark sky over Pingxi, a small mountain district in New Taipei City. Visitors from Taiwan and abroad come here to watch the show and to send up wishes of their own.\n\nThe tradition goes back more than a hundred years. In the past, Pingxi was a poor area surrounded by mountains, and groups of robbers sometimes attacked the villages. When that happened, many villagers ran away to hide in the hills. After the danger had passed, the people who stayed behind would release lanterns into the sky. The lanterns told the families in the hills that it was safe to come home. For this reason, sky lanterns are sometimes called \"peace lanterns.\"\n\nA sky lantern works like a small hot-air balloon. It is made of thin paper on a light frame, with a piece of paper soaked in oil tied to the bottom. When the paper is lit, the air inside the lantern becomes hot. Hot air is lighter than the cooler air around it, so the lantern begins to rise. Before letting it go, visitors use brushes to write their wishes on the four sides, such as \"pass the exam,\" \"good health\" or \"a happy family.\"\n\nToday, the most popular place to send up a lantern is Shifen Old Street. Here, the tracks of the Pingxi Line run right through the middle of the street, with shops on both sides. Between trains, visitors stand on the tracks to light their lanterns. When a train is coming, shopkeepers shout a warning, and everyone quickly steps aside.\n\nHowever, not everyone is happy about the tradition. What goes up must come down, and burned-out lanterns often land in forests, rivers and farms. Some people worry that they could start fires or harm wild animals. To deal with the problem, the local government has offered small rewards to people who collect fallen lanterns, and some shops now sell lanterns made of materials that break down more easily in nature. In this way, Pingxi hopes to keep its tradition while protecting nature.", "passage_zh": "每年元宵節晚上，也就是農曆正月十五日，成千上萬盞發光的天燈會緩緩升上平溪的夜空。平溪是新北市一個位於山區的小行政區。來自台灣各地和國外的遊客都會來到這裡觀賞這場盛會，也親手放飛自己的心願。\n\n這項傳統可以追溯到一百多年前。過去，平溪是一個四面環山的貧窮地區，村莊不時會遭到成群的盜匪襲擊。每當這種情況發生，許多村民就會逃到山裡躲藏。等危險過去後，留在村裡的人就會把燈放上天空，用這些燈告訴躲在山裡的家人：現在可以安全回家了。因此，天燈有時被稱為「平安燈」。\n\n天燈的原理就像一個小型熱氣球。它是用薄紙糊在輕巧的骨架上做成的，底部綁著一張浸過油的紙。紙點燃後，燈內的空氣就會變熱。熱空氣比周圍較冷的空氣輕，所以天燈便開始上升。放手之前，遊客會用毛筆在天燈的四面寫下心願，例如「考試順利」、「身體健康」或「家庭幸福」。\n\n如今，最熱門的放天燈地點是十分老街。在這裡，平溪線的鐵軌就從街道正中央穿過，兩旁都是商店。趁兩班火車之間的空檔，遊客會站在鐵軌上點燃天燈。火車快來的時候，店家會大聲提醒，大家便趕緊退到一旁。\n\n然而，並不是每個人都喜歡這項傳統。有升就有落，燒完的天燈常常掉進森林、河流和農田裡。有些人擔心天燈可能引發火災或傷害野生動物。為了解決這個問題，當地政府提供小獎勵給撿拾掉落天燈的人，有些店家也開始販售用在大自然中較容易分解的材料做成的天燈。平溪希望藉由這些方法，既能保留這項傳統，也能保護大自然。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why robbers often attacked villages in Pingxi long ago", "The history, science and problems of Pingxi's sky lanterns", "How the Pingxi Line was built through Shifen Old Street", "Why visitors should stop sending up sky lanterns"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文依序介紹平溪天燈的由來（第二段）、飛上天的原理（第三段）、熱門地點（第四段），以及帶來的環境問題與解決方法（第五段）。(A) 盜匪只是第二段說明由來的背景；(C) 第四段只提到鐵軌穿過老街，並未說明鐵路如何興建；(D) 第五段說平溪希望保留傳統並保護環境，並沒有主張停止放天燈。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did people in Pingxi first send sky lanterns into the sky?", "options": ["To celebrate a good harvest of rice", "To frighten the robbers away from the village", "To ask the gods to help them pass exams", "To tell villagers hiding in the hills that it was safe to return"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：After the danger had passed, the people who stayed behind would release lanterns... The lanterns told the families in the hills that it was safe to come home. 天燈最初是用來通知躲在山裡的家人可以平安回家。(A) 文中未提及；(B) 天燈是在危險過去後才放，並非用來嚇走盜匪；(C) 祈求考試順利是現代遊客寫的心願（第三段），不是天燈的由來。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"release\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["let go of", "pick up", "paint", "repair"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 the people who stayed behind would release lanterns into the sky，是把天燈「放」上天空；第三段 Before letting it go 描述的也是同一個動作，所以 release 與 let go of（放開、放走）意思最接近。(B) 撿起、(C) 彩繪、(D) 修理都不符合文意。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Based on paragraph 3, what would most likely happen if the air inside a sky lantern did not get hot?", "options": ["It would rise even faster.", "It would burn more brightly.", "It would not be able to rise.", "It would carry more wishes."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說明 Hot air is lighter than the cooler air around it, so the lantern begins to rise. 天燈是靠燈內空氣變熱、比外面的空氣輕才能上升，可推知空氣若沒有變熱，天燈就飛不起來。(A) 與原理相反；(B)(D) 都與第三段的原理無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, what is one way people are dealing with the problems caused by sky lanterns?", "options": ["Allowing lanterns to be sent up only from the train tracks", "Offering small rewards to people who collect fallen lanterns", "Letting people send up lanterns only on the night of the Lantern Festival", "Asking visitors to write shorter wishes on their lanterns"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：the local government has offered small rewards to people who collect fallen lanterns，另外也有店家販售較容易分解的天燈。(A) 第四段只說十分老街的遊客在鐵軌上放天燈，並不是規定；(C)(D) 文中均未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a39", "title": "Nine Days on Foot: Walking with Mazu", "title_zh": "徒步九天：與媽祖同行", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Society"], "passage": "Every spring, a goddess goes on a long walk through central Taiwan, and hundreds of thousands of people follow her. This is the Dajia Mazu Pilgrimage, a religious journey and one of the biggest festivals in Taiwan. For nine days and eight nights, a statue of Mazu rides in a sedan chair, a seat on poles carried by several people, from Jenn Lann Temple in Dajia, Taichung, to a temple in Chiayi County and back again. The whole route is more than 300 kilometers long and passes through four cities and counties.\n\nMazu is a goddess of the sea. According to legend, she was a young woman named Lin Mo who lived on an island off the coast of China about a thousand years ago and helped save fishermen in storms. Because early Chinese settlers had to cross the dangerous Taiwan Strait to reach Taiwan, many of them prayed to Mazu for a safe trip. Today, there are hundreds of Mazu temples across the island.\n\nThe dates of the pilgrimage change every year. On the night of the Lantern Festival, temple leaders ask Mazu to choose them by throwing moon blocks, two curved wooden pieces that give a \"yes\" or \"no\" answer depending on how they land. Once the dates are announced, believers start making plans to join.\n\nLast spring, seventeen-year-old Hsu Yi-ting walked with Mazu for two days. \"My feet hurt after the first ten kilometers,\" she said, \"but I couldn't believe how kind people were.\" Along the road, local families and shops handed out free water, rice balls and snacks, and some volunteers even offered foot massages. Yi-ting also saw people kneel in a long line on the road so that Mazu's sedan chair could pass over them. They believe this brings blessings and good health.\n\nThe pilgrimage has also changed with the times. These days, Mazu's location is tracked by GPS and shown online, so followers can check on their phones where she is and when she will reach their town. For Yi-ting, though, technology was not the most important part. \"Walking with so many strangers made me feel that we were all one big family,\" she said. \"After I graduate, I want to walk the whole way.\"", "passage_zh": "每年春天，都有一位女神在中台灣展開一趟漫長的步行旅程，而且有數十萬人跟隨著她。這就是大甲媽祖遶境進香，它是一趟宗教之旅，也是台灣規模最大的節慶之一。在九天八夜之中，媽祖神像坐在神轎上——也就是由好幾個人用長桿抬著的座椅——從台中大甲的鎮瀾宮出發，前往嘉義縣的一座廟宇，再折返回來。全程超過三百公里，途經四個縣市。\n\n媽祖是海上的女神。根據傳說，她本名林默，是大約一千年前住在中國沿海一座島上的年輕女子，曾在暴風雨中幫忙拯救漁民。由於早期的中國移民必須橫渡危險的台灣海峽才能抵達台灣，所以許多人會向媽祖祈求一路平安。如今，全台各地有數百座媽祖廟。\n\n遶境進香的日期每年都不同。在元宵節晚上，廟方負責人會擲筊請媽祖決定日期。筊是兩塊彎月形的木片，依照落地的方式來表示「是」或「否」。日期一公布，信徒們就開始計畫參加。\n\n去年春天，十七歲的徐以婷跟著媽祖走了兩天。「才走了十公里我的腳就開始痛了，」她說，「但我不敢相信大家竟然這麼親切。」沿路上，當地的住家和商店免費發送開水、飯糰和點心，有些志工甚至提供腳底按摩。以婷還看到人們在路上跪成一長排，讓媽祖的神轎從他們上方經過。他們相信這樣做能帶來保佑和健康。\n\n遶境進香也隨著時代改變。現在，媽祖的位置會以 GPS 追蹤並公布在網路上，信徒可以用手機查看她的位置，以及什麼時候會抵達自己的城鎮。不過對以婷來說，科技並不是最重要的部分。「跟這麼多陌生人一起走路，讓我覺得大家就像一個大家庭，」她說，「畢業以後，我想要走完全程。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The life story of a young woman named Lin Mo", "How temples use GPS to keep track of their visitors", "A long religious walk in honor of Mazu and the people who join it", "Why moon blocks are used in Taiwanese temples"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹大甲媽祖遶境進香：路線與天數（第一段）、媽祖的由來（第二段）、日期的決定方式（第三段），以及參加者以婷的經驗（第四、五段）。(A) 林默只在第二段簡短提及；(B) GPS 追蹤的是媽祖的位置，不是遊客，而且只是第五段的細節；(D) 擲筊只是第三段的細節。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How long does the Dajia Mazu Pilgrimage last?", "options": ["Two days", "Eight days", "Ten days", "Nine days"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：For nine days and eight nights, a statue of Mazu rides in a sedan chair... 遶境進香共九天八夜。(A) 兩天是以婷參加的天數（第四段）；(B)「八」是夜晚的數目；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did many early Chinese settlers pray to Mazu?", "options": ["They had to cross a dangerous sea to get to Taiwan.", "They wanted her to choose the dates of the pilgrimage.", "They hoped she would help them find jobs in Taiwan.", "They believed she could stop robbers from attacking them."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Because early Chinese settlers had to cross the dangerous Taiwan Strait to reach Taiwan, many of them prayed to Mazu for a safe trip. 早期移民要橫渡危險的台灣海峽，所以向海上女神祈求平安。(B) 擲筊選日期是現代遶境的儀式（第三段）；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 3, what does \"they\" in \"depending on how they land\" refer to?", "options": ["The temple leaders", "The moon blocks", "The believers", "The dates"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 throwing moon blocks, two curved wooden pieces that give a \"yes\" or \"no\" answer depending on how they land，會「落地」並依落地方式表示是或否的，是被擲出的筊，因此 they 指 moon blocks。(A) 廟方負責人是擲筊的人；(C) 信徒在下一句才出現；(D) 日期不會落地。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Yi-ting from the last two paragraphs?", "options": ["She has already walked the whole route once.", "She found the walk easy and not tiring at all.", "She was touched by people's kindness and hopes to join again.", "She thinks the GPS was the best part of the trip."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段她說 I couldn't believe how kind people were，第五段又說和陌生人同行讓她覺得 we were all one big family，並表示 After I graduate, I want to walk the whole way，可推知她被沿途的善意感動，並希望再次參加。(A) 她這次只走了兩天；(B) 她說走了十公里腳就痛了；(D) 第五段說 technology was not the most important part。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a40", "title": "Back on Track: The Alishan Forest Railway", "title_zh": "重返軌道：阿里山林業鐵路", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "History"], "passage": "In July 2024, a train full of passengers pulled into Alishan Station, high in the mountains of Chiayi County, and the people waiting there cheered loudly. It was the first time in fifteen years that a train had made the whole trip from Chiayi City up to Alishan. For many people in Taiwan, it felt as if an old friend had finally come home.\n\nThe railway was built during the period of Japanese rule, and trains began running on it in the early 1910s. At first, its main job was not to carry tourists but to bring timber down from the mountains. Alishan was covered with huge, ancient cypress trees, and their strong, sweet-smelling wood was very valuable for building. For decades, trains carried logs down the mountain, and large areas of forest were cut down. As the old forests disappeared, the railway slowly became a line for visitors instead.\n\nTrain lovers around the world know this railway well. In about 70 kilometers, it climbs from around 30 meters above sea level to more than 2,000 meters. Since a train cannot climb a very steep slope directly, the builders used clever designs. At one mountain, the track goes around and around like a spring, circling it three times as it rises. In other places, the train climbs in a Z shape: it moves forward, stops, and then goes backward up the next part of the track. Through the windows, passengers can watch the forest change from bamboo and other warm-weather plants to cool, misty forests of tall trees.\n\nIn August 2009, Typhoon Morakot brought record amounts of rain to southern Taiwan. Landslides destroyed bridges and tunnels and buried parts of the track. Repairs were slow and difficult because the mountains are steep and earthquakes and storms happen often. Section by section, workers rebuilt the line until the whole railway could finally open again.\n\nToday, visitors ride the train to watch the sunrise over a \"sea of clouds,\" walk among giant trees that are more than a thousand years old, and taste the famous high-mountain tea grown nearby. On weekends and holidays, seats often sell out quickly, so travelers are advised to book their tickets early.", "passage_zh": "2024 年 7 月，一列載滿乘客的火車駛進位於嘉義縣高山上的阿里山車站，在那裡等候的人們大聲歡呼。這是十五年來第一次有火車從嘉義市一路開上阿里山，跑完全程。對許多台灣人來說，這種感覺就像一位老朋友終於回家了。\n\n這條鐵路建於日治時期，在 1910 年代初期開始通車。起初，它的主要任務不是載運觀光客，而是把木材從山上運下來。當時阿里山上長滿了巨大的古老檜木，這些木材堅固又帶有香氣，是非常珍貴的建材。數十年間，火車不斷把原木運下山，大片森林也因此遭到砍伐。隨著古老的森林逐漸消失，這條鐵路也慢慢轉變成載運遊客的路線。\n\n世界各地的鐵道迷都很熟悉這條鐵路。在大約七十公里的路程中，它從海拔約三十公尺爬升到兩千多公尺。由於火車無法直接爬上非常陡的坡，建造者採用了巧妙的設計。在某座山上，鐵軌像彈簧一樣一圈又一圈地環繞著山，繞了三圈慢慢往上爬。在其他路段，火車則以 Z 字形爬升：先往前開，停下來，再倒退開上下一段鐵軌。乘客可以從車窗看著外面的森林，從竹林和其他喜歡溫暖氣候的植物，逐漸變成涼爽、雲霧繚繞的高大森林。\n\n2009 年 8 月，莫拉克颱風為南台灣帶來破紀錄的雨量。山崩摧毀了橋梁和隧道，也掩埋了部分鐵軌。由於山勢陡峭，地震和暴風雨又經常發生，修復工程既緩慢又艱難。工人們一段一段地重建鐵路，直到全線終於能夠重新通車。\n\n如今，遊客搭火車上山，觀賞「雲海」上的日出、漫步在樹齡超過一千年的巨木之間，並品嚐附近種植的知名高山茶。週末和假日的座位常常很快就賣完，因此建議旅客及早訂票。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history, design and return of a famous mountain railway", "How high-mountain tea is grown in Alishan", "Why Typhoon Morakot was the worst storm in Taiwan's history", "The daily life of workers who cut down trees in Alishan"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹阿里山林業鐵路：2024 年全線恢復通車（第一段）、興建的歷史（第二段）、爬山的特殊設計（第三段）、颱風損毀與修復（第四段）以及今日的觀光（第五段）。(B) 高山茶只在第五段順帶提到；(C) 文中只說莫拉克颱風帶來破紀錄的雨量，沒說它是史上最嚴重的風災；(D) 文中沒有描述伐木工人的生活。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What was the railway's main purpose when it was first built?", "options": ["To take tourists to see the sunrise", "To carry soldiers into the mountains", "To bring tea leaves to Chiayi City", "To carry wood down from the mountains"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：At first, its main job was not to carry tourists but to bring timber down from the mountains. 起初主要是把木材運下山。(A) 載觀光客是後來才有的用途；(B)(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"timber\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["fresh fruit", "wood used for building", "heavy rain", "wild animals"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段接著說阿里山檜木 their strong, sweet-smelling wood was very valuable for building，而且火車把 logs（原木）運下山，可知 timber 指「木材」，即 wood used for building。(A)(C)(D) 都與上下文無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, how does the train climb the mountain in a Z shape?", "options": ["It circles the same mountain three times.", "It goes through a long tunnel under the mountain.", "It moves forward, stops, and then goes backward up the next part of the track.", "It is pulled up the slope by a strong rope."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：the train climbs in a Z shape: it moves forward, stops, and then goes backward up the next part of the track. (A) 繞山三圈是另一種像彈簧的「螺旋」設計，不是 Z 字形；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 4?", "options": ["The railway was repaired in only a few months.", "Earthquakes caused more damage to the railway than Typhoon Morakot.", "Workers decided to build a completely new railway somewhere else.", "The railway was closed for a long time because the damage was serious and hard to repair."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說莫拉克颱風引起的山崩沖毀橋梁、隧道並掩埋鐵軌，而且 Repairs were slow and difficult；再對照第一段「十五年來第一次」跑完全程，可推知鐵路因損壞嚴重、修復困難而停駛很久。(A) 與「十五年」不符；(B) 文中沒有比較；(C) 工人是 Section by section... rebuilt the line，並非另建新鐵路。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a41", "title": "Hearts in the Sea: Penghu's Stone Fish Traps", "title_zh": "海中的愛心：澎湖的石滬", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Nature"], "passage": "If you stand on a cliff on Qimei Island in Penghu at low tide, you will see one of the most photographed sights in Taiwan: two heart shapes made of stone walls, lying side by side in the shallow sea. This is the Twin Hearts Stone Weir. It looks like a romantic work of art, but it was actually built as a clever trap for catching fish.\n\nA stone weir is a low wall of stones built in the sea near the shore. The idea is simple. When the tide comes in, seawater covers the walls, and fish swim over them to look for food. When the tide goes out, the water flows away through the gaps between the stones, but the fish are trapped inside the walls. Fishermen can then walk in and catch them with nets or even by hand. No boats, no fuel and no bait are needed.\n\nPenghu has more than 500 stone weirs, one of the largest numbers in the world. They were built with two kinds of stone found on the islands: dark basalt, a hard rock formed from volcanic lava, and coral stone, which is light and full of small holes. Building a weir took a lot of work, so it was usually shared by several families. The families took turns collecting the fish, and they all worked together to repair the walls after storms.\n\nIn the past, a weir could catch large numbers of fish during a good season. However, as modern fishing boats caught more and more fish, fewer fish came near the shore. At the same time, many young people moved to big cities on the main island of Taiwan to find jobs. With no one to look after them, many weirs were slowly broken down by the waves.\n\nToday, local people are trying to save this part of their history. Older fishermen are teaching volunteers how to fit the stones together without cement, and some weirs have been rebuilt. Visitors can also join guided tours and walk inside a weir at low tide. If you plan to see the Twin Hearts, remember to check the tide times first. At high tide, the famous hearts disappear under the water.", "passage_zh": "如果你在退潮時站在澎湖七美島的懸崖上，就能看到台灣最常被拍攝的景點之一：兩個由石牆圍成的愛心，並排躺在淺淺的海水中。這就是雙心石滬。它看起來像一件浪漫的藝術品，但其實是一個用來捕魚的巧妙陷阱。\n\n石滬是建在海岸附近海中的低矮石牆。原理很簡單。漲潮時，海水淹過石牆，魚兒會游過牆來覓食。退潮時，海水從石頭之間的縫隙流走，魚卻被困在石牆裡面。漁民接著就可以走進去，用網子甚至徒手把魚抓起來。不需要船、不需要燃料，也不需要魚餌。\n\n澎湖有五百多座石滬，數量名列世界前茅。石滬是用島上的兩種石頭砌成的：一種是深色的玄武岩，是由火山熔岩形成的堅硬岩石；另一種是珊瑚石，重量輕，上面布滿小孔。蓋一座石滬需要花費很多功夫，所以石滬通常由好幾戶人家共有。這些家庭輪流收魚，暴風雨過後也一起合力修補石牆。\n\n過去，在漁獲好的季節，一座石滬可以捕到大量的魚。然而，隨著現代漁船捕撈的魚越來越多，靠近岸邊的魚就變少了。同時，許多年輕人搬到台灣本島的大城市找工作。由於沒有人照顧，許多石滬就慢慢被海浪沖毀了。\n\n如今，當地人正努力保存這段歷史。年長的漁民教導志工如何不用水泥就把石頭堆疊在一起，有些石滬已經重建完成。遊客也可以參加導覽行程，在退潮時走進石滬裡。如果你打算去看雙心石滬，記得先查好潮汐時間。漲潮時，著名的雙心就會消失在海面下。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Penghu is the best place in Taiwan to go fishing by boat.", "Stone weirs are an old and clever way of fishing that people are now trying to protect.", "Young people in Penghu would rather work as fishermen than move away.", "The Twin Hearts Stone Weir was built as a work of art for tourists."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文介紹石滬的捕魚原理（第二段）、建造與共同管理的方式（第三段）、沒落的原因（第四段），以及當地人如何保存修復（第五段）。(A) 石滬捕魚 No boats... are needed，不是坐船捕魚；(C) 第四段說許多年輕人搬到大城市找工作；(D) 第一段說它看起來像藝術品，但其實是捕魚的陷阱。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How does a stone weir catch fish?", "options": ["Fishermen put bait inside the walls to attract fish.", "Boats push the fish toward the stone walls.", "Fish are caught in nets hanging on the walls.", "Fish swim in at high tide and cannot get out when the water goes down."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：When the tide comes in... fish swim over them... When the tide goes out... the fish are trapped inside the walls. 魚在漲潮時游進來，退潮時被困住。(A)(B) 與 No boats, no fuel and no bait are needed 矛盾；(C) 網子是漁民走進去抓魚時用的，不是掛在牆上的。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"shallow\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["not deep", "very cold", "dirty", "far away"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段說退潮時可以看到石牆 lying side by side in the shallow sea，第二段也說漁民可以直接 walk in 走進石滬抓魚，可知那裡的海水很淺，shallow 即 not deep。(B)(C)(D) 文中均無相關線索。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why were many stone weirs broken down?", "options": ["The government told the families to stop using them.", "Tourists climbed on the walls to take photos.", "Fewer fish came near the shore and young people left, so no one took care of the weirs.", "Strong earthquakes destroyed most of the walls."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：fewer fish came near the shore... many young people moved to big cities... With no one to look after them, many weirs were slowly broken down by the waves. 魚變少、年輕人離開，石滬沒人照顧而被海浪沖毀。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Based on the passage, what should a visitor do in order to see the Twin Hearts clearly?", "options": ["Check the tide times and go when the tide is low", "Rent a boat and sail around Qimei Island", "Go at high tide when the water is deepest", "Join the volunteers who rebuild the walls"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：remember to check the tide times first. At high tide, the famous hearts disappear under the water. 第一段也說要在 low tide 時從懸崖上觀看，所以應先查潮汐，在退潮時前往。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 漲潮時雙心會消失在水面下；(D) 參加修復與能否看清雙心無關。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a42", "title": "A Town Shaped by Clay: Yingge", "title_zh": "陶土塑造的小鎮：鶯歌", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Arts", "Culture"], "passage": "When Chen Kai-wen's art class visited Yingge last spring, he expected an easy day. Their teacher had promised that everyone could make a bowl on a pottery wheel. \"How hard can it be?\" Kai-wen said. Twenty minutes later, his third bowl had fallen apart, and his hands and shirt were covered in wet clay.\n\nYingge, a district in New Taipei City, is often called Taiwan's \"pottery town.\" Its history with clay began about 200 years ago, when settlers from Fujian, China, found good clay in the area. There was also coal nearby, which could be burned to heat kilns, the very hot ovens used to harden pottery. With both clay and fuel close at hand, more and more workshops opened. By the 1970s and 1980s, Yingge's factories were producing huge numbers of bowls, cups, tiles and vases, and many of them were sold overseas.\n\nLater, as costs rose, many factories moved to other countries, and the town had to find a new way forward. Instead of making cheap goods in large numbers, Yingge turned to art and tourism. In 2000, the Yingge Ceramics Museum opened. It was the first museum in Taiwan built to tell the story of ceramics, and it shows everything from simple farm jars to modern works of art. Nearby, shops on Yingge Old Street sell handmade teapots and cups, and many let visitors try making pottery themselves.\n\nThe town's name comes from a large rock on a hill above it. People thought the rock looked like a parrot, a bird that can be called \"yingge\" in Chinese. According to an old story, the bird was once a monster that breathed out thick fog so that soldiers got lost in the hills. The famous general Koxinga fired a cannon at it, and the monster turned into stone. Visitors can still hike up the hill to see the rock today.\n\nIn the end, Kai-wen managed to make one small, slightly uneven bowl. It had to be dried and then fired in a kiln, so it was mailed to his home a month later. \"It's not perfect,\" he said, \"but now whenever I see a cup in a shop, I think about how much skill it takes to make one.\"", "passage_zh": "去年春天，陳凱文的美術班到鶯歌校外教學時，他原本以為這會是輕鬆的一天。老師答應大家，每個人都可以用拉坯機做一個碗。「能有多難？」凱文說。二十分鐘後，他做的第三個碗也垮了，雙手和上衣都沾滿了濕濕的陶土。\n\n鶯歌是新北市的一個行政區，常被稱為台灣的「陶瓷之鎮」。它和陶土的淵源始於大約兩百年前，當時從中國福建來的移民在這一帶發現了優質的黏土。附近還產煤，可以拿來燒窯；窯是用來把陶器燒硬的高溫爐子。黏土和燃料都近在手邊，於是開設的工坊越來越多。到了 1970 和 1980 年代，鶯歌的工廠大量生產碗、杯子、磁磚和花瓶，其中許多都外銷到國外。\n\n後來，隨著成本上升，許多工廠遷到其他國家，小鎮必須另尋出路。鶯歌不再大量生產便宜的商品，而是轉向藝術和觀光發展。2000 年，鶯歌陶瓷博物館開幕。這是台灣第一座專門講述陶瓷故事的博物館，展出的作品從樸素的農家陶甕到現代藝術品都有。附近鶯歌老街上的商店販售手工茶壺和杯子，許多店家也讓遊客親手體驗做陶。\n\n這個小鎮的名字來自山丘上的一塊大石頭。人們覺得這塊石頭長得像鸚鵡，而鸚鵡在中文裡也可以叫做「鸚哥」。根據古老的傳說，這隻鳥曾經是一頭怪物，會吐出濃霧讓士兵在山中迷路。著名的將領鄭成功朝它發射大砲，怪物就變成了石頭。今天，遊客仍然可以爬上山丘去看這塊石頭。\n\n最後，凱文終於做出一個小小的、有點歪的碗。這個碗必須先晾乾，再放進窯裡燒製，所以一個月後才寄到他家。「它不完美，」他說，「但現在每當我在店裡看到一個杯子，我就會想到做一個杯子需要多少功夫。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to make a perfect bowl on a pottery wheel", "Why many factories in Taiwan moved overseas", "A pottery town's history and a student's visit there", "How Koxinga turned a monster into stone"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文以凱文到鶯歌做陶的經驗開頭與結尾（第一、五段），中間介紹鶯歌陶瓷業的起源（第二段）、轉型發展藝術與觀光（第三段）以及地名的由來（第四段）。(A) 凱文最後只做出一個 slightly uneven bowl，文章並沒有教人做出完美的碗；(B) 工廠外移只是第三段的背景；(D) 鄭成功的傳說只是第四段說明地名由來的細節。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did pottery workshops grow in Yingge about 200 years ago?", "options": ["Good clay and coal for fuel could both be found nearby.", "The government built a large museum there.", "Many tourists wanted to buy handmade teapots.", "Factories moved there from other countries."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：settlers... found good clay in the area. There was also coal nearby... With both clay and fuel close at hand, more and more workshops opened. 當地同時有黏土和燃料。(B) 博物館到 2000 年才開幕；(C) 觀光是後來才有的發展；(D) 第三段說的是工廠遷往其他國家，方向正好相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "How did Yingge change after many factories moved away?", "options": ["It started producing even more cheap goods.", "It became a center for coal mining.", "It closed all of its pottery shops.", "It focused on art and tourism instead."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Instead of making cheap goods in large numbers, Yingge turned to art and tourism. (A) 與原文相反；(B) 煤只在第二段作為燃料提及；(C) 老街上仍有許多陶瓷商店。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"fired\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["made to leave a job", "heated to become hard", "shot with a gun", "painted with colors"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段 It had to be dried and then fired in a kiln，對照第二段 kilns, the very hot ovens used to harden pottery，可知 fired 指在窯中「燒製」，讓陶器變硬。(A)「被開除」和 (C)「開槍」都是 fire 的其他意思，但不符合此處上下文；(D) 文中未提及上色。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How does Kai-wen most likely feel at the end of the passage?", "options": ["He respects the skill that pottery makers have.", "He is angry that his bowl is not perfect.", "He thinks making pottery is a waste of time.", "He wants to open a pottery factory overseas."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段凱文說 now whenever I see a cup in a shop, I think about how much skill it takes to make one，可見他體會到做陶需要高超的技術，對製陶的人多了一份敬意。(B) 他承認碗不完美，但語氣並不生氣；(C)(D) 文中沒有線索。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a43", "title": "Snow in May: The Tung Blossoms of Hakka Country", "title_zh": "五月雪：客家庄的桐花", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Nature"], "passage": "Snow in May? In most of Taiwan, that sounds impossible. Yet every April and May, parts of the hills in northern and central Taiwan turn white. Small white flowers fall from the trees and cover hiking paths, roads and streams like a thin layer of snow. People call this sight \"May snow.\" The flowers come from the tung tree, which has become a symbol of Hakka culture.\n\nTung trees are not native to Taiwan. They were brought over from southern China, and many were planted during the period of Japanese rule because their seeds produce a very useful oil. Tung oil dries into a hard, waterproof surface, so it was used in paint and to protect wood, boats and paper umbrellas. Many Hakka families lived in the hills where the trees grew, and collecting the seeds became an important way for them to earn money. The seeds, however, are poisonous and must never be eaten.\n\nIn the decades after World War II, cheaper man-made products took the place of tung oil, and the trees were no longer worth much. Since nobody bothered to cut them down, they kept growing on the hillsides. Then, in 2002, the government department in charge of Hakka affairs decided to celebrate the flowers with a festival. The Hakka Tung Blossom Festival was a great success. Today, it brings large numbers of visitors every spring to Hakka towns in places such as Miaoli, Hsinchu and Taoyuan.\n\nThe festival is about more than flowers. Visitors can enjoy Hakka music, walk on trails under the blooming trees, and taste Hakka food such as lei cha, a drink made by grinding tea leaves, nuts and seeds into a powder and adding hot water. Local farmers and shops also sell crafts and snacks, so the festival helps small hill towns earn more income.\n\nIf you plan to go, remember a few simple rules. The best time to see the falling flowers is usually from mid-April to early May. Stay on the paths, and never shake the trees to make the flowers fall faster. The flowers on the ground should be left where they are, so that other visitors can enjoy the \"snow,\" too.", "passage_zh": "五月下雪？在台灣大部分地區，這聽起來根本不可能。然而每年四、五月，台灣北部和中部的部分山丘都會變成一片雪白。小小的白花從樹上飄落，覆蓋在登山步道、道路和溪流上，就像一層薄薄的雪。人們稱這幅景象為「五月雪」。這些花來自油桐樹，而油桐樹已經成為客家文化的象徵。\n\n油桐樹並不是台灣原生的樹種。它們是從中國南方引進的，許多是在日治時期種下的，因為它們的種子可以榨出非常有用的油。桐油乾了以後會形成堅硬又防水的表層，所以被用在油漆中，也用來保護木材、船隻和紙傘。許多客家家庭住在油桐樹生長的山區，採集桐籽便成為他們重要的收入來源。不過，桐籽有毒，千萬不能吃。\n\n第二次世界大戰後的數十年間，較便宜的人造產品取代了桐油，油桐樹也就不再值錢了。由於沒有人費心去砍，它們就繼續在山坡上生長。後來在 2002 年，負責客家事務的政府機關決定為這些花舉辦一個節慶。客家桐花祭非常成功，如今每年春天都為苗栗、新竹和桃園等地的客家城鎮帶來大批遊客。\n\n桐花祭不只是賞花而已。遊客可以欣賞客家音樂、在盛開的油桐樹下沿著步道散步，還可以品嚐客家美食，例如擂茶。擂茶是把茶葉、堅果和種子磨成粉，再加入熱水做成的飲品。當地的農民和商店也會販賣手工藝品和點心，因此桐花祭能幫助山區小鎮增加收入。\n\n如果你打算前往，請記得幾個簡單的規則。觀賞落花的最佳時間通常是四月中旬到五月初。請走在步道上，千萬不要為了讓花快點掉下來而搖晃樹木。地上的落花應該留在原處，讓其他遊客也能欣賞這片「雪景」。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history of tung trees and the Hakka festival for their flowers", "The way tung oil is made from poisonous seeds", "The reason it sometimes snows in Taiwan's hills in May", "The traditional Hakka drink made from tea and nuts"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹油桐花「五月雪」的景象（第一段）、油桐樹引進台灣的經過與桐油的用途（第二段）、客家桐花祭的由來與活動（第三、四段），以及賞花須知（第五段）。(B) 第二段只提到桐籽能產油而且有毒，並未說明製油方法；(C) 「五月雪」指的是像雪一樣飄落的白色桐花，並不是真的下雪；(D) 擂茶只是第四段提到的一種客家美食。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what was tung oil used for?", "options": ["Cooking Hakka food", "Making medicine for colds", "Protecting things from water", "Lighting lanterns during festivals"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Tung oil dries into a hard, waterproof surface, so it was used in paint and to protect wood, boats and paper umbrellas. 桐油用來保護物品、讓東西防水。(A) 第二段特別提醒桐籽有毒，不能吃；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did tung trees stop being valuable?", "options": ["The trees stopped producing seeds.", "Cheaper man-made products replaced tung oil.", "The government cut most of them down.", "Hakka families moved away from the hills."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：cheaper man-made products took the place of tung oil, and the trees were no longer worth much. (A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 第三段說 nobody bothered to cut them down，與此相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"native\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["originally growing in a place", "very expensive", "easy to plant", "dangerous to eat"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段 Tung trees are not native to Taiwan. They were brought over from southern China，後一句說油桐是從中國南方引進的，可知 native 指「原生的、本來就生長在某地的」。(B)(C) 文中沒有相關線索；(D) 有毒的是桐籽，與 native 的意思無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which visitor is following the advice in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Amy shakes a tree so her friend can take a photo of the falling flowers.", "Ben fills a bag with flowers from the ground to take home.", "Cindy leaves the path to get closer to the tallest tree.", "David stays on the path and leaves the fallen flowers where they are."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段提醒：Stay on the paths, and never shake the trees... The flowers on the ground should be left where they are. 只有 David 走在步道上，並讓落花留在原處。(A) 搖樹、(B) 把落花帶回家、(C) 離開步道，都違反了建議。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a44", "title": "Lights on the Water: The Keelung Ghost Festival", "title_zh": "水上的燈火：基隆中元祭", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "History"], "passage": "In many cultures, people fear ghosts. In Taiwan, however, people often treat them more like guests who need to be looked after. According to Taiwanese folk beliefs, the gates of the world of the dead open on the first day of the seventh lunar month, and spirits return to the world of the living for a whole month. This time is known as Ghost Month, and one of its most famous celebrations takes place in the port city of Keelung.\n\nThe Keelung Ghost Festival began in the 1850s after a terrible event. At that time, two groups of Chinese settlers, one from Zhangzhou and one from Quanzhou, fought bloody battles over land and other resources, and many people were killed. Afterward, local leaders buried the dead and held ceremonies for their spirits. To keep the fighting from happening again, they decided that groups of families with the same last name would take turns hosting the festival each year. Instead of fighting with weapons, they would compete to hold the best celebration.\n\nThe highlight of the festival comes on the fourteenth night of the month. First, a long parade with colorful floats, music and dancers moves through the streets. Then the crowd follows it to Badouzi, by the sea. There, people place water lanterns, paper lanterns shaped like small houses, onto the waves and set them on fire. The lanterns are believed to light the way for spirits who died at sea and invite them to enjoy the food offered to them the next day.\n\nMany people believe that the farther a lantern floats before it burns out, the better luck its family group will have in the coming year. So the crowd watches closely as the burning lanterns drift away into the dark sea.\n\nEven outside Keelung, Ghost Month affects daily life across Taiwan. Many families and shops set out tables of food and drinks as offerings to the spirits. Some people avoid swimming, moving house or getting married during the month, and grandparents may warn children not to stay out late at night. Whether or not people believe in ghosts, the Keelung festival reminds everyone of an important lesson from its history: it is far better to compete in peace than to fight.", "passage_zh": "在許多文化中，人們都害怕鬼。然而在台灣，人們往往把祂們當成需要好好招待的客人。根據台灣的民間信仰，農曆七月初一陰間的大門會打開，亡魂會回到陽間停留整整一個月。這段時間被稱為「鬼月」，而其中最有名的祭典之一就在港口城市基隆舉行。\n\n基隆中元祭始於 1850 年代的一場悲劇。當時，分別來自漳州和泉州的兩群中國移民為了爭奪土地和其他資源，爆發了血腥的械鬥，許多人因此喪命。事後，地方領袖埋葬了死者，並為亡魂舉行祭典。為了避免再次發生衝突，他們決定由同姓的宗親團體每年輪流主辦祭典。各團體不再拿著武器打鬥，而是比賽誰能把祭典辦得最好。\n\n祭典的最高潮在農曆七月十四日晚上。首先，一支由五彩花車、音樂和舞者組成的長長遊行隊伍會穿越大街小巷。接著，人群會跟著隊伍來到海邊的八斗子。在那裡，人們把水燈——也就是做成小房子形狀的紙燈——放到海浪上，並點火燃燒。人們相信這些水燈能為在海上喪生的亡魂照亮道路，邀請祂們隔天前來享用為祂們準備的祭品。\n\n許多人相信，水燈在燒完之前漂得越遠，該宗親團體來年的運氣就越好。因此，人群都會目不轉睛地看著燃燒的水燈漂向漆黑的大海。\n\n即使在基隆以外的地方，鬼月也影響著全台灣人的日常生活。許多家庭和商店會擺出一桌桌的食物和飲料，作為獻給亡魂的祭品。有些人會在這個月避免游泳、搬家或結婚，祖父母也可能會提醒孩子晚上不要在外面待太晚。無論人們是否相信有鬼，基隆中元祭都提醒大家一個來自歷史的重要教訓：和平地競爭遠比打鬥好得多。", "questions": [{"q": "According to Taiwanese folk beliefs, what happens during Ghost Month?", "options": ["People travel to Keelung to fight with other groups.", "Families move into new houses for good luck.", "Fishermen go out to sea to catch more fish.", "Spirits of the dead come back to the world of the living."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：the gates of the world of the dead open on the first day of the seventh lunar month, and spirits return to the world of the living for a whole month. (A) 械鬥是 1850 年代的歷史事件，不是鬼月習俗；(B) 第五段說有些人在鬼月會避免搬家，與此相反；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did local leaders decide that family groups would take turns hosting the festival?", "options": ["To make the festival bigger than those in other cities", "To replace fighting with peaceful competition", "To collect money for building new temples", "To decide which group could own the land"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：To keep the fighting from happening again... Instead of fighting with weapons, they would compete to hold the best celebration. 輪流主辦是為了用和平的競爭取代械鬥。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 土地是當初械鬥爭奪的對象，並不是輪流主辦的目的。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"highlight\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the beginning", "the lowest point", "the most exciting part", "the cheapest ticket"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段描述七月十四日晚上的大遊行和放水燈，是整個祭典中最精彩、最受矚目的活動，所以 highlight 指「最精彩的部分」。(A) 鬼月從農曆七月初一就開始了，十四日並不是開頭；(B)(D) 都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What happens at Badouzi on the fourteenth night?", "options": ["Burning water lanterns are sent out onto the sea.", "Food is offered to the spirits at the seaside.", "Two groups of settlers fight over land.", "A parade of floats starts at the harbor."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Then the crowd follows it to Badouzi... people place water lanterns... onto the waves and set them on fire. (B) 祭品是在 the next day（隔天）供奉；(C) 是 1850 年代的歷史事件；(D) 遊行是在市區街道上進行，之後人群才跟著到八斗子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's main message in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Children should never go out during Ghost Month.", "Everyone in Taiwan believes in ghosts.", "The festival teaches people that peace is better than fighting.", "Ghost Month is only celebrated in Keelung."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段最後一句：the Keelung festival reminds everyone of an important lesson from its history: it is far better to compete in peace than to fight. (A) 文中只說祖父母可能提醒孩子晚上別在外面待太晚，並非重點；(B) Whether or not people believe in ghosts 表示並非人人都相信有鬼；(D) 第五段開頭說鬼月影響全台灣。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a45", "title": "The Lions That Fight the Wind: Kinmen's Wind Lion Gods", "title_zh": "與風對抗的獅子：金門風獅爺", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "On Kinmen, a group of islands just a few kilometers off the coast of China, visitors often notice something unusual at the edge of villages: stone lions that stand on two legs like people, some of them wearing red capes. These are the wind lion gods. For hundreds of years, local people have believed that these lions protect their villages from strong winds and bad luck.\n\nWhy would an island need protection from the wind? According to local history, Kinmen was once covered with forests. In the 1600s, however, most of its trees were cut down, partly to build warships. Without trees, nothing could slow down the strong northeast winds that blow from autumn to spring. The winds carried sand across the island, burying farmland and making it very hard to grow crops.\n\nSince people could not control the weather, they turned to their beliefs. They made lion statues out of stone or clay and placed them at village entrances, often facing the direction the wind came from. They believed the lions would block the wind and keep evil spirits away. Today, there are more than 60 traditional wind lion gods on Kinmen, and no two are the same. Some are tall and serious, some are small with big smiles, and some are painted in bright colors. Villagers still offer them food and incense, and on special days they dress them in new red capes.\n\nKinmen looks very different today. After 1949, the islands became an important military base, and the army planted huge numbers of trees to protect the land from the wind and to hide soldiers and equipment. Now, much of Kinmen is green again. The wind lion gods are no longer needed to fight sandstorms, but they have become a symbol of the islands. Their faces appear on signs, T-shirts and souvenirs, and many visitors spend a day riding bicycles around Kinmen to find as many of them as they can.\n\nTo many people, the wind lion gods show how people in the past held on to hope during hard times. As one local guide puts it, \"The lions could not really stop the wind, but they gave farmers the courage to keep going.\"", "passage_zh": "金門是距離中國沿岸只有幾公里的一群島嶼。遊客在這裡的村落邊緣常會注意到一些不尋常的東西：像人一樣用兩條腿站立的石獅子，有些還披著紅色披風。這些就是風獅爺。數百年來，當地人一直相信這些獅子能保護村莊，抵擋強風和厄運。\n\n為什麼一座島嶼會需要抵擋風呢？根據當地的歷史，金門曾經森林茂密。然而在 1600 年代，島上大部分的樹木都被砍伐，其中一部分是為了建造戰船。沒有了樹木，就沒有東西能減緩從秋天吹到春天的強勁東北風。強風把沙子吹過整座島，掩埋了農田，使農作物非常難以生長。\n\n由於人們無法控制天氣，便轉而向信仰尋求幫助。他們用石頭或陶土製作獅子像，擺在村莊入口，通常面向風吹來的方向。他們相信這些獅子能擋住強風，並趕走邪靈。如今金門有六十多尊傳統風獅爺，而且沒有兩尊是一模一樣的。有的高大嚴肅，有的矮小且笑容滿面，有的還塗上了鮮豔的色彩。村民至今仍會用食物和香來祭拜它們，在特別的日子還會幫它們換上新的紅披風。\n\n今天的金門看起來大不相同。1949 年以後，金門成為重要的軍事基地，軍隊種下了大量的樹木，以保護土地免受強風侵襲，並隱藏士兵和裝備。如今，金門大部分地區又恢復了一片綠意。風獅爺已經不再需要對抗風沙，卻成了金門的象徵。它們的臉出現在招牌、T 恤和紀念品上，許多遊客也會花一整天騎腳踏車環島，盡可能找到更多的風獅爺。\n\n對許多人來說，風獅爺展現了過去的人們如何在艱困的時期中懷抱希望。正如一位當地導覽員所說：「獅子其實擋不住風，但它們給了農民繼續努力下去的勇氣。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the army made Kinmen green again after 1949", "Kinmen's wind lion gods, their history and meaning", "Why Kinmen's forests were cut down in the 1600s", "The best way to tour Kinmen by bicycle"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文介紹金門風獅爺：外觀與用途（第一段）、島上樹木被砍光而飽受風沙之苦的背景（第二段）、村民製作與祭拜風獅爺的方式（第三段），以及它們今日成為金門象徵、帶給人們希望的意義（第四、五段）。(A) 軍隊植樹只是第四段的細節；(C) 1600 年代砍樹只是第二段說明風害由來的背景；(D) 騎腳踏車尋找風獅爺只在第四段末順帶一提。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did strong winds cause so many problems on Kinmen?", "options": ["Most of the trees had been cut down, so nothing could slow the wind.", "Warships brought storms to the islands.", "Farmers built their houses too close to the sea.", "The wind blew only in summer, when crops were growing."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：most of its trees were cut down... Without trees, nothing could slow down the strong northeast winds. 樹木被砍光後，強風無法被減緩，沙子因此掩埋農田。(B)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 東北風是 from autumn to spring，不是只在夏天吹。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why did the army plant trees on Kinmen?", "options": ["To build new warships", "To make wind lion gods out of wood", "To sell the wood to other countries", "To protect the land from the wind and to hide soldiers"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：the army planted huge numbers of trees to protect the land from the wind and to hide soldiers and equipment. (A) 為了造戰船而砍樹是 1600 年代的事（第二段）；(B) 風獅爺是用石頭或陶土製作的（第三段）；(C) 文中未提及。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"symbol\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a kind of weapon", "a dangerous storm", "something that represents a place or an idea", "a gift for soldiers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說風獅爺雖然不再需要對抗風沙，但 they have become a symbol of the islands，而且它們的臉出現在招牌、T 恤和紀念品上，可知 symbol 指「代表某地或某種概念的事物」，也就是象徵。(A)(B)(D) 都不符合上下文。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the local guide mean in the last paragraph?", "options": ["The lions really did stop the wind from blowing.", "The lions helped people stay hopeful, even though they had no real power over the weather.", "Farmers should stop believing in the wind lion gods.", "The lions were made to welcome tourists to Kinmen."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段導覽員說 The lions could not really stop the wind, but they gave farmers the courage to keep going，意思是獅子雖然無法真的擋風，卻帶給人們希望與勇氣。(A) 與 could not really stop the wind 相反；(C) 文中沒有這個意思；(D) 風獅爺原本是為了擋風、驅邪而設，不是為了歡迎觀光客。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a46", "title": "\"Your Package Is Waiting\"... or Is It?", "title_zh": "「您的包裹正在等您」……真的嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Life"], "passage": "Last Tuesday, Wen-ting, a second-year student at a vocational high school in Taichung, received a text message while she was waiting for the bus. \"Your package could not be delivered because the address was incomplete. Please update your information within 24 hours, or the package will be returned,\" it said. Below the message was a link. Wen-ting had ordered a pair of sneakers online a few days earlier, so the message did not seem strange at all.\n\nShe clicked the link and found a website that looked just like a real delivery company's page. It asked for her name, address and phone number, and she typed them in. Then it asked her to pay a \"redelivery fee\" of NT$25 by credit card. Wen-ting did not have a credit card, so she asked her older brother for help. As soon as he saw the website, he told her to stop. \"Look at the web address,\" he said. \"It's a long string of strange letters, not the company's real website.\"\n\nWen-ting had almost become the victim of a scam called \"smishing,\" a word made from \"SMS\" (text messages) and \"phishing.\" In phishing, criminals pretend to be a company people trust so that they will give away personal information. The small fee is only bait. What the criminals really want is the card number, which they can use to spend much larger amounts of money later.\n\nThese messages work because they are designed to make people act quickly. They often use words such as \"urgent,\" \"final notice\" or \"within 24 hours.\" They also arrive at a time when many people really are waiting for things they have ordered online.\n\nPolice in Taiwan suggest a few simple rules. First, never click links in messages you did not expect. Instead, open the delivery company's official app or check your order on the shopping website. Second, remember that real companies rarely ask for card information through a text message. Third, if you are not sure, call the 165 Anti-Fraud Hotline. Wen-ting now tells her friends, \"When a message rushes you, slow down.\"", "passage_zh": "上週二，臺中一所高職二年級的學生文婷在等公車時收到一則簡訊：「您的包裹因地址不完整無法配送。請於 24 小時內更新資料，否則包裹將被退回。」訊息下方附有一個連結。文婷幾天前才在網路上買了一雙運動鞋，所以這則訊息看起來一點也不奇怪。\n\n她點了連結，進入一個看起來跟真的物流公司網頁一模一樣的網站。網站要她填寫姓名、地址和電話號碼，她都輸入了。接著網站要求她用信用卡支付新臺幣 25 元的「重新配送費」。文婷沒有信用卡，於是請哥哥幫忙。哥哥一看到這個網站，就叫她停下來。「你看網址，」他說，「那是一長串奇怪的字母，不是那家公司真正的網站。」\n\n文婷差一點就成了一種叫做「smishing（簡訊釣魚）」詐騙的受害者，這個字是由「SMS」（簡訊）和「phishing」（網路釣魚）組合而成。在網路釣魚詐騙中，歹徒會假冒大家信任的公司，讓人交出個人資料。那筆小額費用只是誘餌，歹徒真正想要的是信用卡卡號，之後他們就能用它刷更大筆的金額。\n\n這類訊息之所以有效，是因為它們刻意設計成讓人急著行動。它們常使用「緊急」、「最後通知」或「24 小時內」這類字眼。而且它們出現的時機，正好是許多人真的在等網購商品送達的時候。\n\n臺灣警方建議幾個簡單的原則。第一，絕對不要點選你沒有預期會收到的訊息中的連結，而是打開物流公司的官方 App，或到購物網站查詢訂單。第二，記住真正的公司很少透過簡訊要求提供信用卡資料。第三，如果不確定，就撥打 165 反詐騙諮詢專線。文婷現在常告訴朋友：「當訊息催你的時候，就放慢腳步。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to order sneakers online safely and cheaply", "A common text message scam and how to avoid it", "Why delivery companies charge redelivery fees", "How Wen-ting's brother started working for the police"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從文婷收到假的包裹簡訊說起，第三段說明這是「簡訊釣魚」詐騙，第四、五段說明它為何有效以及如何防範，所以主旨是一種常見的簡訊詐騙與避免方法。(A) 網購運動鞋只是故事背景；(C) 重新配送費是歹徒的誘餌，文章並未說明物流公司為何收費；(D) 文中沒有提到哥哥去當警察。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did the text message NOT seem strange to Wen-ting at first?", "options": ["It came from her brother's phone number.", "It asked her to pay a very large amount of money.", "She was really waiting for something she had bought online.", "She had received the same message many times before."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段最後一句：Wen-ting had ordered a pair of sneakers online a few days earlier, so the message did not seem strange at all. 她剛好在等網購的運動鞋，所以不覺得奇怪。(A) 簡訊不是哥哥傳的；(B) 簡訊本身並沒有要求付錢，後來網站要求的「重新配送費」也只有 NT$25，並非大筆金額；(D) 文中沒有說她以前收過同樣的簡訊。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"bait\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a reward for honest customers", "something used to attract and trick people", "a fee that every delivery company must charge", "a mistake made by careless criminals"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The small fee is only bait. What the criminals really want is the card number... 小額費用只是用來引人上鉤的「誘餌」，真正目的是騙取卡號，所以 bait 指用來吸引並欺騙人的東西。(A) 不是給誠實顧客的獎勵；(C) 這筆費用是假的，並非物流公司必收的費用；(D) 這是歹徒刻意的設計，不是失誤。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Based on paragraph 4, which of the following messages is most likely a smishing message?", "options": ["\"FINAL NOTICE: Your account will be closed today. Click here now to keep it.\"", "\"Thank you for shopping with us. Your order will arrive next week.\"", "\"Hi, it's Mom. I'll be home late tonight. Dinner is in the fridge.\"", "\"Your library book is due on Friday. Please return it at the front desk.\""], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段指出詐騙簡訊會讓人急著行動，常用 \"urgent\"、\"final notice\"、\"within 24 hours\" 等字眼，(A) 同時有「最後通知」、「今天就會關閉」和「立刻點這裡」，最符合這些特徵。(B)(C)(D) 都沒有催促點連結或提供資料，只是一般通知或家人訊息。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If you receive a message like the one Wen-ting got, what should you do according to the police?", "options": ["Click the link quickly before the 24 hours are over.", "Reply to the message and ask for the company's address.", "Pay the fee with a family member's credit card.", "Check your order in the official app or call the 165 hotline."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段警方建議：不要點陌生訊息的連結，改用物流公司的官方 App 或購物網站查詢訂單；不確定時撥打 165 反詐騙諮詢專線。(A) 正是歹徒希望你做的事；(B) 回覆詐騙訊息並非警方建議；(C) 用家人的信用卡付款會讓卡號被盜。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a47", "title": "Three Random Words: Building a Better Password", "title_zh": "三個隨機單字：打造更好的密碼", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Instructions"], "passage": "Every year, security companies publish lists of the passwords people use most often. And every year, the same sad results appear near the top: \"123456,\" \"password\" and \"qwerty,\" which is simply the first six letters on the top row of a keyboard. Passwords like these are easy to remember, but they are also easy to guess. Criminals use computer programs that can try millions of common passwords in a very short time, so a weak password protects almost nothing.\n\nMany people think that a strong password must look like \"T7#q!z2P,\" a mix of capital letters, numbers and symbols. Such passwords are hard to guess, but they are also hard to remember. As a result, people write them on sticky notes or use the same one everywhere. Experts now say that length matters more than strange symbols. Every extra character makes a password much harder for a computer to crack, or break into.\n\nThat is why the UK's National Cyber Security Centre suggests a simple method: choose three random words and put them together. A password like \"purplecactusbicycle\" is long, but it is easy to remember because your brain can turn it into a picture, perhaps a purple cactus riding a bicycle. The words should be truly random. Your pet's name, your birthday or your favorite team are poor choices, because people can often find this information on your social media.\n\nEven a good password should be used for only one account. When a website is hacked, criminals often try the stolen email addresses and passwords on many other websites. If you use the same password for your game account, your email and your bank app, one leak can open all of them.\n\nFinally, turn on two-step verification wherever you can. With this feature, logging in needs both your password and a second proof, such as a code sent to your phone. Even if someone steals your password, they still cannot get in without your phone. It takes a few extra seconds, but those seconds may save you a lot of trouble.", "passage_zh": "每年，資安公司都會公布人們最常使用的密碼排行榜。而每一年，排在前面的都是同樣令人遺憾的結果：「123456」、「password」，以及「qwerty」──這只不過是鍵盤最上排的前六個字母。這類密碼容易記，但也容易被猜到。歹徒使用的電腦程式能在極短的時間內試遍數百萬組常見密碼，所以弱密碼幾乎什麼都保護不了。\n\n很多人以為，強密碼一定要長得像「T7#q!z2P」，混合大寫字母、數字和符號。這樣的密碼很難猜，但也很難記。結果，人們就把密碼寫在便利貼上，或是每個地方都用同一組。專家現在表示，長度比奇怪的符號更重要。每多一個字元，電腦要破解（也就是闖入）這組密碼就會困難許多。\n\n這就是為什麼英國國家網路安全中心建議一個簡單的方法：選三個隨機的單字，把它們連在一起。像「purplecactusbicycle」（紫色仙人掌腳踏車）這樣的密碼很長，卻很好記，因為你的大腦可以把它變成一幅畫面──也許是一株紫色的仙人掌在騎腳踏車。這些單字必須真的是隨機的。寵物的名字、你的生日或你最喜歡的球隊都是不好的選擇，因為別人常常可以在你的社群媒體上找到這些資料。\n\n即使是好密碼，也應該只用在一個帳號上。當某個網站遭駭時，歹徒常會拿偷來的電子郵件地址和密碼到許多其他網站嘗試登入。如果你的遊戲帳號、電子信箱和銀行 App 都用同一組密碼，一次外洩就可能讓所有帳號都被打開。\n\n最後，只要可以，就開啟二階段驗證。有了這個功能，登入時除了密碼之外，還需要第二道證明，例如傳送到你手機的驗證碼。就算有人偷了你的密碼，沒有你的手機還是無法登入。這只多花幾秒鐘，但這幾秒鐘可能幫你省下許多麻煩。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why people often forget their passwords", "To give advice on keeping online accounts safe", "To compare different kinds of computer keyboards", "To show how criminals build their computer programs"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文說明弱密碼的危險，並建議使用三個隨機單字組成的長密碼、一個帳號一組密碼，以及開啟二階段驗證，目的是教讀者保護網路帳號。(A) 文中只提到複雜密碼難記，並非全文重點；(C) 鍵盤只在第一段解釋 qwerty 時出現；(D) 文中沒有說明歹徒如何寫程式。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what is a problem with passwords like \"T7#q!z2P\"?", "options": ["Computers can guess them in a few seconds.", "Most websites do not allow symbols in passwords.", "They are hard to remember, so people write them down or reuse them.", "They are too long to type on a phone."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Such passwords are hard to guess, but they are also hard to remember. As a result, people write them on sticky notes or use the same one everywhere. 這類密碼難記，導致人們把它寫下來或到處重複使用。(A) 文中說這種密碼「很難猜」；(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"leak\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a time when private information is stolen or made public", "a password that is easy to remember", "a game account that has been closed", "a small hole in a water pipe"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段前文提到 When a website is hacked, criminals often try the stolen email addresses and passwords...，接著說 one leak can open all of them，可知 leak 指帳號密碼等私人資料被偷或外洩。(B)(C) 與上下文無關；(D) 是 leak「漏水的破洞」的字面意思，不符合本文語境。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following passwords would the National Cyber Security Centre most likely recommend?", "options": ["123456789", "Lucky0815 (your dog's name and your birthday)", "qwerty2025", "lanternpotatoriver"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段建議選三個「真正隨機」的單字連在一起，lanternpotatoriver（燈籠＋馬鈴薯＋河流）正符合。(A)(C) 是第一段提到的常見弱密碼加上數字；(B) 用寵物名字和生日，第三段說這是 poor choices，因為別人可能從社群媒體查到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How does two-step verification help protect an account?", "options": ["It changes your password automatically every day.", "It stops criminals from making computer programs.", "It asks for a second proof, such as a code sent to your phone.", "It lets you use one password for all of your accounts."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：logging in needs both your password and a second proof, such as a code sent to your phone. 登入時除了密碼還需要第二道證明，所以即使密碼被偷也登不進去。(A)(B) 文中沒有提到；(D) 與第四段「一組密碼只用在一個帳號」的建議相反。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a48", "title": "What One Photo Told a Stranger", "title_zh": "一張照片告訴陌生人的事", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Society"], "passage": "When Yi-chen got a kitten for her sixteenth birthday, she did what many teenagers would do. She took dozens of photos and posted the cutest one on her public social media account. In the picture, the gray kitten was sleeping by the living room window. Within an hour, the photo had more than a hundred likes.\n\nThree days later, Yi-chen got a private message from someone she did not know. \"Cute cat! Your home is near the Lucky Mart on Minsheng Road, right? I walk past there every day.\" Yi-chen felt cold all over. She had never told anyone online where she lived. She deleted the message, blocked the account and told her parents right away.\n\nThe next week, Yi-chen shared the story with her computer teacher, Mr. Lai, and he used her photo, with her permission, as a lesson for the whole class. He asked the students to look carefully at the picture. Through the window, part of a store sign could be seen, showing the name of the branch. On the sofa lay her school jacket, with the school's name printed on it. \"Put these small clues together,\" Mr. Lai said, \"and a stranger can find your neighborhood in a few minutes.\"\n\nHe also explained that a photo can carry information you cannot see. Many smartphones save the exact place where a photo was taken inside the photo file. Most big social media apps remove this hidden data when you upload a picture, but it may stay in a photo that you send as a file or post on some other websites.\n\nMr. Lai then gave the class a short checklist. Check the background for signs, house numbers and uniforms. Turn off location saving in your camera settings. Post travel photos after you get home, not while you are still there. Most importantly, ask yourself, \"Would I be happy if a stranger saw this?\" Yi-chen still posts photos of her kitten, but now she takes them in front of a plain wall.", "passage_zh": "怡辰十六歲生日時得到一隻小貓，她做了許多青少年都會做的事：拍了好幾十張照片，然後把最可愛的一張貼到她公開的社群媒體帳號上。照片裡，那隻灰色小貓睡在客廳的窗邊。不到一個小時，這張照片就有一百多個讚。\n\n三天後，怡辰收到一則陌生人傳來的私訊：「好可愛的貓！你家在民生路那家幸運超商附近，對吧？我每天都會經過那裡。」怡辰全身發冷。她從來沒有在網路上告訴任何人她住在哪裡。她刪除了訊息、封鎖了那個帳號，並立刻告訴爸媽。\n\n隔週，怡辰把這件事告訴電腦老師賴老師，老師在徵得她同意後，用她的照片給全班上了一課。他請學生仔細看這張照片。從窗戶望出去，可以看到一部分商店招牌，上面寫著分店的名稱。沙發上放著她的學校外套，上面印著校名。「把這些小線索拼在一起，」賴老師說，「陌生人幾分鐘內就能找到你住的社區。」\n\n他也解釋，照片可能帶有你看不見的資料。許多智慧型手機會把拍照的確切地點存在照片檔案裡。大部分大型社群媒體 App 會在你上傳照片時移除這些隱藏的資料，但如果你把照片當作檔案傳送，或貼到其他某些網站上，這些資料可能還留在裡面。\n\n接著，賴老師給了全班一份簡短的檢查清單：檢查背景有沒有招牌、門牌號碼和制服；在相機設定中關閉位置儲存功能；旅遊照片等回到家再貼，不要人還在當地就貼出來。最重要的是，問問自己：「如果陌生人看到這張照片，我會覺得沒關係嗎？」怡辰現在還是會貼小貓的照片，不過都改在一面素色的牆前面拍。", "questions": [{"q": "How did the stranger most likely find out where Yi-chen lived?", "options": ["Yi-chen's parents told him.", "He noticed clues in the background of her photo.", "Yi-chen wrote her address under the photo.", "He followed her home from school one day."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段賴老師指出照片中窗外露出寫有分店名稱的商店招牌，沙發上還有印著校名的外套，並說 Put these small clues together... a stranger can find your neighborhood，可知陌生人是從照片背景的線索推測出她家位置。(A)(D) 文中沒有提到；(C) 第二段說她從未在網路上透露住處。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What did Yi-chen do right after she got the stranger's message?", "options": ["She replied and asked who the person was.", "She posted another photo of her kitten.", "She deleted the message, blocked the account and told her parents.", "She moved her kitten to a different room."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段最後一句：She deleted the message, blocked the account and told her parents right away. (A) 她沒有回覆對方；(B) 文中沒有說她立刻又貼照片；(D) 文中沒有提到她把貓移到別的房間。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 4, what does \"this hidden data\" refer to?", "options": ["The number of likes a photo receives", "The name printed on Yi-chen's school jacket", "The private messages sent by strangers", "Information about the place where a photo was taken"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段前一句：Many smartphones save the exact place where a photo was taken inside the photo file. 緊接著的 this hidden data 指的就是存在檔案中、看不見的拍攝地點資料。(A) 按讚數是公開的；(B) 外套上的校名是照片中看得見的線索，出現在第三段；(C) 私訊與照片檔案無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Kai is on a trip to Kenting with his family. According to Mr. Lai's checklist, what should he do with his beach photos?", "options": ["Post them after he returns home.", "Post them right away so his friends can join him.", "Send them as files to people he does not know.", "Turn on location saving so he can remember the places."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段檢查清單：Post travel photos after you get home, not while you are still there. 旅遊照片應該回家後再貼。(B) 正好是清單提醒不要做的事；(C) 第四段說以檔案傳送的照片可能保留位置資料，更不該傳給陌生人；(D) 清單建議關閉位置儲存功能。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the last sentence of the passage?", "options": ["Yi-chen has stopped using social media completely.", "Yi-chen's kitten no longer likes the living room.", "Yi-chen has learned to be careful about what her photos show.", "Yi-chen thinks plain walls make photos more beautiful."], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一句說怡辰仍會貼小貓照片，但現在都在素色牆面前拍，背景不會再露出招牌或制服等線索，可推知她已學會注意照片透露的資訊。(A) 她仍在貼照片，並未停用社群媒體；(B) 文中沒有提到貓的喜好；(D) 她換背景是為了安全，不是為了美觀。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a49", "title": "A Familiar Voice, a Fake Call", "title_zh": "熟悉的聲音，假冒的電話", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Society"], "passage": "One evening, Mrs. Lin's phone rang. It was her daughter, Pei-shan, a university student in Tainan, or at least it sounded like her. \"Mom, I'm in trouble,\" the voice cried. \"I knocked over a man's scooter, and he says I have to pay NT$50,000 today, or he'll call the police. Please don't tell Dad. Just send the money to this account.\" The voice had Pei-shan's soft accent and even her habit of saying \"Mom\" twice when she was upset.\n\nMrs. Lin grabbed her bag and started toward the door. Then she remembered something her family had agreed on a few months earlier. She took a deep breath and asked, \"What was the name of our first dog?\" There was a short silence, and then the line went dead. With shaking hands, she called Pei-shan's number. Her daughter answered after two rings. She was studying in the library and had no idea what her mother was talking about.\n\nThe caller was not Pei-shan at all. It was probably a criminal using an AI voice tool. Today, such tools can copy a person's voice from a short recording, sometimes only a few seconds long. Criminals can easily find these recordings in videos that people post on social media. Then they simply type what they want the fake voice to say.\n\nAlthough the technology is new, the trick is an old one. Calls like this usually share three warning signs: there is an emergency, you are asked for money right away, and you are told to keep it a secret. These signs are designed to stop you from thinking clearly.\n\nExperts suggest two simple defenses. First, if a call makes you nervous, hang up and call the person back on a number you already know. Second, choose a family \"safe word\" or question, something that has never appeared online, and use it to check who is really calling. Mrs. Lin's family had chosen theirs almost as a joke. Now they are very glad they did.", "passage_zh": "某天晚上，林太太的手機響了。打來的是她在臺南讀大學的女兒佩珊，至少聽起來像是她。「媽，我出事了，」那個聲音哭著說，「我把一個男人的機車撞倒了，他說我今天一定要賠新臺幣五萬元，不然他就要報警。拜託不要告訴爸爸，直接把錢匯到這個帳戶就好。」那個聲音有佩珊柔和的口音，甚至連她一難過就會連叫兩聲「媽」的習慣都一樣。\n\n林太太抓起包包，正要往門口走去。這時她想起全家人幾個月前約定好的一件事。她深吸一口氣，問道：「我們家第一隻狗叫什麼名字？」電話那頭沉默了一下，接著就斷線了。她雙手發抖，撥了佩珊的號碼。女兒響兩聲就接起電話。她正在圖書館念書，完全不知道媽媽在說什麼。\n\n打電話來的根本不是佩珊，很可能是歹徒利用 AI 語音工具假冒的。如今，這類工具只需要一小段錄音，有時只要幾秒鐘，就能複製一個人的聲音。歹徒很容易就能從人們發布在社群媒體上的影片裡找到這些錄音。接著，他們只要打出想讓假聲音說的話就行了。\n\n雖然這項科技是新的，但這種騙術其實很老套。這類電話通常有三個共同的警訊：發生了緊急狀況、要你立刻付錢，以及要你保守祕密。這些手法就是為了讓你無法冷靜思考。\n\n專家建議兩個簡單的防範方法。第一，如果某通電話讓你緊張，就先掛斷，再用你原本就知道的號碼回撥給對方。第二，全家一起約定一個「安全暗語」或問題，必須是從來沒有出現在網路上的內容，用它來確認打來的到底是誰。林太太一家當初選定暗語時，幾乎只是當成玩笑。現在，他們非常慶幸當初這麼做了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a university student paid for a scooter accident", "A phone scam that uses a copied voice, and how to protect yourself", "Why parents should not let their children study far from home", "How AI tools help people make funny videos"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先描述林太太接到假冒女兒聲音的詐騙電話，第三段說明歹徒用 AI 複製聲音，第四、五段提出警訊與防範方法。(A) 撞倒機車是歹徒編的故事，佩珊並沒有出事；(C) 文中沒有討論子女是否該離家讀書；(D) 文中的 AI 工具是被用來詐騙，不是做搞笑影片。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did Mrs. Lin believe at first that the caller was her daughter?", "options": ["The call came from Pei-shan's own phone number.", "The caller knew the name of the family's first dog.", "The voice sounded like Pei-shan and even had her speaking habit.", "Pei-shan had told her about the accident earlier that day."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段最後一句：The voice had Pei-shan's soft accent and even her habit of saying \"Mom\" twice when she was upset. 聲音和說話習慣都很像女兒。(A) 文中沒有提到來電號碼；(B) 對方答不出狗的名字就掛斷了；(D) 佩珊根本不知道這件事。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 2, \"the line went dead\" means that ______.", "options": ["the phone call suddenly ended", "the caller started to cry", "the caller gave the right answer", "someone in the family died"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段林太太問了只有家人知道的問題後，There was a short silence, and then the line went dead，接著她就自己撥電話給佩珊，可知對方掛斷、通話突然中斷。(B)(C) 對方只是沉默，沒有哭也沒有回答；(D) 是 dead 字面上「死亡」的意思，不符合本文語境。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the caller most likely hang up after Mrs. Lin's question?", "options": ["The caller was angry that Mrs. Lin had told Pei-shan's father.", "The caller had already received the money.", "The caller wanted Mrs. Lin to call back on another number.", "The caller could not answer because he was not really Pei-shan."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說 The caller was not Pei-shan at all，是用 AI 複製聲音的歹徒；家庭暗語問題（第一隻狗的名字）只有真正的家人知道，歹徒答不出來只好掛斷。(A) 林太太並沒有告訴爸爸；(B) 她還沒有匯款；(C) 文中沒有這樣的情節。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, which of the following would be the best family \"safe word\"?", "options": ["A pet's name that appears in many of your public posts", "A word your family chose together and has never shared online", "Your mother's phone number", "The name of the school you go to"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：choose a family \"safe word\" or question, something that has never appeared online. 安全暗語必須是家人約定、從未在網路上出現的內容。(A) 已公開在貼文中，歹徒查得到；(C)(D) 電話號碼和校名都容易被別人知道。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a50", "title": "From Car Parts to Bubble Tea: The Story of the QR Code", "title_zh": "從汽車零件到珍珠奶茶：QR Code 的故事", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "History"], "passage": "Today, we scan QR codes to order bubble tea, pay bills and open websites. But these little black-and-white squares were not invented for any of these things. They were born in a car parts factory in Japan in 1994.\n\nAt that time, factories used barcodes to keep track of parts. A barcode could hold only about 20 characters, so workers sometimes had to scan many barcodes on a single box. Masahiro Hara, an engineer at Denso, a company that makes car parts, was asked to design something better. According to Hara, the idea came to him while he was playing Go, a board game with black and white stones on a grid. What if information could be stored in two directions, across and down, instead of in a single line?\n\nThe result was the QR code, and \"QR\" stands for \"Quick Response.\" One code can hold up to 7,089 numbers. The three large squares in its corners tell the scanner where the code is and which way it is turned, so it can be read quickly from any angle. A QR code can even be read when part of it is dirty or damaged. Just as importantly, Denso decided not to charge anyone for using the design. This allowed companies around the world to adopt it freely.\n\nFor years, few people outside Japan used QR codes in their daily lives. Things changed when smartphones with good cameras became common. During the COVID-19 pandemic, the codes became part of daily life almost everywhere. In 2021, for example, people in Taiwan scanned a QR code to send a text message before entering a shop or restaurant.\n\nTheir popularity, however, has also attracted criminals. Because people cannot read a QR code with their eyes, a fake one looks exactly like a real one. Some criminals stick their own codes over real ones on posters or parking machines, leading people to fake payment pages. So before you scan, check whether the code is a sticker placed on top of something else, and look at the web address before you enter any information.", "passage_zh": "如今，我們會掃描 QR Code 來點珍珠奶茶、繳帳單和開啟網站。但這些黑白相間的小方塊，當初並不是為了這些用途而發明的。它們誕生於 1994 年日本的一間汽車零件工廠。\n\n當時，工廠用條碼來追蹤零件。一個條碼只能存大約 20 個字元，所以工人有時必須在同一個箱子上掃描許多條碼。汽車零件製造商電裝（Denso）的工程師原昌宏受命設計出更好的東西。根據原昌宏的說法，這個點子是他在下圍棋時想到的；圍棋是一種在格狀棋盤上擺放黑白棋子的遊戲。如果資訊能以橫向和縱向兩個方向儲存，而不是只排成一條線，會怎麼樣呢？\n\n成果就是 QR Code，「QR」代表「Quick Response」（快速回應）。一個 QR Code 最多可以存 7,089 個數字。角落的三個大方塊會告訴掃描器條碼的位置和方向，因此不論從哪個角度都能快速讀取。即使 QR Code 有一部分髒了或損壞，仍然可以讀取。同樣重要的是，電裝決定不向任何人收取使用這項設計的費用。這讓世界各地的公司都能自由採用它。\n\n多年來，日本以外的地方很少有人在日常生活中使用 QR Code。當配備好相機的智慧型手機普及之後，情況就改變了。在新冠肺炎疫情期間，這些條碼幾乎在世界各地都成了日常生活的一部分。例如在 2021 年，臺灣民眾進入商店或餐廳前，要先掃描 QR Code 傳送一則簡訊。\n\n然而，QR Code 的普及也引來了歹徒。因為人無法用眼睛讀出 QR Code 的內容，假的看起來和真的一模一樣。有些歹徒會把自己的條碼貼在海報或停車繳費機上原本的條碼上面，把人引導到假的付款網頁。所以在掃描之前，要先檢查這個條碼是不是貼在其他東西上面的貼紙，並且在輸入任何資料前先看清楚網址。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the passage mainly discuss?", "options": ["The history of the QR code, its uses and a risk it brings", "How to play the board game Go", "Why barcodes are better than QR codes", "How Japanese car factories became successful"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文介紹 QR Code 在 1994 年的誕生經過（第一至三段）、在日常生活中的普及（第四段），以及假 QR Code 帶來的詐騙風險（第五段）。(B) 圍棋只是發明靈感的來源；(C) 文中說條碼容量小，QR Code 是改良版，與選項相反；(D) 文中沒有討論日本車廠如何成功。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was the QR code first designed?", "options": ["To help customers pay for drinks", "To help people check in at restaurants", "To store more information than barcodes for tracking car parts", "To stop criminals from making fake payment pages"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：factories used barcodes to keep track of parts. A barcode could hold only about 20 characters... 原昌宏被要求設計更好的東西，可知 QR Code 最初是為了在汽車零件工廠中存放比條碼更多的資訊。(A)(B) 是後來的用途（第一、四段）；(D) 假 QR Code 是第五段提到的新問題，不是發明目的。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what is the job of the three large squares in a QR code?", "options": ["They store the address of a website.", "They show the scanner where the code is and which way it is turned.", "They prove that the code is not a fake one.", "They make the code look like a Go board."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The three large squares in its corners tell the scanner where the code is and which way it is turned, so it can be read quickly from any angle. (A) 文中沒有說網址存在大方塊裡；(C) 第五段說假的 QR Code 看起來跟真的一模一樣，大方塊無法證明真假；(D) 圍棋只是靈感來源，不是大方塊的功能。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from Denso's decision not to charge anyone for using the QR code design?", "options": ["Denso lost a lot of money and stopped making car parts.", "QR codes could only be used inside Japan.", "Very few companies were interested in QR codes.", "It helped the QR code spread widely around the world."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Denso decided not to charge anyone for using the design. This allowed companies around the world to adopt it freely. 免費使用讓世界各地的公司都能採用，可推知這有助於 QR Code 在全球普及。(A) 文中沒有提到電裝虧損或停產；(B)(C) 都與「世界各地的公司自由採用」相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, what is the safest thing to do before scanning a QR code on a parking machine?", "options": ["Scan it quickly before someone else uses it.", "Enter your card number to see if the page is real.", "Check whether it is a sticker covering the original code.", "Clean the code so it can be read from any angle."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：before you scan, check whether the code is a sticker placed on top of something else. 歹徒會把假條碼貼在停車繳費機原本的條碼上，所以掃描前要先檢查是否為覆蓋上去的貼紙。(A) 匆忙掃描反而危險；(B) 文中提醒輸入資料前要先確認網址，不能用卡號測試；(D) 清潔條碼與防範詐騙無關。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a51", "title": "Can I Take My Power Bank on the Plane?", "title_zh": "行動電源可以帶上飛機嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Travel"], "passage": "Before his first trip abroad, Jun-hao packed a large power bank in his suitcase, thinking it was the safest place for it. At the check-in counter, a member of the airline staff asked him to open the suitcase and take it out. \"Power banks must go in your carry-on bag,\" she explained. Jun-hao was surprised, but there is a good reason behind the rule.\n\nPower banks use lithium-ion batteries, the same kind found in phones and laptops. These batteries store a lot of energy in a small space. If one is damaged, badly made or overheated, it can catch fire, and such fires are hard to put out. In the cabin, crew members can notice smoke quickly and deal with it. In the cargo hold under the floor, however, a fire might grow for a long time before anyone knows. That is why spare batteries are never allowed in checked luggage.\n\nSize matters, too. Airlines measure a battery's energy in watt-hours (Wh), but most power banks only show milliamp-hours (mAh) and voltage (V). To find the watt-hours, multiply the two numbers and divide by 1,000. A common 10,000 mAh power bank at 3.7 V holds about 37 Wh. Power banks up to 100 Wh are usually allowed. Those between 100 and 160 Wh need the airline's approval, and anything larger cannot be taken on a passenger plane at all.\n\nRecently, the rules have become even stricter. In early 2025, a fire broke out on a plane in South Korea, and investigators believed a power bank had started it. Since then, many airlines in Asia, including those in Taiwan, have stopped allowing passengers to use or charge power banks during flights.\n\nSo, before you fly, check the label on your power bank. If its capacity is not shown, it may be refused at security. Keep it in your carry-on bag, and remember to charge your phone before boarding. Jun-hao only lost a few minutes at the check-in counter, but since then, he has checked his bags carefully before every trip.", "passage_zh": "第一次出國前，俊豪把一個大型行動電源放進行李箱，以為那是最安全的地方。到了報到櫃檯，一位航空公司人員請他打開行李箱，把行動電源拿出來。「行動電源一定要放在手提行李裡，」她解釋。俊豪很驚訝，但這項規定背後有充分的理由。\n\n行動電源使用鋰離子電池，和手機、筆記型電腦裡的電池是同一種。這種電池在小小的空間裡儲存了大量的能量。如果電池受損、製造不良或過熱，就可能起火，而這種火很難撲滅。在客艙裡，空服員能很快察覺煙霧並加以處理。然而在地板下方的貨艙裡，火勢可能延燒很久都沒有人發現。這就是為什麼備用電池絕對不能放在託運行李中。\n\n容量大小也很重要。航空公司以瓦特小時（Wh）來計算電池的能量，但大部分行動電源只標示毫安培小時（mAh）和電壓（V）。要算出瓦特小時，就把這兩個數字相乘，再除以 1,000。一個常見的 10,000 mAh、3.7 V 的行動電源大約是 37 Wh。100 Wh 以下的行動電源通常可以攜帶；介於 100 到 160 Wh 之間的需要航空公司核准；超過 160 Wh 的則完全不能帶上客機。\n\n最近，規定變得更加嚴格。2025 年初，南韓一架飛機發生火災，調查人員認為起火原因是一個行動電源。從那之後，亞洲許多航空公司，包括臺灣的航空公司，都不再允許乘客在飛行途中使用行動電源或替行動電源充電。\n\n所以，搭飛機前要先檢查行動電源上的標籤。如果上面沒有標示容量，安檢時可能會被拒絕。把它放在手提行李中，並記得在登機前先把手機充好電。俊豪只在報到櫃檯耽誤了幾分鐘，但從那次之後，他每次出門旅行前都會仔細檢查自己的行李。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain the rules for taking power banks on planes and the reasons for them", "To advertise a new kind of power bank for travelers", "To describe everything that happened on Jun-hao's trip abroad", "To compare lithium-ion batteries with other kinds of batteries"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以俊豪的經驗開頭，接著說明行動電源必須放手提行李的原因（第二段）、容量限制（第三段）、2025 年後的新規定（第四段），以及搭機前該注意的事（第五段）。(B) 文中沒有推銷任何產品；(C) 只提到他在報到櫃檯的經過，並非整趟旅程；(D) 文中沒有比較不同種類的電池。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why are power banks NOT allowed in checked luggage?", "options": ["They are too heavy to put in suitcases.", "A fire in the cargo hold might not be noticed quickly.", "Passengers might forget them at the airport.", "Crew members need them to charge their own phones."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：In the cargo hold under the floor, however, a fire might grow for a long time before anyone knows. That is why spare batteries are never allowed in checked luggage. 貨艙裡起火不易被發現。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Mei has a 30,000 mAh power bank that works at 3.7 V. According to paragraph 3, what should she do?", "options": ["Put it in her suitcase and check it in.", "Nothing special, because it is under 100 Wh.", "Leave it at home, because it can never be taken on a plane.", "Get the airline's approval before taking it on board."], "answer": 3, "explain": "依第三段公式：30,000 × 3.7 ÷ 1,000 = 111 Wh，介於 100 到 160 Wh 之間，need the airline's approval，必須先取得航空公司核准。(A) 第二段說備用電池不能託運；(B) 111 Wh 已超過 100 Wh；(C) 只有超過 160 Wh 才完全不能帶上客機。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What has changed on many airlines in Asia since 2025?", "options": ["Passengers may not use or charge power banks during flights.", "Power banks must now be put in checked luggage.", "Power banks larger than 37 Wh are no longer allowed.", "Passengers must buy their power banks at the airport."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：many airlines in Asia, including those in Taiwan, have stopped allowing passengers to use or charge power banks during flights. (B) 與第二段「不能放託運行李」相反；(C) 37 Wh 只是第三段計算的例子，100 Wh 以下通常可攜帶；(D) 文中沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"refused\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["charged", "repaired", "not accepted", "weighed"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：If its capacity is not shown, it may be refused at security. 沒有標示容量的行動電源在安檢時可能「被拒絕、不被接受」，因為人員無法確認它是否符合規定。(A)「充電」、(B)「修理」、(D)「秤重」都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a52", "title": "Why Does My Phone Know What I Like?", "title_zh": "為什麼我的手機知道我喜歡什麼？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Society"], "passage": "Have you ever watched one funny cat video and then found your screen full of cats for the rest of the night? That is not luck. Behind almost every video app and social media site is a recommendation system, a computer program that decides what to show you next.\n\nTo see how fast it works, Ms. Chiang, a teacher in Kaohsiung, tried an experiment with her class. Students made brand-new accounts on a short-video app using school tablets. One group watched every basketball video to the end and skipped everything else. Another group did the same with cooking videos. After only thirty minutes, the two groups' screens looked completely different. \"It's like the app read our minds,\" said one student.\n\nIn fact, the app was not reading minds; it was reading behavior. Recommendation systems notice what you click on, how long you watch, what you like or share, and what you skip after one second. The more you use an app, the more it learns about you. Its main goal is simple: to keep you watching, because the longer people stay, the more ads the company can show them.\n\nThis can be helpful. It is nice to find songs, recipes or study tips that match your interests. But there is a downside. The writer Eli Pariser called it a \"filter bubble.\" When you only see things you already agree with or enjoy, you may start to believe that everyone thinks the same way you do. Also, because the app always offers \"just one more video,\" it is easy to lose track of time.\n\nYou do not have to delete your apps to stay in control. Search for topics outside your usual interests now and then. Use the \"Not interested\" button when you see something you do not want more of. Follow people with different opinions, and set a time limit for yourself. Remember: the app is working hard to know you, so make sure you also know how the app works.", "passage_zh": "你有沒有過這種經驗：只看了一部好笑的貓咪影片，結果那天晚上整個螢幕都是貓？這可不是運氣。幾乎每個影音 App 和社群網站背後，都有一套推薦系統，也就是決定接下來要給你看什麼內容的電腦程式。\n\n為了看看它運作得有多快，高雄的江老師和班上同學做了一個實驗。學生們用學校的平板電腦，在一個短影音 App 上註冊全新的帳號。其中一組把每一部籃球影片都看到最後，其他影片一律跳過；另一組則對烹飪影片做同樣的事。才過了三十分鐘，兩組的螢幕畫面就完全不同了。「就好像這個 App 能看穿我們的心思一樣，」一位學生說。\n\n事實上，這個 App 讀的不是人心，而是行為。推薦系統會注意你點了什麼、看了多久、對什麼按讚或分享，以及什麼內容你看一秒就滑走。你使用 App 的時間越多，它對你的了解就越多。它的主要目標很簡單：讓你一直看下去，因為人們停留得越久，公司就能給他們看越多廣告。\n\n這可能很有幫助。能找到符合你興趣的歌曲、食譜或讀書技巧是很棒的事。但它也有缺點。作家伊萊．帕理澤把這種現象稱為「過濾泡泡」（filter bubble）。當你只看到自己本來就認同或喜歡的東西，你可能會開始以為每個人的想法都跟你一樣。此外，因為 App 總是提供「再看一部就好」的影片，人很容易忘了時間。\n\n你不必刪除 App 也能保有主控權。偶爾搜尋一些你平常不感興趣的主題。看到不想再看到的內容時，就按「不感興趣」。追蹤一些意見和你不同的人，並替自己設定使用時間上限。記住：App 正努力地了解你，所以你也要確定自己了解 App 是怎麼運作的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to make funny cat videos that many people will watch", "How recommendation systems choose what we see, and how to stay in control", "Why teachers should use tablets in class", "How companies make basketball and cooking videos"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段點出主題「推薦系統」，第二、三段以實驗說明它如何根據使用者行為決定內容，第四段談優缺點，第五段建議如何保有主控權。(A) 貓咪影片只是開頭的例子；(C) 平板只是實驗工具；(D) 籃球和烹飪影片是實驗內容，文章並未說明如何製作影片。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What did Ms. Chiang's experiment show?", "options": ["Apps can really read their users' thoughts.", "Students prefer cooking videos to basketball videos.", "School tablets work more slowly than personal phones.", "A video app can quickly change what it shows based on how people use it."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：只看了三十分鐘，看籃球影片和看烹飪影片的兩組，畫面就 looked completely different，說明 App 會依使用行為很快改變推薦內容。(A) 第三段明說 the app was not reading minds; it was reading behavior，學生的話只是比喻；(B)(C) 實驗並未比較這些。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why do the companies want people to keep watching?", "options": ["So that they can show people more ads", "So that people can find more study tips", "So that their computer programs can take a rest", "So that people will make more new accounts"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段最後一句：to keep you watching, because the longer people stay, the more ads the company can show them. 人們停留越久，公司就能播放越多廣告。(B) 讀書技巧是第四段提到推薦系統的好處，不是公司的目的；(C)(D) 文中沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"downside\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a good point", "a lower floor", "a disadvantage", "a hidden button"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段先說 This can be helpful（好處），接著用 But there is a downside 轉折，後面描述「過濾泡泡」和容易忘了時間等問題，可知 downside 是「缺點、壞處」。(A) 與 But 的轉折相反；(B) 是字面上「下方」的聯想；(D) 與文意無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following would the writer most likely suggest?", "options": ["Delete all video apps right away.", "Search for new topics sometimes and set a time limit.", "Watch only the kinds of videos you already enjoy.", "Press \"like\" on every video you see."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段建議偶爾搜尋平常不感興趣的主題、使用「不感興趣」按鈕、追蹤意見不同的人，並 set a time limit for yourself。(A) 第五段第一句說 You do not have to delete your apps；(C) 正是第四段「過濾泡泡」的問題；(D) 每部影片都按讚只會讓推薦系統收集更多你的行為資料，文中並沒有這樣建議。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a53", "title": "Grandpa's Morning Messages", "title_zh": "爺爺的早安訊息", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Society", "Technology"], "passage": "Every morning at six, my grandpa sends a message to our family LINE group. Usually it is a picture of flowers with the words \"Good morning! Have a nice day!\" We all reply with a sticker, and that is the end of it. But last month, one of his messages started a small family storm.\n\n\"Doctors say that drinking hot lemon water every morning can kill cancer cells!\" the message said. \"Share this with everyone you love!\" Within minutes, my aunt had forwarded it to her friends. I knew it was not true, because our health teacher had talked about this very rumor in class. I started typing, \"Grandpa, this is fake news,\" but my mom saw what I was doing and stopped me before I could press \"Send.\" \"Don't correct him in front of the whole family,\" she said. \"He'll feel embarrassed.\"\n\nSo that weekend, I visited Grandpa and sat down next to him with his phone. I didn't tell him he was wrong. Instead, I asked, \"Shall we check where this came from?\" I showed him how to forward a message to Cofacts, a LINE chatbot run by volunteers in Taiwan. A few seconds later, it replied with a link to an article explaining that there is no scientific evidence for the claim. Grandpa read it twice and laughed. \"I thought I was helping everyone,\" he said.\n\nThen we talked about how to recognize suspicious messages. Many of them do not say which doctor or which study the information comes from. They try to make you feel afraid or excited. And they almost always ask you to share them quickly. Grandpa wrote these three warning signs on a piece of paper and stuck it next to his desk.\n\nNow Grandpa checks every surprising message before he shares it. He has even taught his friends at the community center how to use the chatbot, and they call him \"Detective Chou.\" His good-morning flowers still arrive at six o'clock every day, but the fake health tips have stopped. I learned something too: the best way to fight false information is often with patience, not anger.", "passage_zh": "每天早上六點，我爺爺都會在我們家族的 LINE 群組裡傳一則訊息。通常是一張花的圖片，配上「早安！祝你有美好的一天！」這幾個字。我們大家都回一個貼圖，事情就這樣結束了。但上個月，他的一則訊息在家裡掀起了一場小風暴。\n\n「醫生說，每天早上喝熱檸檬水可以殺死癌細胞！」訊息上寫著，「請分享給你愛的每一個人！」幾分鐘內，我阿姨就已經把它轉傳給她的朋友了。我知道這不是真的，因為我們的健康教育老師上課時才談過這個謠言。我開始打字：「爺爺，這是假新聞。」但媽媽看到我在做什麼，在我按下「傳送」之前就阻止了我。「不要在全家人面前糾正他，」她說，「他會覺得很難為情。」\n\n所以那個週末，我去探望爺爺，拿著他的手機坐到他身旁。我沒有說他錯了，而是問他：「我們要不要查查看這是從哪裡來的？」我教他怎麼把訊息轉傳給 Cofacts（真的假的），這是一個由臺灣志工經營的 LINE 聊天機器人。幾秒鐘後，它回覆了一篇文章的連結，說明這個說法並沒有科學證據。爺爺讀了兩遍，然後笑了。「我還以為我是在幫大家呢，」他說。\n\n接著我們聊到如何辨認可疑的訊息。很多這類訊息都沒有說明資料是來自哪位醫生或哪項研究。它們會試圖讓你感到害怕或興奮。而且它們幾乎總是要你趕快分享出去。爺爺把這三個警訊寫在一張紙上，貼在他的書桌旁邊。\n\n現在，爺爺每次看到令人驚訝的訊息，都會先查證再分享。他甚至教社區活動中心的朋友們使用這個聊天機器人，大家都叫他「周偵探」。他的早安花朵圖片仍然每天六點準時送達，但假的健康小撇步已經不見了。我也學到了一件事：對抗錯誤資訊最好的方法，往往是耐心，而不是憤怒。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the story mainly about?", "options": ["How the writer helped Grandpa learn to check messages before sharing them", "Why drinking lemon water is good for people's health", "How to send stickers in a family LINE group", "Why the writer's aunt stopped using LINE"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文描述作者發現爺爺轉傳假的健康訊息後，私下教他用 Cofacts 查證並辨認可疑訊息，最後爺爺養成先查證再分享的習慣。(B) 文中說「熱檸檬水殺癌細胞」沒有科學證據；(C) 貼圖只是第一段的生活細節；(D) 文中沒有說阿姨停用 LINE。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the writer's mom stop the writer from replying in the family group?", "options": ["She believed the message about lemon water was true.", "She did not want Grandpa to feel embarrassed in front of everyone.", "She thought the writer should be studying instead.", "She wanted to check the message by herself first."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段媽媽說：\"Don't correct him in front of the whole family... He'll feel embarrassed.\" 她不希望爺爺在全家人面前難堪。(A) 她並沒有說訊息是真的；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"suspicious\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["funny", "long", "possibly false or dishonest", "polite"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段在談如何辨認 suspicious messages，後面列出的特徵是沒有來源、刻意引起恐懼或興奮、要你趕快分享，都是假訊息的警訊，所以 suspicious 指「可疑的、可能不實的」。(A)「好笑的」、(B)「長的」、(D)「有禮貌的」都與這些警訊無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT one of the warning signs that Grandpa wrote down?", "options": ["The message does not say where its information comes from.", "The message tries to make you feel afraid or excited.", "The message asks you to share it quickly.", "The message is sent early in the morning."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段列出三個警訊：沒有說明資料來自哪位醫生或哪項研究、試圖讓你害怕或興奮、要你趕快分享，分別對應 (A)(B)(C)。「早上傳送」並不是警訊，爺爺每天早上六點傳的早安圖就不是假訊息。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Grandpa from the last paragraph?", "options": ["He has stopped sending messages to the family group.", "He has become someone who helps others check information.", "He no longer trusts any health information at all.", "He has started working as a real police detective."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說爺爺會先查證再分享，還教社區活動中心的朋友使用聊天機器人，大家叫他 \"Detective Chou\"，可推知他成了幫助別人查證資訊的人。(A) 他的早安圖仍每天六點送達；(C) 文中只說假的健康小撇步不見了，沒說他不相信任何健康資訊；(D)「偵探」只是朋友給他的綽號。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a54", "title": "Bells on Our Backpacks: Sharing the Mountains with Taiwan's Black Bear", "title_zh": "背包上的鈴鐺：與臺灣黑熊共享山林", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature", "Environment"], "passage": "Last summer, our school hiking club spent three days on the Batongguan Historic Trail in Yushan National Park. Before we set off, a park ranger named Mr. Hu gave each of us a small bell to tie to our backpacks. \"These are not for decoration,\" he said. \"They tell the bears that you are coming.\" We laughed, but he was serious.\n\nThe Formosan black bear is the only kind of bear native to Taiwan. It is easy to recognize by the white V-shaped mark on its chest, which looks like a crescent moon. Adult males can weigh more than 100 kilograms. The bears live mainly in mountain forests between 1,000 and 3,500 meters above sea level. No one knows exactly how many are left, but their numbers are small, so the bear is protected by law.\n\nAlthough bears look frightening, Mr. Hu explained that they are actually shy and usually avoid people. Most of their food comes from plants. In autumn, they climb oak trees to eat acorns, the small nuts that help them store fat for the winter. They also eat fruit, insects and sometimes small animals. \"A bear that hears you will usually walk away,\" he said. \"Trouble happens when you surprise one at close range.\"\n\nHe also told us what to do if we met a bear. Stay calm and never run, because running may make the bear chase you. Instead, keep facing the bear and slowly back away while speaking in a low, steady voice. Never feed a bear or move closer to take a photo. At night, we packed our food and trash tightly so that the smell would not attract visitors.\n\nThe bigger danger, however, is people hurting bears. Some hunters set traps called snares to catch wild boars and deer, and bears sometimes step into them by mistake. Several rescued bears have lost toes or even whole paws this way. On our last day, we found fresh claw marks on a tree beside the trail, but no bear. I was a little disappointed, yet I also felt glad. Somewhere in the forest, a bear had heard our bells and chosen to stay out of our way.", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，我們學校的登山社在玉山國家公園的八通關古道走了三天。出發前，一位姓胡的保育巡查員發給我們每人一個小鈴鐺，要我們綁在背包上。「這些可不是裝飾品，」他說，「它們會告訴黑熊你們來了。」我們都笑了，但他是認真的。\n\n臺灣黑熊是臺灣唯一的原生熊類。牠很容易辨認，因為胸前有一塊白色的 V 字形斑紋，看起來像一彎新月。成年公熊的體重可以超過一百公斤。黑熊主要生活在海拔一千到三千五百公尺之間的山區森林。沒有人確切知道還剩下多少隻，但牠們的數量很少，因此受到法律保護。\n\n胡先生解釋，雖然黑熊看起來很可怕，但牠們其實很害羞，通常會避開人類。牠們大部分的食物來自植物。秋天時，牠們會爬上橡樹吃橡實，這種小小的堅果能幫助牠們為冬天儲存脂肪。牠們也吃水果、昆蟲，有時也吃小動物。「聽到你們聲音的熊通常會自己走開，」他說，「麻煩往往發生在你近距離嚇到牠的時候。」\n\n他也告訴我們萬一遇到黑熊該怎麼做。保持冷靜，千萬不要跑，因為逃跑可能會讓熊追你。你應該面對著熊，慢慢往後退，同時用低沉而平穩的聲音說話。絕對不要餵熊，也不要為了拍照而靠近。晚上，我們把食物和垃圾都包得緊緊的，這樣氣味才不會引來「訪客」。\n\n然而，更大的危險其實是人類傷害黑熊。有些獵人會設置一種叫做「吊索」的陷阱來捕捉山豬和鹿，而黑熊有時會不小心踩進去。好幾隻被救援的黑熊就因此失去了腳趾，甚至整隻熊掌。最後一天，我們在步道旁的一棵樹上發現了新鮮的爪痕，卻沒有看到熊。我有一點失望，但也覺得很高興。在森林的某個地方，有一隻熊聽到了我們的鈴聲，選擇避開了我們。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why hikers should stay away from Yushan National Park", "What the writer learned about Taiwan's black bears and how to share the mountains with them", "How park rangers train black bears to stay away from people", "Why the writer's hiking club failed to take a photo of a black bear"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以作者在八通關古道登山的經驗為主軸，依序介紹臺灣黑熊的外型與棲地（第二段）、個性與食性（第三段）、遇熊時的應對方法（第四段）以及牠們面臨的威脅（第五段），也就是如何與黑熊共享山林。(A) 文中沒有勸人別去玉山國家公園；(C) 胡先生教的是登山客，並非訓練黑熊；(D) 文中只說不要為了拍照而靠近熊，從未提到登山社想拍熊；沒看到熊只是結尾的細節。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to Mr. Hu, what is the purpose of the bells?", "options": ["To call for help if a hiker gets lost", "To keep bears away from the hikers' food at night", "To let bears know that people are coming", "To decorate the hikers' backpacks"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段胡先生說 \"They tell the bears that you are coming.\"，鈴鐺是讓熊知道有人來了；第三段他也說聽到人聲的熊通常會自己走開。(A) 文中沒有提到求救；(B) 晚上是靠把食物和垃圾包緊來避免氣味吸引動物，與鈴鐺無關；(D) 胡先生明白說 \"These are not for decoration\"。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what should a hiker do if he or she meets a black bear?", "options": ["Face the bear and move back slowly while talking calmly", "Turn around and run away as fast as possible", "Throw some food to the bear so that it will leave", "Stay still and quietly take a photo of the bear"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：keep facing the bear and slowly back away while speaking in a low, steady voice，也就是面對熊、慢慢後退並用平穩的聲音說話。(B) 文中說 never run；(C) 文中說 Never feed a bear；(D) 文中說不要為了拍照而靠近熊。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"snares\" in the last paragraph refers to ______.", "options": ["bears that have been rescued", "marks that bears leave on trees", "bells that hikers tie to their bags", "traps used to catch wild animals"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：Some hunters set traps called snares to catch wild boars and deer，snares 就是獵人用來捕捉野生動物的陷阱（吊索）。(A) 被救援的熊是受害者；(B) 熊留在樹上的是 claw marks（爪痕）；(C) 鈴鐺是 bells。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did the writer feel glad at the end of the trip?", "options": ["Because the hikers finally saw a bear up close", "Because the claw marks proved that bears are not dangerous", "Because it seemed that a bear had avoided them, just as Mr. Hu said", "Because the trip was over and the writer could go home"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段結尾說 a bear had heard our bells and chosen to stay out of our way，對照第三段胡先生所說「聽到人聲的熊通常會走開」，作者高興是因為熊似乎真的聽到鈴聲而避開了他們。(A) 他們並沒有看到熊；(B) 爪痕只表示有熊來過，不能證明熊不危險；(D) 文中沒有提到作者想回家。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a55", "title": "More Than a Day Off: Understanding Typhoons", "title_zh": "不只是放假：認識颱風", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "For many students in Taiwan, the word \"typhoon\" brings one question to mind: Will there be a day off tomorrow? Every summer, families stay up late waiting for the news. But behind these announcements is a powerful storm that deserves our respect, not just our excitement.\n\nA typhoon is born over warm tropical seas. When the surface of the ocean reaches about 26.5 degrees Celsius or higher, large amounts of warm, wet air rise into the sky. As the air rises, it cools and forms huge clouds, and more air rushes in from below to take its place. Because the Earth is turning, this moving air begins to spin. In the Northern Hemisphere, typhoons spin counterclockwise, the opposite direction to the way a clock's hands move.\n\nAt the center of a strong typhoon is the \"eye,\" an area of calm air that can be dozens of kilometers wide. Inside the eye, the wind is light and the sky may even be blue. This can fool people. When the eye passes over a town, some people think the storm is over and go outside, only to be caught by violent winds again when the other side of the storm arrives.\n\nTaiwan lies right in the common path of typhoons crossing the western Pacific, and most of them arrive between July and October. The Central Mountain Range often weakens a typhoon's winds, but the mountains also force the wet air upward, squeezing heavy rain out of the clouds. In August 2009, Typhoon Morakot dropped about 3,000 millimeters of rain on some mountain areas in just a few days, more than many places receive in a whole year. Floods and landslides destroyed entire villages.\n\nStill, typhoons are not only bad news. Their rain fills the reservoirs that supply water to homes, farms and factories. So the next time a typhoon day off is announced, remember that the decision is made for safety, not for fun. Stay indoors, keep away from rivers, mountains and the coast, and be thankful for the water the storm brings.", "passage_zh": "對許多臺灣學生來說，「颱風」這個詞會讓人立刻想到一個問題：明天會放假嗎？每年夏天，許多家庭都會熬夜等待消息。但在這些宣布的背後，是一個值得我們敬畏、而不只是讓人興奮的強大風暴。\n\n颱風誕生在溫暖的熱帶海洋上。當海面水溫達到約攝氏 26.5 度以上時，大量溫暖潮濕的空氣會上升到天空中。空氣上升時會冷卻並形成巨大的雲，同時更多空氣從下方湧入，填補原本的位置。由於地球在自轉，這些流動的空氣便開始旋轉。在北半球，颱風以逆時針方向旋轉，也就是和時鐘指針轉動的方向相反。\n\n強烈颱風的中心是「颱風眼」，這是一片空氣平靜的區域，寬度可達數十公里。在颱風眼裡，風很小，天空甚至可能是藍色的。這可能會騙過人們。當颱風眼經過某個城鎮時，有些人以為颱風已經結束而走到戶外，結果當風暴的另一側來到時，又遭到猛烈的強風侵襲。\n\n臺灣正好位在颱風橫越西太平洋時常走的路徑上，而大多數颱風在七月到十月之間來襲。中央山脈常會減弱颱風的風力，但山脈也會迫使潮濕的空氣往上升，從雲層中「擠」出大量雨水。2009 年 8 月，莫拉克颱風在短短幾天內就在部分山區降下約 3,000 毫米的雨量，比許多地方一整年的雨量還多。洪水和山崩摧毀了整座村莊。\n\n不過，颱風並不全是壞消息。颱風帶來的雨水會填滿水庫，供應家庭、農田和工廠用水。所以下次宣布放颱風假時，請記得這個決定是為了安全，而不是為了讓你玩樂。待在室內，遠離河川、山區和海邊，並且感謝風暴帶來的水。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?", "options": ["To help readers understand typhoons and take them seriously", "To explain how local governments decide on typhoon days off", "To describe the damage caused by Typhoon Morakot in detail", "To encourage students to have fun on typhoon days off"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段指出颱風假背後是「值得我們敬畏」的強大風暴，接著說明颱風如何形成（第二段）、颱風眼（第三段）、對臺灣的影響（第四段），最後提醒放颱風假是為了安全，可見目的在讓讀者認識並重視颱風。(B) 文中沒有說明政府如何決定是否放假；(C) 莫拉克颱風只是第四段的一個例子；(D) 與最後一段 the decision is made for safety, not for fun 相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, which of the following is needed for a typhoon to form?", "options": ["Cold air coming down from the mountains", "Strong winds blowing in a clockwise direction", "A large area of dry land with high temperatures", "Ocean water that is about 26.5°C or warmer"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：When the surface of the ocean reaches about 26.5 degrees Celsius or higher, large amounts of warm, wet air rise into the sky. 颱風需要約攝氏 26.5 度以上的溫暖海水。(A) 文中沒有提到山上的冷空氣；(B) 北半球的颱風是逆時針旋轉；(C) 颱風誕生在海洋上，不是乾燥的陸地。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why can the eye of a typhoon be dangerous?", "options": ["It brings the heaviest rain of the whole storm.", "People may think the storm has ended and go outside too early.", "It is so wide that it covers the whole of Taiwan.", "Its winds spin faster than those in any other part of the storm."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：some people think the storm is over and go outside, only to be caught by violent winds again when the other side of the storm arrives. 颱風眼風小、天空可能放晴，會讓人誤以為颱風已經過去而出門。(A) 文中沒有說颱風眼的雨最大；(C) 颱風眼寬約數十公里，不可能涵蓋全臺；(D) 文中說颱風眼裡 the wind is light，與此相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"violent\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["gentle", "cool", "extremely strong", "unusual"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段 caught by violent winds again 指颱風的另一側來到時，人們又遭到「猛烈的」強風侵襲，violent 意為「劇烈的、極強的」，與 extremely strong 最接近。(A) gentle「溫和的」意思相反；(B) cool「涼爽的」與 (D) unusual「不尋常的」都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following statements is true according to the passage?", "options": ["Most typhoons hit Taiwan between January and April.", "The Central Mountain Range usually makes typhoon winds stronger.", "Typhoons bring nothing but damage to Taiwan.", "Typhoon rain helps fill the reservoirs that people depend on."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：Their rain fills the reservoirs that supply water to homes, farms and factories. 颱風的雨水能填滿供應用水的水庫。(A) 第四段說大多數颱風在七月到十月來襲；(B) 第四段說中央山脈常會「減弱」颱風的風力；(C) 第五段說 typhoons are not only bad news。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a56", "title": "The Great Escape of Inky the Octopus", "title_zh": "章魚 Inky 的大逃亡", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "One night in 2016, workers at the National Aquarium of New Zealand left the lid of a tank slightly open. When they returned the next morning, Inky, the octopus who lived in that tank, was gone. He had squeezed through the small gap, slid across the floor and found a narrow drainpipe that led to the sea. Inky never came back, and his escape quickly became news around the world.\n\nHow could an animal do something so clever? Part of the answer is its body. An octopus has no bones at all. The only hard part is its beak, a sharp mouth shaped like a parrot's. This means it can pass through almost any opening larger than its beak. Even a fairly large octopus can get through a hole about the size of a coin.\n\nIts brain is also unusual. An octopus has about 500 million nerve cells, far more than most other animals without a backbone. However, around two-thirds of them are not in its head but in its eight arms. As a result, each arm can touch, taste and even make simple decisions on its own. In aquariums, octopuses have learned to open jars, recognize their keepers and even spray water at people they do not like.\n\nOctopuses are also masters of disguise. Special cells in their skin let them change color in less than a second, and some can even change the texture of their skin to look like rocks or seaweed. If an enemy still comes close, they shoot out a cloud of dark ink and swim away. Inside, their bodies are just as strange: an octopus has three hearts and blue blood.\n\nSurprisingly, such a smart animal lives a very short life. Most octopuses live only one or two years, and they grow up alone, without parents to teach them. Everything they know, they must learn by themselves. Perhaps that is why scientists find them so fascinating. Inky's escape was more than a funny news story. It was a reminder that intelligence on Earth can take forms very different from our own.", "passage_zh": "2016 年的某天晚上，紐西蘭國家水族館的工作人員沒有把一個水族箱的蓋子完全蓋好。隔天早上他們回來時，住在那個水族箱裡的章魚 Inky 已經不見了。牠從那道小縫鑽了出去，滑過地板，找到一根通往大海的細窄排水管。Inky 再也沒有回來，牠的逃亡很快就成了全世界的新聞。\n\n動物怎麼可能做出這麼聰明的事？部分答案在於牠的身體。章魚全身完全沒有骨頭，唯一堅硬的部分是牠的喙，也就是一張形狀像鸚鵡嘴的尖銳嘴巴。這表示只要開口比牠的喙大，牠幾乎都能穿過去。即使是體型相當大的章魚，也能鑽過一個大約硬幣大小的洞。\n\n牠的大腦也很特別。一隻章魚大約有五億個神經細胞，遠多於其他大多數無脊椎動物。然而，其中大約三分之二並不在頭部，而是在牠的八隻腕足裡。因此，每一隻腕足都能觸摸、品嚐，甚至自己做出簡單的決定。在水族館裡，章魚曾學會打開瓶罐、認出牠們的飼育員，甚至會朝自己不喜歡的人噴水。\n\n章魚也是偽裝高手。牠們皮膚裡的特殊細胞能讓牠們在不到一秒內改變顏色，有些甚至能改變皮膚的質感，看起來就像岩石或海藻。如果敵人仍然靠近，牠們就會噴出一團黑色墨汁，然後游走。牠們的身體內部同樣奇特：章魚有三顆心臟，而且血液是藍色的。\n\n令人意外的是，這麼聰明的動物壽命卻非常短。大多數章魚只能活一到兩年，而且牠們獨自長大，沒有父母教導。牠們所知道的一切都必須靠自己學會。也許這就是科學家覺得牠們如此迷人的原因。Inky 的逃亡不只是一則有趣的新聞，它也提醒我們：地球上的智慧，可以有和我們截然不同的形式。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How aquariums can stop their animals from escaping", "Why octopuses are in danger in the world's oceans", "The special features that make octopuses clever and unusual animals", "How Inky was finally found and brought back to the aquarium"], "answer": 2, "explain": "文章以 Inky 逃亡事件開頭，接著介紹章魚沒有骨頭的身體（第二段）、特殊的神經系統（第三段）、偽裝能力與三顆心臟（第四段）以及短暫的一生（第五段），主旨是章魚聰明又奇特的各種特徵。(A) 文中沒有談水族館如何防止動物逃脫；(B) 文中沒有提到章魚面臨危險；(D) 第一段說 Inky never came back。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "How did Inky get out of the aquarium?", "options": ["He squeezed through a gap and escaped through a drainpipe.", "A worker carried him to the sea by mistake.", "He broke the glass of his tank with his beak.", "He hid in a jar and was taken out by a visitor."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：had squeezed through the small gap, slid across the floor and found a narrow drainpipe that led to the sea，Inky 從縫隙鑽出，經由排水管逃到海裡。(B) 不是工作人員帶走的；(C) 文中沒有打破玻璃；(D) 打開瓶罐是第三段提到章魚的其他能力，與這次逃脫無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why can an octopus get through very small holes?", "options": ["Its arms can make decisions by themselves.", "It can change the color of its skin.", "It can shoot out ink to make a hole bigger.", "Its body has no bones, and only its beak is hard."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：An octopus has no bones at all. The only hard part is its beak... it can pass through almost any opening larger than its beak. 因為沒有骨頭、只有喙是硬的，所以能鑽過小洞。(A) 腕足能自己做決定是第三段的內容；(B) 變色是用來偽裝；(C) 墨汁是用來擺脫敵人，不能把洞變大。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"disguise\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["fighting enemies with strong arms", "changing one's appearance in order to hide", "swimming very fast in deep water", "making friends with other animals"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說章魚能改變顏色和皮膚的質感，看起來像岩石或海藻，可見 masters of disguise 指「偽裝高手」，disguise 是改變外表來隱藏自己。(A)(C)(D) 都與改變外觀、躲藏起來無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the last paragraph?", "options": ["Young octopuses do not learn their skills from their parents.", "Octopuses are smarter than all other animals.", "Octopuses live longer in aquariums than in the sea.", "Scientists do not think octopuses are worth studying."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：they grow up alone, without parents to teach them. Everything they know, they must learn by themselves. 可推知章魚的本領不是向父母學來的。(B) 文中只說章魚聰明，沒有說牠比所有動物都聰明；(C) 文中沒有比較水族館和海中的壽命；(D) 與 scientists find them so fascinating 相反。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a57", "title": "A Highway That Makes Room for Butterflies", "title_zh": "為蝴蝶讓路的高速公路", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Environment", "Nature"], "passage": "Every spring, drivers on National Freeway No. 3 near Linnei, Yunlin County, may notice something unusual: one lane is closed, not for road work, but for butterflies. On the busiest days, thousands of purple crow butterflies may fly across the highway in a single hour, and the government has decided to make room for them.\n\nPurple crow butterflies have dark brown wings that shine blue and purple in the sunlight. Four kinds of them live in Taiwan. In winter, they gather in warm, sheltered valleys in the south, such as those in Maolin, Kaohsiung, where the mountains block the cold northeast wind. From about November to March, huge numbers of them rest on the trees there, forming what people call \"butterfly valleys.\" It is often said that Taiwan and Mexico, where monarch butterflies spend the winter, are the only two places in the world where butterflies gather in such large groups.\n\nWhen the weather warms up in spring, the butterflies leave the valleys and head north. Many of them travel along the western side of the Central Mountain Range, and near Linnei they must cross the busy highway. Because they fly low, many used to be hit by cars and trucks. Volunteers who studied the butterflies found large numbers of dead ones on the road every spring.\n\nIn 2007, after volunteers and scientists asked for help, the freeway authorities took action. They put up tall nets beside the road, which make the butterflies rise above the traffic. When large numbers are crossing, they also close the outside lane and warn drivers to slow down. Thanks to these steps, far fewer butterflies are killed on the road today.\n\nThis story shows that protecting nature does not always require huge changes. Sometimes it only takes one closed lane, some nets and drivers who are willing to wait a few extra minutes. For a few weeks each spring, a busy highway becomes a road shared by people and butterflies.", "passage_zh": "每年春天，行經雲林縣林內附近國道 3 號的駕駛人，可能會注意到一件不尋常的事：有一條車道被封閉了，原因不是道路施工，而是為了蝴蝶。在最繁忙的日子裡，一小時內可能就有數千隻紫斑蝶飛越這條高速公路，而政府決定為牠們讓出空間。\n\n紫斑蝶的翅膀是深褐色的，在陽光下會閃耀出藍紫色的光澤。臺灣共有四種紫斑蝶。冬天時，牠們會聚集在南部溫暖、有遮蔽的山谷中，例如高雄茂林一帶，那裡的山脈擋住了寒冷的東北風。大約從十一月到隔年三月，大批紫斑蝶停棲在那裡的樹上，形成人們所說的「蝴蝶谷」。常有人說，臺灣和帝王斑蝶過冬的墨西哥，是全世界僅有的兩個蝴蝶會如此大規模聚集的地方。\n\n春天天氣變暖時，紫斑蝶便離開山谷向北飛。許多紫斑蝶沿著中央山脈西側前進，到了林內附近就必須飛越繁忙的高速公路。因為牠們飛得很低，過去有許多紫斑蝶被汽車和卡車撞死。研究紫斑蝶的志工每年春天都在路面上發現大量死去的蝴蝶。\n\n2007 年，在志工和科學家請求協助之後，高速公路主管機關採取了行動。他們在路旁架設高高的網子，讓蝴蝶飛升到車流上方。當大量蝴蝶正在飛越時，他們也會封閉外側車道，並提醒駕駛人減速慢行。多虧了這些措施，如今在路上死亡的蝴蝶已經大幅減少。\n\n這個故事告訴我們，保護大自然不一定需要巨大的改變。有時只需要封閉一條車道、架設一些網子，再加上願意多等幾分鐘的駕駛人。每年春天有幾個星期，一條繁忙的高速公路會成為人類與蝴蝶共享的道路。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why purple crow butterflies are more beautiful than monarch butterflies", "How engineers build highways through high mountains", "Why drivers in Yunlin often get stuck in traffic jams", "How a highway in Taiwan was changed to protect traveling butterflies"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明紫斑蝶春天北返時必須飛越國道 3 號，以及高速公路架設網子、封閉外側車道來保護牠們，主旨是臺灣一條高速公路為了保護遷徙的蝴蝶而做出改變。(A) 文中沒有比較兩種蝴蝶的美；(B) 文中沒有談如何建造公路；(C) 文中沒有提到塞車問題。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, when can people see large numbers of purple crow butterflies in the butterfly valleys?", "options": ["From about July to October", "From about November to March", "Only for a few days in early summer", "All year round"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：From about November to March, huge numbers of them rest on the trees there, forming what people call \"butterfly valleys.\" 大約從十一月到隔年三月可以看到大批紫斑蝶。(A)(C)(D) 的時間都與文中不符；而且第三段說春天變暖後牠們就會離開山谷。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What is the main purpose of the tall nets beside the highway?", "options": ["To make the butterflies fly higher than the cars and trucks", "To catch the butterflies so that scientists can study them", "To stop drivers from leaving the highway", "To keep the cold northeast wind away from the valleys"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：They put up tall nets beside the road, which make the butterflies rise above the traffic. 網子是讓蝴蝶飛高、越過車流。(B) 網子不是用來捕捉蝴蝶的；(C) 文中沒有提到；(D) 擋住東北風的是南部山谷周圍的山脈（第二段），不是網子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"sheltered\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["crowded", "dry", "protected from wind and bad weather", "empty"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段 warm, sheltered valleys... where the mountains block the cold northeast wind，山脈擋住了寒風，可知 sheltered 意為「有遮蔽的、受保護的」。(A) crowded「擁擠的」、(B) dry「乾燥的」、(D) empty「空的」都不符合上下文。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward the steps taken on the highway?", "options": ["Worried", "Approving", "Doubtful", "Uninterested"], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段作者說保護自然不一定需要巨大的改變，並形容這條高速公路成為 a road shared by people and butterflies，語氣正面，可見作者贊同這些措施。(A) worried「擔心的」、(C) doubtful「懷疑的」、(D) uninterested「不感興趣的」都與文中肯定的語氣不符。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a58", "title": "When Thunder Roars: Three Myths About Lightning", "title_zh": "雷聲響起時：關於閃電的三個迷思", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "On summer afternoons in Taiwan, a bright, sunny morning can quickly turn into a thunderstorm. The hot sun heats the ground, warm wet air rises fast, and by three or four o'clock, dark clouds and flashes of lightning fill the sky. A single flash can heat the air around it to about 30,000 degrees Celsius, roughly five times hotter than the surface of the sun. Lightning is amazing to watch, but many people still believe things about it that are simply not true.\n\nMyth 1: Lightning never strikes the same place twice. In fact, lightning often hits the same place again and again, especially tall, pointed objects. The Empire State Building in New York, for example, is struck more than 20 times in an average year. Lightning does not remember where it has been. It simply follows the easiest path to the ground.\n\nMyth 2: The rubber tires of a car protect you. A car with a hard metal roof is a fairly safe place during a storm, but the tires have nothing to do with it. If lightning hits the car, the metal body carries the electricity around the people inside and down into the ground. A car with a soft roof, or a motorcycle, offers no such protection.\n\nMyth 3: If it isn't raining, you are safe. Lightning can strike more than 15 kilometers away from the storm that produces it, so if you can hear thunder, you are close enough to be hit. To judge the distance, count the seconds between the flash and the thunder. Because sound travels about one kilometer every three seconds, a nine-second gap means the storm is only about three kilometers away.\n\nSo what should you do? Remember the saying \"When thunder roars, go indoors.\" A building with electric wires and water pipes, or a hard-topped car, is the best shelter. Do not stand under a tall tree, and stay away from open fields, rooftops and water. After the storm seems to pass, wait at least 30 minutes after the last thunder before going outside again. A little patience is a small price to pay for your safety.", "passage_zh": "在臺灣的夏天，一個晴朗明亮的早晨可能很快就變成雷雨天。炎熱的太陽曬熱地面，溫暖潮濕的空氣迅速上升，到了下午三、四點，天空就布滿烏雲和閃電。一道閃電能把周圍的空氣加熱到大約攝氏三萬度，約是太陽表面溫度的五倍。閃電看起來令人驚嘆，但許多人至今仍相信一些關於閃電、其實根本不正確的說法。\n\n迷思一：閃電不會兩次打在同一個地方。事實上，閃電常常一次又一次地打在同一個地方，尤其是高聳、尖銳的物體。例如，紐約的帝國大廈平均每年會被閃電擊中二十次以上。閃電不會記得自己去過哪裡，它只是沿著通往地面最容易的路徑前進。\n\n迷思二：汽車的橡膠輪胎能保護你。有堅硬金屬車頂的汽車在暴風雨中是相當安全的地方，但這和輪胎毫無關係。如果閃電擊中汽車，金屬車身會把電流從車內乘客的周圍導開，一路導入地面。軟頂的汽車或機車則無法提供這樣的保護。\n\n迷思三：沒有下雨就很安全。閃電可能打在距離產生它的暴風雨 15 公里以上的地方，所以只要你聽得到雷聲，你就近到可能被擊中。要判斷距離，可以計算從看到閃光到聽到雷聲之間的秒數。因為聲音大約每三秒傳播一公里，相隔九秒就表示暴風雨只在大約三公里外。\n\n那麼你該怎麼做呢？記住這句話：「雷聲響，進室內。」有電線和水管的建築物，或是有硬頂的汽車，是最好的避難處。不要站在高大的樹下，並遠離空曠的田野、屋頂和水邊。暴風雨似乎過去之後，也要在最後一次雷聲後等待至少 30 分鐘，再回到戶外。多一點耐心，對你的安全來說只是很小的代價。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why Taiwan has more thunderstorms than New York", "To correct some wrong ideas about lightning and explain how to stay safe", "To describe how the Empire State Building was built", "To compare the temperature of lightning with that of the sun"], "answer": 1, "explain": "文章列出三個關於閃電的迷思並一一說明真相（第二至四段），最後一段提供雷雨時的安全建議，目的是糾正錯誤觀念並說明如何保持安全。(A) 文中沒有比較臺灣和紐約的雷雨次數；(C) 帝國大廈只是第二段的一個例子；(D) 溫度的比較只是第一段的一個細節。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why is a car with a hard metal roof a fairly safe place during a thunderstorm?", "options": ["Its metal body carries the electricity around the passengers and into the ground.", "Its rubber tires stop the electricity from reaching the ground.", "Lightning never strikes cars that are moving.", "Its windows are strong enough to block lightning."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：the metal body carries the electricity around the people inside and down into the ground，汽車安全是因為金屬車身把電流從乘客周圍導入地面。(B) 文中明白說 the tires have nothing to do with it；(C) 文中沒有這種說法；(D) 文中沒有提到車窗。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Kevin sees a flash of lightning and hears the thunder six seconds later. About how far away is the storm?", "options": ["About 6 kilometers", "About 3 kilometers", "About 2 kilometers", "About 18 kilometers"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：sound travels about one kilometer every three seconds，相隔六秒表示距離約 6 ÷ 3 = 2 公里。(A) 誤把秒數當成公里數；(B) 是相隔九秒時的距離；(D) 是把秒數乘以三的錯誤算法。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Amy is playing basketball on an outdoor court. It is not raining, but she hears thunder. According to the passage, what should she do?", "options": ["Keep playing until it starts to rain", "Wait under a tall tree beside the court", "Go up to the school rooftop to see where the storm is", "Go into a nearby building right away"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說只要聽得到雷聲就可能被閃電擊中，沒下雨也不安全；第五段說 \"When thunder roars, go indoors.\"，所以應該立刻進入附近的建築物。(A) 正是迷思三的錯誤觀念；(B) 第五段說不要站在高大的樹下；(C) 第五段說要遠離屋頂。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 2, the word \"It\" in \"It simply follows the easiest path to the ground\" refers to ______.", "options": ["lightning", "the Empire State Building", "the same place", "an average year"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Lightning does not remember where it has been. It simply follows the easiest path to the ground. 前一句的主詞是 Lightning，沿著最容易的路徑到達地面的也是閃電，所以 It 指閃電。(B)(C)(D) 都不會「沿著路徑前往地面」。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a59", "title": "Water Bears: Small but Almost Unbreakable", "title_zh": "水熊蟲：渺小卻幾乎打不倒", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "When Ms. Lai's biology class put a drop of water from some wet moss under a microscope, the students did not expect to see anything special. Instead, they found a tiny creature slowly walking across the glass on eight short legs. It looked like a fat little bear without ears. \"That,\" said Ms. Lai, \"is a tardigrade, also called a water bear. It may be the toughest animal on Earth.\"\n\nTardigrades are very small. Most are less than one millimeter long, about the size of a grain of sand, so you need a microscope to see them clearly. Scientists have found more than 1,000 kinds, and they live almost everywhere: in moss and soil, at the bottom of the sea, in hot springs, on high mountains and even in Antarctica.\n\nWhat makes them famous is their ability to survive. When their home dries up, a tardigrade pulls in its head and legs, loses almost all the water in its body and becomes a dry little ball called a \"tun.\" In this state, its body almost stops working. It can stay this way for years, and when water returns, it comes back to life within a few hours. In one famous study, Japanese scientists brought back to life tardigrades that had been frozen in Antarctic moss for over 30 years.\n\nIn the tun state, tardigrades can survive conditions that would kill almost any other animal. Some have survived temperatures close to -273°C, the coldest temperature possible, and others have lived through heat of over 150°C for a few minutes. In 2007, scientists even sent tardigrades into space and left them outside a spacecraft for ten days. After returning to Earth, some were still alive and later laid healthy eggs.\n\nHowever, water bears are not superheroes. When they are active, they are actually quite delicate and can be killed easily. Their special power only works when they have time to dry out slowly. Scientists hope that studying them may one day help keep medicines safe without refrigerators, or protect astronauts on long space trips. Not bad for an animal you could easily miss with your own eyes.", "passage_zh": "賴老師生物課的學生把一滴從潮濕苔蘚取來的水放在顯微鏡下觀察時，並不期待會看到什麼特別的東西。沒想到，他們發現一隻微小的生物正用八隻短短的腳，慢慢地走過玻璃片。牠看起來像一隻沒有耳朵的胖小熊。「那是緩步動物，」賴老師說，「也叫做水熊蟲。牠可能是地球上最強悍的動物。」\n\n水熊蟲非常小。大多數不到一公釐長，大約是一粒沙子的大小，所以需要用顯微鏡才能看清楚。科學家已經發現了一千多種水熊蟲，牠們幾乎無所不在：苔蘚和土壤裡、海底、溫泉中、高山上，甚至南極洲都有。\n\n牠們出名的原因是驚人的生存能力。當棲息的環境變乾時，水熊蟲會把頭和腳縮起來，失去身體裡幾乎所有的水分，變成一顆叫做「桶狀體」的乾燥小球。在這種狀態下，牠的身體幾乎完全停止運作。牠可以這樣維持好幾年，等水再次出現時，幾個小時內就能「復活」。在一項著名的研究中，日本科學家讓在南極苔蘚中冷凍了三十多年的水熊蟲重新活了過來。\n\n在桶狀體狀態下，水熊蟲能在幾乎會殺死所有其他動物的環境中存活。有些曾在接近攝氏零下 273 度，也就是可能存在的最低溫度下存活，也有些曾在超過攝氏 150 度的高溫中撐過幾分鐘。2007 年，科學家甚至把水熊蟲送上太空，讓牠們在太空船外待了十天。回到地球後，有些水熊蟲仍然活著，之後還產下了健康的卵。\n\n不過，水熊蟲並不是超級英雄。在活動狀態時，牠們其實相當脆弱，很容易死亡。牠們的特殊能力只有在有時間慢慢變乾時才能發揮。科學家希望，研究牠們有一天能幫助我們在沒有冰箱的情況下保存藥品，或是在長途太空旅行中保護太空人。對一種用肉眼很容易就錯過的動物來說，這已經很了不起了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["A tiny animal that can survive in extreme conditions", "How to use a microscope in a biology class", "Why bears are able to live in Antarctica", "How astronauts protect themselves on long space trips"], "answer": 0, "explain": "文章介紹水熊蟲的大小與分布（第二段）、乾燥成桶狀體的生存方式（第三段）、能熬過極冷、極熱和太空環境（第四段），主旨是一種能在極端環境中存活的微小動物。(B) 顯微鏡只是第一段的情境；(C) 水熊蟲並不是真正的熊；(D) 太空人只是最後一段提到的可能應用。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is a \"tun\"?", "options": ["A kind of moss where tardigrades like to live", "The eggs that tardigrades laid after returning from space", "The dry, ball-like state a tardigrade enters when its home dries up", "A machine that scientists use to freeze tardigrades"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：When their home dries up, a tardigrade... becomes a dry little ball called a \"tun.\" 桶狀體是水熊蟲在環境變乾時變成的乾燥小球。(A) 苔蘚是牠們的棲地；(B) 產卵是第四段的內容；(D) 文中沒有提到這種機器。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what happened to the tardigrades that were sent into space in 2007?", "options": ["All of them died because of the cold.", "Some of them survived and later produced healthy eggs.", "They stayed in space for more than 30 years.", "They were kept safely inside the spacecraft."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：After returning to Earth, some were still alive and later laid healthy eggs. 有些水熊蟲存活下來，之後還產下健康的卵。(A) 與 some were still alive 不符；(C) 牠們在太空中只待了十天，三十多年是第三段冷凍研究的時間；(D) 牠們是被放在太空船「外面」。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"delicate\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["delicious", "powerful", "common", "easily harmed"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：When they are active, they are actually quite delicate and can be killed easily. 後半句說明牠們很容易死亡，所以 delicate 意為「脆弱的、容易受傷的」。(A) delicious「美味的」只是字形相近；(B) powerful「強大的」意思相反；(C) common「常見的」不符合文意。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why are scientists interested in studying tardigrades?", "options": ["They want to use tardigrades as food for astronauts.", "They hope to make tardigrades grow as large as real bears.", "They might learn how to keep medicines safe without refrigerators.", "They want to stop tardigrades from spreading to Antarctica."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：Scientists hope that studying them may one day help keep medicines safe without refrigerators... (A) 文中沒有提到把水熊蟲當作食物；(B) 文中沒有要把牠們養得像熊一樣大；(D) 第二段說南極本來就有水熊蟲，科學家並沒有想阻止牠們擴散。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a60", "title": "Never Make a Crow Angry", "title_zh": "千萬別惹烏鴉生氣", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Nature"], "passage": "Imagine you are walking across a university campus when, suddenly, several black birds start diving at your head and crying loudly. You have done nothing wrong today. But years ago, someone wearing the same face did something the birds have never forgotten. This is not the beginning of a horror movie. It is what happened in a famous experiment with American crows.\n\nIn 2006, scientists at the University of Washington in Seattle wanted to find out whether crows could recognize human faces. Wearing a rubber caveman mask, a few researchers caught a small group of wild crows on campus. They put identification rings on the birds' legs and then let them go. The crows were not hurt, but they clearly did not enjoy the experience.\n\nLater, the researchers walked around campus wearing the same caveman mask. Many crows began to \"scold\" them at once, calling loudly and flying close to their heads. When the same people walked the same paths wearing a different mask, the crows ignored them. The birds were not reacting to the people themselves but to the face they had learned to connect with danger.\n\nThe most surprising part came later. Over the next few years, the number of crows scolding the caveman mask actually grew. Most of these birds had never been caught, and some had not even been born when the experiment began. It seems that crows \"tell\" each other about dangerous faces, and young birds learn by watching how their parents and neighbors react. Years after the first crows were caught, the mask could still cause an uproar of angry calls.\n\nCrows belong to the same bird family as ravens and magpies, a group famous for its intelligence. Birds in this family can solve puzzles, remember where they have hidden food, and in some cases even use sticks as tools. The Seattle experiment shows one more skill: a long memory for faces. So the next time a crow watches you from a power line, be polite. It may remember you for much longer than you think.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，你正走在一所大學的校園裡，突然有好幾隻黑色的鳥開始朝你的頭俯衝，還大聲鳴叫。你今天什麼壞事也沒做。但在多年前，有個戴著同一張臉的人做了一件這些鳥從未忘記的事。這不是恐怖電影的開頭，而是一項以美國烏鴉為對象的著名實驗中真實發生的事。\n\n2006 年，西雅圖華盛頓大學的科學家想知道烏鴉是否能認出人類的臉。幾位研究人員戴著橡膠製的原始人面具，在校園裡捕捉了一小群野生烏鴉。他們在這些鳥的腳上套上識別腳環，然後把牠們放走。這些烏鴉並沒有受傷，但顯然很不喜歡這次的經驗。\n\n後來，研究人員戴著同樣的原始人面具在校園裡走動。許多烏鴉立刻開始「責罵」他們，大聲鳴叫，並飛到他們頭部附近。當同樣的人戴著另一種面具走在同樣的路上時，烏鴉卻不理會他們。這些鳥並不是對那些人本身起反應，而是對牠們已經學會和危險聯想在一起的那張臉起反應。\n\n最令人驚訝的部分還在後頭。在接下來的幾年裡，對原始人面具發出責罵的烏鴉數量反而增加了。這些鳥大多從來沒有被抓過，有些甚至在實驗開始時還沒出生。看來烏鴉會互相「告知」危險的臉孔，而幼鳥則是藉由觀察父母和鄰居的反應來學習。在第一批烏鴉被捕捉多年之後，那張面具仍然能引來一陣憤怒的叫聲，造成一片騷動。\n\n烏鴉和渡鴉、喜鵲同屬一個鳥類家族，這個家族以聰明著稱。這個家族的鳥類會解決難題、記得自己把食物藏在哪裡，有些甚至會用樹枝當作工具。西雅圖的實驗則顯示了牠們的另一項本領：對臉孔的長久記憶。所以下次有烏鴉從電線上看著你時，請客氣一點。牠記得你的時間，可能比你想像的久得多。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Crows are dangerous birds that often attack people.", "Scientists should not catch wild birds for experiments.", "Crows are the only birds that can use tools.", "Crows can remember human faces and share this information with other crows."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文說明西雅圖的實驗：烏鴉能認出曾經捕捉牠們的那張「臉」（第三段），並把這個資訊傳給從未被抓過的烏鴉（第四段），最後指出烏鴉對臉孔有長久的記憶。(A) 烏鴉只對特定的面具有反應，並不是常常攻擊人；(B) 文中沒有反對這類實驗；(C) 第五段說這個家族的鳥類有些會使用工具，並沒有說只有烏鴉會。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why did the crows scold the researchers?", "options": ["The researchers were carrying food.", "The researchers were wearing the mask connected with catching crows.", "The researchers walked too close to the crows' nests.", "The crows were hungry and wanted to be fed."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：The birds were not reacting to the people themselves but to the face they had learned to connect with danger. 研究人員戴著當初捕捉烏鴉時的原始人面具，烏鴉把這張臉和危險聯想在一起。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did the same people also walk the same paths wearing a different mask?", "options": ["To make the crows feel more comfortable on campus", "To catch more crows without being noticed", "To check whether the crows were reacting to the face rather than the people", "To scare the crows away from the university"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：When the same people walked the same paths wearing a different mask, the crows ignored them. The birds were not reacting to the people themselves but to the face... 換戴另一種面具，是為了確認烏鴉反應的是那張臉，而不是人本身。(A)(B)(D) 都不是這樣做的目的。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"uproar\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a loud, noisy reaction", "a quiet moment", "a long journey", "a friendly greeting"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段 the mask could still cause an uproar of angry calls，面具仍會引起一陣憤怒的叫聲，uproar 意為「騷動、喧鬧」。(B) a quiet moment「安靜的片刻」意思相反；(C) a long journey「長途旅行」與 (D) a friendly greeting「友善的問候」都不符合文意。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What was the most surprising result of the experiment?", "options": ["The crows forgot the caveman mask after a few days.", "Crows that had never been caught also scolded the caveman mask.", "The crows attacked anyone who walked on campus.", "The crows were seriously hurt when they were caught."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：The most surprising part came later... Most of these birds had never been caught, and some had not even been born... 最令人驚訝的是，從未被抓過的烏鴉也會責罵那張面具。(A) 烏鴉多年後仍記得那張面具；(C) 第三段說烏鴉會忽略戴其他面具的人；(D) 第二段說 The crows were not hurt。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a61", "title": "Lights in the Dark: My First Firefly Night", "title_zh": "黑暗中的光點：我的第一次賞螢之夜", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Nature", "Life"], "passage": "Last April, my aunt took me on a firefly-watching tour at Green Hill Farm in the mountains of Miaoli. I had only seen fireflies in pictures and cartoons, so I was very excited. When we arrived at seven o'clock, the sky was already dark, and our guide, a cheerful woman named Ms. Peng, gathered our group at the start of a forest path.\n\nBefore we started, Ms. Peng explained the rules. \"First, turn off the flashlights on your phones, or cover them with red paper. Strong light confuses the fireflies. Second, no flash photography. Third, stay on the path, because young fireflies live in the grass and soil, and you might step on them. And of course, don't catch them. Let them stay where they belong.\"\n\nAfter ten minutes of walking, I noticed one small yellow-green light, then another, and soon hundreds more. They floated above the bushes like tiny stars that had come down to Earth. Ms. Peng told us that Taiwan has more than 60 kinds of fireflies, and spring is the best time to see many of them. Each kind flashes in its own pattern. The flashes work like a secret language that males and females use to find each other.\n\nI asked whether the fireflies' lights were hot. Ms. Peng smiled and shook her head. A firefly makes light through a chemical reaction inside its body, and this reaction gives off very little heat. That is why scientists call it \"cold light.\" An old-fashioned light bulb, by contrast, wastes most of its energy as heat.\n\nOn the way back, Ms. Peng told us that fireflies are in trouble in many places. Bright streetlights make it hard for them to see each other's signals, and chemicals used on farms can kill them. Adult fireflies live for only about two weeks, so every night matters. Before getting into the car, I turned around for one last look. The forest was still full of tiny blinking lights, and I quietly promised myself to keep my flashlight off.", "passage_zh": "去年四月，阿姨帶我到苗栗山區的綠丘農場參加賞螢活動。我只在圖片和卡通裡看過螢火蟲，所以非常興奮。我們七點抵達時，天色已經暗了，我們的導覽員是一位名叫彭小姐的開朗女士，她把大家集合在一條森林步道的入口。\n\n出發前，彭小姐說明了規則。「第一，請關掉手機的手電筒，或是用紅色的紙蓋住。強光會讓螢火蟲混亂。第二，禁止使用閃光燈拍照。第三，請待在步道上，因為螢火蟲的幼蟲住在草地和土壤裡，你們可能會踩到牠們。當然，也不要抓牠們。讓牠們留在屬於牠們的地方。」\n\n走了十分鐘後，我注意到一個小小的黃綠色光點，接著又一個，很快就出現了好幾百個。它們飄浮在灌木叢上方，就像降落到地面上的小星星。彭小姐告訴我們，臺灣有六十多種螢火蟲，而春天是觀賞許多種螢火蟲的最佳時節。每一種螢火蟲都以自己獨特的模式閃光。這些閃光就像一種祕密語言，雄蟲和雌蟲用它來找到彼此。\n\n我問螢火蟲的光是不是燙的。彭小姐笑著搖搖頭。螢火蟲是透過體內的化學反應發光，而這種反應幾乎不會產生熱。這就是為什麼科學家稱它為「冷光」。相較之下，傳統的燈泡則把大部分的能量浪費在發熱上。\n\n回程時，彭小姐告訴我們，螢火蟲在許多地方正面臨危機。明亮的路燈讓牠們很難看見彼此的訊號，而農田使用的化學藥劑也可能殺死牠們。成年的螢火蟲只能活大約兩個星期，所以每一個夜晚都很重要。上車前，我轉身看了最後一眼。森林裡仍然滿是一閃一閃的小光點，我悄悄地對自己許下承諾：要讓手電筒保持關閉。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to take good photos of fireflies at night", "Why the writer's aunt works as a firefly guide", "The writer's first firefly-watching trip and what the writer learned", "How farmers in Miaoli use chemicals on their farms"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文描述作者第一次到苗栗參加賞螢活動的經過，以及從導覽員那裡學到的規則、螢火蟲閃光的意義、冷光和牠們面臨的危機。(A) 文中反而說禁止使用閃光燈拍照；(B) 導覽員是彭小姐，不是阿姨；(D) 農田的化學藥劑只是最後一段提到的一項威脅。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Ms. Peng ask visitors to stay on the path?", "options": ["Because the forest is full of dangerous snakes", "Because the farm closes at seven o'clock", "Because the fireflies only fly above the path", "Because visitors might step on young fireflies in the grass and soil"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：stay on the path, because young fireflies live in the grass and soil, and you might step on them. (A) 文中沒有提到蛇；(B) 七點是他們抵達農場的時間；(C) 文中沒有說螢火蟲只在步道上方飛。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why do fireflies flash?", "options": ["To help males and females find each other", "To scare away visitors with flashlights", "To keep their bodies warm at night", "To show that spring is coming"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：The flashes work like a secret language that males and females use to find each other. 閃光是雄蟲和雌蟲尋找彼此的方式。(B)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到；而且第四段說螢火蟲的光幾乎不產生熱，不可能用來保暖。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does \"cold light\" in paragraph 4 mean?", "options": ["Light that can only be seen in cold weather", "Light that produces very little heat", "Light that comes from streetlights", "Light that makes people feel cold"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：this reaction gives off very little heat. That is why scientists call it \"cold light.\" 冷光指的是幾乎不產生熱的光。(A) 與天氣冷熱無關；(C) 路燈是第五段提到的威脅；(D) 並不是讓人覺得冷。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the writer's promise at the end?", "options": ["The writer plans to catch some fireflies next time.", "The writer will never visit the farm again.", "The writer is afraid of walking in the dark forest.", "The writer wants to protect fireflies by not disturbing them with light."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段說強光會讓螢火蟲混亂，第五段也說明亮的路燈讓牠們看不見彼此的訊號，因此作者承諾「讓手電筒保持關閉」，可推知作者想藉由不用光線打擾牠們來保護螢火蟲。(A) 規則明說不要抓螢火蟲；(B) 文中沒有這種說法；(C) 作者並沒有表現出害怕。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a62", "title": "Three Words My Father Never Said", "title_zh": "爸爸從沒說出口的三個字", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Life"], "passage": "My father has never said \"I love you\" to me. He didn't say it on my birthdays, or even when I was in the hospital with a broken leg. For a long time, I thought this meant he was a cold person. Parents in movies and online videos hugged their children and said those three words all the time. Why couldn't my dad?\n\nLast winter, I found the answer in an unexpected place: a plate of guava. I was studying late for a mock exam when Dad knocked on my door. He didn't say a word. He just put a plate on my desk and left. The guava was cut into perfect slices, with a little plum powder on the side, just the way I like it. Suddenly I realized that he had done this almost every night for years. I had just never noticed.\n\nThe next day, I told my homeroom teacher, Ms. Lai, about it. She smiled. \"Many fathers of your parents' generation are not used to expressing their feelings in words,\" she explained. \"They show love through actions instead: cutting fruit, picking you up from cram school, or asking 'Have you eaten yet?'\" She called it \"a quiet language of love.\" Once you learn to read it, she said, you start to see it everywhere.\n\nAfter that, I began to pay attention. Every morning, Dad checked the weather before I left for school and put an umbrella by the door if it was going to rain. When I came home late from club practice, the living room light was always on, though he pretended to be watching TV. He had been talking to me all along. I just hadn't understood his language.\n\nStill, I wanted to say something back. So one night, when he brought me a plate of sliced apples, I didn't just say \"Thanks.\" I said, \"Dad, the guava last week was really sweet. You always pick the best ones.\" He looked surprised, then a little shy. \"The man at the market saves them for me,\" he said softly, and hurried out. But I saw him smiling. Maybe we will never say those three words to each other. That's OK. We have found our own way to say them.", "passage_zh": "我爸爸從來沒有對我說過「我愛你」。我過生日時他沒說，甚至我摔斷腿住院時，他也沒說。很長一段時間，我以為這代表他是個冷漠的人。電影和網路影片裡的父母總是擁抱孩子，把這三個字掛在嘴邊。為什麼我爸爸就是說不出口呢？\n\n去年冬天，我在一個意想不到的地方找到了答案：一盤芭樂。那天我為了模擬考熬夜念書，爸爸來敲我的房門。他一句話也沒說，只是把一個盤子放在我的書桌上就離開了。芭樂被切成整整齊齊的片狀，旁邊還附上一點梅粉，正是我喜歡的吃法。我突然意識到，好幾年來他幾乎每天晚上都這麼做，只是我從來沒有注意到。\n\n隔天，我把這件事告訴了班導賴老師。她笑了笑，解釋說：「很多跟你爸媽同一代的父親，不習慣用言語表達自己的感受。他們改用行動來表達愛：切水果、去補習班接你下課，或是問你『吃飽了沒？』」她把這稱為「一種安靜的愛的語言」。她說，一旦你學會解讀它，就會發現它無所不在。\n\n從那之後，我開始留意。每天早上，爸爸都會在我出門上學前查看天氣，如果會下雨，就在門邊放一把傘。當我參加社團練習很晚才回家時，客廳的燈總是亮著，雖然他假裝在看電視。原來他一直都在跟我說話，只是我之前聽不懂他的語言。\n\n不過，我還是想回應些什麼。於是有一天晚上，當他端來一盤切好的蘋果時，我不只是說「謝謝」。我說：「爸，上星期的芭樂真的好甜，你總是挑到最好的。」他看起來很驚訝，接著有點害羞。「市場的老闆會特地留給我，」他小聲地說，然後匆匆走了出去。但我看見他在笑。也許我們永遠不會對彼此說出那三個字，但沒關係，我們已經找到屬於自己的說法了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the writer came to understand Dad's quiet way of showing love", "Why fathers in Taiwan should hug their children more often", "How to choose and cut sweet guavas at the market", "Why the writer did poorly on a mock exam"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文描述作者原本以為爸爸從不說「我愛你」是因為個性冷漠，後來從爸爸每晚送來的切好水果、雨天放在門邊的傘等行動，理解到這是他「安靜的愛的語言」。(B) 文中並未主張爸爸應該多擁抱孩子；(C) 芭樂只是故事的引子，並不是在教人挑水果；(D) 文中沒有提到模擬考考差。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what did the writer suddenly realize?", "options": ["Dad was unhappy that the writer studied so late.", "Dad did not know what kind of fruit the writer liked.", "Dad had brought cut fruit almost every night for years.", "Dad wanted the writer to stop eating while studying."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Suddenly I realized that he had done this almost every night for years. 作者突然意識到，爸爸好幾年來幾乎每晚都送來切好的水果。(A) 爸爸沒有表示不高興；(B) 爸爸附上梅粉，「正是我喜歡的吃法」，可見他很清楚作者的喜好；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"expressing\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["hiding", "showing", "forgetting", "changing"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：are not used to expressing their feelings in words... They show love through actions instead. 後句的 instead 表示這些父親「改用」行動來表現愛，可知 expressing 的意思接近 showing（表達、表現）。(A) hiding「隱藏」與文意相反；(C) forgetting「忘記」、(D) changing「改變」都不合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why was the living room light always on when the writer came home late?", "options": ["Dad forgot to turn it off before going to bed.", "Dad was watching his favorite late-night TV show.", "Dad was too busy with his work to go to sleep.", "Dad was waiting for the writer to get home, though he didn't say so."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：the living room light was always on, though he pretended to be watching TV. pretended（假裝）表示爸爸其實不是真的在看電視，而是在等作者平安回家，只是沒說出口；下一句 He had been talking to me all along 也說明這是他表達關心的方式。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 他只是「假裝」在看電視。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "How did Dad most likely feel when the writer praised the guava?", "options": ["Angry that the writer had noticed", "Pleased but a little embarrassed", "Worried about the writer's exam", "Bored with the conversation"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段作者稱讚芭樂後，爸爸 looked surprised, then a little shy，說完話就匆匆走出去，但作者 saw him smiling。可見他心裡很高興，只是有點不好意思。(A) 文中沒有任何生氣的描述；(C) 這段對話與考試無關；(D) 他在微笑，並不是覺得無聊。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a63", "title": "How Many Friends Can One Person Really Have?", "title_zh": "一個人真的能有多少朋友？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Science", "Society"], "passage": "When Kai turned seventeen, more than two hundred people wished him a happy birthday online. He had over six hundred followers on social media, so he should have felt popular. Instead, he felt strangely lonely. \"I didn't know who I could call if something bad happened,\" he said. Kai's experience raises an interesting question: how many real friends can one person have?\n\nBritish scientist Robin Dunbar has spent years studying this question. In the 1990s, he noticed that monkeys and apes with larger brains lived in larger groups. Based on the size of the human brain, he predicted that people can keep about 150 meaningful relationships at one time. This idea became known as \"Dunbar's number.\" Interestingly, the number seems to appear in real life again and again, from the size of early farming villages to the number of people British families sent Christmas cards to in one study.\n\nHowever, those 150 people are not all equally close. Dunbar describes our relationships as a set of circles. In the center are about five people, the ones you would turn to in a crisis, such as a serious illness or a family emergency. Next come about fifteen good friends, then fifty, and finally 150 people you would still happily greet if you met them at an airport. The bigger the circle, the weaker the relationships in it.\n\nSocial media does not seem to change these limits. In a 2016 study, Dunbar found that Facebook users had about 150 online friends on average, but they felt they could count on only about four of them in a difficult time. Clicking \"follow\" is easy, but real friendship takes time. One American study found that it takes about 50 hours together to turn someone you just met into a casual friend, and more than 200 hours to become close friends.\n\nFor Kai, this was useful news. He stopped worrying about how many likes his posts got and started spending more time with a few teammates from his basketball club. \"I have fewer followers now,\" he says, \"but I have three friends who would come to the hospital at midnight for me. That's the number that matters.\"", "passage_zh": "凱滿十七歲那天，有兩百多人在網路上祝他生日快樂。他在社群媒體上有六百多個追蹤者，照理說應該覺得自己很受歡迎。然而，他卻莫名地感到寂寞。「我不知道如果發生了不好的事，我可以打電話給誰，」他說。凱的經驗帶出了一個有趣的問題：一個人究竟能有多少真正的朋友？\n\n英國科學家羅賓‧鄧巴（Robin Dunbar）多年來一直在研究這個問題。1990 年代，他注意到大腦較大的猴子和猿類，生活的群體也比較大。根據人類大腦的大小，他推測人一次大約能維持 150 段有意義的人際關係。這個概念後來被稱為「鄧巴數字」。有趣的是，這個數字似乎一再出現在現實生活中，從早期農業村落的規模，到某項研究中英國家庭寄送聖誕卡的對象人數，都是如此。\n\n不過，這 150 個人並不是都一樣親近。鄧巴把我們的人際關係描述成一組同心圓。最中心大約有五個人，是你遇到危機時會求助的對象，例如生了重病或家裡發生緊急狀況的時候。接下來是大約十五個好朋友，然後是五十人，最外層則是 150 個你在機場遇到時仍會開心打招呼的人。圈子越大，裡面的關係就越淡。\n\n社群媒體似乎沒有改變這些限制。在 2016 年的一項研究中，鄧巴發現臉書使用者平均約有 150 個網路好友，但他們覺得在困難時真正能依靠的，只有大約四個人。按下「追蹤」很容易，但真正的友誼需要時間。一項美國研究發現，要把剛認識的人變成普通朋友，大約需要一起相處 50 個小時；要成為親密的好友，則需要超過 200 個小時。\n\n對凱來說，這是很有用的消息。他不再擔心自己的貼文得到多少個讚，而是開始花更多時間和籃球社的幾個隊友相處。「我現在的追蹤者變少了，」他說，「但我有三個朋友願意在半夜趕到醫院陪我。那才是真正重要的數字。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why teenagers should stop using social media", "The limits on how many close relationships people can keep", "How monkeys and apes choose their friends", "How to get more followers online"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文以凱的經驗開頭，介紹鄧巴數字、人際關係的同心圓，以及社群媒體無法改變這些限制，主旨是人能維持的親密關係是有限的。(A) 文中沒有叫青少年停用社群媒體；(C) 猴子和猿類只是第二段研究的背景；(D) 與文意相反，凱後來反而不在意追蹤者和按讚數。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to Dunbar, about how many people are in the closest circle of a person's relationships?", "options": ["Five", "Fifteen", "Fifty", "150"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：In the center are about five people, the ones you would turn to in a crisis. 最中心的一圈大約是五個人。(B) 十五人是「好朋友」那一圈；(C) 五十人與 (D) 150 人都是更外層的圈子。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"crisis\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a happy celebration", "a school activity", "a long holiday", "a time of serious trouble"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段 in a crisis 後面舉例 such as a serious illness or a family emergency（生重病或家中發生緊急狀況），可知 crisis 指「危機、遇到嚴重困難的時刻」。(A) 快樂的慶祝、(B) 學校活動、(C) 長假都與這些例子不符。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the 2016 study in paragraph 4?", "options": ["People with more online friends usually have more close friends.", "Facebook helps people become close friends in less than 50 hours.", "Having many online friends does not mean having many people to depend on.", "Most Facebook users have no real friends at all."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說臉書使用者平均約有 150 個網路好友，但困難時覺得能依靠的只有大約四個人，可推論網路朋友多，不代表可以依靠的人多。(A) 與研究結果相反；(B) 文中說變成普通朋友就要約 50 小時，成為親密好友更要超過 200 小時；(D)「完全沒有真朋友」太過絕對，文中說大約有四個人可以依靠。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "In the last paragraph, what does \"That\" in \"That's the number that matters\" refer to?", "options": ["The number of Kai's followers", "The number of likes on Kai's posts", "The number of friends who would really help Kai when he needs them", "Dunbar's number, 150"], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段凱說追蹤者變少了，但有三個朋友願意半夜趕到醫院陪他，接著說 That's the number that matters，That 指的就是這些在需要時真正會幫他的朋友人數。(A)(B) 追蹤者和按讚數正是他不再在意的數字；(D) 150 是鄧巴數字，並不是凱這句話所指的。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a64", "title": "Sorry Is Not Enough: What Makes a Good Apology", "title_zh": "光說對不起還不夠：怎樣才是好的道歉", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Life", "Science"], "passage": "Pei-shan knew she had made a mistake. She had promised to help her best friend, Wen-ting, practice for a singing contest on Saturday afternoon. Instead, she forgot and went to a movie with some other classmates. Wen-ting found out when she saw their photos online. On Monday, Pei-shan sent her a quick message: \"Sorry! But you know how busy I've been lately.\" Wen-ting never replied. Pei-shan had apologized, so why was her friend still upset?\n\nScientists have studied what makes an apology work. In 2016, researchers at Ohio State University in the U.S. asked 755 people to read different apologies and rate how effective they were. The apologies contained different combinations of six parts: saying you feel sorry, explaining what went wrong, admitting that it was your fault, promising not to do it again, offering to fix the problem, and asking for forgiveness.\n\nThe results were clear. The most important part was admitting responsibility, which means saying \"It was my fault\" without making excuses. The second most important was offering to repair the damage, for example, \"Let me make it up to you.\" Surprisingly, the least important part was asking, \"Will you forgive me?\" The study also found that the more parts an apology included, the better it worked.\n\nLooking back at her message, Pei-shan saw what had gone wrong. Her \"sorry\" was followed by \"but,\" and the rest of the sentence was an excuse. It sounded as if she was blaming her busy schedule instead of herself. When \"but\" comes right after \"sorry,\" an apology often turns into a defense of yourself.\n\nThat evening, Pei-shan called Wen-ting. \"I forgot our practice because I didn't write it down, and that was my fault,\" she said. \"I know how much the contest means to you. Can I come over every day this week to help you get ready?\" After a long pause, Wen-ting laughed. \"Only if you bring snacks.\" Pei-shan learned that a real apology is not just about saying the right word. It is about showing that you understand what you did and that you want to make it right.", "passage_zh": "佩珊知道自己犯了錯。她答應過要在星期六下午幫最好的朋友文婷練習歌唱比賽，結果卻忘得一乾二淨，跑去和其他幾個同學看電影。文婷在網路上看到他們的照片，才發現這件事。星期一，佩珊傳了一則簡短的訊息給她：「抱歉！不過你也知道我最近有多忙。」文婷一直沒有回覆。佩珊已經道歉了，為什麼她的朋友還是很不高興呢？\n\n科學家研究過怎樣的道歉才有效。2016 年，美國俄亥俄州立大學的研究人員請 755 個人閱讀各種不同的道歉，並評估這些道歉有多有效。這些道歉由六個部分以不同的方式組合而成：表達歉意、說明哪裡出了錯、承認是自己的錯、承諾不會再犯、提議彌補問題，以及請求原諒。\n\n結果很清楚。最重要的部分是承擔責任，也就是不找藉口地說出「是我的錯」。第二重要的是提出彌補損害的方法，例如「讓我補償你」。令人意外的是，最不重要的部分是問對方「你願意原諒我嗎？」研究也發現，道歉包含的部分越多，效果就越好。\n\n回頭看自己傳的訊息，佩珊明白了問題出在哪裡。她的「抱歉」後面接著「不過」，而句子剩下的部分全是藉口。聽起來就像是她把錯怪到忙碌的行程上，而不是怪自己。當「不過」緊跟在「抱歉」之後，道歉往往就變成了替自己辯護。\n\n那天晚上，佩珊打電話給文婷。「我會忘了我們的練習，是因為我沒有把它記下來，這是我的錯，」她說，「我知道這場比賽對你有多重要。這個星期我可以每天去你家，幫你準備比賽嗎？」電話那頭沉默了好一陣子，然後文婷笑了。「你要帶零食來才行。」佩珊學到了，真正的道歉不只是說出對的字眼，而是要讓對方知道，你明白自己做了什麼，而且你想要彌補。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why friends should never go to movies without each other", "To describe how to prepare for a singing contest", "To show what makes an apology effective through a story and a study", "To report that most people refuse to forgive their friends"], "answer": 2, "explain": "文章先以佩珊失敗的道歉為例，再介紹俄亥俄州立大學的研究，最後描述她如何重新道歉，目的在說明怎樣的道歉才有效。(A) 看電影只是故事情節；(B) 文中沒有教人準備歌唱比賽；(D) 文婷最後原諒了佩珊，文中也沒有這樣的報告。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the Ohio State study, which part of an apology was the LEAST important?", "options": ["Admitting that it was your fault", "Offering to fix the problem", "Explaining what went wrong", "Asking for forgiveness"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：the least important part was asking, \"Will you forgive me?\" 最不重要的部分是請求原諒。(A) 承認是自己的錯是最重要的部分；(B) 提議彌補是第二重要的部分；(C) 說明哪裡出了錯並不是文中所說最不重要的部分。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did Wen-ting most likely not reply to Pei-shan's first message?", "options": ["The message gave an excuse instead of taking real responsibility.", "Wen-ting was too busy practicing for the contest.", "Wen-ting did not see the message until much later.", "The message invited her to go to another movie."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段指出佩珊的「抱歉」後面接著「不過」，剩下的部分都是藉口，聽起來像在怪忙碌的行程而不是怪自己；第三段也說最重要的是不找藉口地承認錯誤。可推知文婷不回覆，是因為這則訊息只有藉口，沒有真正承擔責任。(B)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"defense\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a promise to change", "words that protect yourself from blame", "a funny joke between friends", "a request for help"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說「抱歉」後面加上「不過」和藉口，聽起來是在怪行程而不是怪自己，因此道歉變成了 a defense of yourself，也就是替自己開脫、保護自己不被責備的話。(A) 承諾改變、(C) 朋友間的玩笑、(D) 請求幫忙都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which parts of a good apology did Pei-shan's phone call clearly include?", "options": ["Admitting her fault and offering to help repair the damage", "Blaming her schedule and asking for forgiveness", "Promising never to see movies again and giving a gift", "Describing the movie and inviting Wen-ting to the next one"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段佩珊說 that was my fault（承認是自己的錯），又問 Can I come over every day this week to help you get ready?（提出彌補的方法），正好是研究中最重要的兩個部分。(B) 她這次沒有怪行程，也沒有請求原諒；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a65", "title": "Name It to Tame It: Finding Words for Your Feelings", "title_zh": "說出情緒，就能馴服情緒：為感受找到合適的詞語", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Science"], "passage": "When the school counselor asked Jun-hao how he felt, he gave his usual answer: \"Fine.\" When she asked again, he said, \"Bad, I guess.\" Those were the only words he could find. But \"bad\" was not the whole story. That morning, he had shouted at his little brother for no reason, and he did not understand why he was so angry.\n\nThe counselor showed him a colorful circle called a \"feelings wheel.\" One of the first feelings wheels was designed by a therapist named Gloria Willcox in 1982, and many different versions are used today. In the center are a few basic emotions, such as sad, angry, scared and happy. Moving outward, each one is divided into more specific words. \"Angry,\" for example, may lead to \"frustrated\" or \"jealous.\" With the wheel in front of him, Jun-hao slowly found better words. He felt embarrassed because he had failed a skills test in front of his classmates, and he was worried that his parents would be disappointed.\n\nPutting feelings into words may sound too simple to help, but science suggests that it works. In a 2007 study at the University of California, Los Angeles (UCLA), people looked at pictures of angry or frightened faces while their brains were scanned. When they chose a word to describe the emotion on each face, activity in the amygdala, a part of the brain that reacts to danger and strong feelings, went down. Naming a feeling seemed to calm the brain. Some experts sum this up as \"Name it to tame it.\"\n\nPsychologists also say that the more exact your words are, the better. A person who can only say \"I feel bad\" does not know what to do next. But someone who knows they feel \"lonely\" might call a friend, while someone who feels \"exhausted\" might simply need a good night's sleep. Exact words point to exact solutions.\n\nJun-hao now keeps a small notebook. Every night, he writes down a word or two for the strongest feeling of his day and what caused it. \"It's strange,\" he says. \"When I know what I'm feeling, it gets smaller.\" Last week, he apologized to his brother, and this time he could explain why he had been so upset.", "passage_zh": "學校的輔導老師問俊豪感覺如何時，他給了一貫的回答：「還好。」老師再問一次，他說：「很糟吧，我想。」那是他唯一想得到的詞。但「很糟」並不是事情的全貌。那天早上，他無緣無故對弟弟大吼，而他自己也不明白為什麼會那麼生氣。\n\n輔導老師給他看一個色彩繽紛的圓形圖，叫做「情緒輪」。最早的情緒輪之一，是由一位名叫葛洛莉亞‧威爾柯克斯（Gloria Willcox）的心理治療師在 1982 年設計的，現在則有許多不同的版本。圓心是幾種基本情緒，例如難過、生氣、害怕和快樂。越往外圈，每一種情緒都會被細分成更具體的詞語。例如「生氣」可能延伸出「挫折」或「嫉妒」。看著眼前的情緒輪，俊豪慢慢找到了更貼切的詞。他覺得很難堪，因為他在同學面前沒通過技能測驗；他也很擔心爸媽會失望。\n\n把感受化為文字，聽起來簡單得好像沒什麼用，但科學顯示它確實有效。在美國加州大學洛杉磯分校（UCLA）2007 年的一項研究中，受試者一邊看著憤怒或驚恐的臉孔照片，一邊接受腦部掃描。當他們選出一個詞來描述每張臉上的情緒時，杏仁核的活動就下降了；杏仁核是大腦中對危險和強烈情緒產生反應的部位。替情緒命名，似乎能讓大腦平靜下來。有些專家把這個道理歸納成一句話：「說出情緒，就能馴服情緒。」\n\n心理學家也說，用詞越精確越好。一個只會說「我覺得很糟」的人，不知道接下來該怎麼做。但知道自己感到「寂寞」的人，可能會打電話給朋友；而覺得「筋疲力盡」的人，可能只需要好好睡一覺。精確的詞語，能指向精確的解決方法。\n\n俊豪現在有一本小筆記本。每天晚上，他會用一兩個詞寫下當天最強烈的感受，以及造成這種感受的原因。「很奇怪，」他說，「當我知道自己有什麼感受時，那種感覺就變小了。」上星期，他向弟弟道歉，而這一次，他能說清楚自己當時為什麼那麼不高興。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Students should not show their feelings at school.", "Finding exact words for feelings can help people deal with them.", "Brain scans are the best way to understand our emotions.", "Feelings wheels were invented to help students pass tests."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文透過俊豪的經驗、情緒輪、UCLA 的研究和心理學家的說法，說明用精確的詞語說出感受，有助於處理情緒。(A) 與文意無關；(C) 腦部掃描只是研究使用的方法；(D) 情緒輪是用來找出描述感受的詞語，與通過考試無關。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the UCLA study, what happened when people named the emotions on the faces?", "options": ["They became more frightened of the pictures.", "They forgot the faces more quickly.", "Activity in a brain area that reacts to strong feelings went down.", "They learned to read faces faster than before."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：When they chose a word to describe the emotion on each face, activity in the amygdala... went down. 杏仁核是大腦中對危險和強烈情緒產生反應的部位，它的活動下降了。(A) 與研究結果相反；(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Jun-hao most likely shout at his brother?", "options": ["He felt embarrassed and worried about his failed test but did not understand those feelings.", "His brother had broken his notebook.", "His brother had told their parents about the test.", "He was angry at the school counselor."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段說俊豪無緣無故對弟弟大吼，自己也 did not understand why he was so angry；第二段他藉由情緒輪才找到更貼切的詞：他因為在同學面前沒通過技能測驗而覺得難堪，也擔心爸媽會失望。可推知他大吼其實是因為這些當時還說不出來的難堪與擔心，第五段也說他後來終於能向弟弟解釋自己為什麼那麼不高興。(B) 文中沒有提到筆記本被弄壞；(C) 文中沒有提到弟弟告訴爸媽考試的事；(D) 他是在大吼之後才和輔導老師談話，也沒有對老師生氣。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"exhausted\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["excited", "angry", "bored", "very tired"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說覺得 exhausted 的人 might simply need a good night's sleep（只需要好好睡一覺），可知 exhausted 的意思是「非常疲累、筋疲力盡」。(A) 興奮、(B) 生氣、(C) 無聊，都不是靠睡一覺就能解決的感受。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the feelings wheel?", "options": ["It has basic emotions in the center and more specific words further out.", "It was designed by scientists at UCLA in 2007.", "It contains only four feelings: sad, angry, scared and happy.", "It can only be used by trained doctors."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段說情緒輪的中心是幾種基本情緒，越往外圈就細分成更具體的詞語。(B) 最早的情緒輪之一是心理治療師威爾柯克斯在 1982 年設計的，2007 年的 UCLA 研究與情緒輪無關；(C) such as 只是舉例，而且外圈還有更多詞語；(D) 文中沒有這樣的限制，俊豪自己也能使用。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a66", "title": "The Woman Who Created Mother's Day, and Then Fought Against It", "title_zh": "創立母親節，後來又反對它的女人", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Culture"], "passage": "Every May, flower shops in Taiwan fill up with carnations, and restaurants are fully booked for family dinners. Mother's Day, on the second Sunday of May, is one of the busiest days of the year for many businesses. But the woman who created the holiday would probably hate what it has become.\n\nHer name was Anna Jarvis, and she grew up in the American state of West Virginia. Her mother, Ann Reeves Jarvis, was a community leader who taught local women how to keep their families healthy. During the American Civil War, she also cared for soldiers from both sides. When she died in 1905, Anna wanted to find a way to honor her, and all mothers who had worked so hard for their families.\n\nOn May 10, 1908, the first Mother's Day service was held at a church in Grafton, West Virginia. Anna sent 500 white carnations, her mother's favorite flower, for the people who came. She then wrote thousands of letters to politicians, newspapers and church leaders. Her hard work paid off. In 1914, President Woodrow Wilson officially made the second Sunday in May Mother's Day across the United States.\n\nHowever, Anna soon became unhappy. Flower shops raised the price of carnations, and companies began selling cards, candy and gifts. To Anna, this missed the whole point. She wanted the day to be about personal thanks, not shopping. She once said that sending a printed card only shows that you are too lazy to write to the woman who has done more for you than anyone else in the world. She even insisted on the spelling \"Mother's Day,\" not \"Mothers' Day,\" because she wanted each family to honor its own mother.\n\nFor the rest of her life, Anna fought against the holiday she had started. She spent most of her money on this fight and was even arrested once for protesting at an event where carnations were sold. She died in 1948, without any children of her own.\n\nAnna's story still has a lesson for us. A gift is nice, but what many mothers want most cannot be bought: your time, a handwritten note, or simply a sincere \"Thank you.\"", "passage_zh": "每年五月，臺灣的花店裡擺滿了康乃馨，餐廳也被家庭聚餐訂滿。母親節在五月的第二個星期日，是許多商家一年中最忙碌的日子之一。但創立這個節日的女士，可能會非常討厭它現在的樣子。\n\n她的名字是安娜‧賈維斯（Anna Jarvis），在美國的西維吉尼亞州長大。她的母親安‧瑞夫斯‧賈維斯是一位社區領袖，曾教導當地婦女如何維護家人的健康。美國南北戰爭期間，她也照顧過交戰雙方的士兵。1905 年母親過世後，安娜想找個方式紀念她，也紀念所有為家庭辛苦付出的母親。\n\n1908 年 5 月 10 日，第一場母親節禮拜在西維吉尼亞州格拉夫頓的一間教堂舉行。安娜送去 500 朵白色康乃馨給到場的人，那是她母親最喜歡的花。之後，她寫了數千封信給政治人物、報社和教會領袖。她的努力有了回報。1914 年，美國總統威爾遜正式將五月的第二個星期日訂為全美國的母親節。\n\n然而，安娜很快就感到不滿。花店調漲了康乃馨的價格，各家公司也開始販售卡片、糖果和禮物。對安娜來說，這完全失去了這個節日的意義。她希望這一天是用來親自表達感謝，而不是用來購物。她曾說，寄一張印好的卡片，只代表你懶得寫信給這個世界上為你付出最多的女人。她甚至堅持這個節日要拼成「Mother's Day」，而不是「Mothers' Day」，因為她希望每個家庭都向自己的母親致敬。\n\n安娜的餘生一直在反對這個由她親手創立的節日。她把大部分的錢都花在這場抗爭上，甚至曾因為在一場販售康乃馨的活動中抗議而被逮捕。她在 1948 年過世，自己沒有任何子女。\n\n安娜的故事至今仍值得我們學習。禮物固然不錯，但許多母親最想要的東西是買不到的：你的時間、一張親手寫的字條，或只是一句真誠的「謝謝」。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How flower shops in Taiwan get ready for May", "Why carnations are popular gifts around the world", "The life of President Woodrow Wilson", "How Mother's Day began and why its creator later turned against it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文介紹安娜‧賈維斯為了紀念母親而推動母親節，後來卻因為節日商業化而終生反對它。(A) 花店只是第一段的引言；(B) 康乃馨只是故事中的細節；(C) 威爾遜總統只在第三段出現一次。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did Anna Jarvis send white carnations to the first Mother's Day service?", "options": ["They were her mother's favorite flower.", "They were the cheapest flowers at that time.", "The President asked her to do so.", "White was the official color of West Virginia."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Anna sent 500 white carnations, her mother's favorite flower... 白色康乃馨是她母親最喜歡的花。(B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 威爾遜總統是在 1914 年才正式訂定母親節，與送花無關。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"paid off\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["cost a lot of money", "was successful", "was stopped", "was forgotten"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 Her hard work paid off. 後面接著說 1914 年總統正式訂定母親節，可見她的努力「有了成果、成功了」。(A) pay 雖然有「付錢」的意思，但這裡不是指花了很多錢；(C)(D) 都與母親節成為全國節日的結果相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did Anna Jarvis insist on spelling the holiday \"Mother's Day\"?", "options": ["She thought it looked more beautiful on cards.", "She wanted the day to honor only her own mother.", "She wanted each family to honor its own mother.", "The President chose this spelling in 1914."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：because she wanted each family to honor its own mother. 她堅持使用單數所有格的 Mother's，是希望每個家庭都向自己的母親致敬。(B) 她想紀念的不只是自己的母親，第二段說她也想紀念所有辛苦的母親；(A)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following gifts would Anna Jarvis most likely approve of?", "options": ["An expensive dinner at a crowded restaurant", "A handwritten letter from a daughter to her mother", "A printed card with only a name signed on it", "A large box of chocolates from a department store"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說安娜希望這一天是親自表達感謝，而不是購物，並批評寄印好的卡片代表懶得親手寫信；最後一段也提到母親最想要的是時間、親手寫的字條或真誠的道謝，因此她最可能贊成手寫的信。(A)(D) 都是她反對的商業化消費；(C) 正是她批評的印刷卡片。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a67", "title": "When Your Best Friend Makes a New Friend", "title_zh": "當你的好朋友交了新朋友", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Life", "Society"], "passage": "Yi-chen and Mei had been best friends since third grade. They took the same bus to school, shared earphones and always ate lunch together. So when a new student named Annie moved from Malaysia and joined their class in October, Yi-chen didn't think much about it. Then Mei invited Annie to eat lunch with them. Soon Mei and Annie both joined the dance club, and Yi-chen, who was too shy to dance, suddenly found herself alone after school.\n\nYi-chen began to have a heavy feeling she did not like. When Mei laughed at Annie's jokes, Yi-chen's stomach hurt. When she saw photos of the two of them at dance practice, she wanted to cry. She started giving Mei short, cold answers. One night, she posted a sad song online with the words \"Some people forget you so easily.\" She knew Mei would see it.\n\nWhat Yi-chen was feeling was jealousy, and it is very common in friendships, especially among teenagers. Psychologists explain that jealousy usually comes from fear, the fear of losing someone important to us. In a way, it shows how much a friendship matters. The problem is not the feeling itself but what we do with it. Giving someone the silent treatment or posting hidden messages online usually pushes friends further away.\n\nMei did see the post, and she felt hurt and confused. Luckily, Yi-chen's older sister gave her some advice: \"Tell Mei how you feel, but talk about yourself, not about what she did wrong.\" So the next day, Yi-chen said, \"I've been feeling left out since you joined the dance club. I'm scared you don't need me anymore.\" Mei was quiet for a moment. Then she hugged her. \"I thought you were mad at me! I never stopped wanting to be your friend.\"\n\nThings are different now. Mei and Yi-chen still ride the bus together every morning, and on Fridays the three girls eat noodles together after dance practice. Annie is teaching Yi-chen some Malay, and Yi-chen is showing Annie the best snack shops in town. \"I thought a new friend would take something away from me,\" Yi-chen says. \"In the end, she added something.\"", "passage_zh": "怡辰和小美從三年級起就是最好的朋友。她們搭同一班公車上學，一起分享耳機，午餐也總是一起吃。所以，當一位名叫安妮的新同學在十月從馬來西亞搬來、轉進她們班上時，怡辰並沒有想太多。後來，小美邀請安妮和她們一起吃午餐。不久之後，小美和安妮都加入了舞蹈社，而太害羞、不敢跳舞的怡辰，突然發現自己放學後變得孤單一人。\n\n怡辰開始有一種她不喜歡的沉重感覺。小美聽到安妮的笑話哈哈大笑時，怡辰的胃就痛了起來。看到她們兩人在舞蹈社練習的照片時，她就很想哭。她開始用簡短、冷淡的話回應小美。有一天晚上，她在網路上貼了一首悲傷的歌，還配上一句話：「有些人真的很容易就把你忘了。」她知道小美一定會看到。\n\n怡辰的這種感受就是嫉妒，它在友誼中非常常見，在青少年之間尤其如此。心理學家解釋，嫉妒通常來自恐懼，也就是害怕失去對我們很重要的人。從某個角度來看，它也顯示出這段友誼有多重要。問題不在於這種感受本身，而在於我們如何處理它。跟對方冷戰，或在網路上發表意有所指的訊息，通常只會把朋友推得更遠。\n\n小美確實看到了那則貼文，她覺得既受傷又困惑。幸好，怡辰的姊姊給了她一個建議：「告訴小美你的感受，但要談你自己，不要談她做錯了什麼。」於是隔天，怡辰對小美說：「自從你加入舞蹈社以後，我一直覺得自己被冷落了。我好怕你再也不需要我了。」小美沉默了一下，然後抱住了她。「我還以為你在生我的氣！我從來沒有不想當你的朋友啊。」\n\n現在情況不一樣了。小美和怡辰每天早上仍然一起搭公車，每到星期五，三個女孩會在舞蹈社練習後一起去吃麵。安妮在教怡辰說一些馬來語，而怡辰則帶安妮去鎮上最好吃的小吃店。「我本來以為新朋友會從我身上奪走什麼，」怡辰說，「結果，她反而為我增添了一些東西。」", "questions": [{"q": "What lesson does the story mainly teach?", "options": ["It is better to have only one close friend.", "Shy students should join a dance club.", "Feeling jealous is normal, and talking honestly about it can help.", "New students usually take friends away from others."], "answer": 2, "explain": "故事描述怡辰因為好朋友交了新朋友而嫉妒，第三段說明嫉妒很常見、通常來自害怕失去重要的人，第四段她坦白說出感受後與小美和好，所以主要的啟示是：嫉妒是正常的，誠實說出來會有幫助。(A) 結局是三個人都成了朋友；(B) 文中沒有這樣的建議；(D) 與結尾 she added something 的意思相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why was Yi-chen suddenly alone after school?", "options": ["Mei and Annie had joined the dance club, and Yi-chen was too shy to dance.", "Yi-chen had to take a different bus home.", "Mei had moved to Malaysia with her family.", "Yi-chen's sister did not allow her to go out."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：Mei and Annie both joined the dance club, and Yi-chen, who was too shy to dance, suddenly found herself alone after school. (B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 從馬來西亞搬來的是安妮，不是小美。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"left out\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very tired", "lucky", "busy", "not included"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段怡辰說，自從小美加入舞蹈社後，她一直 feeling left out，而且害怕小美不再需要她，可知 left out 指「被排除在外、沒有被納入」。(A) 很累、(B) 幸運、(C) 忙碌都不符合她當時的處境。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What advice did Yi-chen's older sister give her?", "options": ["Stop talking to Mei for a few weeks.", "Talk about her own feelings instead of blaming Mei.", "Post more messages online so that Mei would notice.", "Make friends with Annie before talking to Mei."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段姊姊說：Tell Mei how you feel, but talk about yourself, not about what she did wrong. 也就是說出自己的感受，而不是指責小美。(A)(C) 正是第三段說會把朋友推得更遠的做法；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does Yi-chen mean when she says, \"In the end, she added something\"?", "options": ["Annie helped Yi-chen with her math homework.", "Annie joined the dance club after Mei did.", "Annie took Mei away from Yi-chen.", "Annie's friendship made Yi-chen's life richer."], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段怡辰說，她原本以為新朋友會奪走什麼，結果 she added something；配合前面三個女孩一起吃麵、互相教語言和介紹小吃店，可知安妮的友誼讓她的生活更豐富。(A) 文中沒有提到；(B) 與這句話的意思無關；(C) 與她的話意思相反。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a68", "title": "Pastry Chef or Office Job? Talking to My Parents About the Future", "title_zh": "當甜點師傅還是坐辦公室？和爸媽談未來", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Life"], "passage": "Ever since he baked his first pineapple cake in junior high, Po-wei has wanted to be a pastry chef. Now a second-year student in the food and beverage department of a vocational high school, he dreams of opening his own bakery one day. There is just one problem: his parents. \"Baking is a nice hobby,\" his father keeps saying, \"but you need a stable job, with regular hours and a steady income. Why not study business and work in an office?\"\n\nPo-wei's situation is common. Many teenagers and their parents disagree about future careers. Usually, the parents are not trying to control their children's lives. They are worried. Po-wei's parents had heard that chefs work long hours, stand all day and earn little money at first. Their worries were not completely wrong, but they did not have the whole picture.\n\nInstead of arguing, Po-wei decided to prepare, as if he were getting ready for an important presentation. With help from his homeroom teacher, he collected information about baking programs at universities of science and technology, the pay of experienced pastry chefs, and the skills certificates he could earn. He also spent his summer vacation working part-time at a local bakery, getting up at five every morning. The job was tiring, but it proved to himself, and to his parents, that he was serious.\n\nWhen the family finally sat down to talk, Po-wei did something important: he listened first. He asked his parents exactly what they were afraid of and wrote down their answers. Then he answered each worry one by one. Because he understood their concern about money, he even offered to take some business classes so that he could run his own bakery well someday.\n\nHis parents did not change their minds overnight. But a week later, his mother came home with a bag of flour and asked him to teach her how to make pineapple cakes. Now his father proudly shares Po-wei's cookies with his coworkers. \"I thought I had to win the argument,\" Po-wei says. \"Actually, I just had to help them stop worrying.\"", "passage_zh": "自從國中烤出第一顆鳳梨酥後，柏維就一直想當甜點師傅。現在他是一所高職餐飲管理科的二年級學生，夢想有一天能開一家屬於自己的烘焙坊。只有一個問題：他的爸媽。「烘焙當興趣很不錯，」爸爸總是這麼說，「但你需要一份安穩的工作，有固定的上班時間和穩定的收入。為什麼不去念商科，以後在辦公室上班呢？」\n\n柏維的處境很常見。許多青少年和爸媽對未來的職業看法不同。通常，父母並不是想控制孩子的人生，而是感到擔心。柏維的爸媽聽說廚師工時很長、要整天站著，而且剛開始賺的錢很少。他們的擔心並不完全是錯的，只是他們沒有看到全貌。\n\n柏維沒有跟爸媽爭吵，而是決定好好準備，就像要準備一場重要的簡報一樣。在班導的協助下，他蒐集了科技大學的烘焙相關科系、資深甜點師傅的薪水，以及他可以考取的技術證照等資料。他還利用暑假到當地一家麵包店打工，每天早上五點就起床。這份工作很累，但也向他自己和爸媽證明了他是認真的。\n\n全家人終於坐下來好好談的時候，柏維做了一件很重要的事：他先傾聽。他問爸媽到底在擔心什麼，並把他們的回答寫下來。接著，他一一回應每一項擔憂。因為他理解爸媽對收入的顧慮，他甚至主動提出要修一些商業課程，以便將來能把自己的烘焙坊經營好。\n\n他的爸媽並沒有在一夜之間就改變想法。但一個星期後，媽媽帶了一袋麵粉回家，請他教她做鳳梨酥。現在，爸爸會驕傲地把柏維做的餅乾分給同事吃。「我原本以為我必須吵贏，」柏維說，「其實，我只需要讓他們不再擔心。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why pastry chefs earn more money than office workers", "How a student dealt with a disagreement with his parents about his future", "How to make pineapple cakes at home", "Why vocational high schools should offer business classes"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文描述柏維想當甜點師傅，但爸媽希望他找安穩的工作；他透過蒐集資料、打工證明決心、先傾聽再回應，化解了與爸媽的意見分歧。(A) 文中沒有比較甜點師傅和上班族的收入；(C) 鳳梨酥只是故事細節；(D) 修商業課程只是柏維提出的做法之一。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what were Po-wei's parents worried about?", "options": ["Opening a bakery in Taiwan costs too much money.", "Po-wei was not good enough at baking.", "Po-wei's grades would drop because of his hobby.", "Chefs work long hours, stand all day and earn little at first."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Po-wei's parents had heard that chefs work long hours, stand all day and earn little money at first. (A)(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"stable\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["exciting", "creative", "steady and safe", "difficult"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段爸爸說 a stable job, with regular hours and a steady income（上班時間固定、收入穩定），後面還建議他到辦公室上班，可知 stable 指「安穩、穩定的」。(A) 刺激的、(B) 有創意的、(D) 困難的都不符合文意。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Po-wei work at a bakery during his summer vacation?", "options": ["To show that he was serious about becoming a pastry chef", "To earn money to buy his own oven", "Because his homeroom teacher made him do it", "Because his parents wanted him to give up baking"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：He also spent his summer vacation working part-time at a local bakery... it proved to himself, and to his parents, that he was serious. 打工是為了證明自己是認真的。(B) 文中沒有提到買烤箱；(C) 班導只協助他蒐集資料；(D) 與文意不符。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from what Po-wei's mother did a week later?", "options": ["She wanted to open a bakery herself.", "She was starting to accept and support his dream.", "She thought his pineapple cakes were not good.", "She wanted him to quit his part-time job."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說爸媽沒有一夜之間就改變想法，但一週後媽媽帶麵粉回家，請他教她做鳳梨酥，接著爸爸也驕傲地把他做的餅乾分給同事，可推知媽媽開始接受並支持他的夢想。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 她是想學做鳳梨酥，並不是覺得不好吃。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a69", "title": "The Thank-You Letter I Almost Didn't Deliver", "title_zh": "差點沒送出的感謝信", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Life"], "passage": "Last month, our health teacher, Mr. Huang, gave us an unusual assignment. \"Think of someone who was very kind to you but whom you never properly thanked,\" he said. \"Write that person a letter. Then, if you can, deliver it in person and read it aloud.\" Some of my classmates groaned. Reading a letter aloud to someone's face sounded terribly embarrassing.\n\nMr. Huang explained that the idea came from Martin Seligman, an American psychologist known as one of the founders of positive psychology, the study of what makes life go well. In a 2005 study, his team asked people to try several different exercises, including this \"gratitude visit.\" The gratitude visit produced the biggest boost in happiness, and the effect lasted for about a month.\n\nI knew right away who I should write to: Auntie Lin, who runs a breakfast shop near my old home. When I was in seventh grade, my mother was in the hospital for three months. My father left for work early and came home late, so I often went to school hungry. Auntie Lin noticed. Every morning, she handed me an egg pancake and a cup of warm soy milk, and she never let me pay. \"Pay me back by studying hard,\" she said.\n\nWriting the letter was easy. Delivering it was not. I almost turned back twice on the bus. When I finally arrived, the shop was busy, and Auntie Lin didn't recognize me at first because I had grown so much. My hands shook as I opened the letter, and by the second paragraph, my voice was shaking too. When I looked up, Auntie Lin was wiping her eyes with her apron.\n\n\"I didn't think you would remember,\" she said quietly. Many children had come through her shop over the years, she told me, but very few ever came back. Before I left, she insisted on making me an egg pancake, and once again, she would not take my money.\n\nMr. Huang was right about the science, but I learned something he hadn't mentioned. Later, I read that people who write thank-you letters usually think the receivers will feel less happy than they really do. I believe it. Auntie Lin may have been even happier than I was.", "passage_zh": "上個月，我們的健康教育老師黃老師出了一份不尋常的作業。「想一個曾經對你非常好，但你從來沒有好好道謝的人，」他說，「寫一封信給那個人。然後，如果可以的話，親自把信送去，並且唸給對方聽。」有些同學發出了哀號。當著別人的面唸信，聽起來實在太難為情了。\n\n黃老師解釋，這個點子來自美國心理學家馬汀‧塞利格曼（Martin Seligman），他被認為是正向心理學的創始人之一；正向心理學研究的是什麼能讓人生過得美好。在 2005 年的一項研究中，他的團隊請受試者嘗試好幾種不同的練習，其中就包括這個「感恩拜訪」。結果，感恩拜訪帶來的幸福感提升最大，而且效果持續了大約一個月。\n\n我馬上就知道要寫給誰：林阿姨，她在我以前住的地方附近開了一家早餐店。我國一的時候，媽媽住院了三個月。爸爸一大早就出門上班，很晚才回家，所以我常常餓著肚子上學。林阿姨注意到了。每天早上，她都會遞給我一份蛋餅和一杯溫豆漿，而且從來不讓我付錢。「好好念書，就是還我了，」她說。\n\n寫信很容易，送信就沒那麼簡單了。在公車上，我有兩次差點就掉頭回家。終於到了的時候，店裡正忙，林阿姨一開始沒認出我，因為我長高了好多。我打開信紙時雙手發抖，唸到第二段時，連聲音也在發抖。當我抬起頭，林阿姨正用圍裙擦著眼淚。\n\n「我沒想到你還記得，」她輕聲說。她告訴我，這些年來有很多孩子在她店裡吃過早餐，但很少有人會再回來。我離開前，她堅持要幫我做一份蛋餅，而且又一次不肯收我的錢。\n\n黃老師說的科學沒有錯，但我還學到了一件他沒提到的事。後來我讀到，寫感謝信的人通常會以為收信的人沒那麼開心，但實際上對方開心得多。我相信這是真的。林阿姨也許比我還要開心。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The writer's experience of thanking someone with a letter, and what the writer learned", "How to run a successful breakfast shop", "Why students should not be given difficult homework", "The life story of the psychologist Martin Seligman"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文描述作者因為一份作業而寫感謝信給林阿姨、親自唸給她聽的經過，以及作者從中學到的事。(B) 早餐店只是故事的背景；(C) 作者並沒有反對這份作業；(D) 塞利格曼只在第二段被提到，用來說明作業的由來。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, which of the following is true about the \"gratitude visit\" in Seligman's study?", "options": ["It was the only exercise the researchers tested.", "It made people happier for about a year.", "It gave people the biggest boost in happiness of all the exercises.", "It worked only when people wrote very long letters."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：The gratitude visit produced the biggest boost in happiness, and the effect lasted for about a month. (A) 文中說研究請受試者嘗試好幾種練習；(B) 效果只持續大約一個月，不是一年；(D) 文中沒有提到信的長度。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Auntie Lin give the writer free breakfast?", "options": ["The writer helped out in her shop every morning.", "The writer's father had asked her to.", "The writer had won a prize at school.", "She noticed that the writer often went to school hungry."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說作者國一時媽媽住院，爸爸早出晚歸，所以 I often went to school hungry. Auntie Lin noticed. 林阿姨注意到作者常餓著肚子上學，才每天送早餐給作者。(A)(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"insisted on\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["refused to think about", "firmly wanted to do, even if the other person said no", "forgot to do", "was afraid of"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段 she insisted on making me an egg pancake, and once again, she would not take my money. 林阿姨堅持要做蛋餅給作者，而且又不肯收錢，表示她非常堅定地要這麼做。(A) 拒絕考慮、(C) 忘記做、(D) 害怕，都不符合文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why was Auntie Lin wiping her eyes with her apron?", "options": ["The kitchen was full of smoke.", "She was angry that the writer had come at a busy time.", "She was deeply moved by the writer's letter.", "She was sad that the writer had moved away."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段作者唸信唸到聲音發抖，抬頭時看到林阿姨用圍裙擦眼淚；第五段她輕聲說「我沒想到你還記得」，可知她是被這封信深深感動而落淚。(A) 文中沒有提到煙；(B) 她並沒有生氣；(D) 文中沒有說她因作者搬走而難過。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a70", "title": "The Seven-Minute Miracle", "title_zh": "七分鐘的奇蹟", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Culture"], "passage": "When a Shinkansen, or bullet train, pulls into Tokyo Station, a line of workers in neat uniforms is already waiting on the platform. As the train stops, they bow together. The passengers step off, and the workers hurry inside. Seven minutes later, the train is spotless and ready for its next trip. Travelers from around the world film this scene, and the media have given it a nickname: the \"seven-minute miracle.\"\n\nThe schedule is extremely tight. Many Shinkansen trains stay at Tokyo Station for only about twelve minutes before leaving again. About two minutes are needed for passengers to get off, and about three for new passengers to get on. That leaves just seven minutes for cleaning. To make this possible, each worker is usually responsible for one car with around 100 seats.\n\nIn that short time, the cleaners do a surprising amount of work. They pick up trash, wipe every table, sweep the floor and check under the seats for lost items such as phones and wallets. They also clean the toilets and change the cloth covers on the headrests. Finally, they turn each row of seats around so that all passengers will face the direction the train is going. When they finish, they step out, line up and bow again before the new passengers board.\n\nThe job was not always so admired. In the past, many cleaners felt that passengers hardly noticed them, and some were even embarrassed to tell people what they did. In the mid-2000s, a new manager tried to change this. He told the workers that they were not just cleaners but part of the travel experience, just like the train crew. Workers were encouraged to share ideas, greet passengers and help travelers who were lost. Slowly, the team began to take pride in its work.\n\nToday, the team is famous far beyond Japan. Business students at Harvard University in the United States have even studied it as an example of excellent teamwork. The lesson is simple: when people feel respected, even an ordinary job can be done in an extraordinary way.", "passage_zh": "當一列新幹線（子彈列車）駛進東京車站時，一排穿著整齊制服的工作人員早已在月台上等候。列車一停下，他們便一起鞠躬。乘客下車後，工作人員立刻快步進入車廂。七分鐘後，列車已經一塵不染，準備好展開下一趟行程。來自世界各地的旅客會拍下這一幕，媒體也替它取了個外號：「七分鐘的奇蹟」。\n\n時間安排極為緊湊。許多新幹線列車在東京車站只停留大約十二分鐘，就會再度出發。乘客下車大約需要兩分鐘，新乘客上車大約需要三分鐘，清潔工作就只剩下七分鐘。為了做到這一點，每位工作人員通常負責一節約有一百個座位的車廂。\n\n在這麼短的時間內，清潔人員完成的工作多得驚人。他們撿拾垃圾、擦拭每一張桌子、掃地，並檢查座位底下是否有手機、錢包等遺失物品。他們也會清潔廁所，並更換頭枕上的布套。最後，他們把每一排座位轉向，讓所有乘客都能面向列車行進的方向。完成後，他們走出車廂、排好隊伍，在新乘客上車前再次鞠躬。\n\n這份工作並非一直都受人欽佩。過去，許多清潔人員覺得乘客幾乎不會注意到他們，有些人甚至不好意思告訴別人自己從事什麼工作。2000 年代中期，一位新來的主管試著改變這種情況。他告訴員工，他們不只是清潔人員，而是旅行體驗的一部分，就像列車上的乘務人員一樣。公司鼓勵員工分享想法、向乘客打招呼，並幫助迷路的旅客。漸漸地，這個團隊開始以自己的工作為榮。\n\n如今，這個團隊的名聲遠遠傳到日本以外。美國哈佛大學的商學院學生甚至把它當作優秀團隊合作的範例來研究。其中的道理很簡單：當人們感到受尊重時，即使是平凡的工作，也能做得不平凡。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why Japan's bullet trains always leave and arrive exactly on time", "How a team of cleaners does a fast, careful job and takes pride in it", "How passengers should behave when they ride the Shinkansen", "Why Tokyo Station has become the busiest train station in Japan"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文描述東京車站新幹線清潔人員如何在七分鐘內完成清潔（第二、三段），以及他們如何開始以自己的工作為榮（第四、五段）。(A) 文中沒有談到列車是否準時；(C) 文中沒有教乘客如何搭車；(D) 文中未提及東京車站是否最繁忙，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why do the workers have only seven minutes to clean a train?", "options": ["They must also clean several other trains on the same platform.", "Japanese law says that cleaning must not take longer than seven minutes.", "Part of the train's short stop is needed for passengers to get off and on.", "Each worker has to clean all the cars of a train alone."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：列車在東京車站只停留約十二分鐘，乘客下車約需兩分鐘、新乘客上車約需三分鐘，That leaves just seven minutes for cleaning。(A)(B) 文中皆未提及；(D) 與第二段「每位工作人員通常負責一節車廂」不符，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as part of the cleaners' work?", "options": ["Turning the rows of seats to face the direction of travel", "Looking for things that passengers have left behind", "Changing the covers on the headrests", "Selling drinks and snacks to new passengers"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段列出清潔人員的工作：撿垃圾、擦桌子、掃地、檢查遺失物品 (B)、清潔廁所、更換頭枕布套 (C)，以及把座位轉向列車行進方向 (A)。文中沒有提到販賣飲料和點心，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"spotless\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very crowded", "completely clean", "perfectly on time", "totally empty"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說清潔人員進入車廂七分鐘後，the train is spotless and ready for its next trip，前後文都在談清潔工作，可知 spotless 指「一塵不染、非常乾淨」。(A) 擁擠、(C) 準時、(D) 空無一人都不符合文意，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraphs 4 and 5?", "options": ["The workers did a better job after they began to feel respected and proud.", "The new manager had once been a student at Harvard University.", "Passengers are now asked to clean their own seats before getting off.", "The cleaners became famous because they were paid much more than before."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說新主管讓員工覺得自己是旅行體驗的一部分，團隊因此 began to take pride in its work；第五段的結論是 when people feel respected, even an ordinary job can be done in an extraordinary way，可推論員工感到受尊重後，工作表現更好。(B) 文中只說哈佛學生研究這個團隊；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a71", "title": "Riding to Work in the Sky: The Cable Cars of La Paz", "title_zh": "搭纜車上班：拉巴斯的空中交通", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Technology"], "passage": "Imagine riding to work in a small glass cabin that hangs from a cable high above a busy city. For thousands of people in La Paz, Bolivia, this is a normal part of the day. La Paz and its neighbor, El Alto, share the world's largest cable car network used as public transportation.\n\nThe reason is geography. La Paz lies in a deep, bowl-shaped valley about 3,600 meters above sea level. On the flat land above it sits El Alto, whose name means \"the high one\" in Spanish. Many people live in El Alto but work or study in La Paz. For years, most people traveled between the two cities by bus or minibus on steep, narrow roads. Traffic jams were common, and the trip could easily take an hour.\n\nIn 2014, the first cable car line opened. Today there are ten lines, each named after a color, such as Red, Yellow and Green. Each cabin carries up to ten passengers, and a new one arrives every few seconds, so people rarely have to wait long. Some trips that once took an hour now take only ten to fifteen minutes.\n\nRosa Mamani, a nurse who lives in El Alto, rides the cable car to her hospital every morning. \"I used to leave home before six, and I was always tired,\" she says. \"Now I can sleep longer, and I enjoy the view of the mountains on the way.\" Because the cable cars run on electricity, they are also quieter and cleaner than the old buses, which filled the streets with smoke.\n\nOf course, the system is not perfect. It cost a lot of money to build, and some people say the government should have spent more on improving buses and roads instead. A cable car also cannot carry as many people as a subway train. Still, in a city where it is very difficult to build tunnels or wide roads, traveling through the sky has turned out to be a smart solution.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，搭乘一個懸掛在纜繩上、高高掠過繁忙城市上空的小玻璃車廂去上班。對玻利維亞拉巴斯的成千上萬人來說，這是每天再平常不過的事。拉巴斯和鄰近的埃爾阿爾托共同擁有全世界最大、作為大眾運輸使用的纜車路網。\n\n原因在於地理環境。拉巴斯位於一個深深的碗狀山谷中，海拔約三千六百公尺。在它上方的平地上是埃爾阿爾托，這個名字在西班牙文中的意思是「高處的那一個」。許多人住在埃爾阿爾托，卻在拉巴斯工作或上學。多年來，大多數人都是在陡峭狹窄的道路上搭公車或小巴往返兩座城市。塞車是家常便飯，一趟路很容易就要花上一個小時。\n\n2014 年，第一條纜車路線通車。如今共有十條路線，每一條都以顏色命名，例如紅線、黃線和綠線。每個車廂最多可載十名乘客，而且每隔幾秒就有一個新車廂到站，所以人們很少需要等很久。有些過去要花一小時的路程，現在只要十到十五分鐘。\n\n住在埃爾阿爾托的護理師羅莎．馬馬尼每天早上搭纜車到醫院上班。「我以前六點前就得出門，總是覺得很累，」她說，「現在我可以多睡一點，還能在路上欣賞山景。」由於纜車靠電力運行，它們也比那些讓街道充滿廢氣的老舊公車更安靜、更乾淨。\n\n當然，這套系統並不完美。它的建造費用很高，有些人認為政府當初應該把更多錢花在改善公車和道路上。纜車的載客量也比不上一列捷運列車。儘管如此，在一個很難開挖隧道或興建寬闊道路的城市裡，從空中通行後來證明是個聰明的解決辦法。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why tourists should ride cable cars to see the mountains of Bolivia", "How a nurse in El Alto found a better job at a hospital in La Paz", "How cable cars became an important way to travel in La Paz and El Alto", "Why the old buses in La Paz caused serious air pollution and noise"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段點出拉巴斯擁有全世界最大的大眾運輸纜車路網，第二至五段說明興建的原因、運作方式、對居民的幫助與缺點，故主旨是纜車成為當地重要的交通方式。(A) 文中沒有推薦觀光；(B) 羅莎只是第四段的例子，而且文中沒有提到她換工作；(D) 老舊公車的廢氣只是第四段的細節，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why was traveling between El Alto and La Paz difficult in the past?", "options": ["The roads were steep and narrow, and traffic was often heavy.", "There were no buses or minibuses between the two cities.", "People needed a special pass to enter La Paz.", "The weather in the valley was usually too cold for travel."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：most people traveled between the two cities by bus or minibus on steep, narrow roads. Traffic jams were common，道路陡峭狹窄，而且經常塞車。(B) 與文中「大多數人搭公車或小巴往返」相反；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"rarely\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["often", "always", "almost never", "sometimes"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說每隔幾秒就有新車廂到站，so people rarely have to wait long，由前後的因果關係可知人們「幾乎不必」等很久，rarely 意為「很少、幾乎不」。(A) 經常、(B) 總是、(D) 有時都與「車廂很快就來」的文意不符，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Rosa Mamani?", "options": ["She has stopped working at the hospital.", "She now leaves home later in the morning than she did before.", "She moved from El Alto to La Paz to be closer to work.", "She prefers taking the bus because it is cheaper."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段羅莎說 I used to leave home before six... Now I can sleep longer，可推論她現在早上出門的時間比以前晚。(A) 她仍每天搭纜車到醫院上班；(C) 文中說她住在埃爾阿爾托；(D) 文中沒有說她偏好搭公車，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a weakness of the cable car system?", "options": ["It was very expensive to build.", "It cannot carry as many people as a subway.", "Some people think the money should have been spent on buses and roads.", "It often has to stop running in bad weather."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段提到的缺點有：建造費用很高 (A)、有人認為錢應該用來改善公車和道路 (C)、載客量比不上捷運列車 (B)。文中沒有提到纜車常因天氣不好而停駛，故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a72", "title": "A Life-Saving Gift: The Story of the Seat Belt", "title_zh": "救命的禮物：安全帶的故事", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Technology"], "passage": "Every time you get into a car, you probably pull a belt across your body and click it into place without thinking. This simple action takes about two seconds, yet it may be the most important thing you do during the whole trip. The kind of seat belt most of us use today was invented in Sweden more than sixty years ago, and its story is about more than clever engineering.\n\nMost early cars had no belts at all. Later, some cars had lap belts, which went across the hips only. In a serious crash, these belts could not stop the upper body from flying forward, so a person's head might still hit the steering wheel or the window. The belt itself could also cause injuries to the stomach. Besides, many drivers found belts uncomfortable and simply did not wear them.\n\nIn 1958, the Swedish car company Volvo hired an engineer named Nils Bohlin. He had previously worked on ejection seats, which shoot pilots out of fighter planes in an emergency, so he understood how the human body moves at high speed. Within a year, he came up with a new design. One strap crossed the hips, and another went across the chest, forming a V shape. Both were joined at a single buckle beside the seat, so the belt could be put on with one hand. In 1959, the first Volvo cars with this \"three-point\" belt went on sale in Sweden.\n\nThen Volvo made an unusual decision. The company held a patent on the design, which meant it could have stopped other companies from copying it or made them pay to use it. Instead, Volvo allowed every car maker in the world to use the idea for free. Its leaders believed that saving lives was more important than making money from the invention.\n\nThat decision changed road safety around the world. Today, three-point belts can be found in almost every car, and Volvo estimates that they have saved more than a million lives. So the next time you buckle up, even in the back seat, remember that the belt across your chest was once a gift.", "passage_zh": "每次坐進車裡，你大概都會不假思索地把安全帶拉過身體、扣好。這個簡單的動作只要大約兩秒鐘，卻可能是你整趟路程中最重要的一件事。我們今天大多使用的這種安全帶，是六十多年前在瑞典發明的，而它的故事不只關於巧妙的工程設計。\n\n大多數早期的汽車根本沒有安全帶。後來，有些汽車裝了只橫過臀部的腰帶式安全帶。發生嚴重車禍時，這種安全帶無法阻止上半身向前衝，所以人的頭部仍可能撞上方向盤或車窗。安全帶本身也可能造成腹部受傷。此外，許多駕駛覺得安全帶不舒服，乾脆就不繫。\n\n1958 年，瑞典汽車公司富豪（Volvo）聘請了一位名叫尼爾斯．博林的工程師。他先前曾參與研發彈射座椅，這種座椅能在緊急時刻把飛行員從戰鬥機中彈射出去，因此他很了解人體在高速下會如何移動。不到一年，他就想出了新的設計：一條帶子橫過臀部，另一條斜過胸前，形成一個 V 字形。兩條帶子在座位旁用同一個扣環扣住，因此只用一隻手就能繫上。1959 年，第一批配備這種「三點式」安全帶的富豪汽車在瑞典上市。\n\n接著，富豪做了一個不尋常的決定。這家公司擁有這項設計的專利，也就是說，它原本可以阻止其他公司仿造，或要求對方付費才能使用。然而，富豪卻讓全世界每一家汽車製造商免費使用這個設計。公司的領導者認為，拯救生命比靠這項發明賺錢更重要。\n\n這個決定改變了全世界的行車安全。如今，幾乎每一輛汽車都裝有三點式安全帶，而富豪估計，它們已經拯救了超過一百萬條生命。所以，下次你扣上安全帶時──即使是坐在後座──請記得，橫過你胸前的這條帶子，曾經是一份禮物。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history of car racing and fast sports cars in Sweden", "How the three-point seat belt was invented and given to the world", "Why fighter pilots need ejection seats in an emergency", "How to choose the safest car and seat belts for your family"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從早期安全帶的問題（第二段），談到博林發明三點式安全帶（第三段），以及富豪免費公開這項設計（第四、五段）。(A) 文中未提到賽車；(C) 彈射座椅只是說明博林背景的細節；(D) 文中沒有教人如何選車，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what was one problem with lap belts?", "options": ["They often broke apart when a car hit something at high speed.", "They were too expensive for most drivers to add to their cars.", "They could not keep the upper body from moving forward in a crash.", "They were made only for the front seats of Swedish cars."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：In a serious crash, these belts could not stop the upper body from flying forward，腰帶式安全帶無法阻止上半身向前衝。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention that Nils Bohlin had worked on ejection seats?", "options": ["To show that he had useful knowledge for designing a seat belt", "To explain why he later left Volvo to work for an airplane maker", "To suggest that cars should also have ejection seats", "To show that he had once been a famous fighter pilot himself"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說他做過彈射座椅，so he understood how the human body moves at high speed，作者提到這點是為了說明他具備設計安全帶所需的知識。(B) 文中沒有說他離開富豪；(C) 文中沒有建議汽車裝彈射座椅；(D) 他是工程師，不是飛行員，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"patent\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a safety test that every new car must pass before it is sold", "a large amount of money paid to an engineer for a new idea", "a special type of buckle that holds two straps together", "an official right to be the only one who can make or use an invention"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段解釋擁有 patent 代表公司 could have stopped other companies from copying it or made them pay to use it，可知 patent 是「專利」，也就是只有自己能製造或使用某項發明的正式權利。(A) 安全測試、(B) 一大筆錢、(C) 扣環都不符合句意，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward Volvo's decision in paragraph 4?", "options": ["Doubtful", "Angry", "Admiring", "Uninterested"], "answer": 2, "explain": "作者在第四段說富豪認為拯救生命比賺錢更重要，第五段又說這個決定 changed road safety around the world，並把安全帶形容為 a gift，可見作者對這個決定抱持讚賞的態度。(A) 懷疑、(B) 生氣、(D) 不感興趣都不符合，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a73", "title": "Why Flying East Feels Worse: Understanding Jet Lag", "title_zh": "為什麼往東飛比較難受？認識時差", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Health"], "passage": "Last summer, Yu-ting flew from Taipei to Paris for a two-week art camp. France is six hours behind Taiwan in summer, so she expected to feel terrible when she arrived. To her surprise, she felt fine. She was a little sleepy on the first evening, but she went to bed at ten, woke up early the next morning and was ready to explore the city.\n\nThe trip home was a different story. After landing in Taipei, Yu-ting lay awake in bed until three in the morning. In her morning classes, she could hardly keep her eyes open, and she had no appetite at lunch. It took her almost a week to feel normal again. Why was the second half of her trip so much harder?\n\nThe answer lies in the body's internal clock, a group of cells in the brain that controls when we feel sleepy, hungry or alert. This clock normally follows a cycle of about 24 hours. When we fly across several time zones quickly, the clock cannot change at once. It keeps telling the body to sleep or wake up according to the time back home. This problem is called jet lag. In general, the body clock can only move about one hour a day.\n\nDirection matters, too. For most people, the natural body clock runs a little longer than 24 hours, so it is easier to stay up later than to go to sleep earlier. Flying west, as Yu-ting did on her way to Paris, makes the day longer, which suits the body. Flying east makes the day shorter. When she got home, her body still thought that 11 p.m. in Taipei was only 5 p.m., so falling asleep was nearly impossible.\n\nThere are ways to make jet lag easier. Light is the strongest signal for the body clock, so after flying east, get plenty of bright morning sunlight and avoid screens late at night. Try to eat and sleep at local times, and keep any naps under thirty minutes. These steps cannot remove jet lag completely, but they can help your body catch up with the clock on the wall.", "passage_zh": "去年夏天，郁婷從臺北飛到巴黎，參加為期兩週的藝術營。夏天時法國比臺灣慢六個小時，所以她以為抵達後會很不舒服。出乎她意料的是，她覺得精神還不錯。第一天晚上她有點睏，但她十點上床睡覺，隔天一早醒來，就準備好去探索這座城市了。\n\n回家的旅程卻是另一回事。回到臺北後，郁婷躺在床上，直到凌晨三點都睡不著。早上上課時，她幾乎睜不開眼睛，午餐時也沒有胃口。她花了將近一個星期才恢復正常。為什麼旅程的後半段會辛苦這麼多呢？\n\n答案就在於人體的生理時鐘，也就是大腦中一群負責控制我們何時感到想睡、飢餓或清醒的細胞。這個時鐘通常遵循大約二十四小時的週期。當我們在短時間內飛越好幾個時區時，生理時鐘無法立刻調整，仍然依照家鄉的時間告訴身體該睡覺或該醒來。這個問題就叫做時差。一般來說，生理時鐘一天只能調整大約一個小時。\n\n飛行的方向也有影響。對大多數人來說，天生的生理時鐘週期比二十四小時稍長一些，所以晚一點睡比早一點睡容易。像郁婷去巴黎時那樣往西飛，會讓一天變長，比較適合身體；往東飛則會讓一天變短。她回到家時，身體仍然以為臺北的晚上十一點只是下午五點，所以幾乎不可能睡得著。\n\n有一些方法可以減輕時差的影響。光線是影響生理時鐘最強烈的訊號，所以往東飛之後，早上要多曬明亮的陽光，晚上則要避免看螢幕。盡量依照當地時間吃飯和睡覺，小睡也不要超過三十分鐘。這些方法無法完全消除時差，但能幫助你的身體追上牆上的時鐘。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To describe the art camp that Yu-ting joined in Paris", "To suggest that students should not travel abroad during the school year", "To compare the weather in Taiwan and France", "To explain why jet lag is often worse after flying east and how to deal with it"], "answer": 3, "explain": "文章先以郁婷去程輕鬆、回程辛苦的經驗開頭（第一、二段），再說明生理時鐘與飛行方向的關係（第三、四段），最後提供減輕時差的方法（第五段）。(A) 藝術營只是故事背景；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What happened to Yu-ting after she returned to Taipei?", "options": ["She fell asleep early every evening and woke up before sunrise.", "She caught a cold on the plane and missed a week of school.", "She could not sleep until late and felt very sleepy in her morning classes.", "She felt fine and was full of energy at school the next morning."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Yu-ting lay awake in bed until three in the morning. In her morning classes, she could hardly keep her eyes open。(A)(D) 與文意相反；(B) 文中沒有提到感冒或請假，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Yu-ting feel fine when she arrived in Paris?", "options": ["Flying west made her day longer, which is easier for the body clock.", "She took a long nap as soon as she arrived at her hotel.", "Paris and Taipei are in the same time zone during the summer.", "She had slept through the whole flight from Taipei to Paris."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Flying west, as Yu-ting did on her way to Paris, makes the day longer, which suits the body。往西飛讓一天變長，比較符合生理時鐘，所以她到巴黎時覺得還好。(B)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 第一段說夏天時法國比臺灣慢六小時，兩地並不在同一時區，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"internal\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very accurate", "electronic", "from another country", "inside the body"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說 internal clock 是 a group of cells in the brain，也就是存在於體內的時鐘，internal 意為「內部的」。(A) 很準確、(B) 電子的、(C) 來自外國的都不符合文意，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, which is the best advice for someone who has just flown east?", "options": ["Take a long nap in the afternoon to make up for lost sleep.", "Use your phone in bed until you feel sleepy.", "Go outside and get bright sunlight in the morning.", "Eat meals at the same times as in your home country."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：after flying east, get plenty of bright morning sunlight。(A) 文中建議小睡不要超過三十分鐘；(B) 文中建議晚上避免看螢幕；(D) 文中建議依照當地時間吃飯，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a74", "title": "The Mother Road Turns 100", "title_zh": "「母親之路」一百歲了", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Travel"], "passage": "On November 11, 1926, a new highway in the United States was given a number that would one day be famous around the world: 66. Route 66 ran about 3,900 kilometers from Chicago, Illinois, to Santa Monica, California, a seaside city next to Los Angeles on the Pacific coast. Along the way, it crossed eight states and passed through farmland, deserts and mountains. In November 2026, the road turns 100 years old.\n\nIn the 1930s, Route 66 became a road of hope. Years of dry weather and huge dust storms ruined farms in states such as Oklahoma, and thousands of families packed everything they owned into old cars and drove west to look for work in California. The writer John Steinbeck described their difficult journey in his 1939 novel The Grapes of Wrath, where he called Route 66 \"the mother road.\" The name stuck.\n\nAfter World War II, the road became a symbol of fun and freedom. More American families owned cars and had time for vacations. Small businesses appeared along the highway to serve them: gas stations, diners and motels with bright neon signs. Some motels even let guests sleep in rooms shaped like tepees, the cone-shaped tents of some Native American peoples. A popular 1946 song invited travelers to \"get your kicks on Route 66.\"\n\nBut the good times did not last. In 1956, the government began building a network of wide, fast interstate highways. One by one, parts of Route 66 were bypassed, as drivers chose the new roads that went around the small towns. When the last section was replaced in 1984, many towns along the old road lost their customers. The following year, Route 66 was officially removed from the national highway system.\n\nYet the mother road refused to disappear. Local people worked to save old buildings and signs, and \"Historic Route 66\" signs now guide drivers along much of the original path. Visitors from Europe, Asia and beyond come to drive it, and the 2006 animated movie Cars, whose sleepy desert town was inspired by Route 66, introduced the road to a new generation. Today, people don't drive Route 66 to get somewhere quickly. For them, the journey itself is the destination.", "passage_zh": "1926 年 11 月 11 日，美國一條新公路得到了一個日後聞名世界的編號：66。66 號公路從伊利諾州的芝加哥延伸到加州的聖塔莫尼卡──緊鄰洛杉磯、位於太平洋岸邊的一座海濱城市，全長約三千九百公里。沿途它穿越八個州，經過農田、沙漠和山脈。2026 年 11 月，這條公路就滿一百歲了。\n\n1930 年代，66 號公路成了一條希望之路。連年乾旱和巨大的沙塵暴摧毀了奧克拉荷馬州等地的農田，成千上萬的家庭把所有家當塞進老舊的汽車裡，一路向西到加州找工作。作家史坦貝克在 1939 年的小說《憤怒的葡萄》中描寫了他們艱辛的旅程，並把 66 號公路稱為「母親之路」。這個名稱從此流傳下來。\n\n第二次世界大戰後，這條公路成為歡樂與自由的象徵。越來越多美國家庭擁有汽車，也有時間去度假。公路沿線出現許多為他們服務的小商家：加油站、小餐館，以及掛著明亮霓虹燈招牌的汽車旅館。有些汽車旅館甚至讓客人住在圓錐帳篷形狀的房間裡，那是某些美洲原住民傳統的帳篷樣式。1946 年一首流行歌曲還邀請旅人「到 66 號公路上找樂子」。\n\n但好景不常。1956 年，政府開始興建由寬敞、快速的州際公路組成的路網。66 號公路一段接一段地被繞過，因為駕駛人選擇了繞開小鎮的新道路。1984 年最後一段被取代後，舊公路沿線的許多小鎮失去了顧客。隔年，66 號公路正式從國家公路系統中除名。\n\n然而，這條母親之路並沒有就此消失。當地居民努力保存老建築和招牌，如今「歷史 66 號公路」的路標引導駕駛人走過原始路線的大部分路段。來自歐洲、亞洲和其他地方的遊客專程來開這條路，而 2006 年的動畫電影《汽車總動員》中那座寧靜的沙漠小鎮，正是以 66 號公路為靈感，讓新一代認識了這條公路。如今，人們開上 66 號公路不是為了快速抵達某個地方。對他們來說，旅程本身就是目的地。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to plan a road trip across the United States on Route 66", "The life and famous novels of the writer John Steinbeck", "Why interstate highways are better than old roads", "The history of Route 66, from its beginning to the present"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文依時間順序介紹 66 號公路：1926 年誕生（第一段）、1930 年代的希望之路（第二段）、戰後的度假熱潮（第三段）、被州際公路取代（第四段），以及今日的復興（第五段）。(A) 文中沒有教人規劃旅程；(B) 史坦貝克只出現在第二段；(C) 文中沒有比較兩者好壞，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did many families travel on Route 66 in the 1930s?", "options": ["They wanted to visit the beaches of California on vacation.", "Their farms were ruined, so they went west to find work.", "They were moving to Chicago to work in factories.", "They wanted to see the places described in a famous novel."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：dry weather and huge dust storms ruined farms... families... drove west to look for work in California。(A) 他們不是去度假；(C) 他們是往西去加州，不是去芝加哥；(D) 小說是描寫他們的旅程，並非他們出發的原因，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, who gave Route 66 the name \"the mother road\"?", "options": ["A singer in a popular 1946 song", "The makers of the movie Cars", "A writer, in a novel about families traveling west", "The government, when the road was first built"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：John Steinbeck described their difficult journey in his 1939 novel The Grapes of Wrath, where he called Route 66 \"the mother road\"。(A) 1946 年的歌曲是邀請旅人到 66 號公路找樂子；(B) 電影是 2006 年才推出；(D) 文中未提及，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"bypassed\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["repaired and made wider for more traffic", "avoided by new roads that went around them", "sold to private companies to build shops", "closed for a long time because of dust storms"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說 parts of Route 66 were bypassed, as drivers chose the new roads that went around the small towns，可知 bypassed 意為「被（新路）繞過」。(A) 修繕拓寬、(C) 賣給民間公司、(D) 因沙塵暴關閉都不符合文意，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"the journey itself is the destination\" in the last paragraph?", "options": ["People drive Route 66 mainly to enjoy the trip, not to arrive somewhere fast.", "Route 66 is now the fastest way to get from Chicago to Los Angeles.", "Most visitors get lost because the old road has no signs.", "Travelers have to drive the whole road in one day."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段前一句說 people don't drive Route 66 to get somewhere quickly，可見人們開這條路是為了享受旅程本身，而不是快速抵達目的地。(B) 與文意相反；(C) 文中說有「歷史 66 號公路」路標指引；(D) 文中未提及，故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a75", "title": "Thirsty Ships: The Panama Canal's Water Problem", "title_zh": "口渴的運河：巴拿馬運河的缺水難題", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Environment", "Business"], "passage": "Every year, thousands of ships carrying everything from cars to bananas pass through the Panama Canal, a waterway about 80 kilometers long that connects the Atlantic and Pacific Oceans. Before the canal opened in 1914, a ship traveling from New York to San Francisco had to sail all the way around the southern tip of South America. The canal made that journey about 13,000 kilometers shorter.\n\nMany people imagine the canal as a long, flat channel of seawater, but it actually works like a giant water staircase. The land in the middle of Panama is too high for ships to sail straight across, so ships enter huge chambers called locks. Gates close behind them, and fresh water from a man-made lake, Gatun Lake, flows in and lifts each ship about 26 meters. After crossing the lake, the ship is lowered in the same way on the other side. The whole trip usually takes eight to ten hours.\n\nThis clever system has one weakness: it depends on rain. No pumps are used. Instead, gravity pulls water down from the lake into the locks, and afterward the water flows out to sea. Each ship that passes through uses about 200 million liters of fresh water, enough to fill around 80 Olympic swimming pools.\n\nIn 2023, Panama had one of the driest years in its history, and the water level of Gatun Lake dropped sharply. The canal had to cut the number of ships allowed to cross each day and limit how much cargo they could carry. Long lines of ships waited at both ends, and some companies paid millions of US dollars to move to the front of the line. Shipping costs rose, and some ships chose much longer routes instead.\n\nThe drought also revealed a bigger problem. Gatun Lake supplies drinking water to about half of Panama's people, so the canal and the country's families depend on the same water. Panama is now planning a new reservoir, a man-made lake for storing water, to collect more rain. As the world's climate changes, even a famous engineering wonder must learn to live with less water.", "passage_zh": "每年都有數千艘船隻通過巴拿馬運河，載運的貨物從汽車到香蕉應有盡有。這條水道長約八十公里，連接大西洋與太平洋。在運河於 1914 年開通之前，從紐約開往舊金山的船必須一路繞過南美洲的最南端。運河讓這段航程縮短了大約一萬三千公里。\n\n許多人把運河想像成一條又長又平、充滿海水的水道，但它其實像一座巨大的「水上階梯」。巴拿馬中部的地勢太高，船隻無法直接航行通過，所以船會駛進名為「船閘」的巨大閘室。閘門在船後關上，來自人工湖加通湖的淡水流入閘室，把每艘船抬高約二十六公尺。橫越湖面後，船再以同樣的方式在另一側降下。整段航程通常需要八到十個小時。\n\n這套巧妙的系統有一個弱點：它得靠下雨。運河不使用抽水機，而是靠重力讓湖水往下流進船閘，之後這些水便流入大海。每艘通過的船大約會用掉兩億公升的淡水，足以裝滿大約八十座奧運標準游泳池。\n\n2023 年，巴拿馬經歷了史上最乾燥的年份之一，加通湖的水位大幅下降。運河當局不得不減少每天允許通行的船隻數量，並限制船隻的載貨量。運河兩端都有長長的船隊在等候，有些公司甚至付出數百萬美元，只為了排到隊伍的最前面。航運成本因此上漲，有些船則改走遠得多的航線。\n\n這場乾旱也揭露了一個更大的問題。加通湖供應巴拿馬約一半人口的飲用水，因此運河和全國的家庭仰賴的是同一批水。巴拿馬目前正計畫興建一座新的水庫，也就是用來儲水的人工湖，以收集更多雨水。隨著全球氣候變遷，即使是著名的工程奇蹟，也必須學會在水更少的情況下運作。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the canal depends on fresh water and what happened when water ran short", "Why ships traveling from New York no longer sail to San Francisco", "How Gatun Lake became a popular place for swimming and boating", "Why Panama decided to build a canal between two oceans in 1914"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一、二段介紹運河的功能與船閘的運作方式，第三段指出運河仰賴雨水，第四、五段說明 2023 年乾旱造成的影響與未來的挑戰，故主旨是運河仰賴淡水，以及缺水時發生的問題。(B) 與第一段相反，運河反而讓這條航線變短；(C) 文中未提到游泳；(D) 1914 年只是第一段的細節，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happens to a ship inside a lock?", "options": ["Pumps push seawater into the lock so that the ship can move faster.", "Fresh water flows in and raises the ship to a higher level.", "Workers unload the cargo to make the ship lighter.", "The ship waits there for eight to ten hours."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：fresh water from a man-made lake, Gatun Lake, flows in and lifts each ship about 26 meters。(A) 第三段明言 No pumps are used，而且流入的是淡水不是海水；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 八到十小時是通過整條運河所需的時間，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "About how much fresh water does one ship use to pass through the canal?", "options": ["26 million liters", "80 million liters", "13,000 million liters", "200 million liters"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Each ship that passes through uses about 200 million liters of fresh water。(A) 26 是船被抬高的公尺數；(B) 80 是可裝滿的游泳池數量；(C) 13,000 是航程縮短的公里數，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why did shipping costs rise in 2023?", "options": ["Ships had to pay for all the fresh water they used in the locks.", "Gatun Lake was closed to all ships for the whole year because of the drought.", "Fewer ships could cross each day, so many had to wait or take longer routes.", "The canal was closed for repairs, so ships had to sail around South America."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說運河減少每天通行的船數並限制載貨量，船隻大排長龍，接著說 Shipping costs rose, and some ships chose much longer routes instead，可推論成本上漲是因為通行船數減少，船隻必須等待或繞遠路。(A)(D) 文中皆未提及；(B) 文中只說減少每天允許通行的船數，並沒有全年封閉，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"revealed\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["hid", "showed", "solved", "caused"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說乾旱 revealed a bigger problem，接著說明加通湖同時供應運河用水和全國約一半人口的飲用水，可知 revealed 意為「顯示、揭露」出原本存在的問題。(A) 隱藏、(C) 解決、(D) 造成都不符合文意，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a76", "title": "The Map That Is Wrong on Purpose", "title_zh": "故意「畫錯」的地圖", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["History", "Arts"], "passage": "Imagine standing on a subway platform in a city you have never visited. You look at the map on the wall, and within seconds you know which line to take and where to change trains. This easy experience owes a lot to a man named Harry Beck and his map of the London Underground, the world's oldest subway system, which opened in 1863.\n\nBefore Beck, the Underground's maps tried to show exactly where every station was. The lines were drawn on top of the real streets, so they twisted and turned. In the city center, stations were packed so closely together that their names were hard to read, while stations in the suburbs were spread far apart near the edges of the map. As more lines were built, the maps became more and more confusing.\n\nBeck worked for the Underground as an engineering draftsman, a person who makes technical drawings. In 1931, he had a simple but brilliant idea. Passengers traveling underground cannot see the streets above them, so they do not need to know real distances. They only need to know the order of the stations and where the lines meet. In his free time, Beck drew a new map using only straight lines, which ran up and down, from side to side, or at 45-degree angles. He placed the stations at almost equal distances and gave the crowded city center more space. The result looked a little like a diagram of an electrical circuit.\n\nAt first, his bosses thought the design was too strange. But in 1933, they printed a small number of pocket maps as a test. Passengers loved them, and the map was soon printed in large numbers. Beck continued to improve it for more than twenty years.\n\nBecause Beck's map is \"wrong\" on purpose, it sometimes fools people. On the map, Leicester Square and Covent Garden stations look just as far apart as many other stations, but they are actually less than 300 meters from each other. Many tourists take the train for a trip that would be quicker on foot. Still, Beck's idea of making a map clear rather than exact spread around the world. Today, subway maps from Tokyo to Taipei follow his basic rules.", "passage_zh": "想像你站在一座從未去過的城市的捷運月台上。你看了看牆上的路線圖，幾秒鐘內就知道該搭哪一條線、在哪裡轉車。這種輕鬆的體驗，很大一部分要歸功於一位名叫哈利．貝克的人，以及他為倫敦地鐵繪製的路線圖。倫敦地鐵於 1863 年通車，是全世界最古老的地下鐵系統。\n\n在貝克之前，倫敦地鐵的路線圖試圖標出每個車站的確切位置。路線是畫在真實的街道上，所以彎來彎去。在市中心，車站擠得太近，站名很難看清楚；郊區的車站則在地圖邊緣分散得很開。隨著越來越多路線興建，路線圖也變得越來越令人困惑。\n\n貝克在倫敦地鐵擔任工程製圖員，也就是繪製技術圖面的人。1931 年，他想到一個簡單卻絕妙的點子：在地底下搭車的乘客看不到上方的街道，所以他們不需要知道真實的距離，只需要知道車站的順序，以及各條路線在哪裡交會。貝克利用空閒時間畫了一張新地圖，只使用直線──上下垂直、左右水平，或是四十五度的斜線。他把車站之間的距離畫得幾乎相等，並給擁擠的市中心更多空間。畫出來的成果看起來有點像電路圖。\n\n起初，他的上司覺得這個設計太奇怪了。但在 1933 年，他們試印了少量的口袋地圖。乘客非常喜愛，這張地圖很快就大量印製。貝克持續改良它長達二十多年。\n\n因為貝克的地圖是刻意「畫錯」的，它有時會讓人上當。在地圖上，萊斯特廣場站和柯芬園站之間的距離看起來和許多其他車站一樣，但實際上兩站相距不到三百公尺。許多觀光客會搭車走一段其實步行更快的路程。儘管如此，貝克「清楚比精確更重要」的想法傳遍了全世界。如今，從東京到臺北，各地的捷運路線圖都遵循他的基本原則。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How the London Underground became the world's oldest subway system", "How Harry Beck made a clear subway map that ignores real distances", "Why tourists in London should walk instead of taking the train", "How electrical circuits are designed and drawn by engineers"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段引出貝克的倫敦地鐵路線圖，第二段說明舊地圖的問題，第三、四段描述貝克如何設計新地圖並獲得成功，第五段談它的影響，故主旨是貝克設計出不呈現真實距離、卻清楚易懂的路線圖。(A) 1863 年通車只是第一段的細節；(C) 是第五段的例子；(D) 電路圖只是附帶提及，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what was a problem with the older Underground maps?", "options": ["They did not show the real streets of London above the tunnels.", "They were printed only in black and white, so the lines looked the same.", "Stations in the city center were so crowded that their names were hard to read.", "They showed only the stations in the suburbs, not those in the city center."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：In the city center, stations were packed so closely together that their names were hard to read。(A) 舊地圖正是把路線畫在真實街道上；(B) 文中未提及顏色；(D) 第二段說舊地圖市中心和郊區的車站都有標示，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Beck believe that real distances were not important on a subway map?", "options": ["Passengers underground cannot see the streets, so they only need the order of stations.", "Most passengers already knew the streets of London very well and did not need help.", "The real distances between all the stations on each line were almost the same.", "His bosses told him to make the map as small as possible so it could fit in a pocket."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Passengers traveling underground cannot see the streets above them, so they do not need to know real distances. They only need to know the order of the stations and where the lines meet。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 與第五段「萊斯特廣場站和柯芬園站實際相距不到三百公尺」的說法不符，可見各站真實距離並不相同；(D) 第四段只說上司起初覺得設計太奇怪，沒有說是上司要求縮小地圖，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"suburbs\" in paragraph 2 refers to ______.", "options": ["the busiest shopping streets in the middle of the city", "the deep tunnels that run under the River Thames", "famous places that tourists from abroad like to visit", "the areas at the edge of a city, away from the center"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段把 the city center 和 the suburbs 對比：市中心的車站擠在一起，郊區的車站則 spread far apart near the edges of the map，可知 suburbs 指「市郊，遠離市中心的地區」。(A) 與市中心的意思相反；(B)(C) 文中皆未提及，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why do many tourists take the train between Leicester Square and Covent Garden?", "options": ["The walk between the two stations is long and dangerous at night.", "The map makes the two stations look farther apart than they really are.", "There is no road or sidewalk that connects the two stations.", "Tickets for very short trips like this one are free for tourists."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：On the map, Leicester Square and Covent Garden stations look just as far apart as many other stations, but they are actually less than 300 meters from each other，地圖讓兩站看起來比實際遠，所以觀光客會搭車。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及；而且第五段說這段路 would be quicker on foot，步行反而更快，故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a77", "title": "The King of the Road: Jeepneys in the Philippines", "title_zh": "道路之王：菲律賓的吉普尼", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Culture", "Society"], "passage": "On the busy streets of Manila, the capital of the Philippines, it is hard to miss a jeepney. These long, colorful vehicles are covered with bright paintings, shiny metal decorations and names such as \"Lucky Star\" or \"Queen of the South.\" For millions of Filipinos, jeepneys are the cheapest way to get to school or work, and they are so common that people call them the \"King of the Road.\"\n\nThe jeepney's story began after World War II. When American soldiers left the Philippines, they left behind many military jeeps. Filipinos turned these into something new. They made the jeeps longer, added roofs and two long benches at the back, and painted them in eye-catching colors. A vehicle built for war became a symbol of everyday life.\n\nRiding a jeepney is a lesson in trust. Passengers climb in at the back and sit facing each other. To pay, you hand your money to the person next to you and say \"Bayad po,\" a polite way of saying \"Here's my fare.\" The money is passed from hand to hand until it reaches the driver, and your change comes back the same way. When you want to get off, you simply call out \"Para po!\" and the driver pulls over.\n\nHowever, the King of the Road is in trouble. Many jeepneys are decades old, and their old diesel engines fill the air with black smoke. In 2017, the government started a program to replace them with modern vehicles that cause less pollution, have air conditioning and are safer. Ramon Cruz, who has driven the same jeepney in Manila for thirty years, understands the reasons but is still worried. \"A new one costs more than two million pesos,\" he says. \"How can a driver like me pay for that?\"\n\nMany drivers have protested, and parts of the program have been delayed again and again. Some people want the old jeepneys to disappear quickly for the sake of cleaner air. Others fear that a piece of Filipino culture, along with many drivers' jobs, will be lost. Perhaps the best answer is a new jeepney that is clean and safe but still carries the bright colors and friendly spirit of the old one.", "passage_zh": "在菲律賓首都馬尼拉繁忙的街道上，吉普尼很難不引人注意。這些車身長、色彩繽紛的車輛上布滿鮮豔的彩繪、閃亮的金屬裝飾，還有像「幸運星」或「南方女王」這樣的名字。對數以百萬計的菲律賓人來說，吉普尼是上學或上班最便宜的交通方式，而且它們隨處可見，因此人們稱它們為「道路之王」。\n\n吉普尼的故事始於第二次世界大戰之後。美軍離開菲律賓時，留下了許多軍用吉普車。菲律賓人把它們改造成全新的交通工具：他們把吉普車加長，加上車頂，在後方裝上兩排長椅，再漆上醒目的顏色。一輛為戰爭而打造的車，就這樣成了日常生活的象徵。\n\n搭乘吉普尼是一堂關於信任的課。乘客從車尾上車，面對面坐著。付錢時，你把錢交給旁邊的人，並說「Bayad po」，這是「這是我的車資」的禮貌說法。錢會一手傳一手，直到交到司機手上，找的零錢也會用同樣的方式傳回來。想下車時，只要喊一聲「Para po！」，司機就會靠邊停車。\n\n然而，這位道路之王正面臨困境。許多吉普尼已經有幾十年的車齡，老舊的柴油引擎讓空氣中充滿黑煙。2017 年，政府推動一項計畫，要以汙染較少、有冷氣而且更安全的新式車輛取代它們。在馬尼拉開同一輛吉普尼三十年的拉蒙．克魯茲明白其中的理由，卻仍然很擔心。「一輛新車要價超過兩百萬披索，」他說，「像我這樣的司機怎麼付得起？」\n\n許多司機發起抗議，這項計畫的部分內容也一再延後。有些人為了讓空氣更乾淨，希望老吉普尼盡快消失；另一些人則擔心，一部分的菲律賓文化會隨著許多司機的工作一起消失。或許最好的答案是一種新的吉普尼：它乾淨又安全，卻依然保有老吉普尼鮮豔的色彩和友善的精神。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to drive a jeepney safely on the busy streets of Manila", "Why American soldiers came to the Philippines during World War II", "The history and culture of jeepneys, and the challenge they now face", "How much money jeepney drivers earn each day and how they spend it"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一至三段介紹吉普尼的外觀、起源和搭乘文化，第四、五段說明它因汰換計畫而面臨的困境。(A) 文中沒有教人開車；(B) 美軍只是第二段的背景；(D) 文中沒有提到司機每天的收入，故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how did jeepneys begin?", "options": ["Filipinos changed the military jeeps that American soldiers left behind.", "A Japanese company designed them for the Philippine government.", "They were first built as small buses to carry tourists around Manila.", "The government bought them from the United States in 2017."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：When American soldiers left the Philippines, they left behind many military jeeps. Filipinos turned these into something new。(B)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 2017 年是政府推動汰換計畫的時間，故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "If you are riding a jeepney and want to get off, what should you do?", "options": ["Pass your money to the driver.", "Say \"Bayad po\" to the person next to you.", "Ring the bell above the window.", "Call out \"Para po!\""], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：When you want to get off, you simply call out \"Para po!\" and the driver pulls over。(A)(B) 是付車資的方式；(C) 文中沒有提到按鈴，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer say that \"riding a jeepney is a lesson in trust\"?", "options": ["Drivers let passengers ride for free if they are polite.", "Passengers depend on strangers to pass their money and change correctly.", "Passengers must trust the driver to choose the fastest route.", "Jeepneys have no seats, so passengers must hold on to each other."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說乘客把錢交給旁邊的人，一手傳一手送到司機手上，零錢也用同樣方式傳回來，可見乘客必須信任陌生人會把錢和零錢正確傳遞。(A)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 文中說乘客坐在兩排長椅上，故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "How does Ramon Cruz feel about the government's program?", "options": ["He is excited because he can finally buy a new vehicle.", "He does not care because he plans to stop driving soon.", "He understands why it started, but he is worried about the cost.", "He is angry because he thinks old jeepneys cause no pollution."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：Ramon Cruz... understands the reasons but is still worried，他擔心新車要價超過兩百萬披索，自己付不起。(A) 與他的擔憂相反；(B) 文中沒有說他打算不再開車；(D) 文中沒有說他否認汙染問題，故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a78", "title": "The Missing Holiday Pay: Reading My First Payslip", "title_zh": "消失的國定假日工資：讀懂我的第一張薪資單", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Money", "Career"], "passage": "Last fall, I started my first part-time job at a bubble tea shop. I was paid NT$200 an hour. When my payslip for October arrived, I was so excited that I only looked at the final number and smiled. My older brother, who works as an accountant, told me to read it more carefully. \"A payslip is a record of your work,\" he said, \"and records can have mistakes.\"\n\nSo we read it together. The first line showed my working hours: 64 hours in total. Next to it was my pay before deductions, NT$12,800. Deductions are amounts taken out of your pay before you receive it. Mine included a small amount for labor insurance. My brother explained that in Taiwan, part-time workers also have the right to labor insurance. The employer pays 70 percent of the cost, the government pays 10 percent, and the worker pays only 20 percent. He added that my boss had to put at least 6 percent of my pay into a retirement account in my name.\n\nThen my brother noticed something. \"Didn't you work on October 10?\" he asked. I did. National Day is a public holiday, but the shop was open, and I worked a six-hour shift that day. By law, if a worker agrees to work on a national holiday, the employer must pay double for those hours. On my payslip, however, those six hours were paid at the normal rate.\n\nI felt nervous about talking to my boss. I was only seventeen, and I didn't want to seem rude. My brother suggested that I bring photos of the work schedule and ask a polite question instead of making a complaint. The next day, I said, \"Could you check my pay for October 10? I think holiday hours are paid double.\" My manager looked at the payslip, apologized, and said the computer system had not been set up correctly. The missing money was added to my next payment.\n\nNow I know that every worker, even a teenager with a part-time job, should understand their payslip. Keep a record of your hours, learn your basic rights, and don't be afraid to ask. Reading a payslip takes five minutes, but it can protect money you worked hard for.", "passage_zh": "去年秋天，我在一家手搖飲料店開始了人生第一份打工，時薪是新臺幣 200 元。十月的薪資單送來時，我興奮得只看了最後的金額就笑了。我哥哥是會計師，他要我讀得仔細一點。他說：「薪資單是你工作的紀錄，而紀錄是有可能出錯的。」\n\n於是我們一起讀。第一行列出我的工作時數：總共 64 小時。旁邊是扣款前的薪資，新臺幣 12,800 元。「扣款」是指在你拿到薪水之前，先從薪資中扣掉的金額。我的扣款包括一小筆勞保費。哥哥解釋，在臺灣，兼職人員也有參加勞工保險的權利。保費由雇主負擔百分之七十、政府負擔百分之十，勞工自己只付百分之二十。他還告訴我，老闆必須另外提撥至少相當於我薪資百分之六的錢，存進我名下的退休金帳戶。\n\n接著哥哥發現了一件事。他問：「你十月十日不是有上班嗎？」我確實有。國慶日是國定假日，但店裡照常營業，我那天上了六個小時的班。依照法律規定，如果勞工同意在國定假日上班，雇主必須以加倍的工資支付這些時數。然而在我的薪資單上，這六個小時卻是以平常的時薪計算。\n\n要跟老闆談這件事，我覺得很緊張。我才十七歲，不想顯得沒禮貌。哥哥建議我帶著班表的照片，用禮貌的方式提出疑問，而不是去抱怨。隔天我說：「可以請您幫我確認一下十月十日的薪水嗎？我記得國定假日的工時是加倍計薪的。」店長看了薪資單後向我道歉，說是電腦系統沒有設定好。少給的錢後來加進了我下一次的薪水裡。\n\n現在我知道，每一位勞工，即使是打工的青少年，都應該看懂自己的薪資單。記錄自己的工作時數、了解基本權益，而且不要害怕發問。讀一張薪資單只要五分鐘，卻能保護你辛苦工作賺來的錢。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?", "options": ["To explain why bubble tea shops often need part-time workers", "To show why young workers should read and understand their payslips", "To complain that the shop did not pay the writer on time", "To encourage teenagers to work on national holidays for more money"], "answer": 1, "explain": "作者以自己發現國定假日工資少算的經驗，在最後一段總結：every worker... should understand their payslip，並提醒讀者記錄工時、了解權益，故主旨是說明年輕勞工應讀懂薪資單。(A) 文中未談飲料店為何需要人手；(C) 問題是少算加倍工資，不是遲發薪水；(D) 作者並未鼓勵大家在假日上班賺錢。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "According to the passage, how much of the cost of labor insurance does the worker pay?", "options": ["6 percent", "10 percent", "70 percent", "20 percent"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：The employer pays 70 percent of the cost, the government pays 10 percent, and the worker pays only 20 percent. 勞工只付百分之二十。(A) 6 percent 是雇主提撥退休金的比例；(B) 是政府負擔的比例；(C) 是雇主負擔的比例。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "If the October 10 shift had been paid correctly, what would the writer's pay before deductions have been?", "options": ["NT$12,800", "NT$13,400", "NT$14,000", "NT$15,200"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說 64 小時的扣款前薪資為 12,800 元（64 × 200），其中國慶日的 6 小時只以平常的時薪計算。第三段指出國定假日工時應加倍，所以這 6 小時還要再多付 6 × 200 = 1,200 元，正確金額為 12,800 + 1,200 = 14,000 元。(A) 是原本算錯的金額；(B) 13,400 元只多加了 600 元（半倍）；(D) 15,200 元是把 6 小時的加倍工資 2,400 元整筆再加上去，重複計算了已付的 1,200 元。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "The word \"rate\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the amount of money paid for each hour", "the speed at which someone works", "the number of customers in a shop", "the time when a shift begins"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 those six hours were paid at the normal rate，指這六小時是用「平常的計薪標準」計算，而法律規定應加倍，可知 rate 指每小時支付的金額（時薪）。(B) 工作速度、(C) 客人數量、(D) 上班時間都與「計薪」的上下文不符。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the writer's manager?", "options": ["The manager refused to pay the writer for the holiday shift.", "The manager probably did not make the mistake on purpose.", "The manager asked the writer to work fewer hours in November.", "The manager thought the writer was rude to ask about the pay."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說店長看了薪資單後 apologized（道歉），並解釋是 the computer system had not been set up correctly，之後也把少給的錢補進下一次的薪水，可推知店長應該不是故意少付。(A) 與「錢補進下次薪水」相反；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 店長向作者道歉，並沒有認為作者無禮。故選 (B)"}]}, {"id": "a79", "title": "Paid to Shop? The Secret Work of Mystery Shoppers", "title_zh": "逛街也能賺錢？神秘客的秘密工作", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Business", "Career"], "passage": "Last Saturday, Lin Mei-ling ordered a sandwich and an iced coffee at a fast-food restaurant in Taichung. She looked like any other customer, but she wasn't. While she waited, she quietly noticed how long it took for someone to greet her, whether the tables were clean, and if the cashier repeated her order correctly. Mei-ling, a 20-year-old university student, is a mystery shopper.\n\nMystery shoppers are people hired to visit shops, restaurants, banks or hotels as ordinary customers and report on their experience. Companies use them because managers cannot be everywhere at once. A restaurant chain with two hundred branches, for example, needs to know whether a customer in a small town gets the same service as one in the city center. The idea is not new. In the 1940s, some American banks and stores hired people to pose as customers to check whether their workers were honest.\n\nThe job sounds like fun, but it takes skill. Before each visit, Mei-ling receives a list of things to check. She cannot take notes in front of the staff, so she types everything into her phone as soon as she leaves. Her report has to be fair and exact. \"The cashier was friendly\" is not enough; she must write down the time, what was said, and how long she waited. \"I'm not there to catch people,\" she explains. \"I'm there to describe what really happened.\"\n\nThe pay is not high. Mei-ling earns NT$300 to NT$500 per visit, and the company pays back the cost of her meal. For her, the real value is what she learns. \"Now I notice things most customers don't, like whether a worker looks up when the door opens,\" she says. She hopes to work in hotel management, and these visits are good training.\n\nHowever, she warns teenagers to be careful. Some online ads offering high pay for \"secret shopping\" are scams. In a common trick, the \"company\" sends you money, asks you to test a money transfer service by sending most of it to another person, and later the first payment turns out to be fake. Real mystery shopping companies never ask you to send or pay money. If a job sounds too good to be true, it probably is.", "passage_zh": "上星期六，林美玲在臺中一家速食店點了一份三明治和一杯冰咖啡。她看起來跟其他客人沒什麼兩樣，但她並不是一般的客人。在等餐的時候，她悄悄留意店員過了多久才來招呼她、桌子乾不乾淨，以及收銀員有沒有正確複誦她點的餐。二十歲的大學生美玲，是一名神秘客。\n\n神秘客是受雇以一般顧客身分造訪商店、餐廳、銀行或飯店，再回報自身消費經驗的人。公司之所以聘用他們，是因為主管不可能同時出現在每個地方。舉例來說，一家有兩百間分店的連鎖餐廳，需要知道小鎮上的顧客是否得到和市中心顧客一樣的服務。這個做法並不新。早在 1940 年代，美國就有一些銀行和商店雇人假扮成顧客，檢查員工是否誠實。\n\n這份工作聽起來很好玩，但需要技巧。每次造訪之前，美玲會收到一份要檢查的項目清單。她不能在店員面前做筆記，所以一離開店裡，就馬上把所有細節輸入手機。她的報告必須公正又精確。光寫「收銀員很友善」是不夠的，她必須記下時間、對方說了什麼，以及她等了多久。她解釋：「我不是去抓別人的錯，而是去描述真正發生的事。」\n\n這份工作的報酬並不高。美玲每次造訪可以賺新臺幣 300 到 500 元，公司也會支付她的餐費。對她來說，真正的價值在於學到的東西。她說：「現在我會注意到大多數客人不會注意的事，例如門打開時，店員有沒有抬頭看。」她希望將來能從事飯店管理的工作，而這些造訪正是很好的訓練。\n\n不過，她提醒青少年要小心。有些以高薪招募「秘密購物員」的網路廣告，其實是詐騙。一種常見的手法是，「公司」先匯一筆錢給你，要你把其中大部分轉給另一個人，藉此「測試」某個匯款服務，結果後來發現第一筆款項是假的。真正的神秘客公司絕不會要你匯錢或付錢。如果一份工作好得令人難以置信，那它多半就不是真的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How fast-food restaurants in Taichung train their cashiers", "Why university students should work in hotel management", "What mystery shoppers do and what people should know about the job", "How banks in the 1940s stopped their workers from stealing money"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文以美玲為例，介紹神秘客的工作內容（第一、二段）、需要的技巧（第三段）、報酬與收穫（第四段），以及要小心的詐騙（第五段）。(A) 文中未談速食店如何訓練收銀員；(B) 飯店管理只是美玲個人的志向；(D) 1940 年代的銀行只是第二段提到的歷史背景，並非主旨。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "According to the passage, why do companies hire mystery shoppers?", "options": ["Their managers cannot check every shop or branch themselves.", "They want customers to spend more money in their shops.", "They need extra workers on busy weekends.", "Their own workers are usually dishonest."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Companies use them because managers cannot be everywhere at once. 主管無法同時出現在每個地方，所以需要神秘客去檢查各分店的服務。(B)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第二段只說 1940 年代有銀行用來檢查員工是否誠實，並沒有說員工通常不誠實。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The phrase \"pose as\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["complain to", "take pictures of", "work together with", "pretend to be"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段 hired people to pose as customers to check whether their workers were honest，這些人其實不是真正的客人，而是「假扮成」客人去觀察員工，pose as 意思接近 pretend to be。(A) 向……抱怨、(B) 拍照、(C) 合作，都不符合神秘客的做法。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "Which of the following would Mei-ling most likely write in her report?", "options": ["\"The staff seemed nice, and I liked the coffee a lot.\"", "\"At 2:15 p.m., the cashier said 'Welcome!' about 40 seconds after I came in.\"", "\"The cashier was lazy and should be fired right away.\"", "\"I think this restaurant is better than the one near my school.\""], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段說報告必須 fair and exact，只寫 \"The cashier was friendly\" 不夠，must write down the time, what was said, and how long she waited。(B) 同時包含時間、說了什麼和等了多久，最符合要求。(A)(D) 是個人感受，不夠精確；(C) 帶有批評與處罰的意味，違反她說的 \"I'm not there to catch people\"。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, which job ad should a teenager be most careful about?", "options": ["One that pays NT$400 for each visit to a restaurant", "One that gives you a list of things to check before each visit", "One that asks you to send part of a payment to another person", "One that pays back the cost of the meal you order"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段說常見的詐騙是「公司」先匯錢給你，再要你把大部分轉給別人，並強調 Real mystery shopping companies never ask you to send or pay money，所以要你轉錢給別人的廣告最可疑。(A)(B)(D) 都是第三、四段描述的正常神秘客工作內容。故選 (C)"}]}, {"id": "a80", "title": "A New Kind of Farmer: Flying Drones over the Rice Fields", "title_zh": "新一代農夫：在稻田上空飛無人機", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Career"], "passage": "When Chen Kuan-yu finished college in Taipei, everyone expected him to find an office job in the city. Instead, he moved back to his family's village in Yunlin County and became a farmer. But Kuan-yu does not spend his days bent over in the rice fields with a heavy sprayer on his back. He works with a remote control in his hands, flying a large drone over the crops.\n\nHis drone carries a tank of fertilizer or pesticide, a chemical that protects plants from insects and disease. It flies about two meters above the rice and sprays evenly as it moves. Spraying one hectare by hand can take a farmer several hours under the hot sun, but the drone can finish the same area in about fifteen minutes. Because the drone does the spraying, the farmer also stays away from the chemicals and does not breathe them in.\n\nKuan-yu's services are in high demand. Many farmers in Taiwan are now over sixty, and such tiring work is hard for them. Young people who could help are often working in the cities. So older farmers in the area pay Kuan-yu to spray their fields. During the busiest months, he flies from early morning until the wind gets too strong. Drones cannot fly safely in strong wind or heavy rain, and each battery lasts only about ten minutes, so he always brings several charged ones.\n\nBecoming a drone pilot was not as easy as playing a video game. Kuan-yu had to pass a written test and a flying test to get a government license. He also had to learn about different crops, the right amount of each chemical, and the rules for flying near roads and houses. \"One mistake can damage a neighbor's vegetables or hurt someone,\" he says. \"Being careful is the most important part of the job.\"\n\nKuan-yu believes technology can make farming attractive to young people again. \"People think farming means hard work and low pay,\" he says. \"But farms today need people who understand machines, data and plants. My grandfather taught me how to grow rice. Now I teach him how to read the field maps on my tablet.\"", "passage_zh": "陳冠宇在臺北讀完大學時，大家都以為他會在城市裡找一份辦公室的工作。結果他卻搬回雲林縣家鄉的村子，當起了農夫。不過，冠宇並不是整天彎著腰、背著沉重的噴藥桶在稻田裡工作。他工作時手裡拿的是遙控器，操控一架大型無人機在農作物上空飛行。\n\n他的無人機載著一桶肥料或農藥，農藥是一種保護植物不受昆蟲和疾病侵害的化學藥劑。無人機在稻子上方大約兩公尺處飛行，一邊移動一邊均勻地噴灑。用人工噴灑一公頃的田地，農夫可能得在大太陽底下忙上好幾個小時，但無人機大約十五分鐘就能完成同樣的面積。因為由無人機負責噴灑，農夫也能遠離這些化學藥劑，不會把它們吸進體內。\n\n冠宇的服務非常搶手。臺灣有許多農夫如今都已超過六十歲，這麼辛苦的工作對他們來說很吃力。而原本可以幫忙的年輕人，往往都在城市裡工作。因此，當地年紀較大的農夫會付錢請冠宇幫忙噴灑他們的田。在最忙碌的那幾個月，他從一大早一直飛到風變得太大為止。無人機在強風或大雨中無法安全飛行，而且每顆電池只能撐大約十分鐘，所以他總是帶著好幾顆充好電的電池。\n\n成為無人機操作員可不像打電動那麼簡單。冠宇必須通過筆試和飛行術科測驗，才能取得政府核發的執照。他也必須學習各種農作物的知識、每種化學藥劑的正確用量，以及在道路和房屋附近飛行的規定。他說：「一個失誤就可能毀了鄰居的菜，甚至傷到人。小心謹慎是這份工作最重要的部分。」\n\n冠宇相信，科技能讓年輕人重新覺得務農是有吸引力的。他說：「大家都以為務農就是工作辛苦、收入又低。但現在的農場需要懂機器、懂數據、也懂植物的人。我爺爺教我怎麼種稻，現在換我教他怎麼看懂我平板上的農田地圖。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why most young people in Taiwan do not want to live in the countryside", "How farmers in Yunlin grow rice without using any chemicals", "The dangers of playing video games for too many hours", "A young man who uses drones to make farm work easier and safer"], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文介紹冠宇回鄉用無人機替農夫噴灑農藥，說明無人機省時、讓農夫遠離化學藥劑（第二段），以及這份工作需要的技能與他對務農的看法。(A) 年輕人在城市工作只是第三段的一個細節；(B) 與文中使用肥料與農藥的內容不符；(C) 第四段只是用打電動來比喻，並非主題。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "According to the passage, about how long does the drone take to spray one hectare?", "options": ["Ten minutes", "Fifteen minutes", "Several hours", "From early morning until the wind gets strong"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：the drone can finish the same area in about fifteen minutes，同樣一公頃的面積無人機大約十五分鐘完成。(A) 十分鐘是第三段提到的電池續航時間；(C) 好幾個小時是人工噴灑所需的時間；(D) 是他在最忙碌月份的工作時間。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Why do older farmers in the area pay Kuan-yu to spray their fields?", "options": ["The work is too tiring for them, and young people are often away in the cities.", "The government does not allow farmers over sixty to use pesticide.", "Kuan-yu sells his services at a lower price than other workers.", "Their own drones cannot fly in strong wind or heavy rain."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說許多農夫已超過六十歲，such tiring work is hard for them，而且 Young people who could help are often working in the cities，所以他們付錢請冠宇代為噴灑。(B)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 強風大雨無法飛行是所有無人機的限制，文中也沒說這些農夫有自己的無人機。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The phrase \"in high demand\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["very expensive", "hard to understand", "wanted by many people", "available only in summer"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段 Kuan-yu's services are in high demand 之後說明農夫年紀大、年輕人不在身邊，所以 older farmers in the area pay Kuan-yu to spray their fields，可知很多人需要他的服務，in high demand 意思是「需求量大、很搶手」。(A) 文中沒有提到價格；(B)(D) 與上下文無關。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "What does Kuan-yu mean when he says, \"Now I teach him how to read the field maps on my tablet\"?", "options": ["His grandfather no longer wants to grow rice.", "Tablets are more useful than real experience on a farm.", "His grandfather taught him nothing important about farming.", "Old farming knowledge and new technology can both be shared in a family."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段冠宇說 My grandfather taught me how to grow rice. Now I teach him how to read the field maps on my tablet. 爺爺傳授種稻的經驗，他則教爺爺使用新科技，表示傳統知識和新技術可以在家人之間互相分享。(A)(C) 與「爺爺教他種稻」不符；(B) 文中並沒有說平板比實際經驗更有用，他反而強調需要懂機器、數據和植物的人。故選 (D)"}]}, {"id": "a81", "title": "The Interview Started Before I Sat Down", "title_zh": "還沒坐下，面試就開始了", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Life"], "passage": "Last July, I had my first real job interview, for a summer position at the Harbor Science Museum in Kaohsiung. The job was to help children at the hands-on exhibits. I prepared for days. I practiced my answers in front of a mirror, ironed my best shirt and printed three copies of my résumé. I thought I was ready for every question.\n\nI arrived twenty minutes early. In the lobby, an older man in a gray uniform was trying to carry a big box of paper cups through the door, and some cups had fallen onto the floor. I was nervous and wanted to review my notes, but I put my bag down, held the door and helped him pick up the cups. Then I asked the woman at the front desk where the interview would be. She smiled, wrote down my name and told me to wait.\n\nThe interview itself went well, I thought. The manager, Ms. Hsu, asked why I wanted the job and how I would handle a crying child. At the end, she said something surprising: \"Mr. Lo told me you helped him with the cups, and Amy at the front desk said you were very polite. That tells me more than your answers do.\" Mr. Lo, it turned out, was the head of the museum's cleaning team, and Ms. Hsu always asked him and the receptionist about every candidate.\n\nI got the job. Weeks later, I asked Ms. Hsu why she cared so much about the lobby. She explained that in an interview, everyone tries to show their best side. \"But in the lobby, people think nobody important is watching,\" she said. \"How you treat someone who can't give you the job shows how you will treat our visitors and your coworkers.\"\n\nSince then, I have learned that many employers do something similar. Some ask the receptionist for an opinion; others notice how candidates treat the waiter during a lunch meeting. So if you have an interview soon, remember that it may begin the moment you walk through the door. Be kind to everyone, not because someone might be watching, but because it is the right thing to do.", "passage_zh": "去年七月，我參加了人生第一場正式的工作面試，應徵高雄港灣科學博物館的暑期職缺，工作內容是在動手操作的展品區協助小朋友。我準備了好幾天：對著鏡子練習回答、把最好的一件襯衫燙平，還印了三份履歷。我以為自己已經準備好面對任何問題了。\n\n我提早二十分鐘到達。在大廳裡，一位穿著灰色制服的年長男士正想把一大箱紙杯搬進門，有一些紙杯掉到了地上。我很緊張，原本想再看一下筆記，但我還是放下包包，幫他扶著門，並幫他把紙杯撿起來。接著我向櫃檯的小姐詢問面試地點。她微笑著寫下我的名字，請我稍候。\n\n我覺得面試本身進行得很順利。經理許小姐問我為什麼想要這份工作，以及遇到哭鬧的小孩時會怎麼處理。面試最後，她說了一段令我意外的話：「羅先生跟我說你幫他撿了紙杯，櫃檯的 Amy 也說你非常有禮貌。這比你的回答更能讓我了解你。」原來，羅先生是博物館清潔團隊的主管，而許小姐每次都會向他和櫃檯人員詢問每一位應徵者的表現。\n\n我錄取了。幾個星期後，我問許小姐為什麼這麼在意大廳裡發生的事。她解釋，面試的時候，每個人都會努力表現出自己最好的一面。她說：「但在大廳裡，大家會以為沒有重要的人在看。你怎麼對待一個無法給你工作的人，就能看出你將來會怎麼對待我們的參觀者和你的同事。」\n\n從那之後，我才知道許多雇主也會做類似的事。有些人會詢問櫃檯人員的意見，有些人則會在午餐會談時，留意應徵者怎麼對待服務生。所以，如果你最近要參加面試，請記得：從你走進門的那一刻起，面試可能就已經開始了。對每個人都要親切友善，不是因為可能有人在看，而是因為這是應該做的事。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main lesson of this story?", "options": ["Arriving early is the best way to get any job.", "Science museums prefer workers who are good with children.", "How you treat everyone around you can matter as much as your interview answers.", "Candidates should spend their waiting time reviewing their notes."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段許小姐說作者幫忙撿紙杯、對櫃檯有禮貌 tells me more than your answers do，最後一段也提醒讀者面試可能從走進門就開始，要 Be kind to everyone。(A) 提早到只是故事細節，並非重點；(B) 文中沒有比較博物館的用人偏好；(D) 作者正是放下筆記去幫忙，才得到好評價，與此選項相反。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "Who was Mr. Lo?", "options": ["The head of the museum's cleaning team", "The manager who interviewed the writer", "Another student who wanted the same job", "A visitor who was bringing his children to the museum"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Mr. Lo, it turned out, was the head of the museum's cleaning team. 羅先生是博物館清潔團隊的主管，也就是第二段在大廳搬紙杯的那位男士。(B) 面試作者的是經理許小姐；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "According to Ms. Hsu, why does she care about how candidates act in the lobby?", "options": ["The lobby is the place where most visitors complain.", "She wants to know whether candidates can carry heavy boxes.", "She does not have enough time to ask many questions in the interview.", "People show their real behavior when they think no one important is watching."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段許小姐說 in the lobby, people think nobody important is watching，而且 How you treat someone who can't give you the job shows how you will treat our visitors and your coworkers，也就是在大廳的表現更能看出一個人真實的樣子。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 她在意的是待人態度，不是搬東西的能力。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "The word \"candidate\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a person who cleans a building", "a person who is trying to get a job", "a person who visits a museum", "a person who works at the front desk"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 Ms. Hsu always asked him and the receptionist about every candidate，許小姐向清潔主管和櫃檯詢問的是每一位來面試的人，第五段 how candidates treat the waiter 也是指應徵者，candidate 意思是「應徵者、候選人」。(A) 是羅先生的工作；(C) 是參觀者 visitor；(D) 是 receptionist。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "In paragraph 3, what does \"That\" in \"That tells me more than your answers do\" refer to?", "options": ["The writer's kind and polite behavior before the interview", "The three copies of the writer's résumé", "The writer's answer about how to handle a crying child", "The writer's plan to arrive twenty minutes early"], "answer": 0, "explain": "That 前面一句是 Mr. Lo told me you helped him with the cups, and Amy at the front desk said you were very polite，指作者在面試前幫忙撿紙杯、對櫃檯有禮貌的表現，這比面試回答更能讓經理了解作者。(B)(D) 與上下文無關；(C) 正是 That 拿來比較的 your answers，不會是 That 本身。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a82", "title": "A Year Down Under: My Cousin's Working Holiday", "title_zh": "在南半球的一年：表姊的打工度假", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Travel", "Career"], "passage": "When my cousin Ya-ting turned twenty-four, she surprised the whole family. She quit her job at a bank in Taichung and flew to Australia on a working holiday visa. This kind of visa lets young people travel in another country and take jobs there to pay for their trip. Australia accepts Taiwanese applicants aged 18 to 30, and they can stay for up to twelve months.\n\nHer first weeks in Melbourne were hard. Rent was expensive, and nobody called her back after she handed out her résumé at dozens of cafés. \"I thought my English was good,\" she told me on a video call. \"But Australians speak fast and use a lot of slang. When a customer asked for a 'flat white,' I had no idea it was a kind of coffee!\" After a month, she finally found work washing dishes in a small restaurant.\n\nLater, she decided to stay a second year. To do this, she had to work for about three months in certain jobs in regional Australia, such as on farms. So she left the city for a small farm town in Queensland and spent the season picking strawberries. On some farms, workers were paid by the bucket instead of by the hour. Fast pickers did well, but slow pickers like Ya-ting earned very little at first. She learned to read every job offer carefully before saying yes.\n\nLooking back, Ya-ting says the year taught her more than any class. She learned to cook for herself, manage her money and solve problems in a second language. She also made friends from Korea, Germany and Brazil. However, she does not think a working holiday is right for everyone. \"Some people think it's an easy way to get rich. It isn't,\" she says. \"You need a plan, some savings and the courage to ask for help.\"\n\nNow back in Taiwan, Ya-ting works for a travel company that plans trips for Australian visitors. Her English and her experience helped her get the job. \"My year down under wasn't a break from my career,\" she says, using a popular nickname for Australia. \"It was the beginning of it.\"", "passage_zh": "表姊雅婷二十四歲那年，讓全家人都嚇了一跳。她辭掉在臺中一家銀行的工作，拿著打工度假簽證飛到了澳洲。這種簽證讓年輕人可以在另一個國家旅行，並在當地工作來支付旅費。澳洲接受十八到三十歲的臺灣人申請，最長可以停留十二個月。\n\n她在墨爾本的頭幾個星期很辛苦。房租很貴，她在幾十家咖啡店投了履歷，卻沒有人回電。她在視訊通話中告訴我：「我本來以為自己的英文不錯，但澳洲人講話很快，又用很多俚語。有位客人點了一杯『flat white』，我完全不知道那是一種咖啡！」過了一個月，她終於在一家小餐廳找到洗碗的工作。\n\n後來，她決定再多待一年。要做到這一點，她必須在澳洲的偏遠地區從事特定工作大約三個月，例如在農場工作。於是她離開城市，搬到昆士蘭的一個農業小鎮，花了一整季採草莓。有些農場不是按小時計薪，而是按桶計酬。採得快的人收入不錯，但像雅婷這樣採得慢的人，一開始賺得非常少。她因此學會在答應之前，仔細閱讀每一份工作條件。\n\n回想起來，雅婷說那一年教會她的比任何一堂課都多。她學會自己煮飯、管理金錢，並用第二語言解決問題。她也交到了來自韓國、德國和巴西的朋友。不過，她並不認為打工度假適合每一個人。她說：「有些人以為這是輕鬆致富的方法，其實不是。你需要一個計畫、一些存款，還有開口求助的勇氣。」\n\n現在雅婷回到臺灣，在一家為澳洲旅客規劃行程的旅行社工作。她的英文能力和這段經歷幫助她得到了這份工作。她用了一個常用來稱呼澳洲的暱稱「down under」說：「我在 down under 的那一年並不是職涯的中斷，而是職涯的起點。」", "questions": [{"q": "According to the passage, which of the following people could apply for a working holiday visa to Australia?", "options": ["A 16-year-old Taiwanese high school student", "A 27-year-old Taiwanese office worker", "A 33-year-old Taiwanese engineer", "A 40-year-old Taiwanese teacher"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：Australia accepts Taiwanese applicants aged 18 to 30，只有十八到三十歲的臺灣人可以申請，二十七歲符合條件。(A) 未滿十八歲；(C)(D) 都超過三十歲。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Why did Ya-ting move to a farm town in Queensland?", "options": ["Rent in the farm town was cheaper than in Melbourne.", "She could not find any job in the city.", "She wanted to learn how to grow her own food.", "Working there allowed her to stay in Australia for a second year."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：she decided to stay a second year. To do this, she had to work for about three months in certain jobs in regional Australia, such as on farms. 她是為了能多留一年才去農場工作。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 第二段說她一個月後已在餐廳找到洗碗的工作。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "The word \"regional\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["away from the big cities", "near the beach", "full of tourists", "close to the airport"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 in regional Australia, such as on farms，接著說 she left the city for a small farm town，可知 regional 指「大城市以外的地區、地方」。(B)(C)(D) 在文中都找不到依據。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about being \"paid by the bucket\"?", "options": ["Every worker gets the same amount of money each day.", "Workers have to buy their own buckets before they start.", "The more fruit a worker picks, the more money he or she earns.", "It is the best way for new workers to make money."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說按桶計酬時 Fast pickers did well, but slow pickers like Ya-ting earned very little，可推知收入取決於採收的數量，採得越多賺得越多。(A) 若每人收入相同，就不會有快慢的差別；(B) 文中未提及；(D) 雅婷剛開始採得慢、賺得很少，與此相反。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "What is Ya-ting's attitude toward working holidays?", "options": ["She thinks they are a waste of time for young people.", "She thinks they are valuable but require careful preparation.", "She believes they are the fastest way to become rich.", "She feels they are only suitable for people with perfect English."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段雅婷說那一年 taught her more than any class，但也提醒 a working holiday is not right for everyone，需要 a plan, some savings and the courage to ask for help；第五段更說那是職涯的起點，可見她認為打工度假很有價值，但需要好好準備。(A) 與她的正面評價相反；(C) 她明確說打工度假並不是輕鬆致富的方法（It isn't）；(D) 她自己的英文一開始也不完美。故選 (B)"}]}, {"id": "a83", "title": "More Than Selling Snacks: Behind the Convenience Store Counter", "title_zh": "不只是賣零食：便利商店櫃檯後的工作", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Society"], "passage": "At 6:55 a.m., Huang Po-wei puts on his uniform and steps behind the counter of a convenience store in New Taipei. In the next eight hours, he will do much more than sell drinks and rice balls. \"People think this job is easy,\" says the 19-year-old, who works full-time and takes classes in the evening. \"Then they watch me for ten minutes and change their minds.\"\n\nTaiwan has more than 13,000 convenience stores, one of the highest numbers per person in the world. They are popular because they offer so many services. Customers can pay electricity and phone bills, pick up packages they ordered online, send parcels, print documents, add money to their transit cards and buy train or concert tickets. Each service has its own steps, and the clerk has to know all of them. During his first month, Po-wei carried a small notebook full of instructions in his pocket.\n\nThe morning rush is the hardest part. Office workers want coffee, students want breakfast, and everyone is in a hurry. In just a few minutes, Po-wei may heat a sandwich, make three lattes, scan a pile of bills and search the back room for a customer's package. He also has to check that no food on the shelves is out of date. Because he must handle so many tasks at once, his manager says the most important skill is not speed but staying calm.\n\nNot every customer is kind. Some shout when the line is long, and others blame the clerk for problems with online shops. Po-wei has learned to listen first and then explain clearly. \"Usually, people are angry at the situation, not at me,\" he says. On the other hand, many regular customers greet him by name, and one elderly man brings him a bag of oranges every Lunar New Year.\n\nPo-wei does not plan to stay at the counter forever. He is studying logistics, the business of moving goods from one place to another, and hopes to manage a delivery center someday. He believes the store has prepared him well. \"Here I learned how packages travel, how to deal with people and how to solve problems fast,\" he says. \"That's a lot to learn from a job people call simple.\"", "passage_zh": "早上六點五十五分，黃柏維穿上制服，走進新北市一家便利商店的櫃檯後方。接下來的八個小時，他要做的事遠遠不只是賣飲料和飯糰。這位十九歲的年輕人白天做全職工作，晚上去上課。他說：「大家都以為這份工作很簡單，但只要看我做十分鐘，他們就會改變想法。」\n\n臺灣有超過一萬三千家便利商店，以人口比例來說是全世界最多的地區之一。便利商店受歡迎，是因為它們提供非常多種服務。顧客可以繳電費和電話費、領取網購的包裹、寄送包裹、列印文件、替交通卡加值，還能買火車票或演唱會門票。每一種服務都有自己的操作步驟，店員必須全部都會。剛上班的第一個月，柏維口袋裡總是放著一本寫滿操作說明的小筆記本。\n\n早上的尖峰時段是最辛苦的。上班族要買咖啡，學生要買早餐，而且每個人都在趕時間。短短幾分鐘內，柏維可能要加熱一份三明治、做三杯拿鐵、刷一疊帳單，還要到後面的倉庫找顧客的包裹。他也必須檢查貨架上的食物有沒有過期。因為他必須同時處理這麼多工作，店長說最重要的能力不是速度，而是保持冷靜。\n\n並不是每位顧客都很客氣。有些人在排隊很長時大吼大叫，也有人把網路商店的問題怪到店員頭上。柏維學會先傾聽，再清楚地說明。他說：「通常大家氣的是當下的狀況，而不是我。」另一方面，許多常客會叫他的名字跟他打招呼，還有一位老先生每年農曆新年都會帶一袋橘子給他。\n\n柏維並不打算一直待在櫃檯後面。他正在學習物流，也就是把貨物從一個地方運送到另一個地方的行業，希望將來能管理一個配送中心。他認為這家店讓他做好了準備。他說：「我在這裡學到包裹是怎麼運送的、怎麼跟人相處，還有怎麼快速解決問題。能從一份大家口中『很簡單』的工作學到這麼多，收穫真的不少。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of this passage?", "options": ["To show that working in a convenience store is harder than many people think", "To explain how to order products from online shops", "To advertise a new convenience store in New Taipei", "To compare convenience stores in Taiwan with those in other countries"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段柏維說 People think this job is easy... Then they watch me for ten minutes and change their minds，接著各段描述店員要會的各種服務、尖峰時段的忙碌和面對顧客的壓力，目的在說明這份工作比一般人想的困難。(B) 文中未教人網購；(C) 不是廣告；(D) 第二段只提到臺灣便利商店數量多，並沒有和其他國家詳細比較。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "Which of the following services is NOT mentioned in the passage?", "options": ["Paying phone bills", "Sending parcels", "Adding money to transit cards", "Opening a bank account"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段列出的服務有 pay electricity and phone bills、pick up packages、send parcels、print documents、add money to their transit cards、buy train or concert tickets，並沒有提到開銀行帳戶。(A)(B)(C) 都在第二段中出現。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "According to Po-wei's manager, what is the most important skill for the job?", "options": ["Working very fast", "Making good coffee", "Staying calm", "Remembering customers' names"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段最後一句：his manager says the most important skill is not speed but staying calm. 店長認為最重要的是保持冷靜，而不是速度，所以 (A) 正好相反；(B)(D) 雖然是工作中的細節，但店長沒有說它們最重要。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "The phrase \"regular customers\" in paragraph 4 refers to customers who ______.", "options": ["always pay with cash", "come to the store often", "buy only ordinary products", "shout when the line is long"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段 many regular customers greet him by name，這些客人會叫出柏維的名字，表示他們常常來店裡、彼此熟悉，regular customers 指「常客」。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 是同段前面提到的不友善顧客，並非常客的意思。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "What does Po-wei mean when he says, \"Usually, people are angry at the situation, not at me\"?", "options": ["He thinks most customers are rude people.", "He believes the store should hire more clerks.", "He does not take customers' anger personally.", "He wants angry customers to complain to his manager."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段說有些顧客會因排隊太久大吼，或把網路商店的問題怪到店員頭上，柏維卻認為大家氣的是「狀況」而不是他本人，表示他不把顧客的怒氣當成針對自己，因此能先傾聽再說明。(A) 他並沒有批評顧客；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)"}]}, {"id": "a84", "title": "Gold Medals for Skilled Hands: The WorldSkills Competition", "title_zh": "給巧手的金牌：國際技能競賽", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Career", "Education"], "passage": "Imagine cutting and coloring hair while a team of judges watches your every move and a clock counts down above your head. One wrong cut could cost you a medal. For young people at the WorldSkills Competition, this is not a bad dream. It is the chance of a lifetime.\n\nWorldSkills is sometimes called the \"Olympics of skills.\" It began in Spain in 1950 as a contest between young workers from Spain and Portugal. Today, it is held every two years in a different city, and more than eighty countries and regions are members. Competitors take part in over fifty skills, from cooking, bricklaying and car painting to web design and robotics. Most competitors must be 22 years old or younger.\n\nKao Jun-hao, a hairdressing student from a vocational high school in Tainan, was one of them. To win a place on his national team, he first had to do well in Taiwan's own skills competitions. Then came more than a year of training. He practiced on mannequin heads, the plastic heads with real hair used in hairdressing schools, for up to ten hours a day. \"My fingers hurt, and some days I wanted to quit,\" he says. \"But every haircut made me a little faster.\"\n\nAt the competition, judges from different countries give points for precision, creativity and safety. A haircut that is only two millimeters too short or a few minutes too late can lose valuable points. Besides gold, silver and bronze medals, competitors who reach a very high international standard receive a Medallion for Excellence. Jun-hao did not win gold, but he came home with a Medallion and great pride.\n\nSupporters say the competition sends an important message: skilled workers are just as valuable as office workers. Many countries need more electricians, mechanics and chefs, and WorldSkills helps young people see these jobs as careers to be proud of. Today, Jun-hao works in a busy salon in Kaohsiung and trains younger students in his free time. \"A medal lasts forever,\" he says, \"but skills are something you use every single day.\"", "passage_zh": "想像一下：你正在剪髮、染髮，一群評審盯著你的每個動作，頭頂上的時鐘還在倒數計時。只要剪錯一刀，就可能與獎牌擦身而過。對國際技能競賽的年輕選手來說，這不是一場惡夢，而是一生難得的機會。\n\n國際技能競賽有時被稱為「技能奧運」。它於 1950 年在西班牙開始，當時是西班牙和葡萄牙年輕勞工之間的比賽。如今這項競賽每兩年在不同的城市舉辦一次，會員國家和地區超過八十個。選手們參加的職類超過五十種，從烹飪、砌磚、汽車噴漆，到網頁設計和機器人都有。大多數選手的年齡必須在二十二歲以下。\n\n來自臺南一所高職、主修美髮的高俊豪就是其中一員。為了進入國家代表隊，他必須先在臺灣本身的技能競賽中取得好成績，接著是一年多的訓練。他在假人頭上練習，也就是美髮學校使用、裝有真髮的塑膠頭模，一天最多練習十個小時。他說：「我的手指好痛，有些日子真的很想放棄。但每剪一次頭髮，我就變得更快一點。」\n\n比賽中，來自不同國家的評審會依照精準度、創意和安全來評分。剪出來的頭髮只要短了兩公釐，或晚了幾分鐘完成，都可能失去寶貴的分數。除了金、銀、銅牌之外，達到極高國際水準的選手還能獲得「優勝獎」。俊豪雖然沒有拿到金牌，卻帶著優勝獎章和滿滿的驕傲回國。\n\n支持者認為，這項競賽傳達了一個重要的訊息：技術人員和辦公室上班族一樣有價值。許多國家都需要更多的電工、技師和廚師，而國際技能競賽幫助年輕人把這些工作看成值得驕傲的職業。現在，俊豪在高雄一家生意興隆的髮廊工作，空閒時還會指導學弟妹。他說：「獎牌可以永遠保存，但技能是你每天都會用到的東西。」", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How to become a famous hairdresser in Kaohsiung", "An international contest that celebrates young people's job skills", "The history of sports competitions between Spain and Portugal", "Why office jobs are better than skilled jobs"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文介紹國際技能競賽的由來與規則（第二、四段），以高俊豪為例說明選手的訓練（第三段），最後說明這項比賽讓人看見技術工作的價值（第五段）。(A) 俊豪在高雄工作只是細節；(C) 西班牙和葡萄牙只是比賽的起源，而且比的是技能不是運動；(D) 與第五段「技術人員和辦公室上班族一樣有價值」不符。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "According to the passage, when and where did WorldSkills begin?", "options": ["In Portugal, in 1950", "In Taiwan, after the first national skills competition", "In Spain, in 1950", "In a different city every two years"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：It began in Spain in 1950 as a contest between young workers from Spain and Portugal. 比賽於 1950 年在西班牙開始。(A) 葡萄牙只是當年參賽的國家之一；(B) 文中未提及；(D) 每兩年換城市舉辦是現在的情況，不是起源。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "Based on the passage, which of the following people could most likely compete at WorldSkills?", "options": ["A 21-year-old car painting student", "A 30-year-old chef who owns a restaurant", "A 45-year-old electrician with many years of experience", "A 26-year-old office worker who likes robots"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Most competitors must be 22 years old or younger. 大多數選手必須在二十二歲以下，而 car painting 也是第二段列出的比賽職類之一，所以二十一歲學汽車噴漆的學生最可能參賽。(B)(C)(D) 年齡都超過二十二歲。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The word \"precision\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["speed", "price", "kindness", "exactness"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段 judges... give points for precision 後面接著說 A haircut that is only two millimeters too short... can lose valuable points，只差兩公釐都會扣分，可知 precision 指「精確、精準」，與 exactness 最接近。(A) 速度是同句後半「晚幾分鐘」才相關，並非 precision 的意思；(B)(C) 與上下文無關。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the Medallion for Excellence?", "options": ["It allows competitors without a gold, silver or bronze medal to be honored for their high level.", "It is given to every competitor who finishes all the tasks in the competition.", "It is only for older competitors who have worked in their skill for years.", "It is worth more than a gold medal and is given to only one person."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說除了金、銀、銅牌之外，competitors who reach a very high international standard receive a Medallion for Excellence，而俊豪沒拿到金牌仍獲得此獎，可推知沒拿到前三名的選手只要達到很高水準，也能獲得肯定。(B) 必須達到很高的國際標準，並非人人都有；(C) 文中未提及，且選手大多在二十二歲以下；(D) 文中沒有說它比金牌更有價值或只頒給一人。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a85", "title": "Four Days a Week: Can a Shorter Work Week Really Work?", "title_zh": "一週只上四天班：縮短工時真的可行嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Business", "Society"], "passage": "Imagine working only four days a week and still getting the same pay. In many countries, including Taiwan, the normal work week is five days, or about forty hours. But in recent years, some companies and governments have started to ask a surprising question: what if people could do the same amount of work in less time?\n\nOne of the best-known experiments took place in Iceland. Between 2015 and 2019, about 2,500 workers in offices, preschools, hospitals and other public workplaces cut their hours from 40 to 35 or 36 a week, with no cut in pay. Researchers found that in most workplaces, the amount and quality of work stayed the same or even improved. Workers said they felt less stressed and had more time for their families and hobbies.\n\nIn 2022, a trial took place in the United Kingdom. Sixty-one companies, with about 2,900 workers, tried a four-day week for six months. The trial followed the \"100-80-100\" model: workers receive 100 percent of their pay for 80 percent of the time, as long as they keep 100 percent of their output. To make this possible, many teams shortened meetings and found ways to waste less time. At the end of the trial, 56 of the 61 companies decided to continue the shorter week.\n\nHowever, a four-day week is not easy for every job. A restaurant, a hospital or a bus company cannot simply close for an extra day, so these workplaces need more staff or careful planning. Some workers in the trials also felt pressure to fit five days of work into four, which made their workdays more tiring. Critics point out another problem: the companies in the trials chose to join, so they may have been more likely to succeed than an average company.\n\nWill the four-day week become normal one day? Nobody knows yet. But the experiments have changed the way many people think about work. Perhaps the most important question is not how many hours people work, but what they achieve in those hours, and whether they have enough energy left for the rest of their lives.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，一週只上四天班，卻還能領到一樣的薪水。在許多國家，包括臺灣，正常的工作週是五天，也就是大約四十個小時。但近幾年來，有些公司和政府開始提出一個令人意外的問題：如果人們能用更少的時間完成同樣多的工作，那會怎麼樣？\n\n最有名的實驗之一發生在冰島。2015 年到 2019 年間，大約 2,500 名在辦公室、幼兒園、醫院和其他公家單位工作的員工，把每週工時從 40 小時減少到 35 或 36 小時，而且薪水沒有減少。研究人員發現，在大多數的工作場所，工作的數量和品質都維持不變，甚至還有進步。員工們表示，他們覺得壓力變小了，也有更多時間陪伴家人和從事嗜好。\n\n2022 年，英國也進行了一項試驗。六十一家公司、大約 2,900 名員工試行了六個月的四天工作週。這項試驗採用「100-80-100」模式：只要員工維持百分之百的工作產出，就能用百分之八十的工作時間，領取百分之百的薪水。為了做到這一點，許多團隊縮短了會議時間，並設法減少浪費的時間。試驗結束時，六十一家公司中有五十六家決定繼續實施較短的工作週。\n\n然而，四天工作週並不適用於每一種工作。餐廳、醫院或公車公司不能說多休一天就多休一天，所以這些工作場所需要更多人手或仔細的安排。在試驗中，也有些員工感到壓力，因為他們必須把五天的工作塞進四天裡完成，讓每個工作天變得更累。批評者還指出另一個問題：參加試驗的公司都是自己選擇加入的，所以它們可能比一般公司更容易成功。\n\n四天工作週有一天會成為常態嗎？目前還沒有人知道。不過，這些實驗已經改變了許多人對工作的看法。也許最重要的問題不在於人們工作了多少小時，而在於他們在這些時間裡完成了什麼，以及他們是否還留有足夠的精力過生活中其他的部分。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why Taiwan should change its work week to four days right away", "How workers in Iceland spend their free time with their families", "What experiments with shorter work weeks found, and the problems they may face", "Why hospitals and restaurants need more workers than other companies"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二、三段介紹冰島和英國縮短工時試驗的結果，第四段說明四天工作週的困難與批評，第五段則反思工作的意義。(A) 文中只提到臺灣一般每週工作四十小時，並未主張臺灣立刻改變；(B) 只是第二段的細節；(D) 醫院、餐廳只是第四段舉的例子。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "In the Iceland experiment, how many hours a week did the workers work after the change?", "options": ["35 or 36 hours", "About 32 hours", "40 hours", "Four days of 10 hours"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：cut their hours from 40 to 35 or 36 a week，冰島的員工把每週工時從 40 小時減為 35 或 36 小時。(C) 40 小時是減少之前的工時；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "In the \"100-80-100\" model, what does the number \"80\" refer to?", "options": ["The percentage of pay that workers receive", "The number of companies in the trial", "The percentage of output that workers must keep", "The percentage of the usual working time"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：workers receive 100 percent of their pay for 80 percent of the time, as long as they keep 100 percent of their output，80 指的是一般工作時間的百分之八十。(A) 薪水是 100%；(C) 產出也必須維持 100%；(B) 參加試驗的公司有 61 家。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why might the results of the trials look better than they really are?", "options": ["Many workers did not tell the truth to the researchers.", "The companies that took part chose to join, so they may have been more likely to succeed.", "Most companies in the trials were restaurants and bus companies.", "The trials lasted only one week, which was too short."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段最後一句：the companies in the trials chose to join, so they may have been more likely to succeed than an average company. 參加的公司是自願加入的，本來就可能比一般公司更容易成功，所以結果可能看起來比較好。(A) 文中未提及；(C) 餐廳、公車公司是用來說明「不易實施」的例子，並未說它們參加試驗；(D) 英國試驗為期六個月，冰島更長達數年。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "The word \"output\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the amount of work that is produced", "the money that a company spends", "the time a worker spends in meetings", "the number of days in a work week"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 as long as they keep 100 percent of their output，指員工即使工時減少，也要維持原本的「工作成果、產出」，接著說團隊縮短會議、減少浪費時間，就是為了完成同樣多的工作，故 output 指產出的工作量。(B)(C)(D) 都不是員工必須維持的「工作成果」。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a86", "title": "The Glue That Didn't Stick: How the Post-it Note Was Born", "title_zh": "黏不牢的膠水：便利貼的誕生", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Business", "Science"], "passage": "In 1968, a scientist named Spencer Silver was working in a laboratory at 3M, an American company. He was trying to make a very strong glue for building airplanes. Instead, he made something strange: a glue that was sticky but weak. It could hold a piece of paper in place, yet the paper could be pulled off easily without tearing and then stuck down again. Nobody could think of a use for it, so for years this \"failed\" glue just sat on the shelf.\n\nSilver did not give up. He kept telling people at 3M about it, and one of them was Art Fry, another scientist there. Fry sang in his church choir every week, and he had a small but annoying problem. He marked the songs in his songbook with little pieces of paper, but they kept falling out. In 1974, he had an idea: what if he put some of Silver's glue on the paper? The bookmark would stay in place without damaging the pages.\n\nFry soon realized that his sticky bookmark could do much more. One day, he stuck one with a question on it to a report for his boss, and his boss wrote an answer on the same little note. At that moment, Fry understood that the sticky paper was not just a bookmark. It was a new way to share short messages.\n\nEven so, success did not come quickly. When 3M first tested the product in four cities in 1977, few people bought it, because they did not understand what it was for. So in 1978, the company gave away free samples in Boise, a city in the state of Idaho. Once people had tried the notes, most of them wanted to buy more. In 1980, the product went on sale across the United States as the Post-it Note.\n\nEven the famous yellow color was an accident. When the team was making early samples, the lab next door happened to have only yellow scrap paper, so that is what they used. Today, the notes come in many colors and sizes and are used all over the world. Their story reminds us that a \"mistake\" is sometimes just an idea waiting for the right problem.", "passage_zh": "1968 年，一位名叫史賓塞・席佛的科學家在美國 3M 公司的實驗室工作。他原本想做出一種非常強力、可以用來製造飛機的膠水，結果卻做出了一種奇怪的東西：一種有黏性、但黏力很弱的膠水。它能把一張紙固定在原位，但紙可以輕易撕下而不會破掉，之後還能再黏回去。沒有人想得出它能拿來做什麼，所以好幾年來，這個「失敗」的膠水就一直被擱在架子上。\n\n席佛並沒有放棄。他不斷向 3M 的同事介紹這項發明，其中一位聽眾是同樣在那裡工作的科學家亞特・傅萊。傅萊每個星期都在教會的唱詩班唱歌，而他有一個小小卻惱人的問題：他用小紙片在歌本裡標記要唱的歌，但紙片老是掉出來。1974 年，他想到一個點子：如果在紙片上塗一些席佛的膠水呢？這樣書籤就能固定不動，又不會損壞書頁。\n\n傅萊很快就發現，他的黏性書籤能做的事遠不止於此。有一天，他把一張寫了問題的書籤貼在要交給上司的報告上，上司就在同一張小紙條上寫下了回答。就在那一刻，傅萊明白這種會黏的紙不只是書籤，而是一種分享簡短訊息的新方法。\n\n即使如此，成功並沒有很快到來。1977 年 3M 第一次在四個城市試賣這項產品時，買的人很少，因為大家不明白它是做什麼用的。於是在 1978 年，公司在愛達荷州的波夕市免費發送樣品。人們一旦試用過，大多數都想再多買一些。1980 年，這項產品以「Post-it 便利貼」的名稱在全美上市。\n\n就連那著名的黃色也是意外。團隊在製作早期樣品時，隔壁實驗室剛好只有黃色的廢紙，於是他們就用了黃色。如今，便利貼有各種顏色和尺寸，全世界都在使用。它的故事提醒我們：一個「錯誤」有時只是一個還在等待合適問題出現的點子。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why 3M stopped making strong glue for airplanes.", "How a weak glue that seemed useless became a popular product.", "How Art Fry became a famous church singer.", "Why yellow is the most popular color for office products."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從第一段席佛做出黏力很弱、看似失敗的膠水，到傅萊想到用途、3M 推廣上市，說明便利貼如何誕生。(A) 文中沒說 3M 停止製造強力膠；(C) 唱詩班只是傅萊想到點子的背景；(D) 第五段只說黃色是意外，並未說它是最受歡迎的顏色。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Why did Art Fry want a new kind of bookmark?", "options": ["His boss asked him to design one for a report.", "His songbook was too heavy to carry to church.", "He wanted to sell bookmarks to the people in his choir.", "The paper bookmarks in his songbook kept falling out."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：He marked the songs in his songbook with little pieces of paper, but they kept falling out. 他用來標記的紙片老是從歌本掉出來。(A) 上司是第三段他用便條紙詢問的對象，並沒有要他設計書籤；(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What happened after 3M gave away free samples in Boise in 1978?", "options": ["Most people who tried the notes wanted to buy more.", "People still did not understand what the notes were for.", "3M decided to stop selling the product in other cities.", "The company changed the color of the notes to yellow."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Once people had tried the notes, most of them wanted to buy more. 試用過的人大多想再買。(B) 是 1977 年四個城市試賣時的情形；(C) 與 1980 年在全美上市的事實相反；(D) 第五段說黃色是製作早期樣品時就用了，並非此時才改。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The word \"annoying\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["exciting", "dangerous", "irritating", "expensive"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段 a small but annoying problem 指紙片一直掉出來這個「小小卻惱人」的問題，annoying（令人煩惱的）與 irritating（令人惱火的）意思最接近。(A) 令人興奮的、(B) 危險的、(D) 昂貴的，都不符合紙片掉落這種小麻煩的語意。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "What lesson does the writer want readers to learn from the story?", "options": ["Scientists should always follow their original plans.", "A failure may become useful when it meets the right problem.", "New products sell well as soon as they are put in stores.", "Free samples are the only way to make people buy something."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段最後一句：a \"mistake\" is sometimes just an idea waiting for the right problem，看似錯誤的發明遇上合適的問題就能變得有用。(A) 席佛的膠水正是偏離原計畫的成果；(C) 與第四段「成功沒有很快到來」相反；(D) 說法太絕對，文中並未這樣主張。故選 (B)"}]}, {"id": "a87", "title": "Six Dots That Opened a World: Louis Braille", "title_zh": "打開世界的六個點：路易・布萊爾", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Education", "History"], "passage": "Every day, millions of blind people around the world read books, check medicine labels and find the right elevator button by touching small raised dots. This reading system is called braille. Surprisingly, it was invented by a teenager.\n\nLouis Braille was born in 1809 in a small village near Paris, France. His father made leather goods such as saddles for horses. When Louis was three, he was playing with a sharp tool in his father's workshop when it slipped and hurt one of his eyes. The wound became infected, and the infection soon spread to his other eye. By the age of five, he was completely blind.\n\nLouis was a bright child, and at ten he won a place at a special school for blind children in Paris. The school had a few books with large raised letters, but they were heavy, and reading them letter by letter was very slow. Then, in 1821, a former army captain named Charles Barbier visited the school. He had created \"night writing,\" a system of raised dots that let soldiers read messages in the dark without using a light. However, his system used up to twelve dots for each sound, so each symbol was too large to feel with a single fingertip.\n\nLouis saw the problem and set to work. By 1824, when he was only fifteen, he had created a much simpler system. Each letter was made from a \"cell\" of just six dots, arranged in two columns of three. Because one cell could be felt with a single touch, readers could move their fingers quickly along a line. Later, Louis also used the dots to write numbers and music.\n\nHowever, his system was not accepted right away. Some sighted teachers preferred the old raised letters, and for a time the new system was even banned at the school. But Louis, who became a teacher there himself, and his students kept using it. He died in 1852, at the age of 43. Two years later, the school finally made braille its official system.\n\nToday braille is used in many languages, including Chinese. Louis Braille's life shows that the people who face a problem are often the best ones to solve it.", "passage_zh": "每天，世界各地有數百萬名視障者靠著觸摸小小的凸點來讀書、確認藥品標籤，以及找到正確的電梯按鈕。這種閱讀系統叫做點字。令人驚訝的是，它是一位青少年發明的。\n\n路易・布萊爾 1809 年出生於法國巴黎附近的一個小村莊。他的父親製作馬鞍等皮革製品。路易三歲時，在父親的工作坊裡玩一件尖銳的工具，工具一滑，傷到了他的一隻眼睛。傷口受到感染，感染很快又擴散到另一隻眼睛。到了五歲，他已經完全失明。\n\n路易是個聰明的孩子，十歲時獲得巴黎一所盲童學校的入學資格。學校裡有幾本印著大型凸起字母的書，但這些書很重，而且一個字母一個字母地摸讀非常緩慢。後來，在 1821 年，一位名叫查爾斯・巴比耶的前陸軍上尉來到學校參訪。他發明了一種「夜間書寫」，是用凸點組成的系統，讓士兵不必點燈也能在黑暗中讀取訊息。不過，他的系統每個聲音最多要用到十二個點，因此每個符號都太大，無法用一個指尖一次摸完。\n\n路易看出了問題所在，便開始動手研究。到了 1824 年，年僅十五歲的他已經創造出一套簡單許多的系統。每個字母都由一個只有六個點的「點字格」組成，排成兩直行、每行三個點。因為一格只要指尖一碰就能摸出來，讀者的手指可以沿著一行字快速移動。後來，路易也用這些點來書寫數字和樂譜。\n\n然而，他的系統並沒有立刻被接受。有些看得見的老師比較喜歡舊式的凸起字母，這套新系統甚至一度在學校裡被禁止使用。但後來也在這所學校任教的路易和他的學生們，仍然繼續使用它。他在 1852 年過世，享年 43 歲。兩年後，學校終於將點字定為正式使用的系統。\n\n如今，點字已運用於包括中文在內的許多語言。路易・布萊爾的一生告訴我們：面對問題的人，往往正是最適合解決問題的人。", "questions": [{"q": "How did Louis Braille lose his sight?", "options": ["He was born blind in a small village near Paris.", "He became sick after reading in the dark for too long.", "An accident with a sharp tool led to an infection that spread to both eyes.", "He was hurt by a soldier while visiting an army camp."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：他三歲時在父親的工作坊玩尖銳工具，傷到一隻眼睛，The wound became infected, and the infection soon spread to his other eye. 傷口感染後擴散到另一隻眼睛，五歲時完全失明。(A) 他並非天生失明；(B)(D) 文中沒有提到。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "Why did Charles Barbier create \"night writing\"?", "options": ["To let soldiers read messages in the dark without a light.", "To help blind children learn to write music.", "To make books with large raised letters lighter.", "To teach Louis Braille how to read and write."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：a system of raised dots that let soldiers read messages in the dark without using a light，夜間書寫是讓士兵不必點燈就能在黑暗中讀取訊息。(B) 用點字寫樂譜是布萊爾後來做的事；(C) 文中沒有提到；(D) 巴比耶只是來學校參訪，並非為了教布萊爾而發明。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "Why was Louis Braille's system easier to use than Barbier's?", "options": ["It used large raised letters instead of dots.", "It was written on lighter and cheaper paper.", "It used twelve dots so that each sound was clearer.", "Each cell had only six dots and could be felt with one touch."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Each letter was made from a \"cell\" of just six dots... Because one cell could be felt with a single touch, readers could move their fingers quickly，六點一格、一摸就能讀出，所以讀得快。(A) 大型凸起字母是舊式書本的做法；(B) 文中未提及；(C) 十二點正是巴比耶系統的缺點。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "The word \"banned\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["praised", "not allowed", "taught", "made easier"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段說有些老師偏好舊式字母，新系統 was even banned at the school，但學生仍繼續使用，可知 banned 是「被禁止」，與 not allowed 意思最接近。(A) 受到稱讚、(C) 被教授、(D) 被簡化，都與「沒有立刻被接受」的上下文不符。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "What does the writer most likely want to show in the last paragraph?", "options": ["People who face a problem themselves often understand best how to solve it.", "Braille is now used only in European languages.", "Blind students should not try to invent new things.", "Louis Braille became rich because of his invention."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段：the people who face a problem are often the best ones to solve it，布萊爾本身是視障者，最了解問題並解決了它。(B) 與「包括中文在內的許多語言」相反；(C) 與文意相反；(D) 文中沒有提到他因此致富。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a88", "title": "The Doctor Who Told Doctors to Wash Their Hands", "title_zh": "要醫生洗手的醫生", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "History"], "passage": "Today, washing your hands seems like the most basic rule of good health. But less than 200 years ago, even doctors did not think it was important. One of the first people to show that clean hands could save lives was a Hungarian doctor named Ignaz Semmelweis. His story is both inspiring and sad.\n\nIn 1846, Semmelweis began working at a large hospital in Vienna, Austria. The hospital had two clinics where women gave birth, and something about them puzzled him. In the first clinic, where babies were delivered by doctors and medical students, about one in ten mothers died of a terrible illness called childbed fever. In the second clinic, which was run by midwives, women trained to help with births, far fewer mothers died. Some women even begged to be sent to the second clinic.\n\nIn 1847, a doctor friend of Semmelweis was cut by a student's knife while examining a dead body. He soon became sick and died, and his illness looked just like childbed fever. Semmelweis then noticed something important. The doctors and students often came straight from examining dead bodies to help women give birth, but the midwives never did this. Perhaps the doctors were carrying something deadly on their hands.\n\nAt that time, nobody knew about germs. Still, Semmelweis ordered every doctor and student to wash their hands in a chlorine solution before examining patients. The result was amazing. Within months, deaths in the first clinic fell sharply, to about one or two mothers in a hundred.\n\nYou might think other doctors thanked him. Instead, many of them were offended. They did not like the idea that they themselves were causing their patients' deaths. Semmelweis was also slow to publish his results, and he often argued angrily with people who disagreed. He lost his job in Vienna and later returned to Hungary. He died in 1865, at only 47, before his idea was widely accepted.\n\nIn the years that followed, scientists such as Louis Pasteur and Joseph Lister showed that tiny germs cause many diseases. The world finally understood that Semmelweis had been right. Today, he is remembered as a pioneer of handwashing, a simple habit that still protects millions of people every day.", "passage_zh": "今天，洗手似乎是維持健康最基本的規則。但在不到兩百年前，就連醫生也不認為洗手很重要。最早證明乾淨的雙手能拯救生命的人之一，是一位名叫伊格納茲・塞麥爾維斯的匈牙利醫生。他的故事既激勵人心，又令人難過。\n\n1846 年，塞麥爾維斯開始在奧地利維也納的一家大醫院工作。這家醫院有兩間讓婦女生產的產科病房，其中有件事讓他百思不解。在第一病房，接生的是醫生和醫學生，大約每十位產婦就有一位死於一種叫做「產褥熱」的可怕疾病。而在由助產士（受過訓練、協助生產的女性）負責的第二病房，死亡的產婦卻少得多。有些婦女甚至哀求院方把她們送到第二病房。\n\n1847 年，塞麥爾維斯的一位醫生朋友在檢查遺體時，被學生的刀子割傷。他很快就病倒並過世了，而他的病症看起來和產褥熱一模一樣。塞麥爾維斯這時注意到一件重要的事：醫生和學生常常剛檢查完遺體，就直接去幫婦女接生，但助產士從來不做這種事。也許醫生的手上帶著某種致命的東西。\n\n當時沒有人知道細菌的存在。儘管如此，塞麥爾維斯還是要求每一位醫生和學生在檢查病人之前，都要用含氯的溶液洗手。結果令人驚訝：短短幾個月內，第一病房的死亡人數大幅下降，降到大約每一百位產婦中只有一到兩位死亡。\n\n你也許以為其他醫生會感謝他。相反地，許多人覺得受到冒犯。他們不喜歡「是自己害死了病人」這種說法。塞麥爾維斯也遲遲沒有發表研究結果，還常常和意見不同的人激烈爭吵。他失去了在維也納的工作，後來回到匈牙利。他在 1865 年過世，年僅 47 歲，生前沒能看到自己的觀念被普遍接受。\n\n在接下來的幾年裡，巴斯德和李斯特等科學家證明了許多疾病是由微小的細菌引起的。世人終於明白塞麥爾維斯是對的。如今，他被視為推廣洗手的先驅，而洗手這個簡單的習慣，至今每天仍保護著數百萬人。", "questions": [{"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did some women beg to be sent to the second clinic?", "options": ["The second clinic was closer to their homes.", "The doctors in the second clinic were more famous.", "The second clinic was cleaner and more comfortable.", "Far fewer mothers died in the second clinic."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：In the second clinic... far fewer mothers died. Some women even begged to be sent to the second clinic. 第二病房死亡的產婦少得多，所以婦女哀求被送去那裡。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 第二病房是由助產士負責，不是醫生。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What made Semmelweis think that the doctors' hands might be the problem?", "options": ["The midwives told him that the doctors never washed their hands.", "His friend died of a similar illness after being cut while examining a dead body.", "He found germs on the doctors' hands with a microscope.", "Louis Pasteur wrote him a letter about germs."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：他的朋友在檢查遺體時被刀割傷，病逝時的症狀 looked just like childbed fever，他因此聯想到醫生剛摸過遺體就去接生。(A) 文中沒有提到；(C) 第四段說 At that time, nobody knew about germs，當時沒人知道細菌；(D) 最後一段說巴斯德等人是在後來才證明細菌致病，文中也沒有提到巴斯德寫信給他。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "The word \"offended\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["grateful", "confused", "upset", "frightened"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：You might think other doctors thanked him. Instead, many of them were offended，Instead 表示與「感謝」相反，且他們不喜歡「自己害死病人」的說法，可知 offended 是「被冒犯而不高興的」，與 upset 意思最接近。(A) 感激的與文意相反；(B) 困惑的、(D) 害怕的都不符合上下文。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 5, why did many doctors refuse to accept Semmelweis's idea?", "options": ["They did not want to believe that they were causing their patients' deaths.", "Washing their hands in chlorine took too much time and money.", "The number of deaths in the first clinic did not change.", "Semmelweis had already lost his job in Vienna."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段：They did not like the idea that they themselves were causing their patients' deaths. 醫生們不願接受自己就是病人死亡的原因。(B) 文中未提及；(C) 與第四段死亡人數大幅下降相反；(D) 他失去工作是被反對之後的結果，不是原因。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward Semmelweis?", "options": ["Critical, because he caused the deaths of many mothers.", "Doubtful, because his results were never proven.", "Respectful, and sorry that he was not recognized in his lifetime.", "Amused, because his idea about handwashing seems silly today."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段說他的故事 both inspiring and sad，最後一段稱他為 a pioneer of handwashing（洗手的先驅），並惋惜他生前觀念未被接受，可見作者對他是尊敬且同情的。(A) 害死產婦的是當時不洗手的做法，並非他；(B) 最後一段說世人證明他是對的；(D) 作者並未覺得洗手的觀念可笑。故選 (C)"}]}, {"id": "a89", "title": "Light from the Wind: William Kamkwamba's Windmill", "title_zh": "風帶來的光：威廉・坎寬巴的風車", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "Education"], "passage": "In 2001, a terrible drought hit Malawi, a small country in southeastern Africa. With almost no rain, the corn died in the fields, and millions of people went hungry. William Kamkwamba, a 14-year-old boy from a farming family, ate just one small meal a day. Worse, his parents could no longer pay his school fees, so he had to leave school.\n\nBut William did not want to stop learning. He began visiting a small library at a nearby primary school. His English was not very good, so he studied the pictures and diagrams in the science books carefully. One book, called Using Energy, had a picture of a windmill on its cover. William learned that wind could turn blades, and that turning blades could produce electricity. He thought, \"If I build a windmill, my family can have light at night.\"\n\nThere was no money to buy parts, so William searched a scrapyard, a place where old and broken machines are thrown away. He collected a broken bicycle, the fan from an old tractor, plastic pipes and a small generator from a bicycle light. For the tower, he cut down tall, straight trees. Many villagers laughed at him and called him crazy as he carried home piles of junk.\n\nIn 2002, William finally finished his windmill. When the wind blew, the blades turned, and a small light bulb began to glow. Soon his home had electric lights and a working radio, things very few families in his village had. Later, he built more windmills, including one that pumped water for his family's fields.\n\nNews of the boy who brought power to his village spread. Journalists wrote about him, and he was invited to tell his story at international meetings. With help from supporters, William returned to school and later graduated from Dartmouth College in the United States in 2014. His story became a book and a film, both called The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind, meaning the boy who caught the wind's power and put it to work.\n\nIn one of his talks, William gave this advice to young people facing hard times: \"Trust yourself and believe. Whatever happens, don't give up.\"", "passage_zh": "2001 年，一場嚴重的乾旱襲擊了非洲東南部的小國馬拉威。由於幾乎沒有下雨，田裡的玉米都枯死了，數百萬人因此挨餓。十四歲的威廉・坎寬巴出身農家，每天只吃得到一小餐。更糟的是，他的父母再也付不起他的學費，他只好離開學校。\n\n但威廉不想停止學習。他開始到附近一所小學的小圖書館看書。他的英文不太好，所以他仔細研究科學書裡的圖片和圖解。其中一本叫做《使用能源》的書，封面上有一座風車的圖片。威廉從書中得知，風能轉動葉片，而轉動的葉片能產生電力。他心想：「如果我能做出一座風車，我們家晚上就能有燈光了。」\n\n因為沒有錢買零件，威廉就到廢料場去找，那是丟棄老舊、損壞機器的地方。他收集了一輛壞掉的腳踏車、一台舊曳引機的風扇、幾根塑膠管，以及腳踏車車燈用的小型發電機。至於塔架，他砍下又高又直的樹木來搭建。他把一堆堆破銅爛鐵搬回家時，許多村民都嘲笑他，說他瘋了。\n\n2002 年，威廉終於完成了他的風車。風一吹，葉片就轉動，一個小燈泡亮了起來。很快地，他家就有了電燈和能用的收音機，這些都是村裡很少人家擁有的東西。後來，他又做了更多風車，其中一座能抽水灌溉他家的田地。\n\n這個男孩為村子帶來電力的消息很快傳開了。記者報導他的故事，他也受邀到國際會議上分享經歷。在支持者的幫助下，威廉重返校園，後來在 2014 年從美國的達特茅斯學院畢業。他的故事被寫成書，也拍成電影，名稱都叫《馭風男孩》（The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind），意思是捕捉風的力量、讓它為人所用的男孩。\n\n在一次演講中，威廉給正面臨困境的年輕人這樣的建議：「相信自己，懷抱信念。無論發生什麼事，都不要放棄。」", "questions": [{"q": "Why did William Kamkwamba have to leave school?", "options": ["His family could not afford his school fees after the drought.", "He wanted to spend all his time building windmills.", "His school was closed because there was no electricity.", "His English was too poor for him to pass his classes."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段：旱災造成饑荒，his parents could no longer pay his school fees, so he had to leave school，父母付不起學費。(B) 他是離開學校之後才開始研究風車；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 第二段雖說他英文不好，但那不是他離開學校的原因。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "How did William learn about windmills even though his English was not very good?", "options": ["A teacher at the primary school explained the science to him.", "He watched videos about windmills on the Internet.", "He carefully studied the pictures and diagrams in library books.", "Villagers who had built windmills before taught him."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：His English was not very good, so he studied the pictures and diagrams in the science books carefully. 他靠仔細研究圖書館書中的圖片和圖解來學習。(A)(B) 文中沒有提到；(D) 第三段說村民嘲笑他，並沒有教他。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "Which of the following did William NOT use to build his first windmill?", "options": ["The fan from an old tractor", "A car engine", "Plastic pipes", "A broken bicycle"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段列出他用的材料：a broken bicycle, the fan from an old tractor, plastic pipes and a small generator from a bicycle light，另外用樹木做塔架，其中並沒有汽車引擎。(A)(C)(D) 都是文中提到的材料。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "In paragraph 5, the word \"harnessed\" in the book title The Boy Who Harnessed the Wind is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["was afraid of", "ran faster than", "complained about", "controlled and used"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段在書名後解釋 meaning the boy who caught the wind's power and put it to work，即捕捉風的力量並加以利用，所以 harnessed 是「控制並利用」，選 controlled and used。(A) 害怕、(B) 跑得比……快、(C) 抱怨，都不符合他用風力發電的故事。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What is the main purpose of this passage?", "options": ["To explain how farmers in Malawi grow corn.", "To warn readers about the dangers of collecting junk.", "To compare different ways of producing electricity.", "To show how a boy's curiosity and hard work changed his life and village."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文敘述威廉在饑荒中失學，卻靠自學和不放棄的精神做出風車，為家鄉帶來電力，最後重返校園，旨在展現好奇心與努力如何改變他和村子。(A) 玉米只是第一段的背景；(B) 文中沒有警告撿廢料的危險；(C) 文中只談風力，沒有比較發電方式。故選 (D)"}]}, {"id": "a90", "title": "The Vegetable Seller Who Gave Away Millions", "title_zh": "捐出千萬的賣菜阿嬤", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Society", "Money"], "passage": "In 2010, the American magazine Time published its yearly list of the 100 most influential people in the world. The list included presidents, business leaders and movie stars. It also included a woman who sold vegetables at a market in Taitung, in eastern Taiwan. Her name was Chen Shu-chu.\n\nChen was born in 1950 into a poor family. When she was thirteen, her mother became seriously ill, but the family could not pay the hospital in time, and her mother died. After finishing elementary school, Chen gave up her studies to help her father at the family's vegetable stall. For decades after that, she worked at the market almost every day, from early in the morning until evening.\n\nChen lived a very simple life. She ate plain meals, wore simple clothes and rarely bought anything for herself. Her vegetables were cheap, so her profit on each sale was only a few dollars. Still, she saved carefully, one coin at a time. Over the years, these small amounts slowly added up.\n\nThen she did something that surprised many people: she gave most of her savings away. She donated money to help poor children and to support a children's home. She also gave NT$4.5 million to her old elementary school to build a new library, so that the children there could enjoy reading. In total, she donated more than NT$10 million, an amazing amount for someone who earned so little each day.\n\nChen never wanted to be famous. She seemed uncomfortable with all the attention, and she did not think she had done anything special. When people asked why she gave so much, her answer was simple: money is only useful when it is given to people who need it. Her story shows that you do not have to be rich to make a difference. Small amounts, saved patiently and shared kindly, can change many lives.", "passage_zh": "2010 年，美國《時代》雜誌公布了年度「全球百大最具影響力人物」名單。名單上有總統、企業領袖和電影明星，也有一位在臺灣東部臺東的市場賣菜的婦人。她的名字是陳樹菊。\n\n陳樹菊 1950 年出生於一個貧窮的家庭。她十三歲時，母親生了重病，但家裡沒辦法及時付錢給醫院，母親因此過世。小學畢業後，陳樹菊放棄升學，到家裡的菜攤幫父親的忙。此後的幾十年，她幾乎每天都在市場工作，從清晨一直忙到傍晚。\n\n陳樹菊過著非常簡樸的生活。她吃得簡單，穿著樸素，很少為自己買任何東西。她的菜賣得很便宜，所以每筆生意只賺得到幾塊錢。即使如此，她仍然一塊錢一塊錢地仔細存起來。多年下來，這些小錢慢慢累積成一大筆錢。\n\n接著，她做了一件讓許多人驚訝的事：她把大部分的積蓄都捐了出去。她捐錢幫助貧困的兒童，也支持一所兒童之家。她還捐了新臺幣 450 萬元給她就讀過的小學興建新的圖書館，讓那裡的孩子能享受閱讀。她總共捐出了超過新臺幣一千萬元，對一位每天收入那麼少的人來說，這是一筆驚人的數目。\n\n陳樹菊從來不想出名。面對這麼多關注，她似乎很不自在，也不認為自己做了什麼特別的事。當別人問她為什麼捐這麼多錢時，她的回答很簡單：錢要給需要的人才有用。她的故事告訴我們，不必是有錢人，也能帶來改變。耐心存下、真心分享的小錢，能改變許多人的生命。", "questions": [{"q": "Why was it surprising that Chen Shu-chu was on Time's list in 2010?", "options": ["She had never left Taiwan before.", "She was an ordinary vegetable seller, not a famous or powerful person.", "She was the youngest person on the list.", "She had worked for the magazine as a reporter."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說名單上多是總統、企業領袖和電影明星，It also included a woman who sold vegetables at a market in Taitung，一位市場賣菜的婦人出現在名單上，與其他名人形成對比。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "What happened when Chen was thirteen?", "options": ["Her mother died after the family could not pay the hospital in time.", "She opened her own vegetable stall in Taipei.", "She won a prize for being the best student in her school.", "Her father gave her money to build a library."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：When she was thirteen, her mother became seriously ill, but the family could not pay the hospital in time, and her mother died. (B) 她是在臺東幫父親顧菜攤，不是在臺北自己開店；(C) 文中未提及；(D) 捐錢蓋圖書館的是她自己，且是多年以後的事。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "How was Chen able to donate so much money?", "options": ["She sold her vegetables at very high prices.", "She received a large amount of money from Time magazine.", "She lived simply and saved small amounts over many years.", "Her old elementary school helped her raise the money."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段：她生活簡樸、很少為自己買東西，she saved carefully, one coin at a time. Over the years, these small amounts slowly added up. (A) 與「她的菜賣得很便宜」相反；(B) 文中沒說雜誌給她錢；(D) 是她捐錢給母校，而不是學校幫她籌錢。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "The word \"profit\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["the price that customers pay for vegetables", "the number of customers she has each day", "the money she gives to other people", "the money she earns after paying her costs"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Her vegetables were cheap, so her profit on each sale was only a few dollars. 菜價便宜，所以每筆生意「賺到的錢」只有幾塊，profit 指扣除成本後的利潤。(A) 是售價而非利潤；(B) 是顧客人數；(C) 是捐款，與這句的語意不合。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What is the main message of the passage?", "options": ["Selling vegetables is the best way to become rich.", "You do not need to be rich to make a difference in other people's lives.", "Famous magazines only choose people who want attention.", "Children should leave school early to help their families."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：Her story shows that you do not have to be rich to make a difference. 全文以陳樹菊省吃儉用、捐出積蓄的故事，說明不必富有也能幫助別人。(A) 文中沒有這種說法；(C) 與第五段「她從來不想出名」相反；(D) 她失學是因家境所迫，作者並沒有鼓勵學生輟學。故選 (B)"}]}, {"id": "a91", "title": "The Little Ramp That Helps Everyone", "title_zh": "幫助每個人的小斜坡", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Society", "History"], "passage": "The next time you pull a suitcase down the street or push a baby stroller, look down at the corner of the sidewalk. You will probably see a gentle slope that connects the sidewalk to the road. This simple design is called a curb cut. Most people never notice it, but it has a surprising history.\n\nFor a long time, most sidewalks ended in a sharp step, called a curb. For people in wheelchairs, even a curb about fifteen centimeters high was like a wall. Without help, they could not cross the street. After World War II, many soldiers returned home with serious injuries, and a few American cities, such as Kalamazoo, Michigan, built small ramps for them. However, such ramps remained rare for many years.\n\nThings began to change in the early 1970s in Berkeley, California. A group of disabled students and activists there, including Ed Roberts, wanted to live independent lives. They wanted to go to class, go shopping and visit friends on their own. They asked the city again and again for curb cuts. According to a famous story, some of them grew tired of waiting. One night, they went out with hammers and cement and made rough ramps themselves. Soon after, Berkeley began building official curb cuts at street corners.\n\nThen something unexpected happened. The ramps were made for wheelchair users, but almost everyone started to use them. Parents with strollers, travelers with heavy suitcases, delivery workers with carts, cyclists and older people who walked slowly all found that the ramps made life easier. Today, laws in the United States and many other places require ramps like these on new sidewalks.\n\nThis is now called the \"curb-cut effect\": when something is designed to help one group of people, it often ends up helping many others too. You can see the same effect in other places. Subtitles were first created for viewers who cannot hear well, but today many people turn them on when they watch videos on a noisy bus. A small change for a few can become a big change for all.", "passage_zh": "下次你在街上拖著行李箱或推著嬰兒車時，不妨低頭看看人行道的轉角。你很可能會看到一道和緩的斜坡，把人行道和馬路連接起來。這種簡單的設計叫做「緣石坡道」。大多數人從來沒注意過它，但它有一段令人意想不到的歷史。\n\n很長一段時間，大部分的人行道邊緣都是一道陡直的台階，叫做「緣石」。對坐輪椅的人來說，即使只有大約十五公分高的緣石，也像一道牆。沒有別人幫忙，他們就無法過馬路。第二次世界大戰後，許多士兵身負重傷返鄉，於是美國有少數城市，例如密西根州的卡拉馬祖，為他們建造了小斜坡。不過，這樣的斜坡在之後許多年裡仍然很少見。\n\n情況在 1970 年代初期的加州柏克萊開始改變。當地有一群身心障礙學生和社運人士，包括艾德・羅伯茲在內，希望過獨立自主的生活。他們想自己去上課、去購物、去拜訪朋友。他們一次又一次地要求市政府設置緣石坡道。根據一個著名的故事，其中有些人等得不耐煩了，某天晚上，他們帶著鐵鎚和水泥出門，自己做出了粗糙的斜坡。不久之後，柏克萊便開始在街角興建正式的緣石坡道。\n\n接著，意想不到的事情發生了。這些斜坡原本是為輪椅使用者設計的，結果幾乎每個人都開始使用。推嬰兒車的父母、拖著沉重行李箱的旅客、推著推車的送貨員、騎腳踏車的人，以及走路緩慢的長者，都發現斜坡讓生活變得更方便。如今，美國和許多其他地方的法律，都規定新建的人行道必須設置這樣的斜坡。\n\n這種現象現在被稱為「緣石坡道效應」（curb-cut effect）：為了幫助某一群人而做的設計，最後往往也幫助了其他許多人。在其他地方也能看到同樣的效應。字幕最初是為聽力不好的觀眾而設計，但現在許多人在嘈雜的公車上看影片時也會打開字幕。為少數人做的小改變，可以成為造福所有人的大改變。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why sidewalks in the United States are higher than those in other countries.", "How soldiers in World War II built roads in American cities.", "How a design made for wheelchair users ended up helping many other people.", "Why people should not use hammers and cement on city streets."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文介紹緣石坡道的歷史：原本為輪椅使用者設計，後來推嬰兒車的父母、旅客等人都受惠，並由此說明「緣石坡道效應」（curb-cut effect）。(A) 文中沒有比較各國人行道高度；(B) 士兵是斜坡的受益者，並非建路的人；(D) 只是第三段故事的細節，並非主旨。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why did Kalamazoo build small ramps after World War II?", "options": ["To make the city more beautiful for visitors.", "To help delivery workers move their carts.", "To make it easier for children to ride bicycles.", "To help soldiers who came home with serious injuries."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：After World War II, many soldiers returned home with serious injuries, and a few American cities, such as Kalamazoo, Michigan, built small ramps for them. 斜坡是為受傷返鄉的士兵而建。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 送貨員是第四段提到後來受惠的人，並非當初建造的原因。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the activists in Berkeley who made their own ramps?", "options": ["They were tired of the city's slow action and decided to do something themselves.", "They were workers hired by the city to build ramps.", "They wanted to stop people from using the sidewalks.", "They did not really need ramps to cross the street."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：他們一再要求市政府設置斜坡，some of them grew tired of waiting，於是晚上自己帶著鐵鎚和水泥做斜坡，可推知他們對市政府行動緩慢不耐煩，決定自己動手。(B) 他們是學生和社運人士，不是市府僱用的工人；(C) 與他們想方便出門的目的相反；(D) 他們正是需要斜坡的身心障礙者。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "The word \"independent\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["busy and tiring", "not needing help from others", "rich and comfortable", "living far away from the city"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：wanted to live independent lives. They wanted to go to class, go shopping and visit friends on their own. 後一句說明他們想「靠自己」去上課、購物、拜訪朋友，所以 independent 是「獨立、不需依靠別人」。(A) 忙碌疲累、(C) 富裕舒適、(D) 住在遠離城市的地方，都不符合上下文。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Which of the following is the best example of the \"curb-cut effect\"?", "options": ["A school builds a new gym that only its basketball team can use.", "A phone company makes an expensive phone just for business people.", "Large, clear signs made for older people also help tourists find their way.", "A city closes a road so that only buses can drive on it."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段定義 curb-cut effect：when something is designed to help one group of people, it often ends up helping many others too。為長者設計的大型清楚標示也幫助了觀光客，正符合「為某群人設計，結果幫助更多人」。(A)(B)(D) 都只服務特定的一群人，並沒有讓其他人也受惠。故選 (C)"}]}, {"id": "a92", "title": "Clearing the Way: Mary Anderson and the Windshield Wiper", "title_zh": "看清前方的路：瑪麗・安德森與雨刷", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Technology", "History"], "passage": "On a snowy winter day in 1902, a woman from Alabama named Mary Anderson was visiting New York City. She was riding a streetcar, a kind of electric train that runs on rails along the street. As snow and ice built up on the front window, she noticed that the driver could hardly see. Again and again, he had to open the window or stop the streetcar to clean the glass by hand. Cold air blew in on the passengers, and the trip was slow and uncomfortable.\n\nMost passengers just complained, but Anderson started thinking. Why couldn't the driver clean the window from inside? Back home, she drew a simple design: a rubber blade on the outside of the window, connected to a handle inside. By moving the handle, the driver could make the blade swing back and forth across the glass, pushing away the rain and snow. When the weather was good, the device could be taken off.\n\nIn 1903, Anderson received a patent for her invention. A patent is an official document that says an invention belongs to you, so that others cannot copy it for a number of years. She then tried to sell the rights to a company in Canada, but the company turned her down. It said the device was not useful enough to make money. Some people even worried that the moving blade would distract drivers and cause accidents.\n\nAnderson's patent ran out in 1920. By then, cars had become much more common, and drivers needed to see clearly in all kinds of weather. Companies began to make windshield wipers based on similar ideas, and soon wipers became a normal part of every car. Sadly, because her patent had ended, Anderson never earned any money from her idea.\n\nToday, windshield wipers can be found on almost every car, bus and train. In 2011, more than a hundred years after that snowy streetcar ride, Mary Anderson was added to the National Inventors Hall of Fame in the United States. Her story shows that great ideas often come from paying attention to everyday problems that other people simply accept.", "passage_zh": "1902 年一個下雪的冬日，一位來自阿拉巴馬州、名叫瑪麗・安德森的女士正在紐約市旅遊。她搭乘一輛路面電車，也就是一種沿著街道上的軌道行駛的電動列車。隨著冰雪在前方的車窗上越積越多，她注意到司機幾乎看不見路。司機一次又一次地打開車窗，或是停下電車，用手清理玻璃。冷風灌進來吹在乘客身上，這趟車程既緩慢又難受。\n\n大部分乘客只是抱怨，安德森卻開始思考：為什麼司機不能在車內清理車窗呢？回家後，她畫出了一個簡單的設計：車窗外有一片橡膠刮片，連接到車內的一根把手。司機只要移動把手，就能讓刮片在玻璃上來回擺動，把雨水和雪推開。天氣好的時候，這個裝置還可以拆下來。\n\n1903 年，安德森為她的發明取得了專利。專利是一種官方文件，證明某項發明屬於你，讓別人在一定的年限內不能抄襲。之後，她試著把這項權利賣給加拿大的一家公司，但對方拒絕了她，說這個裝置不夠實用，賺不了錢。有些人甚至擔心，移動的刮片會讓司機分心而造成事故。\n\n安德森的專利在 1920 年到期。那時汽車已經普遍得多，駕駛人在各種天氣下都需要看清楚前方。各家公司開始根據類似的構想製造雨刷，雨刷很快就成了每輛車的基本配備。遺憾的是，由於專利已經到期，安德森從來沒有因為她的點子賺到任何錢。\n\n如今，幾乎每一輛汽車、公車和火車上都看得到雨刷。2011 年，在那趟下雪天的電車之旅一百多年後，瑪麗・安德森被列入美國國家發明家名人堂。她的故事告訴我們，偉大的點子往往來自留意那些別人只是默默接受的日常問題。", "questions": [{"q": "What problem did Mary Anderson notice on the streetcar?", "options": ["The streetcar was too crowded for all the passengers.", "The heater on the streetcar was not working.", "The streetcar driver did not know the way in New York.", "The driver could hardly see because snow and ice covered the window."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段：As snow and ice built up on the front window, she noticed that the driver could hardly see. 冰雪堆積在車窗上，司機幾乎看不見路。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(B) 文中說冷風吹進來，是因為司機開窗清玻璃，並非暖氣故障。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how did Anderson's device work?", "options": ["It used hot air to melt the snow on the window.", "The driver moved a handle inside to swing a rubber blade outside.", "A passenger climbed out to clean the window with a brush.", "It worked by itself whenever it started to rain."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：a rubber blade on the outside of the window, connected to a handle inside. By moving the handle, the driver could make the blade swing back and forth，司機在車內移動把手，讓車窗外的橡膠刮片來回擺動。(A) 文中未提到熱風；(C) 文中清理車窗的是司機，而且是用手，並沒有乘客爬出去用刷子清理，這也不是她的裝置；(D) 她的裝置需要司機手動操作，不是自動的。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "The phrase \"turned her down\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["refused her offer", "paid her a lot of money", "helped her improve the design", "asked her to work for them"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：the company turned her down. It said the device was not useful enough to make money. 公司認為這個裝置賺不了錢，可知 turned her down 是「拒絕她」，與 refused her offer 意思最接近。(B)(C)(D) 都是接受或合作的意思，與上下文相反。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "Why did Anderson never earn money from windshield wipers?", "options": ["She gave her idea to a car company for free.", "Her design did not work in heavy snow.", "By the time wipers became popular, her patent had already ended.", "The Canadian company copied her design and sold it secretly."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：她的專利在 1920 年到期，那時汽車才普遍、雨刷才被大量生產，because her patent had ended, Anderson never earned any money from her idea. (A) 她曾想賣掉權利，並非免費送出；(B) 文中沒說設計在大雪中無效；(D) 加拿大公司是拒絕購買，文中沒說對方抄襲。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "What lesson does the writer most likely want readers to learn?", "options": ["Paying attention to everyday problems can lead to useful inventions.", "It is dangerous to ride a streetcar in winter.", "Inventors should always sell their ideas to big companies.", "A good invention will always make its inventor rich."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段：great ideas often come from paying attention to everyday problems that other people simply accept. 留意日常生活中的問題能帶來實用的發明。(B) 只是故事背景；(C) 文中沒有這樣的建議；(D) 與安德森沒有因雨刷賺到錢的事實相反。故選 (A)"}]}, {"id": "a93", "title": "Would You Take the Stairs If They Played Music?", "title_zh": "如果樓梯會彈奏音樂，你會走樓梯嗎？", "level": "Reading · B1", "tags": ["Health", "Society"], "passage": "Everyone knows that walking up stairs is good exercise. Yet when people can choose between the stairs and an escalator, most of them stand on the escalator. Simply telling people to \"be healthier\" usually does not work. So in 2009, a team in Stockholm, Sweden, tried a different idea: what if taking the stairs was just more fun?\n\nThe experiment took place at Odenplan, a busy subway station where a staircase ran right next to an escalator. One night, workers covered each step with black and white panels, so the stairs looked like the keys of a giant piano. Under the steps, they placed sensors, small devices that can notice pressure or movement, and connected them to speakers. When someone stepped on a stair, it played a musical note.\n\nThe next morning, commuters on their way to work were surprised. Some stopped and smiled. Others jumped up and down the steps to play simple tunes, and parents walked up and down with their children again and again. According to the team, 66 percent more people than usual chose the stairs instead of the escalator.\n\nThe piano stairs were part of a project called \"The Fun Theory,\" created for the car company Volkswagen as a kind of advertisement. The idea behind it was that fun is one of the easiest ways to change people's behavior. The project also included a \"bottle bank arcade,\" where people got points like in a video game for recycling glass bottles, and \"the world's deepest trash can,\" which made a long falling sound whenever someone threw rubbish into it.\n\nOf course, the experiment had its limits. It was not a careful scientific study, and the excitement might disappear once people got used to the music. Still, the idea has spread, and piano stairs have since appeared in cities around the world. The lesson is useful for anyone who wants to build a better habit: make it more enjoyable. Study with a friend, play music while you clean your room, or turn your exercise into a game. Small changes like these can make good choices feel easy.", "passage_zh": "大家都知道爬樓梯是很好的運動。然而，當人們可以在樓梯和手扶梯之間選擇時，大多數人還是會站上手扶梯。光是叫大家「要更健康」通常沒什麼效果。所以在 2009 年，瑞典斯德哥爾摩的一個團隊嘗試了一個不同的點子：如果走樓梯變得更好玩呢？\n\n這項實驗在歐登廣場站（Odenplan）進行，那是一個繁忙的捷運站，站內有一座樓梯就緊鄰著手扶梯。某天晚上，工作人員在每一階樓梯貼上黑白相間的面板，讓整座樓梯看起來就像一架巨型鋼琴的琴鍵。他們在階梯底下裝了感應器（一種能偵測壓力或動作的小型裝置），並把它們連接到喇叭。只要有人踩上一階樓梯，它就會發出一個音符。\n\n隔天早上，趕著上班的通勤族都很驚訝。有些人停下腳步露出微笑；有些人在階梯上跳上跳下，彈奏簡單的曲子；還有父母帶著孩子一次又一次地上下走著。根據團隊的說法，選擇走樓梯而不搭手扶梯的人比平常多了百分之六十六。\n\n鋼琴樓梯是一個名為「樂趣理論」計畫的一部分，這項計畫是為福斯汽車公司打造的一種廣告。它背後的想法是：樂趣是改變人們行為最簡單的方法之一。這項計畫還包括一台「回收瓶電玩機」，人們回收玻璃瓶時能像玩電玩一樣得分；以及「世界上最深的垃圾桶」，每當有人把垃圾丟進去，它就會發出一段長長的墜落聲。\n\n當然，這項實驗也有它的限制。它並不是嚴謹的科學研究，而且等人們聽慣了音樂，新鮮感可能就會消失。儘管如此，這個點子已經傳播開來，此後世界各地的城市都出現了鋼琴樓梯。對任何想培養好習慣的人來說，這個啟示都很有用：讓它變得更有趣。和朋友一起讀書、打掃房間時放點音樂，或是把運動變成一場遊戲。像這樣的小改變，能讓好的選擇變得輕而易舉。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Making a healthy choice more fun can change what people do.", "Escalators in Stockholm are often too crowded to use.", "Volkswagen built a new subway station in Sweden.", "Piano lessons are a good way for people to get exercise."], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文以斯德哥爾摩的鋼琴樓梯為例，說明 fun is one of the easiest ways to change people's behavior，讓健康的選擇變有趣就能改變人的行為。(B) 文中未提到手扶梯太擠；(C) 福斯只是這個廣告計畫的委託者，並未建造車站；(D) 文中說的是走樓梯，不是學鋼琴。故選 (A)"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happened when someone stepped on a stair?", "options": ["The escalator next to it stopped moving.", "A light on the stair turned on.", "The stair played a musical note.", "A video started on a screen in the station."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段最後一句：When someone stepped on a stair, it played a musical note. 踩上樓梯就會發出一個音符。(A)(B)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (C)"}, {"q": "According to the team, how did the piano stairs change people's behavior?", "options": ["66 percent of the passengers stopped using the subway.", "People spent 66 minutes playing music on the stairs.", "The escalator was used 66 percent more than before.", "66 percent more people than usual chose the stairs over the escalator."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：According to the team, 66 percent more people than usual chose the stairs instead of the escalator. 選擇走樓梯的人比平常多了百分之六十六。(A)(B) 與數字的意義不符；(C) 正好相反，是走樓梯的人變多，不是手扶梯。故選 (D)"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"the experiment had its limits\" in paragraph 5?", "options": ["The stairs could only be used by a few people at a time.", "The results were not scientifically proven and might not last.", "The music was too loud for people at the station.", "The project cost Volkswagen too much money."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段接著說明限制：It was not a careful scientific study, and the excitement might disappear once people got used to the music. 這不是嚴謹的科學研究，效果也可能在人們習慣後消失。(A)(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (B)"}, {"q": "Based on the passage, which advice would the writer most likely give a student who finds studying boring?", "options": ["Stop studying until it feels interesting again.", "Study only on the escalator on the way to school.", "Ask teachers to give less homework.", "Find a way to make studying more enjoyable, such as studying with a friend."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：The lesson is useful for anyone who wants to build a better habit: make it more enjoyable. Study with a friend... 作者主張讓好習慣變得有趣，例如和朋友一起讀書。(A) 停止讀書不是建立好習慣；(B) 文中沒有這種建議；(C) 與文中「讓事情變有趣」的做法無關。故選 (D)"}]}, {"id": "a94", "title": "More Than a Lucky Accident: The Long Road to Penicillin", "title_zh": "不只是幸運的意外：盤尼西林的漫長之路", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "History"], "passage": "In September 1928, the Scottish scientist Alexander Fleming returned to his London laboratory after a summer holiday and noticed something strange. On a glass dish he had left behind, a blue-green mold had begun to grow, and around it the bacteria he had been studying had died. This moment is often called one of the luckiest accidents in scientific history. Yet the full story of penicillin is far longer and more complicated, and it involves many more people than Fleming alone.\n\nFleming deserves credit for recognizing the importance of what he saw. Many researchers would have thrown the spoiled dish away, but he grew the mold, found that it released a substance capable of killing several kinds of harmful bacteria, and named it penicillin. However, he struggled to separate the substance from the liquid in which the mold grew, and the small amounts he produced were unstable and quickly lost their power. After publishing his results in 1929, he turned to other projects, and for nearly a decade his discovery attracted little attention.\n\nThe breakthrough came at the University of Oxford, where a team led by Howard Florey and Ernst Chain began studying penicillin in 1939. Their colleague Norman Heatley designed clever equipment to extract and purify the drug, at times using milk containers and hospital bedpans. In 1940, mice infected with deadly bacteria survived after being treated, while untreated mice died. Early the next year, the team first used it to treat a person, a police officer with a severe infection. He began to improve within a day, but the supply ran out before he was cured, and he died. The case showed that the drug worked; the real problem was producing enough of it.\n\nWith Britain at war, its factories could not make the drug on a large scale, so Florey traveled to the United States for help. Scientists in Peoria, Illinois, developed ways to grow the mold in huge tanks, and a moldy melon from a local market turned out to contain a strain that produced far more penicillin than Fleming's original mold. By the D-Day landings in June 1944, there was enough penicillin to treat wounded Allied soldiers. In 1945, Fleming, Florey and Chain shared the Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.\n\nThe history of penicillin suggests that great discoveries rarely depend on a single moment of genius. The French scientist Louis Pasteur once said that chance favors only the prepared mind, but turning a lucky observation into a life-saving medicine also requires patience, teamwork and money. Fleming himself warned that bacteria could become resistant if the drug was misused. Today, antibiotic resistance is a serious threat to global health, which is why doctors urge patients to use antibiotics only when necessary.", "passage_zh": "1928 年 9 月，蘇格蘭科學家亞歷山大‧弗萊明放完暑假回到倫敦的實驗室，注意到一件奇怪的事。在他離開前留下的一個玻璃培養皿上，長出了一團藍綠色的黴菌，而黴菌周圍他原本在研究的細菌都死了。這一刻常被稱為科學史上最幸運的意外之一。然而，盤尼西林完整的故事要漫長、複雜得多，參與其中的人也遠遠不只弗萊明一個。\n\n弗萊明確實值得肯定，因為他看出了眼前現象的重要性。許多研究人員可能會直接把這個被汙染的培養皿丟掉，但他把黴菌培養起來，發現它會釋放出一種能殺死好幾種有害細菌的物質，並將它命名為盤尼西林。然而，他很難把這種物質從培養黴菌的液體中分離出來，而且他製造出的少量盤尼西林並不穩定，很快就會失去效力。1929 年發表研究結果之後，他便轉向其他研究計畫，接下來將近十年，他的發現幾乎沒有受到關注。\n\n突破出現在牛津大學。1939 年，由霍華德‧弗洛里與恩斯特‧錢恩帶領的團隊開始研究盤尼西林。他們的同事諾曼‧希特利設計了巧妙的設備來萃取並純化這種藥物，有時甚至用上了牛奶容器和醫院的便盆。1940 年，感染致命細菌的老鼠在接受治療後存活下來，而沒有接受治療的老鼠都死了。隔年年初，團隊第一次用它來治療病人，對象是一名嚴重感染的警察。他在一天之內就開始好轉，但藥物在他痊癒之前就用完了，最後他還是過世了。這個病例證明這種藥有效；真正的問題在於如何製造出足夠的數量。\n\n當時英國正處於戰爭之中，國內的工廠無法大量生產這種藥，於是弗洛里前往美國尋求協助。伊利諾州皮奧里亞的科學家發展出在巨大槽桶中培養黴菌的方法，而且從當地市場買來的一顆發霉的甜瓜，竟然含有一種菌株，產出的盤尼西林比弗萊明原本的黴菌多得多。到了 1944 年 6 月諾曼第登陸時，已經有足夠的盤尼西林可以治療受傷的盟軍士兵。1945 年，弗萊明、弗洛里和錢恩共同獲得諾貝爾生理學或醫學獎。\n\n盤尼西林的歷史顯示，偉大的發現很少只靠某個天才的靈光一閃。法國科學家路易‧巴斯德曾說，機會只眷顧有準備的人；但要把一次幸運的觀察變成能救命的藥物，還需要耐心、團隊合作和資金。弗萊明本人就曾警告，如果這種藥被濫用，細菌可能會產生抗藥性。如今，抗生素抗藥性已成為全球健康的嚴重威脅，這也是為什麼醫生呼籲病人只在必要時才使用抗生素。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Fleming's discovery alone turned penicillin into a famous medicine.", "Penicillin became a useful drug only through the long efforts of many people.", "Antibiotics should no longer be used because bacteria have become resistant.", "The Second World War stopped scientists from doing medical research."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段末句指出盤尼西林的故事 far longer and more complicated，且 involves many more people than Fleming alone；第二到四段依序說明弗萊明、牛津團隊與美國科學家的貢獻，第五段也總結偉大發現需要 patience, teamwork and money，故主旨是 (B)。(A) 與第一段末句相反；(C) 第五段只說要在必要時才用抗生素，並非不再使用；(D) 戰爭反而促使英美合作量產，文中未說研究被停止。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what problem did Fleming have with penicillin?", "options": ["He could not make it pure and stable enough to be useful.", "He found that it killed only one kind of bacteria.", "His laboratory refused to let him publish his results.", "The mold died soon after he started to grow it."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：he struggled to separate the substance from the liquid... and the small amounts he produced were unstable and quickly lost their power，意即他無法把盤尼西林分離純化，產物又不穩定，故選 (A)。(B) 與同段 killing several kinds of harmful bacteria 相反；(C) 他在 1929 年已發表結果；(D) 文中說他成功培養了黴菌，並未說黴菌死掉。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention the police officer in paragraph 3?", "options": ["To prove that penicillin was too dangerous to give to humans", "To explain why the Oxford team stopped studying penicillin", "To show that doctors in Oxford made a serious medical mistake", "To show that penicillin worked but could not yet be made in large amounts"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說這位警察 began to improve within a day，但 the supply ran out before he was cured，接著直接點出 The case showed that the drug worked; the real problem was producing enough of it，作者舉此例是要說明藥物有效但產量不足，故選 (D)。(A) 他用藥後是好轉的，並非藥物危險；(B) 團隊沒有停止研究，第四段弗洛里還到美國尋求量產；(C) 他死亡是因為藥用完，文中未指醫療疏失。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"strain\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["pressure", "injury", "type", "container"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段 a moldy melon... turned out to contain a strain that produced far more penicillin than Fleming's original mold，此處 strain 指黴菌的「菌株、品系」，也就是某一種類，與 type 最接近，故選 (C)。strain 雖也有「壓力」(A)、「拉傷」(B) 的意思，但不符合本句談黴菌的語境；(D) container 是第三段提到的牛奶容器，與 strain 無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE according to the passage?", "options": ["The Oxford team tested penicillin on mice before giving it to a human patient.", "Fleming planned his 1928 experiment carefully in order to find penicillin.", "Penicillin was first produced in huge amounts in British factories.", "Fleming received the 1945 Nobel Prize by himself."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段先說 In 1940, mice infected with deadly bacteria survived after being treated，接著 Early the next year 才第一次用來治療病人，故 (A) 正確。(B) 第一段說這是一場 accident，並非事先計畫；(C) 第四段說英國工廠無法大量生產，是在美國發展出大槽培養法；(D) 第四段說諾貝爾獎由弗萊明、弗洛里和錢恩三人共享。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a95", "title": "The Planet Found with a Pen: How Mathematics Discovered Neptune", "title_zh": "用筆找到的行星：數學如何發現海王星", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "History"], "passage": "Most planets in our solar system were found in the obvious way: someone looked up and noticed a point of light moving slowly against the stars. Neptune is different. Before anyone knowingly saw it, its position had already been worked out on paper, which is why a French scientist of the time said it had been discovered \"with the point of a pen.\"\n\nThe story begins with Uranus, which William Herschel discovered in 1781. Using Isaac Newton's laws of gravity, astronomers calculated the path Uranus should follow around the Sun. Over the following decades, however, the planet refused to stay on schedule. Sometimes it was slightly ahead of its predicted position, and sometimes it fell behind. There seemed to be two likely explanations: either Newton's laws did not work at such great distances, or an unseen object was pulling Uranus off course.\n\nIn 1845, the French mathematician Urbain Le Verrier decided to test the second idea. Working backward from the tiny irregularities in Uranus's motion, he calculated where an unknown planet would have to be in order to cause them. He then sent his prediction to Johann Galle at the Berlin Observatory. Galle received the letter on September 23, 1846, and searched that very night. In less than an hour, he and his assistant found a faint object about one degree from the spot Le Verrier had named. It was Neptune.\n\nThe discovery soon led to an argument. In England, a young Cambridge mathematician, John Couch Adams, had made similar calculations, but British astronomers had been slow to act on them. For a time, Britain and France argued over who deserved the honor. Strangely, Neptune had been seen long before: Galileo recorded it in his notes in 1612 and 1613, but he mistook it for a star.\n\nThe same method, however, does not always succeed. Le Verrier later noticed that Mercury's orbit did not quite match Newton's predictions either, so he proposed another hidden planet, called Vulcan, circling even closer to the Sun. Several observers claimed to have spotted it, but Vulcan does not exist. The real answer came in 1915, when Albert Einstein's general theory of relativity showed that Newton's laws need a small correction near a massive object like the Sun. This time, it was the theory, not the sky, that was missing something.\n\nAstronomers still reason this way today. Some believe that the strange orbits of distant icy objects beyond Neptune may be caused by an undiscovered \"Planet Nine,\" although no one has found it yet. The story of Neptune reminds us that mathematics can point scientists in the right direction, but only careful observation can show whether a prediction is correct.", "passage_zh": "太陽系中大多數的行星都是用最直接的方式發現的：有人抬頭望向天空，注意到一個光點在群星之間緩緩移動。海王星卻不一樣。在任何人有意識地看見它之前，它的位置就已經在紙上被算出來了，這也是為什麼當時一位法國科學家說，它是「用筆尖」發現的。\n\n故事要從天王星說起，它是威廉‧赫歇爾在 1781 年發現的。天文學家運用牛頓的萬有引力定律，算出天王星繞行太陽應該走的路徑。然而在接下來的幾十年裡，這顆行星卻始終不肯照著時間表走。有時它比預測的位置稍微超前，有時又落後。看來最可能的解釋有兩種：要不是牛頓的定律在這麼遙遠的距離下不適用，就是有某個看不見的天體正把天王星拉離軌道。\n\n1845 年，法國數學家奧本‧勒維耶決定驗證第二種想法。他從天王星運行中那些細微的不規則變化往回推算，算出一顆未知的行星必須位在哪裡，才會造成這些變化。接著，他把預測結果寄給柏林天文台的約翰‧伽勒。伽勒在 1846 年 9 月 23 日收到這封信，當天晚上就展開搜尋。不到一個小時，他和助手就在距離勒維耶所指位置約一度的地方，找到一個黯淡的天體。那就是海王星。\n\n這項發現很快就引發了爭議。在英國，一位年輕的劍橋數學家約翰‧庫奇‧亞當斯也做過類似的計算，但英國的天文學家遲遲沒有據此採取行動。有一段時間，英國和法國為了這份榮耀該歸誰而爭論不休。奇妙的是，其實早就有人看過海王星：伽利略在 1612 年和 1613 年的筆記中記錄過它，卻誤以為那是一顆恆星。\n\n然而，同樣的方法並不一定都會成功。勒維耶後來注意到，水星的軌道也和牛頓定律的預測不太吻合，於是他提出另一顆隱藏的行星，稱為「火神星」，在比水星更靠近太陽的地方繞行。好幾位觀測者都聲稱看到了它，但火神星根本不存在。真正的答案在 1915 年才出現：愛因斯坦的廣義相對論指出，在太陽這種質量巨大的天體附近，牛頓的定律需要做些微的修正。這一次，缺了點什麼的是理論，而不是天空。\n\n天文學家至今仍用這種方式推理。有些人認為，海王星外圍一些遙遠冰質天體的奇特軌道，可能是由一顆尚未被發現的「第九行星」所造成，不過至今還沒有人找到它。海王星的故事提醒我們，數學能為科學家指出正確的方向，但只有仔細的觀測，才能證明預測是否正確。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How Galileo discovered Neptune by accident in the 1600s", "Why Newton's laws of gravity turned out to be completely wrong", "How Neptune was predicted by calculation and what this method can teach us", "How astronomers today are searching for a ninth planet"], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文從第一段「海王星的位置先在紙上被算出來」開始，說明勒維耶如何從天王星的軌道偏差推算出海王星，再以火神星與第九行星為例討論這種方法的限制與現代應用，故選 (C)。(A) 第四段說伽利略誤以為它是恆星，並沒有發現它；(B) 第五段只說牛頓定律在太陽附近需要 a small correction，並非完全錯誤；(D) 第九行星只是最後一段的例子。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what problem did astronomers notice about Uranus?", "options": ["It was too faint to be seen even with the best telescopes.", "It moved much faster than any other planet in the solar system.", "It appeared to be pulled toward the Sun by Newton's laws.", "Its actual position did not match the path they had calculated."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：the planet refused to stay on schedule. Sometimes it was slightly ahead of its predicted position, and sometimes it fell behind，表示天王星的實際位置和計算出的路徑不符，故選 (D)。(A) 赫歇爾在 1781 年就發現了它，並非看不到；(B) 文中只說它有時超前、有時落後，沒有和其他行星比較速度；(C) 文中說的是有未知天體可能把它 pulling... off course，並非被牛頓定律拉向太陽。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about Galileo's observation in paragraph 4?", "options": ["He saw Neptune but did not realize that it was a planet.", "He was the first person to calculate Neptune's position.", "He helped Le Verrier find Neptune in 1846.", "He believed that Neptune was pulling Uranus off course."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：Galileo recorded it in his notes in 1612 and 1613, but he mistook it for a star，他看到了海王星，卻誤以為是恆星，可推知他並不知道那是一顆行星，故選 (A)。(B) 計算位置的是勒維耶與亞當斯；(C) 伽利略是 1600 年代的人，不可能參與 1846 年的搜尋；(D) 天王星在 1781 年才被發現，伽利略當時不可能這樣想。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"irregularities\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["careful measurements", "unexpected changes", "serious mistakes", "regular patterns"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段 the tiny irregularities in Uranus's motion 指的就是第二段所說天王星「有時超前、有時落後」、偏離預測路徑的情形，也就是不規則、出乎預期的變化，故選 (B)。(A) 測量是天文學家做的事，不是天王星運動的特徵；(C) 這些偏差不是人為錯誤，而是另一顆行星的引力造成的；(D) regular patterns 與 irregular 意思相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, how was the case of Vulcan different from the case of Neptune?", "options": ["Vulcan was predicted by Adams, while Neptune was predicted by Le Verrier.", "Vulcan was found in Berlin, while Neptune was found in Cambridge.", "Vulcan was seen by Galileo, while Neptune was seen by no one before 1846.", "Vulcan's problem was solved by a better theory, not by a hidden planet."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段說火神星根本不存在，水星軌道的問題是靠 1915 年愛因斯坦的廣義相對論修正牛頓定律才解決，並以 it was the theory, not the sky, that was missing something 作結；而海王星的情況則是真的有一顆隱藏的行星，故選 (D)。(A) 火神星也是勒維耶提出的；(B) 火神星從未被找到，海王星是在柏林發現的；(C) 第四段說伽利略看到的是海王星。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a96", "title": "Glasses for a Telescope: How Hubble Was Rescued", "title_zh": "幫望遠鏡配眼鏡：哈伯如何起死回生", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Technology"], "passage": "When the Hubble Space Telescope was carried into orbit by the space shuttle Discovery in April 1990, astronomers expected the clearest pictures of the universe ever taken. Above the blurring effect of Earth's atmosphere, Hubble was supposed to see distant stars and galaxies in sharp detail. Within weeks, however, it became clear that something was terribly wrong. The first images were fuzzy, and the telescope, which had cost about 1.5 billion US dollars, quickly became a joke on late-night television.\n\nThe problem lay in Hubble's main mirror, which is 2.4 meters wide. It had been ground to the wrong shape: its outer edge was too flat by roughly one-fiftieth of the thickness of a sheet of paper. That tiny error meant that light hitting the edge of the mirror and light hitting the center were focused in slightly different places, so every image was blurred. An investigation later found that an instrument used to test the mirror had been put together incorrectly. Other tests had shown signs of the error, but they were dismissed because engineers trusted the main instrument more.\n\nBringing the telescope back to Earth would have been too risky and expensive, and replacing a mirror in space was impossible. Yet engineers noticed something hopeful. The mirror had been made extremely precisely; it was simply made precisely to the wrong shape. Because the mistake was the same all the way around the mirror, it could be measured exactly, and an exact correction could be designed, in the same way that glasses correct a person's eyesight.\n\nIn December 1993, seven astronauts aboard the space shuttle Endeavour flew up to Hubble. During five long spacewalks, they installed a device called COSTAR, which used small mirrors to correct the light before it reached several instruments, and they replaced Hubble's main camera with a new one that had the correction built into it. When the first new images arrived, the stars were sharp points of light instead of blurry spots.\n\nWhat followed has made Hubble one of the most successful scientific instruments in history. In 1995, it stared at a tiny, seemingly empty patch of sky for ten days and revealed thousands of galaxies. Its observations have also helped scientists estimate the age of the universe at about 13.8 billion years. Later instruments were all designed with the correction included, so COSTAR was removed in 2009 and now sits in a museum.\n\nHubble's early failure is still taught to engineers today. It shows how much depends on careful, independent testing, and how dangerous it is to ignore results that do not fit our expectations. It also proves that a serious mistake, if it is studied honestly, can be turned into a remarkable success.", "passage_zh": "1990 年 4 月，哈伯太空望遠鏡由發現號太空梭送上軌道時，天文學家都期待它能拍出史上最清晰的宇宙照片。哈伯位在地球大氣層之上，不受大氣造成的模糊影響，照理說應該能清楚看見遙遠的恆星和星系。然而不到幾週，大家就發現事情嚴重不對勁。最早傳回的影像模糊不清，這座花費約 15 億美元的望遠鏡，很快就成了深夜電視節目上的笑柄。\n\n問題出在哈伯寬 2.4 公尺的主鏡。這面鏡子被磨成了錯誤的形狀：它的外緣太平了，誤差大約只有一張紙厚度的五十分之一。這個微小的誤差，使得照在鏡子邊緣的光和照在中央的光，聚焦在稍微不同的位置，所以每一張影像都是模糊的。後來的調查發現，一台用來檢測這面鏡子的儀器在組裝時出了錯。其實其他檢測曾顯示出誤差的跡象，但因為工程師比較信任那台主要的儀器，這些結果都被忽略了。\n\n把望遠鏡帶回地球太冒險也太昂貴，而在太空中更換鏡子更是不可能的事。不過，工程師注意到一件令人燃起希望的事。這面鏡子其實做得極為精確，只是精確地被做成了錯誤的形狀。由於整面鏡子的誤差在每個方向都一樣，因此可以被精準地測量出來，並據此設計出精準的修正方式，就像眼鏡能矯正一個人的視力一樣。\n\n1993 年 12 月，七名太空人搭乘奮進號太空梭飛到哈伯身邊。他們在五次漫長的太空漫步中，安裝了一個名為 COSTAR 的裝置，利用幾面小鏡子在光線進入數台儀器之前先加以修正；他們也把哈伯的主相機換成一台內建修正功能的新相機。當第一批新影像傳回來時，恆星成了清晰的光點，而不再是模糊的斑點。\n\n接下來的發展，讓哈伯成為史上最成功的科學儀器之一。1995 年，它花了十天凝視天空中一小塊看似空無一物的區域，結果發現了數千個星系。它的觀測也幫助科學家估算出宇宙的年齡大約是 138 億年。後來的儀器在設計時都已經納入了修正功能，所以 COSTAR 在 2009 年被拆下，如今陳列在博物館中。\n\n直到今天，哈伯早期的失敗仍是工程師要學習的教材。它說明了仔細而獨立的檢測有多麼重要，也說明忽視不符合預期的結果有多麼危險。它同時證明，一個嚴重的錯誤只要能被誠實地檢討，也可以轉變成非凡的成功。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How a serious flaw in the Hubble Space Telescope was found and corrected", "Why space telescopes take clearer pictures than telescopes on the ground", "How astronomers used Hubble to calculate the age of the universe", "Why the space shuttle program was stopped after 1993"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文依序說明哈伯升空後影像模糊（第一段）、原因是主鏡形狀錯誤（第二段）、工程師想出修正方法（第三段）、1993 年太空人前往修復（第四段），以及之後的成就與教訓（第五、六段），故選 (A)。(B) 只是第一段的背景；(C) 宇宙年齡只是第五段的一個例子；(D) 文中完全沒有提到太空梭計畫停止。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what caused Hubble's blurry images?", "options": ["The telescope was damaged by the atmosphere during launch.", "The astronauts installed the main camera in the wrong place.", "The mirror was given the wrong shape because a testing instrument was assembled incorrectly.", "The mirror was too small to collect light from distant galaxies."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說主鏡 had been ground to the wrong shape，外緣太平，使光線聚焦在不同位置；調查發現 an instrument used to test the mirror had been put together incorrectly，故選 (C)。(A) 文中未提到發射時受損，而且哈伯本來就在大氣層之上；(B) 太空人是後來才去修復，並非造成問題的人；(D) 問題在於形狀而非大小。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why were engineers able to design a correction for the mirror?", "options": ["The mirror could easily be brought back to Earth and reshaped.", "The error was regular and could be measured exactly.", "The other tests had already corrected most of the error.", "The mirror's center was too flat, while its edge was perfect."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：Because the mistake was the same all the way around the mirror, it could be measured exactly, and an exact correction could be designed，因為誤差是規律一致的，可以精確測量，才能設計出精確的修正，故選 (B)。(A) 同段說帶回地球 too risky and expensive；(C) 第二段說其他檢測的結果被忽略，並沒有修正誤差；(D) 第二段說太平的是外緣 (outer edge)，不是中央。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"dismissed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["repeated several times", "sent to other scientists", "carefully checked", "treated as unimportant"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Other tests had shown signs of the error, but they were dismissed because engineers trusted the main instrument more. 工程師比較相信主要儀器，所以其他顯示誤差的檢測結果被「不予理會、當作不重要」，故選 (D)。第六段也提到 how dangerous it is to ignore results，可互相印證。(C) 若有仔細檢查就會發現錯誤，與文意相反；(A)(B) 都不符合上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which statement would the writer most likely agree with?", "options": ["Expensive projects like Hubble are usually a waste of money.", "A mistake can lead to valuable lessons if people examine it honestly.", "Engineers should always trust one main instrument over other tests.", "Hubble's later success was mainly a matter of good luck."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第六段最後一句：a serious mistake, if it is studied honestly, can be turned into a remarkable success，作者認為錯誤若能被誠實檢討，就能帶來寶貴的教訓甚至成功，故選 (B)。(A) 第五段稱哈伯為史上最成功的科學儀器之一，與此相反；(C) 第二、六段正是在批評只相信主要儀器而忽略其他結果；(D) 文中強調的是精確測量與太空人的修復工作，並非運氣。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a97", "title": "A Push in the Right Direction: The Day NASA Hit an Asteroid", "title_zh": "推一把：NASA 撞擊小行星的那一天", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Technology"], "passage": "About 66 million years ago, an asteroid roughly ten kilometers wide struck what is now Mexico and helped wipe out the dinosaurs. Far smaller objects can still cause damage. In 2013, a rock only about 20 meters across exploded above the Russian city of Chelyabinsk, and around 1,500 people were injured, mostly by flying glass from broken windows. These events raise an obvious question: if a dangerous asteroid were heading toward Earth, could we do anything to stop it?\n\nOn September 26, 2022, NASA tried to find out. Its Double Asteroid Redirection Test, or DART, deliberately crashed a spacecraft weighing about 570 kilograms into an asteroid called Dimorphos at more than 20,000 kilometers per hour. Dimorphos, which is about 160 meters wide, travels around a larger asteroid named Didymos. Neither of them poses any threat to our planet. The goal was simply to test whether a fast-moving spacecraft could push an asteroid slightly off its path.\n\nThe choice of target was a clever one. Measuring a small change in an asteroid's journey around the Sun would be extremely difficult. Dimorphos, however, circled Didymos once every 11 hours and 55 minutes, and each time one asteroid passed in front of the other, telescopes on Earth could detect a tiny dip in their combined brightness. By timing these dips before and after the crash, scientists could tell precisely how much the orbit had changed.\n\nThe results were better than expected. NASA had hoped to shorten the orbit by at least 73 seconds, but the impact reduced it by about 32 minutes. The main reason was the enormous cloud of rock and dust that the crash blasted into space. Like gas shooting out of a rocket, this material pushed Dimorphos even harder. The asteroid seems to be a loose pile of rubble rather than one solid rock, which made the effect stronger.\n\nStill, DART does not mean that Earth is safe. A small push only becomes a large miss if it has many years to add up, so the method works only if a dangerous asteroid is spotted long before it arrives. Astronomers have already found most near-Earth asteroids larger than one kilometer, but fewer than half of those 140 meters or more across, which could destroy a city, have been located. For this reason, NASA is building a new space telescope designed to hunt for them.\n\nMeanwhile, the European Space Agency launched a mission called Hera in 2024 to study Dimorphos up close. For the first time in history, humans have changed the motion of a natural object in space. Unlike the dinosaurs, we may one day be able to defend ourselves, as long as we keep watching the sky.", "passage_zh": "大約 6,600 萬年前，一顆寬約十公里的小行星撞擊了今天的墨西哥一帶，並促成了恐龍的滅絕。就算是小得多的天體，也仍然可能造成災害。2013 年，一顆直徑只有大約 20 公尺的岩石在俄羅斯車里雅賓斯克市上空爆炸，造成約 1,500 人受傷，大多是被破碎窗戶的玻璃碎片所傷。這些事件引出一個顯而易見的問題：如果有一顆危險的小行星正朝地球飛來，我們能做些什麼來阻止它嗎？\n\n2022 年 9 月 26 日，NASA 試著找出答案。它的「雙小行星重定向測試」，簡稱 DART，刻意讓一艘重約 570 公斤的太空船，以每小時超過兩萬公里的速度撞上一顆名為「迪莫弗斯」的小行星。迪莫弗斯寬約 160 公尺，繞著一顆較大的小行星「迪蒂莫斯」運行。這兩顆小行星都不會對地球造成任何威脅。這次任務的目的只是要測試：高速飛行的太空船能不能把小行星稍微推離原本的路徑。\n\n選擇這個目標是很聰明的做法。要測量一顆小行星繞太陽運行時的微小變化，是非常困難的事。然而，迪莫弗斯每 11 小時 55 分鐘繞迪蒂莫斯一圈，每當其中一顆小行星從另一顆前方經過時，地球上的望遠鏡就能偵測到兩者總亮度出現微小的下降。只要比較撞擊前後這些亮度下降的時間，科學家就能精確得知軌道改變了多少。\n\n結果比預期的更好。NASA 原本希望讓軌道週期至少縮短 73 秒，但這次撞擊卻讓它縮短了約 32 分鐘。主要原因是撞擊把大量的岩石和塵埃噴射到太空中。就像從火箭噴出的氣體一樣，這些物質把迪莫弗斯推得更用力。這顆小行星似乎是一堆鬆散的碎石，而不是一整塊堅固的岩石，因此讓這種效果更加明顯。\n\n儘管如此，DART 並不代表地球從此就安全了。小小的一推必須經過許多年的累積，才會變成大幅度的偏離，所以這個方法只有在危險的小行星抵達前很久就被發現時才有效。天文學家已經找到大部分直徑超過一公里的近地小行星，但直徑 140 公尺以上、足以摧毀一座城市的小行星，被找到的還不到一半。因此，NASA 正在建造一座專門用來搜尋它們的新太空望遠鏡。\n\n同時，歐洲太空總署也在 2024 年發射了名為「赫拉」的任務，要近距離研究迪莫弗斯。這是人類史上第一次改變太空中天然物體的運動。和恐龍不同，我們也許有一天真的能保護自己，只要我們持續觀察天空。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer mention the dinosaurs and the city of Chelyabinsk in paragraph 1?", "options": ["To prove that asteroids hit Earth every few years", "To explain how scientists first discovered Dimorphos", "To compare the sizes of asteroids in Mexico and Russia", "To show why the danger of asteroids is worth taking seriously"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段先舉恐龍滅絕和車里雅賓斯克有約 1,500 人受傷兩個例子，再提出問題：if a dangerous asteroid were heading toward Earth, could we do anything to stop it? 目的是說明小行星的威脅值得認真看待，引出 DART 任務，故選 (D)。(A) 文中沒有說每隔幾年就有撞擊；(B) 第一段完全沒提到迪莫弗斯；(C) 作者並非要比較大小，而是說明大小不同的天體都可能造成災害。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why did NASA choose Dimorphos as its target?", "options": ["Because changes in its orbit around Didymos could be measured accurately.", "Because it was moving toward Earth at a dangerous speed.", "Because it was the largest asteroid near Earth.", "Because it did not travel around the Sun."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說迪莫弗斯繞著迪蒂莫斯運行，地球上的望遠鏡可以偵測亮度的下降，By timing these dips before and after the crash, scientists could tell precisely how much the orbit had changed，故選 (A)。(B) 第二段明說 Neither of them poses any threat to our planet；(C) 迪莫弗斯只有約 160 公尺寬，比它繞行的迪蒂莫斯還小；(D) 文中只說測量它繞太陽的變化很困難，並非它不繞太陽。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "After the impact, about how long did it take Dimorphos to travel once around Didymos?", "options": ["11 hours and 55 minutes", "12 hours and 27 minutes", "11 hours and 23 minutes", "73 seconds less than before"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說撞擊前迪莫弗斯 circled Didymos once every 11 hours and 55 minutes；第四段說撞擊 reduced it by about 32 minutes。11 小時 55 分減去 32 分鐘約為 11 小時 23 分，故選 (C)。(A) 是撞擊前的時間；(B) 是誤把時間加上 32 分鐘；(D) 73 秒只是 NASA 原本希望達到的最低目標，實際縮短得更多。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 5?", "options": ["Finding dangerous asteroids early is just as important as being able to push them.", "Asteroids smaller than one kilometer cannot cause serious damage.", "A spacecraft like DART can stop any asteroid a few days before it hits.", "NASA has already located every asteroid that could destroy a city."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段說 the method works only if a dangerous asteroid is spotted long before it arrives，又提到足以摧毀城市的小行星被找到的不到一半，所以 NASA 要建新望遠鏡去搜尋，可推知及早發現和推動小行星的能力一樣重要，故選 (A)。(B) 同段說直徑 140 公尺以上的小行星就足以摧毀城市；(C) 與「小小一推需要許多年累積」相反；(D) 與「被找到的還不到一半」相反。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"deliberately\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["by accident", "on purpose", "very slowly", "without danger"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段說 DART deliberately crashed a spacecraft... into an asteroid，同段最後又說明 The goal was simply to test whether a fast-moving spacecraft could push an asteroid，可見撞擊是事先計畫好的，deliberately 意為「刻意地、故意地」，與 on purpose 相同，故選 (B)。(A) by accident 意思相反；(C) 太空船時速超過兩萬公里，並不慢；(D) without danger 與這個字的意思無關。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a98", "title": "Ripples in Space: The Hundred-Year Hunt for Gravitational Waves", "title_zh": "太空中的漣漪：追尋重力波的百年之旅", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science"], "passage": "In 1916, Albert Einstein made a remarkable prediction. According to his general theory of relativity, massive objects bend space and time around them, and when such objects speed up or crash into each other, they should send ripples spreading through the universe, much as a stone thrown into a pond creates waves. Einstein called these ripples gravitational waves, but he doubted that anyone would ever detect them. By the time they reached Earth, he believed, they would be far too weak to measure.\n\nA century later, scientists proved him half right. The waves were indeed incredibly weak, but they were not impossible to catch. The Laser Interferometer Gravitational-Wave Observatory, or LIGO, has two huge L-shaped detectors in the US states of Washington and Louisiana. Each has two arms four kilometers long. A laser beam is split in two, sent down both arms, reflected by mirrors and then combined again. If a gravitational wave passes through, it stretches one arm and squeezes the other by far less than the width of a single proton, slightly changing the pattern of the returning light.\n\nMeasuring such a tiny change is extremely difficult because almost anything can shake the mirrors: a passing truck, waves crashing on a distant beach, or an earthquake on the other side of the world. To reduce this noise, the mirrors hang from special systems that absorb vibration. Building two detectors about 3,000 kilometers apart also helps. A local vibration will appear in only one of them, but a genuine gravitational wave, traveling at the speed of light, should be recorded by both within a few thousandths of a second.\n\nThat is exactly what happened on September 14, 2015. Both detectors picked up a signal lasting about one-fifth of a second. When it was turned into sound, it became a short \"chirp\" that rose quickly in pitch. Researchers worked out that it had been produced by two black holes, about 36 and 29 times as massive as the Sun, spiraling into each other some 1.3 billion light-years away. The discovery was announced in February 2016, and three of the project's leaders won the 2017 Nobel Prize in Physics.\n\nThe achievement matters because, until then, astronomers had studied the universe almost entirely through light and other forms of radiation. Black holes give off no light at all, yet they can now be \"heard.\" In 2017, detectors caught the collision of two neutron stars, which telescopes then observed as well, showing that such crashes create heavy elements like gold.\n\nToday, detectors in Italy and Japan have joined the search, and hundreds of these cosmic events have been recorded. For most of history, we watched a silent movie of the universe. Now the movie finally has a soundtrack.", "passage_zh": "1916 年，愛因斯坦提出了一個非凡的預測。根據他的廣義相對論，質量巨大的物體會讓周圍的空間和時間彎曲；當這樣的物體加速或彼此相撞時，應該會發出向整個宇宙擴散的漣漪，就像把石頭丟進池塘會激起水波一樣。愛因斯坦把這些漣漪稱為重力波，但他懷疑是否有人能偵測到它們。他認為，等到這些波傳到地球時，早已微弱得無法測量。\n\n一個世紀之後，科學家證明他只說對了一半。這些波確實極為微弱，但並非不可能捕捉。「雷射干涉重力波天文台」，簡稱 LIGO，在美國華盛頓州和路易斯安那州各有一座巨大的 L 形偵測器。每座偵測器都有兩條長四公里的臂。一道雷射光被分成兩道，分別射向兩條臂，經鏡子反射後再重新合在一起。如果有重力波通過，它會把其中一條臂拉長、另一條臂壓縮，變化量比一顆質子的寬度還要小得多，使反射回來的光的圖樣產生些微的改變。\n\n要測量這麼微小的變化極為困難，因為幾乎任何事物都可能讓鏡子晃動：一輛經過的卡車、遠方海灘上拍打的海浪，或是地球另一端發生的地震。為了減少這些干擾，鏡子都懸掛在能吸收震動的特殊裝置上。把兩座偵測器蓋在相距約 3,000 公里的地方也有幫助。局部的震動只會出現在其中一座偵測器中，但真正的重力波以光速傳遞，應該會在千分之幾秒內被兩座偵測器都記錄下來。\n\n2015 年 9 月 14 日，事情正是這樣發生的。兩座偵測器都接收到一個持續約五分之一秒的訊號。把它轉換成聲音後，就成了一聲音調迅速升高的短促「啁啾聲」。研究人員推算出，這個訊號是由兩個分別約為太陽 36 倍和 29 倍質量的黑洞，在大約 13 億光年外互相盤旋、合而為一所產生的。這項發現在 2016 年 2 月公布，計畫的三位領導人也獲得了 2017 年的諾貝爾物理學獎。\n\n這項成就之所以重要，是因為在此之前，天文學家幾乎完全是透過光和其他形式的輻射來研究宇宙。黑洞完全不發光，如今卻能被「聽見」。2017 年，偵測器捕捉到兩顆中子星的碰撞，接著望遠鏡也觀測到了這次事件，證明這類碰撞會產生像金這樣的重元素。\n\n如今，義大利和日本的偵測器也加入了搜尋的行列，已經記錄到數百起這類宇宙事件。在人類歷史的大部分時間裡，我們看的是一部宇宙的無聲電影。如今，這部電影終於有了配樂。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Einstein's theory of relativity has been proved completely wrong.", "Scientists finally detected gravitational waves, giving astronomy a new way to study the universe.", "LIGO was built mainly to measure earthquakes on the other side of the world.", "Black holes are the only objects in the universe that produce gravitational waves."], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文從愛因斯坦的預測談起，說明 LIGO 如何在 2015 年首次偵測到重力波，並在第五、六段指出它讓天文學家多了一種「聽」宇宙的方法，故選 (B)。(A) 第二段說科學家證明他 half right，重力波確實存在；(C) 地震是第三段提到的干擾來源，並非建造目的；(D) 第五段提到中子星碰撞也被偵測到。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why does LIGO use two detectors far away from each other?", "options": ["So that scientists in two states can share the cost equally", "So that one detector can keep working while the other is repaired", "So that the laser beam can travel 3,000 kilometers", "So that scientists can tell a real wave from a local vibration"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：A local vibration will appear in only one of them, but a genuine gravitational wave... should be recorded by both within a few thousandths of a second. 兩座偵測器相距遙遠，是為了分辨真正的重力波和當地的震動，故選 (D)。(A)(B) 文中未提及；(C) 每條臂只有四公里長，3,000 公里是兩座偵測器之間的距離。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the passage, what produced the signal detected on September 14, 2015?", "options": ["Two neutron stars that crashed and created gold", "A powerful earthquake on the other side of the world", "Two black holes that spiraled into each other and merged", "A star about 36 times as massive as the Sun exploding"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：it had been produced by two black holes, about 36 and 29 times as massive as the Sun, spiraling into each other some 1.3 billion light-years away，故選 (C)。(A) 中子星碰撞是第五段提到的 2017 年事件；(B) 地震是會干擾偵測器的雜訊，不是訊號來源；(D) 36 倍是其中一個黑洞的質量，並非恆星爆炸。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about black holes from paragraph 5?", "options": ["They could hardly be studied with ordinary telescopes before gravitational waves were detected.", "They are the main source of heavy elements such as gold.", "They give off more light than any other objects in space.", "They were first discovered by LIGO in 2017."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段說過去天文學家幾乎完全靠光和其他輻射研究宇宙，而 Black holes give off no light at all, yet they can now be \"heard\"，可推知在偵測到重力波之前，很難用一般望遠鏡研究黑洞，故選 (A)。(B) 文中說產生金等重元素的是中子星碰撞；(C) 與 give off no light at all 相反；(D) 2017 年偵測到的是中子星碰撞，而且第四段 2015 年的訊號已來自黑洞。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"genuine\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["weak", "local", "dangerous", "real"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段把 A local vibration（局部震動，屬於雜訊）和 a genuine gravitational wave 對比，後者才是科學家要找的真正訊號，genuine 意為「真正的、真實的」，與 real 最接近，故選 (D)。(A) 重力波雖然微弱，但 genuine 並不是這個意思；(B) local 正是句中與 genuine 對比的字；(C) dangerous 不合文意。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a99", "title": "The Woman Who Measured the Universe", "title_zh": "丈量宇宙的女性", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "History"], "passage": "How far away is a star? For centuries, this was one of the hardest questions in astronomy. A star that looks bright in the night sky might be close to us and quite dim, or very far away and extremely powerful, and there was no easy way to tell the difference. Surprisingly, the key to solving this puzzle was found by a woman who was not even allowed to use her observatory's large telescopes.\n\nHenrietta Swan Leavitt joined the Harvard College Observatory in the 1890s as one of its \"computers.\" At the time, this word referred not to machines but to women hired to examine photographic glass plates of the night sky, measure the brightness of thousands of stars and record the results by hand. The work was tedious and poorly paid, and women were rarely allowed to operate the big telescopes themselves. Leavitt, who gradually lost much of her hearing, nevertheless worked with extraordinary patience and accuracy.\n\nStudying plates of the Magellanic Clouds, two great star systems visible from the Southern Hemisphere, she identified more than 1,700 variable stars, whose brightness changes over time. She paid special attention to a type called Cepheids, which grow brighter and dimmer in a steady rhythm lasting from a few days to several weeks. In 1912, after comparing Cepheids in the Small Magellanic Cloud, she reported a striking pattern: the brighter a Cepheid was, the longer its cycle took.\n\nThis finding was far more powerful than it might sound. Since all the stars in the Small Magellanic Cloud are roughly the same distance from Earth, differences in how bright they appeared had to reflect real differences in how much light they gave off. Therefore, by timing a Cepheid's cycle, astronomers could work out its true brightness. Comparing that with how bright it looked from Earth then revealed its distance, in the same way that we can judge how far away a lamp is if we know how strong its bulb is.\n\nIn the 1920s, Edwin Hubble found Cepheids in the Andromeda Nebula and used Leavitt's method to measure their distance. The result showed that Andromeda was far too distant to be part of our own Milky Way. It was a separate galaxy, and the universe was suddenly much larger than most scientists had imagined.\n\nLeavitt did not live to see this revolution. She died of cancer in 1921, and in 1925 a Swedish mathematician, unaware of her death, tried to nominate her for the Nobel Prize, which cannot be awarded after a person has died. Today her discovery is often called Leavitt's Law, a lasting reminder that important science is sometimes done by people whose work goes unnoticed for too long.", "passage_zh": "一顆恆星離我們有多遠？好幾個世紀以來，這一直是天文學中最難回答的問題之一。一顆在夜空中看起來很亮的恆星，可能離我們很近、本身其實相當黯淡，也可能離我們非常遙遠、本身極為明亮，而且沒有簡單的方法能分辨。令人意外的是，解開這個謎題的關鍵，竟然是由一位連天文台的大型望遠鏡都不被允許使用的女性找到的。\n\n亨麗埃塔‧斯旺‧勒維特在 1890 年代進入哈佛大學天文台，成為那裡的「計算員」之一。在當時，這個詞指的不是機器，而是受雇來檢視夜空照相玻璃底片的女性，她們要測量數千顆恆星的亮度，再用手把結果記錄下來。這份工作單調乏味，薪水又低，而且女性很少能親自操作大型望遠鏡。勒維特的聽力逐漸喪失了大半，但她仍以非凡的耐心和精確度投入工作。\n\n她在研究麥哲倫星雲的底片時──那是兩個在南半球可以看見的巨大星系統──找出了一千七百多顆變星，也就是亮度會隨時間改變的恆星。她特別注意一種叫做造父變星的恆星，它們會以穩定的節奏變亮又變暗，一個週期從幾天到好幾週不等。1912 年，她比較了小麥哲倫星雲中的造父變星之後，發表了一個驚人的規律：造父變星越亮，它的週期就越長。\n\n這項發現的威力遠比聽起來強大得多。由於小麥哲倫星雲中的所有恆星和地球的距離大致相同，它們看起來亮度上的差異，必定反映出它們實際發出的光有多少的差別。因此，只要測量造父變星的週期，天文學家就能推算出它真正的亮度。再把這個亮度和它從地球上看起來的亮度相比，就能得知它的距離；這就像只要知道一盞燈的燈泡有多強，就能判斷這盞燈離我們有多遠一樣。\n\n1920 年代，愛德溫‧哈伯在仙女座星雲中找到了造父變星，並用勒維特的方法測量它們的距離。結果顯示，仙女座實在太遠了，不可能是我們銀河系的一部分。它是另一個獨立的星系，宇宙一下子變得比大多數科學家想像的還要大得多。\n\n勒維特沒能親眼看到這場革命。她在 1921 年因癌症過世；1925 年，一位不知道她已經過世的瑞典數學家想提名她角逐諾貝爾獎，但這個獎不能頒給已經過世的人。如今，她的發現常被稱為「勒維特定律」，持續提醒著我們：重要的科學成就，有時是由那些長久以來未受重視的人所完成的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why women were not allowed to use telescopes in the 1890s", "How Edwin Hubble discovered the Andromeda galaxy by himself", "How Leavitt's discovery made it possible to measure distances in space", "Why Cepheid stars are brighter than other stars"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段提出「恆星有多遠」這個難題，並說解開關鍵的是一位女性；第三、四段說明勒維特發現造父變星的規律，以及如何用它算出距離；第五段說哈伯用她的方法發現仙女座是另一個星系，故選 (C)。(A) 只是第二段的背景；(B) 第五段說哈伯用的是 Leavitt's method，並非獨力完成；(D) 文中沒有說造父變星比其他恆星亮。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what pattern did Leavitt report in 1912?", "options": ["Brighter Cepheids take longer to complete one cycle.", "Stars in the Southern Hemisphere are brighter than those in the north.", "Variable stars become dimmer as they get older.", "Cepheids change their brightness in random ways."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段最後一句：In 1912, after comparing Cepheids in the Small Magellanic Cloud, she reported a striking pattern: the brighter a Cepheid was, the longer its cycle took. 越亮的造父變星週期越長，故選 (A)。(B)(C) 文中都沒有提到；(D) 同段說造父變星以 a steady rhythm（穩定的節奏）變亮變暗，並非隨機。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why was it important that the Cepheids Leavitt compared were all in the Small Magellanic Cloud?", "options": ["Because these stars could be seen through Harvard's largest telescope", "Because these stars were the closest ones to Earth", "Because these stars never changed in brightness", "Because their differences in brightness could not be caused by different distances"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：Since all the stars in the Small Magellanic Cloud are roughly the same distance from Earth, differences in how bright they appeared had to reflect real differences in how much light they gave off. 因為距離大致相同，亮度差異不可能是距離造成的，而是真實亮度的差別，故選 (D)。(A) 第一、二段說她不被允許使用大型望遠鏡，她研究的是照相底片；(B) 文中沒說它們最近；(C) 她研究的正是亮度會變化的變星。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"tedious\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["dangerous", "boring and repetitive", "exciting and new", "easy to finish"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段描述計算員的工作是檢視玻璃底片、測量數千顆恆星的亮度，再 record the results by hand，可見工作重複又費時，tedious 意為「冗長乏味的」，與 boring and repetitive 最接近，故選 (B)。(C) 與文意相反；(A) 文中沒有提到危險；(D) 測量數千顆恆星並不容易完成。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE according to the passage?", "options": ["Leavitt won the Nobel Prize in 1925 for her work on Cepheids.", "Leavitt took the photographs of the Small Magellanic Cloud with her own telescope.", "Hubble used Leavitt's method to show that Andromeda lies outside the Milky Way.", "Leavitt's discovery made the universe seem smaller than scientists had believed."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段：Edwin Hubble... used Leavitt's method... The result showed that Andromeda was far too distant to be part of our own Milky Way，故 (C) 正確。(A) 第六段說她 1921 年已過世，1925 年的提名無法成立，因為諾貝爾獎不頒給過世的人；(B) 她研究的是天文台的照相底片，而且女性很少能操作大型望遠鏡；(D) 第五段說宇宙 suddenly much larger，與此相反。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a100", "title": "Traffic Jam in Orbit: The Growing Problem of Space Junk", "title_zh": "軌道上的大塞車：日益嚴重的太空垃圾問題", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Technology"], "passage": "Imagine a highway where broken-down cars are never towed away, where pieces of old tires and bumpers keep flying around at deadly speed, and where new vehicles join the traffic every week. That is roughly what the space around our planet has become. Since the Soviet Union launched Sputnik in 1957, humans have left behind dead satellites, used rocket stages and countless fragments. Today, tens of thousands of objects larger than ten centimeters are being tracked, and scientists estimate that there are more than a million pieces larger than one centimeter.\n\nSuch small objects may not sound dangerous, but in low orbit they travel at around 28,000 kilometers per hour, so even a tiny fragment carries enormous energy. In 1983, a fleck of paint chipped a window of a space shuttle, and a one-centimeter piece of metal can disable a satellite completely. The International Space Station has had to change its course dozens of times to avoid debris, and on several occasions astronauts have sheltered in their spacecraft, ready to escape.\n\nThe real worry is that collisions multiply. In 2009, a working American communications satellite crashed into a dead Russian satellite above Siberia, creating more than 2,000 trackable pieces. Missile tests in which several countries deliberately destroyed their own satellites have added thousands more. Every collision produces new fragments, and every new fragment makes another collision more likely.\n\nAs early as 1978, NASA scientist Donald Kessler warned that if orbits became crowded enough, this process could turn into a chain reaction. Pieces would smash into satellites, which would break into more pieces, until certain orbits became too dangerous to use for generations. This idea, now known as the Kessler syndrome, has become more urgent as private companies launch networks of thousands of satellites. Since weather forecasts, navigation and many communications depend on satellites, the consequences would be felt by almost everyone on Earth.\n\nExperts agree that prevention is the first step. Satellites can be designed to fall back and burn up in the atmosphere when their missions end, and in 2022 the United States reduced the time allowed for this from 25 years to five for the satellites it licenses. Engineers are also testing ways to remove existing junk, such as a net that successfully captured a target in orbit in 2018. However, cleanup missions are expensive, and international law does not allow one country to remove another country's satellite without permission.\n\nIn many ways, space junk resembles plastic in the ocean. Each user gains something by using the shared space, while the cost is spread among everyone. Space may seem endless, but the useful orbits around Earth are a limited resource, and protecting them will require cooperation before the traffic jam becomes impossible to clear.", "passage_zh": "想像一條公路，上面拋錨的車子永遠不會被拖走，舊輪胎和保險桿的碎片不斷以致命的速度四處亂飛，而每個星期都還有新的車輛加入車流。這大致就是我們地球周圍的太空如今變成的樣子。自從蘇聯在 1957 年發射史普尼克號以來，人類已經留下了失效的衛星、用過的火箭節段和數不清的碎片。如今，有數萬個大於十公分的物體正受到追蹤，而科學家估計，大於一公分的碎片超過一百萬個。\n\n這麼小的物體聽起來也許不危險，但在低軌道上，它們的速度大約是每小時 28,000 公里，因此就算是很小的碎片也帶有巨大的能量。1983 年，一小片油漆就在太空梭的窗戶上撞出了缺口；而一塊一公分大的金屬碎片，就可能讓一顆衛星完全失去功能。國際太空站已經有數十次必須改變航道來閃避碎片，也有好幾次，太空人必須躲進他們的太空船裡，隨時準備逃生。\n\n真正令人擔心的是，碰撞會不斷倍增。2009 年，一顆運作中的美國通訊衛星在西伯利亞上空撞上一顆已失效的俄羅斯衛星，產生了兩千多個可追蹤的碎片。好幾個國家曾進行飛彈試驗，刻意摧毀自己的衛星，又增加了數千個碎片。每一次碰撞都會產生新的碎片，而每一個新碎片都會讓下一次碰撞更有可能發生。\n\n早在 1978 年，NASA 科學家唐納德‧凱斯勒就提出警告：如果軌道變得夠擁擠，這個過程可能演變成連鎖反應。碎片會撞上衛星，衛星又會碎裂成更多碎片，直到某些軌道變得太危險，好幾個世代都無法使用。這個概念如今被稱為「凱斯勒症候群」；隨著民間公司發射由數千顆衛星組成的衛星網路，這個問題也變得更加迫切。由於天氣預報、導航和許多通訊都仰賴衛星，一旦發生，地球上幾乎每個人都會受到影響。\n\n專家們都同意，預防是第一步。衛星可以設計成在任務結束後落回大氣層並燒毀；2022 年，美國把它所核發執照的衛星可以留在軌道上的期限，從 25 年縮短為五年。工程師也在測試清除現有垃圾的方法，例如在 2018 年，曾有一張網在軌道上成功捕捉到一個目標物。然而，清理任務的費用十分昂貴，而且國際法不允許一個國家在未經許可的情況下，移除另一個國家的衛星。\n\n從許多方面來看，太空垃圾就像海洋中的塑膠。每個使用者都從這個共享的空間中得到好處，代價卻由所有人一起分擔。太空看似無邊無際，但地球周圍有用的軌道其實是有限的資源；要保護它們，就必須在這場大塞車變得無法疏通之前攜手合作。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Private companies should stop launching satellites into space.", "The International Space Station is no longer safe for astronauts.", "Space junk has already made all orbits around Earth impossible to use.", "Space junk is a growing danger that calls for prevention, cleanup and cooperation."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文先說明太空垃圾的數量與危險（第一、二段），再說明碰撞會倍增並可能引發凱斯勒症候群（第三、四段），最後提出預防與清除的方法，並強調需要合作（第五、六段），故選 (D)。(A) 作者只說衛星網路使問題更迫切，沒有主張停止發射；(B) 太空站只是第二段的例子；(C) 第四段說的是「可能」讓某些軌道無法使用，並非已經發生。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what happened in 2009?", "options": ["A missile test destroyed an American communications satellite.", "An active satellite crashed into a dead one, producing over 2,000 trackable pieces.", "A fleck of paint broke a window of the International Space Station.", "Russia removed one of its old satellites from orbit."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：In 2009, a working American communications satellite crashed into a dead Russian satellite above Siberia, creating more than 2,000 trackable pieces. 運作中的美國通訊衛星撞上失效的俄羅斯衛星，故選 (B)。(A) 飛彈試驗是各國摧毀「自己的」衛星，與 2009 年的事件不同；(C) 油漆碎片撞到的是 1983 年的太空梭窗戶；(D) 文中沒有提到俄羅斯移除衛星。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "If the \"Kessler syndrome\" began, what would most likely happen?", "options": ["Some orbits would become too dangerous to use for a very long time.", "All the debris would quickly fall back and burn up in the atmosphere.", "Satellites would be able to avoid one another more easily.", "Weather forecasts would become more accurate than before."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段說凱斯勒症候群是一種連鎖反應：碎片撞上衛星、衛星再碎裂成更多碎片，until certain orbits became too dangerous to use for generations，故選 (A)。(B) 與連鎖反應使碎片越來越多的說法相反；(C) 軌道會更擁擠，衛星更難閃避；(D) 同段說天氣預報仰賴衛星，一旦發生會受到負面影響。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"disable\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["speed up", "build again", "stop from working", "follow closely"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段說小碎片 carries enormous energy，a one-centimeter piece of metal can disable a satellite completely，意指一公分的金屬碎片就能讓衛星「完全失去功能」，disable 與 stop from working 最接近，故選 (C)。(A)(B)(D) 都不符合碎片撞擊造成損壞的上下文。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer compare space junk to plastic in the ocean in the last paragraph?", "options": ["To show that most space junk is made of plastic", "To point out that a shared resource can be damaged when users do not share responsibility for it", "To suggest that cleaning the ocean is more important than cleaning space", "To explain how space junk falls into the ocean after burning up"], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段：Each user gains something by using the shared space, while the cost is spread among everyone，並說有用的軌道是 a limited resource，需要合作保護。作者藉海洋塑膠的例子說明共享資源在大家只顧使用時容易被破壞，故選 (B)。(A) 文中未說太空垃圾多為塑膠；(C) 作者沒有比較兩者何者較重要；(D) 文中說衛星會在大氣層中燒毀，沒有提到落入海洋。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a101", "title": "One Twin in Space, One on Earth: What NASA Learned from the Kelly Brothers", "title_zh": "一個在太空，一個在地球：NASA 從凱利兄弟身上學到的事", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "Scientists who study the human body often wish they could compare a person with an exact copy who had lived a different life. In 2015, NASA found the next best thing. Astronaut Scott Kelly spent 340 days aboard the International Space Station, while his identical twin brother Mark, a retired astronaut, stayed on Earth. Because the two men share the same genes, any differences that appeared between them during the mission were likely to have been caused by life in space.\n\nTen research teams collected blood, urine and other samples from both brothers before, during and after the flight, and published their main results in 2019. One of the most surprising findings involved telomeres, the protective caps at the ends of our chromosomes, which are often compared to the plastic tips on shoelaces. Telomeres normally get shorter as people grow older, so scientists expected Scott's to shrink in the harsh conditions of space. Instead, they became longer. Within days of his return, however, most of them shortened again, and some ended up shorter than before the flight.\n\nThe researchers also found that the activity of thousands of Scott's genes changed in orbit, probably in response to stress, radiation and weightlessness. About 90 percent of these changes disappeared within six months of landing, but a few linked to the immune system and to repairing damaged DNA remained. Other tests revealed swelling at the back of his eyes, a thickening of an important artery, and a slight drop in the speed and accuracy of his thinking after he came home.\n\nNot all news coverage got the science right. Some headlines claimed that seven percent of Scott's DNA had changed and that the brothers were no longer identical twins. NASA quickly corrected this mistake. Scott's DNA itself had not changed; what had changed was how strongly certain genes were switched on or off. It is a bit like turning the volume of a song up or down: the sound changes, but the song itself stays the same.\n\nThe study matters because a trip to Mars and back could take two to three years, with far more exposure to radiation than on the space station. On the whole, the results suggest that the human body is remarkably resilient, recovering from most of the changes caused by a year in orbit. Yet some effects clearly need further investigation before astronauts travel that far.\n\nThe researchers themselves are careful not to claim too much. A study of only two people cannot prove what will happen to every astronaut, so they see it as a starting point rather than a final answer. As more people spend longer periods in space, their bodies will help complete the picture that the Kelly twins began.", "passage_zh": "研究人體的科學家常常希望，能拿一個人和另一個過著不同生活、卻一模一樣的「複製人」來比較。2015 年，NASA 找到了最接近這個理想的方法。太空人史考特‧凱利在國際太空站待了 340 天，而他的同卵雙胞胎兄弟、已經退休的太空人馬克則留在地球上。由於兩人擁有相同的基因，任務期間他們之間出現的任何差異，很可能都是太空生活造成的。\n\n十個研究團隊在飛行前、飛行中和飛行後，收集了兩兄弟的血液、尿液和其他樣本，並在 2019 年發表了主要的研究結果。其中最令人意外的發現和端粒有關。端粒是位在染色體末端的保護帽，常被比喻成鞋帶末端的塑膠套。端粒通常會隨著年齡增長而變短，所以科學家原本預期，在太空嚴苛的環境中，史考特的端粒會縮短。結果它們反而變長了。然而，他回到地球後幾天內，大部分的端粒又縮短了，有些甚至變得比飛行前還短。\n\n研究人員也發現，史考特有數千個基因的活動在軌道上發生了變化，可能是為了因應壓力、輻射和失重狀態。在他返回地球後的六個月內，這些變化大約有百分之九十消失了，但仍有少數與免疫系統以及修復受損 DNA 有關的變化持續存在。其他檢查也顯示，他的眼睛後方出現腫脹，一條重要動脈的管壁變厚，而且他回家之後，思考的速度和準確度都略微下降。\n\n並不是所有的新聞報導都正確理解了這項科學。有些標題宣稱史考特有百分之七的 DNA 改變了，兩兄弟也不再是同卵雙胞胎。NASA 很快就更正了這個錯誤。史考特的 DNA 本身並沒有改變；改變的是某些基因被開啟或關閉的程度。這有點像把一首歌的音量調大或調小：聽起來的聲音變了，但歌曲本身還是一樣。\n\n這項研究之所以重要，是因為往返火星一趟可能要花兩到三年，而且受到的輻射比在太空站上多得多。整體而言，研究結果顯示人體的恢復力相當驚人，在軌道上待了一年所造成的大多數變化都能復原。不過，在太空人前往那麼遠的地方之前，有些影響顯然還需要進一步研究。\n\n研究人員自己也很小心，不會過度宣稱研究的意義。一項只有兩個人的研究，無法證明每一位太空人都會發生什麼事，因此他們把它視為一個起點，而不是最終的答案。隨著越來越多人在太空中待得更久，他們的身體將幫助我們把凱利雙胞胎開始拼湊的這幅圖畫拼得更完整。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["What a study of twin astronauts revealed about the effects of a long stay in space", "Why identical twins should not be allowed to travel into space", "How Scott Kelly prepared his body for a trip to Mars", "Why news reports about science are usually wrong"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段介紹 NASA 以凱利雙胞胎進行研究，第二、三段說明史考特在太空中端粒、基因活動與身體的變化，第五、六段討論研究對火星任務的意義與限制，故選 (A)。(B) 文中沒有這種主張；(C) 史考特待的是國際太空站，並未前往火星；(D) 第四段只說「有些」報導有誤，而非新聞通常都錯。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why were the Kelly brothers especially useful for this study?", "options": ["Both of them had spent a year on the space station before.", "Mark was able to collect samples from Scott while he was in orbit.", "Since they share the same genes, differences between them could be linked to spaceflight.", "They were the only astronauts who agreed to give blood samples."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段：Because the two men share the same genes, any differences that appeared between them during the mission were likely to have been caused by life in space. 兩人基因相同，出現的差異就能歸因於太空生活，故選 (C)。(A) 文中只說史考特這次待了 340 天；(B) 樣本是由研究團隊收集，馬克留在地球上；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what happened to Scott's telomeres?", "options": ["They became shorter in space and stayed short after he returned.", "They became longer in space but mostly shortened again after he returned.", "They did not change at all during the mission.", "They grew longer after he returned to Earth."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：科學家原本預期端粒會縮短，Instead, they became longer. Within days of his return, however, most of them shortened again，即在太空中變長，回到地球後大多又縮短，故選 (B)。(A) 與 Instead, they became longer 相反；(C) 端粒明顯有變化；(D) 變長是發生在太空中，回到地球後反而縮短。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer compare gene activity to the volume of a song in paragraph 4?", "options": ["To show that Scott listened to music to reduce stress in space", "To prove that the brothers were no longer identical twins", "To explain why seven percent of Scott's DNA was damaged", "To make clear that his genes worked differently while his DNA stayed the same"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段說 Scott's DNA itself had not changed; what had changed was how strongly certain genes were switched on or off，接著用調整歌曲音量比喻：聲音變了，歌曲本身不變，目的是說明基因活動改變但 DNA 本身沒變，故選 (D)。(A) 文中未提到他聽音樂；(B)(C) 正是 NASA 更正的錯誤報導內容。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"resilient\" in paragraph 5 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["able to recover from difficult conditions", "easily damaged by small changes", "unable to change at all", "likely to become ill"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第五段 the human body is remarkably resilient, recovering from most of the changes caused by a year in orbit，後半句說明人體能從大多數變化中「復原」，resilient 意為「有恢復力的、能迅速復原的」，故選 (A)。(B)(D) 與「能復原」的意思相反；(C) 第二、三段顯示身體確實有變化，只是大多能恢復。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a102", "title": "Why Cities Run a Fever: The Urban Heat Island", "title_zh": "城市為什麼會發燒：都市熱島效應", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Science"], "passage": "On a summer evening in Taipei, walking from a leafy park onto a busy street lined with concrete buildings can feel like stepping into an oven. This is not just your imagination. Scientists have measured the difference for decades, and they have a name for it: the urban heat island. A city can be several degrees warmer than the countryside around it, and the gap tends to be largest after sunset, when rural areas cool quickly but the city stays stubbornly warm.\n\nThe main reason lies in the materials cities are built from. Asphalt roads, concrete walls and dark rooftops absorb a large share of the sunlight that falls on them and store it as heat. During the day they can become far hotter than the air; at night they slowly release that heat back into the streets. Fields and forests behave differently. Plants release water through their leaves, and as this water evaporates, it carries heat away, much as sweat cools the human body. When soil and trees are replaced by pavement, this natural cooling system disappears.\n\nHuman activity makes the problem worse. Cars, factories and, ironically, air conditioners all give off waste heat. On a hot night, thousands of air-conditioning units push warm air out of buildings and into narrow streets, which encourages people to turn their own machines up even higher. Tall buildings packed closely together also trap heat and block the wind that might otherwise carry it away.\n\nThis extra warmth is more than uncomfortable. During heat waves, high night-time temperatures prevent the body from recovering, which is why elderly people and those who work outdoors face serious health risks. Hotter cities also use more electricity for cooling, putting pressure on power grids at exactly the moment demand is highest.\n\nFortunately, the heat island is one environmental problem that cities can tackle locally. Painting roofs white or covering them with reflective coatings bounces sunlight back into the sky. Street trees provide shade and restore some of the cooling effect of evaporation. Some cities have gone further. In Seoul, a busy elevated highway was torn down in the early 2000s to uncover the Cheonggyecheon stream that had been buried beneath it, and studies found that the area along the restored stream became noticeably cooler than nearby roads.\n\nNone of these measures will stop global warming on its own, but they can make the hottest days more bearable for millions of people. As more of the world's population moves into cities, the way we design streets, roofs and parks may matter just as much as the air conditioners we buy.", "passage_zh": "在臺北的夏日傍晚，從綠樹成蔭的公園走到兩旁林立水泥建築的熱鬧街道上，感覺就像走進了烤箱。這不只是你的錯覺。科學家測量這種溫差已經有數十年，並為它取了一個名字：都市熱島。城市可能比周圍的鄉間高出好幾度，而且這個差距通常在日落之後最大——那時鄉間會迅速降溫，城市卻頑固地維持溫熱。\n\n主要原因在於建造城市的材料。柏油路、水泥牆和深色屋頂會吸收照射在它們上面的大部分陽光，並把它儲存成熱能。白天時，它們的溫度可能遠高於空氣；到了夜晚，它們又慢慢把這些熱釋放回街道上。田野和森林則不一樣。植物會透過葉子釋放水分，而當這些水分蒸發時，就會把熱帶走，就像汗水讓人體降溫一樣。當土壤和樹木被鋪面取代，這套天然的降溫系統也就消失了。\n\n人類的活動讓問題更加嚴重。汽車、工廠，以及諷刺的是，冷氣機，都會排出廢熱。在炎熱的夜晚，成千上萬臺冷氣機把熱空氣從建築物裡推到狹窄的街道上，這又促使人們把自家的冷氣開得更強。緊密相鄰的高樓也會困住熱氣，並擋住原本可能把熱帶走的風。\n\n這些額外的熱不只是讓人不舒服而已。熱浪期間，夜間的高溫讓身體無法恢復，這就是為什麼長者和在戶外工作的人會面臨嚴重的健康風險。較熱的城市也需要用更多電力來降溫，正好在用電需求最高的時刻對電網造成壓力。\n\n幸好，熱島是城市能在地方層級處理的環境問題之一。把屋頂漆成白色或塗上反光塗料，可以把陽光反射回天空。行道樹能提供遮蔭，並恢復部分蒸發所帶來的降溫效果。有些城市做得更多。在首爾，一條繁忙的高架道路在 2000 年代初期被拆除，讓原本被埋在下方的清溪川重見天日；研究發現，沿著這條復原溪流的區域明顯比附近的道路涼爽。\n\n這些措施沒有一項能單獨阻止全球暖化，但它們可以讓數百萬人在最炎熱的日子裡好過一些。隨著世界上越來越多人口移入城市，我們如何設計街道、屋頂和公園，可能和我們買什麼冷氣機一樣重要。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["Why air conditioners are the main cause of global warming", "Why cities are warmer than the countryside and what can be done about it", "How Seoul became the coolest city in Asia", "Why people should move from cities back to the countryside"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說明城市比鄉間溫暖的「都市熱島」現象，第二、三段解釋原因，第四段說明影響，第五段提出白色屋頂、行道樹、復原溪流等對策，故主旨為 (B)。(A) 冷氣機只是第三段提到的廢熱來源之一，且末段說這些措施無法單獨阻止全球暖化，文中並未說冷氣是暖化主因；(C) 首爾只是一個例子，文中沒說它是亞洲最涼爽的城市；(D) 末段反而提到越來越多人移入城市，並未建議搬回鄉下。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how do plants help keep an area cool?", "options": ["They block all sunlight from reaching the ground.", "They store heat during the day and release it at night.", "Water evaporating from their leaves carries heat away.", "They create wind that blows hot air out of the area."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Plants release water through their leaves, and as this water evaporates, it carries heat away, much as sweat cools the human body. 植物葉子釋出的水分蒸發時會帶走熱，故選 (C)。(B) 白天儲熱、夜晚釋放熱的是柏油路、水泥牆等城市材料，不是植物；(A)(D) 文中皆未提到。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"ironically\" in paragraph 3 suggests that ______.", "options": ["machines meant to cool people down end up making the streets hotter", "air conditioners are too expensive for most families", "air conditioners give off less heat than cars and factories", "people should never use air conditioners at night"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段說汽車、工廠，以及 ironically（諷刺的是）冷氣機都會排出廢熱：冷氣原本是用來降溫的，卻把熱空氣排到街道上，讓街道更熱，形成與原本目的相反的結果，故選 (A)。(B) 文中沒有提到價格；(C) 文中沒有比較冷氣與汽車、工廠排熱量的多寡；(D) 作者沒有要求人們晚上完全不開冷氣。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred about the Cheonggyecheon project in Seoul?", "options": ["It was built mainly to provide electricity for the city.", "It made the whole city of Seoul cooler than the countryside.", "It replaced an old stream with a modern highway.", "It showed that bringing back water and open space can lower local temperatures."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：首爾拆除高架道路，讓被埋在下方的清溪川重見天日，研究發現 the area along the restored stream became noticeably cooler than nearby roads，可推論恢復水域與開放空間能降低當地氣溫，故選 (D)。(A) 文中未提發電；(B) 變涼的只是溪流沿岸，並非整個首爾比鄉間涼；(C) 方向相反，是拆掉高架道路、恢復溪流。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's attitude toward solutions such as white roofs and street trees?", "options": ["Strongly opposed, because they cost too much", "Uninterested, because only global action matters", "Hopeful but realistic about what they can achieve", "Certain that they will put an end to global warming"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段以 Fortunately 開頭介紹這些對策，末段又說 None of these measures will stop global warming on its own, but they can make the hottest days more bearable，可見作者對其效果抱持希望，但也清楚它們的限制，故選 (C)。(A)(B) 與作者正面的語氣不符；(D) 與末段「無法單獨阻止全球暖化」相反。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a103", "title": "Blue Carbon: The Climate Heroes Growing in the Mud", "title_zh": "藍碳：生長在泥灘裡的氣候英雄", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Nature"], "passage": "When people think of forests that fight climate change, they usually picture the Amazon rainforest or the vast pine forests of the north. Few imagine a muddy coastline where twisted trees stand in salty water. Yet mangrove forests, which grow along tropical and subtropical coasts, are among the most effective natural carbon stores on Earth. Together with salt marshes and seagrass meadows, they hold what scientists call \"blue carbon\": carbon captured by ocean and coastal ecosystems.\n\nLike all plants, mangroves take in carbon dioxide from the air as they grow. What makes them special is what happens underground. Their roots sit in waterlogged mud that contains very little oxygen, so fallen leaves and dead roots break down extremely slowly. Instead of returning to the atmosphere, much of the carbon is buried and can remain locked in the soil for centuries. As a result, a hectare of mangroves can store up to four times as much carbon as a hectare of tropical rainforest, mostly beneath the surface.\n\nMangroves protect people as well as the climate. Their dense, tangled roots slow down waves and reduce the damage caused by storm surges. The shallow water among the roots also serves as a nursery, where young fish, crabs and shrimp can hide from predators until they are large enough to survive in open water. For millions of coastal families, healthy mangroves mean both safer homes and bigger catches.\n\nDespite these benefits, mangroves have been disappearing for decades. Large areas have been cleared for shrimp farms, ports and tourist resorts, while others have been cut down for timber and charcoal. According to the UN Food and Agriculture Organization, roughly a fifth of the world's mangroves were lost between 1980 and 2005. Worse still, destroying a mangrove does not simply stop it from absorbing carbon. When the mud is dug up or dried out, carbon stored over centuries can escape quickly, turning a carbon store into a source of greenhouse gases.\n\nTaiwan offers a small example of what protection can achieve. At the mouth of the Tamsui River grows a forest of a mangrove species known locally as shui bi zai, or \"water pen,\" named after its long, pen-shaped seedlings, which hang from the branches before dropping into the mud. The area has been a nature reserve since 1986, and today visitors can stroll along a wooden boardwalk and watch fiddler crabs waving their oversized claws at low tide.\n\nProtecting mangroves will never replace the need to cut emissions from cars and factories. However, scientists increasingly argue that blue carbon ecosystems deserve as much attention as rainforests. Saving a muddy shoreline may not sound as exciting as saving a jungle, but for the climate, it can be just as important.", "passage_zh": "當人們想到對抗氣候變遷的森林時，腦中浮現的通常是亞馬遜雨林或北方廣大的松樹林。很少人會想到一片泥濘的海岸，上面長著枝幹扭曲、泡在鹹水中的樹木。然而，生長在熱帶與亞熱帶海岸的紅樹林，卻是地球上最有效的天然儲碳場所之一。紅樹林與鹽沼、海草床一起儲存了科學家所說的「藍碳」：也就是由海洋與海岸生態系統所捕捉的碳。\n\n和所有植物一樣，紅樹林在生長時會從空氣中吸收二氧化碳。它們的特別之處在於地底下發生的事。紅樹林的根部位於浸滿水、幾乎不含氧氣的泥土中，所以落葉和死去的根分解得非常緩慢。這些碳大多不會回到大氣中，而是被埋藏起來，可以在土壤中封存好幾個世紀。因此，一公頃紅樹林所儲存的碳，最多可達一公頃熱帶雨林的四倍，而且大部分都藏在地表之下。\n\n紅樹林保護的不只是氣候，也保護人們。它們茂密、糾結的根能減緩海浪，降低暴潮造成的災害。根部之間的淺水區也是一座「育幼場」，幼小的魚、蟹和蝦可以躲在這裡避開掠食者，直到長大到能在開闊海域生存為止。對數百萬個沿海家庭來說，健康的紅樹林代表更安全的家園和更豐富的漁獲。\n\n儘管有這些好處，紅樹林數十年來仍不斷消失。大片紅樹林被砍除，用來興建養蝦場、港口和觀光度假村，還有一些則被砍來做木材和木炭。根據聯合國糧食及農業組織的資料，1980 年到 2005 年之間，全世界大約有五分之一的紅樹林消失了。更糟的是，破壞紅樹林不只是讓它無法再吸收碳而已。當泥土被挖開或變乾時，累積了好幾個世紀的碳可能會迅速釋放出來，使原本的儲碳場所變成溫室氣體的來源。\n\n臺灣提供了一個小小的例子，說明保護可以帶來什麼成果。淡水河口生長著一片紅樹林，樹種在當地被稱為「水筆仔」，名稱來自它細長、像筆一樣的胎生苗——這些幼苗會先掛在枝條上，之後才掉進泥灘裡。這個區域自 1986 年起成為自然保留區，如今遊客可以沿著木棧道漫步，在退潮時觀察招潮蟹揮舞著大得不成比例的螯。\n\n保護紅樹林永遠無法取代減少汽車和工廠排放的必要。不過，越來越多科學家主張，藍碳生態系統應該得到和雨林一樣多的關注。拯救一片泥濘的海岸線聽起來也許不像拯救一片叢林那麼令人興奮，但對氣候來說，它可能同樣重要。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To explain why mangroves are valuable and why they need protection", "To compare different ways of running a shrimp farm", "To teach readers how to plant a mangrove forest", "To argue that rainforests are no longer important for the climate"], "answer": 0, "explain": "全文說明紅樹林的儲碳能力（第二段）、保護海岸與漁業的功能（第三段）、遭破壞的情況（第四段），並以淡水河口為保護的例子，末段呼籲重視藍碳生態系統，故目的為 (A)。(B) 養蝦場只是第四段提到破壞紅樹林的原因之一；(C) 文中未教人種植；(D) 末段說藍碳應與雨林 as much attention，並未說雨林不重要。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, why can carbon stay in mangrove soil for a very long time?", "options": ["Mangrove trees live for thousands of years.", "Salt water slowly turns the carbon into rock.", "The waterlogged mud has little oxygen, so dead plant material breaks down slowly.", "Fish and crabs eat the fallen leaves before they can rot."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Their roots sit in waterlogged mud that contains very little oxygen, so fallen leaves and dead roots break down extremely slowly. 泥土缺氧使落葉和死根分解得很慢，碳因此被埋藏好幾個世紀，故選 (C)。(A)(B)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"nursery\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a hospital for injured sea animals", "a farm where shrimp are raised for sale", "a school for the children of fishing families", "a place where young creatures can grow up in safety"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段說根部之間的淺水區 serves as a nursery, where young fish, crabs and shrimp can hide from predators until they are large enough to survive，可知 nursery 在此指幼小生物能安全成長的地方（育幼場），故選 (D)。(A) 文中沒有提到受傷的動物；(B) 養蝦場是第四段破壞紅樹林的原因，與此無關；(C) 是 nursery「托兒所」的字面聯想，不合上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 4 about clearing mangroves to build shrimp farms?", "options": ["It is one of the best ways to reduce greenhouse gases.", "It can add carbon to the atmosphere instead of removing it.", "It stopped completely after 2005.", "It has made coastal homes safer from storms."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段說紅樹林被砍除以興建養蝦場等，且 When the mud is dug up or dried out, carbon stored over centuries can escape quickly, turning a carbon store into a source of greenhouse gases，可推知開闢養蝦場反而可能把碳釋放到大氣中，故選 (B)。(A) 與此相反；(C) 文中只提到 1980 到 2005 年的損失，未說之後完全停止；(D) 第三段指出紅樹林能保護海岸，砍除反而會讓家園較不安全。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is true about the mangroves at the mouth of the Tamsui River?", "options": ["They were planted by tourists in 1986.", "They have been protected as a nature reserve since 1986.", "Their name comes from the shape of their leaves.", "Visitors are not allowed to enter the area."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第五段：The area has been a nature reserve since 1986，故選 (B)。(A) 1986 年是成為保留區的年份，並非遊客種植；(C) 「水筆仔」的名稱來自 pen-shaped seedlings（筆狀的幼苗），不是葉子；(D) 文中說遊客可以 stroll along a wooden boardwalk（沿著木棧道漫步）。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a104", "title": "Green Labels, Grey Truths: How to Spot Greenwashing", "title_zh": "綠色標籤，灰色真相：如何識破「漂綠」", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Business"], "passage": "Walk down the aisle of any supermarket and you will see the color green everywhere: leaves printed on shampoo bottles, the word \"natural\" on snack bags, and promises that a product is \"eco-friendly\" or \"kind to the planet.\" As more shoppers worry about climate change and plastic pollution, companies have discovered that appearing environmentally responsible is good for sales. Unfortunately, looking green and being green are not the same thing. When a company makes itself or its products seem better for the environment than they really are, the practice is called greenwashing.\n\nThe term was coined in the 1980s by an American environmentalist, Jay Westerveld. He noticed a card in a hotel room asking guests to reuse their towels to \"save the environment.\" He suspected that the hotel cared less about the planet than about cutting its laundry bills, especially as it was busy expanding at the time. Since then, the tricks have become far more sophisticated.\n\nOne common trick is vague language. Words such as \"green,\" \"natural\" and \"eco\" sound positive but have no fixed meaning, so they are almost impossible to check. Another is the hidden trade-off, in which a company highlights one small benefit while ignoring much bigger problems, such as a clothing brand that promotes one collection made from recycled bottles while producing millions of cheap garments. A third is the irrelevant claim. A spray can labeled \"CFC-free\" may sound responsible, but CFCs have been banned in most countries for decades, so every competing product is free of them too.\n\nGreenwashing causes harm in several ways. It misleads shoppers who are genuinely trying to make better choices, and it rewards companies for clever advertising rather than for reducing pollution. It can also make people cynical: if every product claims to be green, consumers may stop believing environmental claims altogether, including honest ones. Governments have started to act. The European Union, for example, has passed rules banning general claims such as \"environmentally friendly\" unless companies can prove them.\n\nShoppers can protect themselves by asking a few simple questions. Is the claim specific, such as \"made from 70 percent recycled plastic,\" or is it just a pleasant-sounding word? Is it supported by a trusted, independent certification, or by a logo the company designed itself? Does the claim cover the whole product, or only a small part of it, like the cap or the box?\n\nNone of this means that every green label is a lie. Many businesses are making real efforts to cut waste and emissions. The point is that the color of a package should never replace evidence. A truly responsible product rarely needs to shout about being natural; it simply shows you the facts.", "passage_zh": "走進任何一家超市的走道，你都會看到到處都是綠色：洗髮精瓶身上印著樹葉，零食包裝上寫著「天然」，還有各種標榜產品「環保」或「對地球友善」的承諾。隨著越來越多消費者擔心氣候變遷和塑膠汙染，企業發現，讓自己看起來對環境負責有助於銷售。不幸的是，「看起來很綠」和「真的很綠」並不是同一回事。當一家公司讓自己或自家產品顯得比實際上更有益於環境時，這種做法就叫做「漂綠」。\n\n這個詞是在 1980 年代由美國環保人士傑伊‧韋斯特維爾德所創。他注意到飯店房間裡的一張卡片，請房客重複使用毛巾以「拯救環境」。他懷疑這家飯店在乎的其實不是地球，而是想減少洗衣費用，尤其當時這家飯店正忙著擴建。從那時起，這類手法已經變得精巧許多。\n\n一種常見的手法是使用含糊的字眼。像「綠色」、「天然」和「環保」這些字聽起來很正面，卻沒有固定的定義，因此幾乎無法查證。另一種是「隱藏的取捨」，也就是公司強調一個小小的優點，卻忽略大得多的問題——例如某個服飾品牌大力宣傳一個用回收寶特瓶製成的系列，同時卻生產數百萬件廉價衣服。第三種則是「無關的聲明」。一罐標示「不含氟氯碳化物」的噴霧聽起來很負責任，但氟氯碳化物在大多數國家早已禁用數十年，所以每一項同類競爭產品其實也都不含它。\n\n漂綠會在好幾個方面造成傷害。它誤導了真心想做出更好選擇的消費者，也讓企業因為巧妙的廣告、而不是因為減少汙染而獲得回報。它還可能讓人變得憤世嫉俗：如果每樣產品都宣稱自己很環保，消費者可能乾脆不再相信任何環保聲明，連誠實的也一併懷疑。各國政府已經開始採取行動。舉例來說，歐盟已通過規定，禁止企業使用「對環境友善」這類籠統的說法，除非能證明其真實性。\n\n消費者可以透過問幾個簡單的問題來保護自己。這項聲明具體嗎？例如「以 70% 回收塑膠製成」，還是只是一個聽起來很舒服的字眼？它有沒有可信的獨立認證支持，還是只有公司自己設計的標誌？這項聲明涵蓋的是整個產品，還是只有其中一小部分，例如瓶蓋或外盒？\n\n這並不代表每一個綠色標籤都是謊言。許多企業確實在努力減少廢棄物和排放。重點在於，包裝的顏色永遠不該取代證據。一項真正負責任的產品很少需要大聲強調自己有多天然；它只會把事實攤在你眼前。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["The history of environmental laws in the European Union", "Why green packaging always attracts more customers", "What greenwashing is, why it is harmful, and how to recognize it", "How hotels can save money by washing fewer towels"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段定義漂綠，第二段說明名稱由來，第三段介紹常見手法，第四段說明其害處，第五段教消費者如何辨識，故主旨為 (C)。(A) 歐盟規定只是第四段的一個例子；(B) 文中說綠色外觀有助銷售，但重點是它可能誤導人，且並未說「總是」吸引顧客；(D) 毛巾只是第二段說明名稱由來的故事。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Why did Jay Westerveld doubt the message on the hotel card?", "options": ["He thought the hotel mainly wanted to save money on laundry.", "The hotel did not provide any towels for its guests.", "The card was written in a language he could not read.", "The hotel charged guests extra for clean towels."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：He suspected that the hotel cared less about the planet than about cutting its laundry bills，他懷疑飯店真正在意的是減少洗衣費用，而且飯店當時正忙著擴建，故選 (A)。(B)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"cynical\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["careless", "distrustful", "generous", "excited"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：It can also make people cynical: if every product claims to be green, consumers may stop believing environmental claims altogether. 冒號後說明消費者可能什麼環保聲明都不再相信，可知 cynical 指「憤世嫉俗、不信任的」，與 distrustful 最接近，故選 (B)。(A) 粗心的、(C) 慷慨的、(D) 興奮的，都不符合「不再相信」的文意。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Following the advice in paragraph 5, which label is MOST likely to be a trustworthy environmental claim?", "options": ["\"100% natural and green!\"", "\"Eco-friendly cap\"", "\"CFC-free, for a cleaner planet\"", "\"Made from 70% recycled plastic,\" with a certificate from an independent organization"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段建議檢查聲明是否具體、是否有獨立認證、是否涵蓋整個產品。(D) 有具體數字，又有獨立機構的認證，最符合條件，故選 (D)。(A) 只是含糊的字眼（第三段 vague language）；(B) 只涉及瓶蓋這一小部分；(C) 是第三段所說的「無關的聲明」，因為氟氯碳化物早已禁用。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, why is a \"CFC-free\" label an example of an irrelevant claim?", "options": ["CFCs are actually good for the environment.", "CFCs were banned long ago, so similar products are free of them anyway.", "The label is too small for most shoppers to read.", "Only products made in Europe contain CFCs."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：CFCs have been banned in most countries for decades, so every competing product is free of them too. 氟氯碳化物早已禁用，同類產品本來就不含，標榜「不含」並沒有特別意義，故選 (B)。(A) 與事實相反；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a105", "title": "A Backup Copy for the World's Crops: The Svalbard Seed Vault", "title_zh": "為全世界的作物備份：斯瓦巴全球種子庫", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Science"], "passage": "Anyone who has lost an important computer file knows the value of a backup copy. Farmers and scientists face a similar risk on a much larger scale. Over thousands of years, people have developed an enormous variety of crops, from countless types of rice and wheat to beans and potatoes, each adapted to particular soils, climates and diseases. If these varieties disappear, the genetic information they carry is gone forever. To protect it, the world has built what is sometimes called the \"Doomsday Vault.\"\n\nThe Svalbard Global Seed Vault opened in 2008 on a remote Norwegian island in the Arctic Ocean, roughly halfway between mainland Norway and the North Pole. Its location was chosen with great care. The vault is dug deep into a sandstone mountain, high enough above sea level to stay dry even if polar ice melts. The surrounding permafrost, ground that stays frozen all year round, acts as a natural freezer, while cooling machines keep the storage rooms at about minus 18 degrees Celsius, a temperature at which many seeds can survive for decades or even centuries.\n\nAlthough the building belongs to the Norwegian government, the seeds inside do not. Instead, the vault works like a safe-deposit box at a bank. Gene banks around the world send duplicate samples of the seeds they already hold, and only the bank that deposited a box is allowed to take it out again. Today the vault holds well over a million samples from almost every country on Earth.\n\nFor years, many people assumed the vault would only be needed after a global catastrophe. Then, in 2015, the first withdrawal took place for a very human reason: war. A research center that had kept an important gene bank in Aleppo, Syria, could no longer work there safely because of the conflict. It requested its backup seeds, planted them in Lebanon and Morocco, harvested new seeds, and later sent fresh copies back to Svalbard. The system had worked exactly as intended.\n\nClimate change, one of the very threats the vault was designed to guard against, has also tested the vault itself. After an unusually warm and wet autumn in 2016, meltwater from thawing permafrost leaked into the entrance tunnel. The water never reached the seeds, but Norway spent millions of dollars making the entrance more waterproof.\n\nOf course, frozen seeds alone cannot feed the world. Crop varieties also need to be grown in real fields, where they continue to adapt to changing conditions. Still, as rising temperatures, new pests and extreme weather put pressure on agriculture, the quiet rooms beneath the Arctic snow offer humanity something valuable: a second chance.", "passage_zh": "任何曾經弄丟重要電腦檔案的人，都知道備份的價值。農民和科學家面臨著類似的風險，只是規模大得多。數千年來，人類培育出種類極多的作物，從數不清的稻米和小麥品種，到各種豆類和馬鈴薯，每一種都適應了特定的土壤、氣候和病害。如果這些品種消失，它們所攜帶的遺傳資訊就會永遠失去。為了保護這些資訊，世界建造了一座有時被稱為「末日種子庫」的設施。\n\n斯瓦巴全球種子庫於 2008 年啟用，位於北極海中一座偏遠的挪威島嶼上，大約在挪威本土和北極點的中間。它的地點經過審慎挑選。種子庫深深挖進一座砂岩山中，海拔高度足以讓它即使在極地冰層融化時也能保持乾燥。周圍的永凍土——也就是一整年都維持結凍的土地——就像一座天然冷凍庫，而冷卻機器則讓儲藏室保持在大約攝氏零下 18 度，在這個溫度下，許多種子可以存活數十年甚至數百年。\n\n雖然這棟建築屬於挪威政府，裡面的種子卻不是。這座種子庫的運作方式就像銀行的保險箱。世界各地的基因庫把自己已經保存的種子複製一份樣本寄來，而且只有存放箱子的那個基因庫才能把它取出來。如今，種子庫保存了遠超過一百萬份樣本，來自地球上幾乎每一個國家。\n\n多年來，許多人以為只有在全球性的大災難之後才會用到這座種子庫。然而，2015 年第一次提領種子，原因卻非常「人為」：戰爭。一個曾在敘利亞阿勒坡保存重要基因庫的研究中心，因為戰亂而無法再安全地在當地運作。它申請取回備份的種子，在黎巴嫩和摩洛哥種植、收成新的種子，之後再把新的複本送回斯瓦巴。這套系統完全按照原本的設計發揮了作用。\n\n氣候變遷正是這座種子庫原本要防範的威脅之一，而它也考驗了種子庫本身。2016 年一個異常溫暖又潮濕的秋天過後，永凍土融化的水滲進了入口隧道。融水從未碰到種子，但挪威仍花了數百萬美元，讓入口更加防水。\n\n當然，光靠冷凍的種子無法養活全世界。作物品種也需要在真正的田地裡種植，才能持續適應不斷變化的環境。儘管如此，隨著氣溫上升、新的病蟲害和極端天氣對農業造成壓力，北極冰雪下那些安靜的儲藏室為人類提供了一樣寶貴的東西：第二次機會。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer mention losing an important computer file in paragraph 1?", "options": ["To compare the seed vault to a backup that protects something irreplaceable", "To show that seeds can now be stored on computers", "To warn readers about the danger of computer viruses", "To explain how scientists record genetic information"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段先說弄丟電腦檔案的人都知道備份的價值，接著說作物品種一旦消失，遺傳資訊就 gone forever，再引出種子庫，可見作者是以「備份檔案」比喻種子庫保護無法取代的作物資源，故選 (A)。(B)(C)(D) 都誤解了這個比喻，文中並未提到用電腦儲存種子、電腦病毒或記錄遺傳資訊的方法。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT mentioned as a reason the vault's location was chosen?", "options": ["It is high enough above sea level to stay dry.", "The permafrost around it keeps it naturally cold.", "It is dug deep inside a sandstone mountain.", "It is close to the world's major farming areas."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段提到種子庫 dug deep into a sandstone mountain (C)、high enough above sea level to stay dry (A)，以及周圍的 permafrost acts as a natural freezer (B)。文中說它位於偏遠的北極島嶼，並未說靠近主要農業區，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In paragraph 3, the vault is compared to a \"safe-deposit box\" at a bank because ______.", "options": ["people must pay a high fee every time they use it", "it keeps other people's property safe, and only the owner can take it out", "it is located inside a bank in Norway", "the seeds inside are sold to whoever offers the most money"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段：建築屬於挪威政府，但種子不屬於挪威；各地基因庫寄存種子，而且 only the bank that deposited a box is allowed to take it out again，就像銀行保險箱只有存放者能取出自己的東西，故選 (B)。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，(D) 也與「只有存放者能取出」相矛盾。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What happened after the first withdrawal of seeds in 2015?", "options": ["The seeds were destroyed because of the war in Syria.", "The vault stopped accepting seeds from other gene banks.", "The seeds were grown in Lebanon and Morocco, and new copies were sent back to Svalbard.", "Norway took ownership of the seeds from Syria."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：研究中心取回備份種子後 planted them in Lebanon and Morocco, harvested new seeds, and later sent fresh copies back to Svalbard，故選 (C)。(A) 種子並未被毀，反而成功種植；(B) 文中未提及；(D) 與第三段「種子不屬於挪威」相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the last paragraph?", "options": ["The vault has made growing crops in fields unnecessary.", "The writer sees the vault as valuable but not a complete solution.", "Seeds stored in the vault can adapt to climate change on their own.", "The writer thinks the vault should be moved to a warmer place."], "answer": 1, "explain": "末段先說 frozen seeds alone cannot feed the world，作物仍須在田裡種植以持續適應環境，接著用 Still 轉折，稱種子庫給人類 a second chance，可推論作者認為種子庫很寶貴，但並非萬全的解決辦法，故選 (B)。(A) 與「仍需在田地種植」相反；(C) 文中說的是在田裡生長的作物才能持續適應，不是冷凍的種子；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a106", "title": "The Hole That Is Healing: Lessons from the Ozone Layer", "title_zh": "正在癒合的破洞：臭氧層給我們的啟示", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "History"], "passage": "High above our heads, mostly between 15 and 35 kilometers up, lies a thin layer of gas that makes life on land possible. The ozone layer absorbs most of the sun's harmful ultraviolet (UV) radiation, which can cause skin cancer and eye damage in humans and harm crops and tiny ocean plankton. For most of history, nobody knew this invisible shield existed, let alone that human activity could damage it.\n\nIn 1974, two chemists, Mario Molina and Sherwood Rowland, published a warning. They argued that chlorofluorocarbons, or CFCs, chemicals widely used in refrigerators, air conditioners and spray cans, could drift into the upper atmosphere, where sunlight would break them apart and release chlorine. Each chlorine atom could then destroy ozone molecules again and again, and later estimates suggested that one atom could destroy as many as 100,000. Many manufacturers dismissed the idea as alarmist, since CFCs were cheap, stable and seemed perfectly harmless.\n\nThe proof arrived in dramatic form. In 1985, scientists from the British Antarctic Survey reported that ozone levels over Antarctica had been dropping sharply every spring. Satellite images soon revealed a vast area of thinning ozone, which the media nicknamed the \"ozone hole.\" The public was shocked, and suddenly the issue could no longer be ignored.\n\nWhat happened next is often described as the most successful environmental agreement in history. In 1987, countries signed the Montreal Protocol, agreeing to reduce and eventually end the production of CFCs and other ozone-destroying chemicals. The agreement was strengthened several times as the science became clearer, and in 2009 it became the first treaty in United Nations history to be ratified by every member country. Industries that had warned that replacing CFCs would be extremely costly developed substitutes faster than expected. In 1995, Molina, Rowland and the Dutch scientist Paul Crutzen shared the Nobel Prize in Chemistry for their work.\n\nThe results are now visible. A 2023 assessment backed by the United Nations concluded that the ozone layer is on track to recover to its 1980 levels over most of the world by around 2040, and over Antarctica by around 2066. Because many CFCs are also powerful greenhouse gases, the treaty has also helped to slow global warming.\n\nWhy did the world succeed with ozone when progress on climate change has been so much slower? Part of the answer is that only a limited number of chemicals and industries were involved, and replacements were available. Climate change, by contrast, is linked to almost every part of modern life. Even so, the ozone story offers a hopeful lesson: when the science is clear, the public is concerned and governments act together, even a global environmental problem can be turned around.", "passage_zh": "在我們頭頂上方，大多位於 15 到 35 公里高的地方，有一層薄薄的氣體，讓陸地上的生命得以存在。臭氧層吸收了太陽大部分有害的紫外線，這些紫外線會使人罹患皮膚癌、傷害眼睛，也會損害農作物和海洋中微小的浮游生物。在人類歷史的大部分時間裡，沒有人知道這面看不見的盾牌存在，更別說知道人類活動可能會破壞它。\n\n1974 年，兩位化學家馬里奧‧莫利納和舍伍德‧羅蘭發表了一項警告。他們主張，氟氯碳化物（CFCs）——一種廣泛用於冰箱、冷氣機和噴霧罐的化學物質——可能會飄到高層大氣，在那裡被陽光分解並釋放出氯。每一個氯原子接著就能一再地破壞臭氧分子，後來的估計顯示，一個氯原子就可能破壞多達十萬個臭氧分子。許多製造商認為這種說法是危言聳聽而不予理會，因為氟氯碳化物便宜、穩定，而且看起來完全無害。\n\n證據以戲劇性的方式出現了。1985 年，英國南極調查局的科學家發表報告，指出南極上空的臭氧濃度每年春天都急劇下降。衛星影像很快就顯示出一大片臭氧變稀薄的區域，媒體稱之為「臭氧洞」。大眾大為震驚，這個問題突然再也無法被忽視。\n\n接下來發生的事，常被形容為史上最成功的環境協定。1987 年，各國簽署了《蒙特婁議定書》，同意減少並最終停止生產氟氯碳化物和其他會破壞臭氧的化學物質。隨著科學證據越來越明確，這項協定經過了數次強化；到了 2009 年，它成為聯合國歷史上第一個獲得所有會員國批准的條約。那些曾警告取代氟氯碳化物將非常昂貴的產業，研發替代品的速度比預期快得多。1995 年，莫利納、羅蘭與荷蘭科學家保羅‧克魯岑因為這方面的研究，共同獲得諾貝爾化學獎。\n\n如今成果已經清楚可見。一份由聯合國支持的 2023 年評估報告指出，世界大部分地區上空的臭氧層可望在 2040 年左右恢復到 1980 年的水準，南極上空則約在 2066 年恢復。由於許多氟氯碳化物也是強效的溫室氣體，這項條約也連帶幫助減緩了全球暖化。\n\n為什麼世界在臭氧問題上能成功，在氣候變遷上的進展卻慢了這麼多？部分原因是，臭氧問題只牽涉到數量有限的化學物質和產業，而且已經有替代品可用。相較之下，氣候變遷幾乎與現代生活的每個層面都有關。即使如此，臭氧的故事仍然帶給我們充滿希望的啟示：當科學證據明確、大眾關心，而且各國政府共同行動時，即使是全球性的環境問題也能被扭轉。", "questions": [{"q": "Which of the following best expresses the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["CFCs are still widely used in refrigerators around the world.", "The ozone layer will never recover from the damage caused by humans.", "Protecting the ozone layer shows that global cooperation can solve an environmental problem.", "Climate change is a smaller problem than the ozone hole."], "answer": 2, "explain": "全文依序說明臭氧層的功能、氟氯碳化物的危害、臭氧洞的發現、《蒙特婁議定書》的成功與臭氧層的恢復，末段總結 when the science is clear, the public is concerned and governments act together, even a global environmental problem can be turned around，故選 (C)。(A) 議定書已逐步停止生產氟氯碳化物；(B) 與第五段「可望恢復」相反；(D) 末段反而說氣候變遷牽涉更廣、更難解決。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, how do CFCs damage the ozone layer?", "options": ["They block sunlight from reaching the upper atmosphere.", "Sunlight breaks them apart, releasing chlorine that destroys ozone.", "They turn ozone into ultraviolet radiation.", "They kill the plankton that produce ozone."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：CFCs 飄到高層大氣後，sunlight would break them apart and release chlorine，而每個氯原子會一再破壞臭氧分子，故選 (B)。(A)(C) 文中皆未提及；(D) 第一段只說紫外線會傷害浮游生物，並未說浮游生物製造臭氧。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"dismissed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["rejected as not worth considering", "explained in great detail", "supported with new evidence", "kept secret from the public"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：Many manufacturers dismissed the idea as alarmist, since CFCs were cheap, stable and seemed perfectly harmless. 製造商認為這種說法危言聳聽，又覺得氟氯碳化物無害，可知 dismissed 指「不予理會、駁回」，故選 (A)。(B)(C) 與製造商的態度相反；(D) 這項警告已公開發表，並非被保密。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "According to the 2023 assessment, when is the ozone layer over Antarctica expected to return to its 1980 levels?", "options": ["Around 2009", "Around 2023", "Around 2040", "Around 2066"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段：the ozone layer is on track to recover to its 1980 levels over most of the world by around 2040, and over Antarctica by around 2066，南極上空約在 2066 年恢復，故選 (D)。(C) 2040 年是世界大部分地區的恢復時間；(A) 2009 年是條約獲所有會員國批准的年份；(B) 2023 年是評估報告發表的年份。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to the last paragraph, why was the ozone problem easier to solve than climate change?", "options": ["Scientists understood climate change much better than ozone.", "It involved fewer chemicals and industries, and substitutes were available.", "Only rich countries were affected by the ozone hole.", "The public has never been interested in climate change."], "answer": 1, "explain": "末段：only a limited number of chemicals and industries were involved, and replacements were available，而氣候變遷則幾乎與現代生活的每個層面相關，故選 (B)。(A)(C)(D) 文中皆未提及，(A) 也與作者的推論方向不符。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a107", "title": "Cities That Drink the Rain", "title_zh": "會「喝」雨水的城市", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Society"], "passage": "For most of the twentieth century, engineers treated rainwater in cities as a waste product. The goal was to get rid of it as quickly as possible: rain fell on roofs and roads, ran into drains, and rushed through concrete channels into the nearest river or sea. This worked well enough for ordinary storms. But as climate change brings heavier and more sudden downpours, many cities are discovering that their pipes simply cannot carry water away fast enough.\n\nThe problem is partly caused by the city itself. In a forest or a field, much of the rain soaks into the soil, where it is stored and slowly released. In a modern city, however, most surfaces, such as roofs, roads and parking lots, are impermeable, meaning that water cannot pass through them. During a heavy storm, nearly every drop runs off at once, and drains designed decades ago overflow. Streets turn into rivers and basements fill with water.\n\nA different idea, often called the \"sponge city,\" turns the traditional approach upside down. Instead of fighting rainwater, a sponge city tries to absorb, store and reuse it, much as a natural landscape does. The term became widely known after China launched a national sponge city program in 2015, but similar ideas have shaped cities from Rotterdam to Copenhagen.\n\nThe tools of a sponge city are surprisingly simple. Permeable pavements let rain sink into the ground below. Green roofs, covered with soil and plants, hold water and release it gradually. Rain gardens, which are shallow planted areas beside roads, collect runoff from the street. Even public spaces can be designed to flood on purpose. In Rotterdam, for instance, a \"water square\" serves as a basketball court and meeting place on dry days but fills up like a pond during a storm, holding the water until the drains can cope.\n\nCopenhagen offers a striking example of why such ideas matter. In July 2011, a cloudburst dropped more rain on the city in a couple of hours than it normally receives in two months, causing damage worth hundreds of millions of euros. In response, the city drew up a plan to redesign streets so that they guide floodwater toward parks and the harbor.\n\nSponge cities are not a perfect solution. They need space, careful maintenance and cooperation between planners and residents, and no design can absorb every extreme storm. Yet they offer more than flood protection. Green areas that store water also cool the air, provide homes for birds and insects, and give city dwellers pleasant places to relax. In a warming world, learning to work with the rain rather than against it may be one of the smartest choices a city can make.", "passage_zh": "在二十世紀的大部分時間裡，工程師把城市中的雨水當成一種廢棄物。目標是盡快把它排掉：雨水落在屋頂和道路上，流進排水溝，再沿著水泥渠道奔流到最近的河川或大海。這種做法對一般的暴雨還算有效。但隨著氣候變遷帶來更猛烈、更突然的豪雨，許多城市發現，它們的管線根本來不及把水排走。\n\n這個問題有一部分是城市本身造成的。在森林或田野中，大部分的雨水會滲入土壤，在那裡被儲存起來，再慢慢釋放。然而在現代城市裡，大多數的表面，例如屋頂、道路和停車場，都是不透水的，也就是說水無法穿過它們。下大雨時，幾乎每一滴雨都會同時流走，而數十年前設計的排水溝就會滿溢。街道變成河流，地下室也跟著淹水。\n\n一種常被稱為「海綿城市」的不同理念，把傳統做法完全顛倒過來。海綿城市不和雨水對抗，而是像自然地景一樣，嘗試吸收、儲存並再利用雨水。這個名詞在中國於 2015 年推出全國性的海綿城市計畫後廣為人知，但從鹿特丹到哥本哈根，類似的理念也早已影響了許多城市的樣貌。\n\n海綿城市的工具出奇地簡單。透水鋪面讓雨水能滲入下方的地面。覆蓋著土壤和植物的綠屋頂可以留住雨水，再慢慢釋放。雨水花園——也就是道路旁略為低窪、種有植物的區域——能收集從街道流下來的逕流。就連公共空間也可以刻意設計成會淹水的地方。例如在鹿特丹，一座「水廣場」在晴天時是籃球場和聚會場所，暴雨時則會像池塘一樣蓄滿水，把水留住，直到排水系統能夠應付為止。\n\n哥本哈根提供了一個引人注目的例子，說明這些理念為什麼重要。2011 年 7 月，一場暴雨在短短一兩個小時內降在這座城市的雨量，比它平常兩個月的雨量還多，造成高達數億歐元的損失。為此，市政府擬定了一項計畫，重新設計街道，讓街道把洪水導向公園和港口。\n\n海綿城市並不是完美的解決辦法。它們需要空間、細心的維護，以及規劃者和居民之間的合作，而且沒有任何設計能吸收每一場極端暴雨。然而，它們提供的不只是防洪。能儲水的綠地也能讓空氣降溫、為鳥類和昆蟲提供棲地，並讓城市居民有舒適的地方可以放鬆。在一個逐漸暖化的世界裡，學會與雨水合作而非對抗，可能是一座城市所能做出最聰明的選擇之一。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Old drainage pipes should simply be replaced with bigger ones.", "Cities can reduce flooding by absorbing and storing rainwater instead of only draining it away.", "Rotterdam and Copenhagen have the best public sports facilities in Europe.", "Climate change has made heavy rain less common in cities."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一、二段說明傳統排水方式在豪雨下不敷使用，第三段提出「海綿城市」——吸收、儲存並再利用雨水，第四、五段舉例說明做法，末段評估其優缺點，故主旨為 (B)。(A) 文中並未主張只換更大的管線，而是改變思維；(C) 籃球場只是水廣場的例子；(D) 與第一段 heavier and more sudden downpours 相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"impermeable\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["easy to clean", "very old and damaged", "covered with plants", "not letting water pass through"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：most surfaces... are impermeable, meaning that water cannot pass through them，句中 meaning that 直接解釋了這個字：「水無法穿過」，故選 (D)。(C) 覆蓋植物的是第四段的綠屋頂，正好相反；(A)(B) 與文意無關。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What is special about the \"water square\" in Rotterdam?", "options": ["It is used for sports on dry days and stores water during storms.", "It pumps rainwater directly into the sea.", "It is open to the public only in winter.", "It was built to provide drinking water for the city."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：a \"water square\" serves as a basketball court and meeting place on dry days but fills up like a pond during a storm，晴天是籃球場，暴雨時則蓄水，故選 (A)。(B) 文中說它把水留住直到排水系統能應付，並非直接抽進海裡；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why does the writer mention the 2011 cloudburst in Copenhagen?", "options": ["To show that sponge cities cannot work in northern Europe", "To prove that cities no longer need drains", "To explain why the city began to plan for extreme rain in a new way", "To compare the weather in Copenhagen with the weather in China"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段開頭說哥本哈根是 a striking example of why such ideas matter，接著描述 2011 年暴雨造成嚴重損失，In response 市政府擬定計畫，重新設計街道把洪水導向公園和港口，可見作者提這場暴雨是為了說明該市為何開始用新方式因應極端降雨，故選 (C)。(A) 與文意相反；(B) 第四段提到仍需等排水系統 cope，並未說不需要排水溝；(D) 文中未比較兩地天氣。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following best describes the writer's view of sponge cities in the last paragraph?", "options": ["They are useless because they take up too much space.", "They can completely prevent every kind of flood.", "They have limits, but their benefits make them worth pursuing.", "They should only be built in large Asian cities."], "answer": 2, "explain": "末段先承認海綿城市 not a perfect solution（需要空間、維護與合作，也無法吸收每一場極端暴雨），再用 Yet 轉折，指出它們還能降溫、提供棲地與休憩空間，並稱之為 one of the smartest choices，可見作者認為它有限制但值得推動，故選 (C)。(A) 作者並未說它沒用；(B) 與 no design can absorb every extreme storm 相反；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a108", "title": "Fix It, Don't Bin It: The Fight for the Right to Repair", "title_zh": "修好它，別丟掉：爭取「維修權」的行動", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Technology"], "passage": "When a smartphone screen cracks or the battery no longer lasts a full day, many owners face a frustrating choice. An official repair can cost almost as much as a new phone, and independent repair shops may be unable to get the right parts. Faced with these obstacles, millions of people simply buy a new phone, and the old one ends up in a drawer or a rubbish bin.\n\nMultiplied across the world, these small decisions create an enormous environmental problem. According to a United Nations report, the world produced about 62 million tonnes of electronic waste in 2022, and only around a fifth of it was documented as properly collected and recycled. Discarded electronics contain toxic substances such as lead and mercury, which can leak into soil and water. They also contain valuable metals like gold and copper, which are wasted when devices are dumped or burned. Moreover, most of a smartphone's carbon footprint comes from manufacturing it, not using it, so keeping a phone working for longer is one of the simplest ways to reduce its impact.\n\nThis is why a growing movement is demanding the \"right to repair.\" Its supporters argue that people who buy a product should be able to fix it themselves or take it to any repair shop they choose. To make that possible, manufacturers would need to sell spare parts at reasonable prices, publish repair manuals, and stop using software locks that prevent replacement parts from working unless the company itself installs them.\n\nSome governments have begun to listen. In 2021, France began requiring products such as smartphones and laptops to display a \"repairability index,\" a score out of 10 showing how easy they are to fix. In 2024, the European Union adopted a law requiring manufacturers to offer repairs for certain products, such as washing machines and smartphones, even after the warranty has expired.\n\nManufacturers have often resisted these changes. They argue that opening devices can be dangerous, that poorly trained repairers may damage products, and that sharing detailed designs could reveal trade secrets. Some of these concerns are reasonable; lithium batteries, for example, can catch fire if handled carelessly. Critics point out, however, that companies also earn more when customers replace devices instead of repairing them.\n\nUltimately, the debate is about our relationship with the things we own. For decades, consumers have been encouraged to treat electronics as disposable and to upgrade every year or two. The right-to-repair movement promotes a different attitude, one in which a cracked screen is a small problem to be solved rather than an excuse to start again. If it succeeds, the greenest phone may turn out to be the one already in your pocket.", "passage_zh": "當智慧型手機的螢幕摔裂，或電池撐不到一整天時，許多手機主人都會面臨一個令人沮喪的選擇。官方維修的費用可能幾乎和買一支新手機一樣貴，而獨立維修店又可能拿不到合適的零件。面對這些阻礙，數百萬人乾脆直接買新手機，舊手機則被丟進抽屜或垃圾桶。\n\n這些小小的決定放大到全世界，就形成了一個巨大的環境問題。根據聯合國的一份報告，2022 年全世界產生了大約 6,200 萬公噸的電子廢棄物，其中只有大約五分之一有紀錄顯示經過妥善的回收處理。被丟棄的電子產品含有鉛和汞等有毒物質，可能滲入土壤和水中。它們也含有金和銅等珍貴金屬，當這些裝置被傾倒或焚燒時，這些金屬就浪費掉了。此外，一支智慧型手機的碳足跡大部分來自製造過程，而不是使用過程，所以讓手機用得更久，是減少其影響最簡單的方法之一。\n\n這就是為什麼有越來越多人投入一項運動，要求「維修權」。支持者主張，人們買了一項產品，就應該能夠自己修理，或拿去任何自己選擇的維修店修理。要做到這一點，製造商必須以合理的價格販售備用零件、公開維修手冊，並停止使用軟體鎖——這種鎖會讓更換的零件除非由原廠安裝，否則無法正常運作。\n\n有些政府已經開始聽取這些意見。2021 年，法國開始要求智慧型手機和筆記型電腦等產品必須標示「可維修指數」，以滿分 10 分的分數顯示產品有多容易修理。2024 年，歐盟通過一項法律，要求製造商即使在保固期結束後，也必須為洗衣機、智慧型手機等特定產品提供維修服務。\n\n製造商常常抗拒這些改變。他們主張，拆開裝置可能很危險，訓練不足的維修人員可能會弄壞產品，而且公開詳細的設計可能會洩漏商業機密。其中有些顧慮是合理的；例如鋰電池如果處理不慎，就可能起火。不過，批評者指出，當顧客換新而不是修理裝置時，企業也能賺到更多錢。\n\n說到底，這場爭論關乎我們與自己所擁有的物品之間的關係。數十年來，消費者一直被鼓勵把電子產品當作用過即丟的東西，每一兩年就換新一次。維修權運動提倡一種不同的態度：螢幕裂了只是一個有待解決的小問題，而不是重新買一支的藉口。如果這項運動成功，最環保的手機，可能就是已經在你口袋裡的那一支。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main purpose of the passage?", "options": ["To advertise a new type of long-lasting smartphone battery", "To explain the right-to-repair movement and the debate around it", "To teach readers how to replace a cracked phone screen", "To argue that people should stop using electronic devices"], "answer": 1, "explain": "全文先說明手機難以維修的情況與電子廢棄物問題，再介紹維修權運動的訴求（第三段）、各國的立法（第四段）、製造商的反對與批評者的回應（第五段），末段總結，故目的為 (B)。(A)(C) 文中沒有推銷電池或教人換螢幕；(D) 作者主張的是讓裝置用得更久，不是停止使用電子產品。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to the UN report, about how much of the electronic waste produced in 2022 was documented as properly recycled?", "options": ["About one fifth", "About half", "About 62 percent", "Almost all of it"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：the world produced about 62 million tonnes of electronic waste in 2022, and only around a fifth of it was documented as properly collected and recycled，故選 (A)。(C) 62 是電子廢棄物的總量（6,200 萬公噸），不是百分比；(B)(D) 與 only around a fifth 不符。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is NOT something the right-to-repair movement asks manufacturers to do?", "options": ["Sell spare parts at reasonable prices", "Publish repair manuals", "Stop using software locks on replacement parts", "Give customers a free new phone every two years"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段列出運動的訴求：sell spare parts at reasonable prices (A)、publish repair manuals (B)、stop using software locks (C)。文中從未要求每兩年免費換新手機，這反而與「讓手機用得更久」的主張相反，故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "The word \"disposable\" in paragraph 6 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["expensive and valuable", "difficult to use", "meant to be thrown away after a short time", "easy to repair at home"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第六段：consumers have been encouraged to treat electronics as disposable and to upgrade every year or two，每一兩年就換新，可知 disposable 指「用過即丟的」，故選 (C)。(D) 與文意相反，正是維修權運動想改變的態度；(A)(B) 與上下文無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by saying \"the greenest phone may turn out to be the one already in your pocket\"?", "options": ["Green-colored phones are better for the environment.", "Phones should be kept in a pocket to protect them from damage.", "Using your current phone for longer is often better for the environment than buying a new one.", "New phones are now made entirely from recycled materials."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段指出手機的碳足跡大多來自製造過程，因此 keeping a phone working for longer 是減少影響最簡單的方法；末段這句話的意思是：繼續使用（並修理）手上的手機，往往比買新手機更環保，故選 (C)。(A) 誤把 greenest（最環保的）當成顏色；(B)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a109", "title": "Too Hot for Boys? Climate Change and the Future of Sea Turtles", "title_zh": "熱到生不出公的？氣候變遷與海龜的未來", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Environment", "Nature"], "passage": "For humans and most other mammals, whether a baby is male or female is decided by its genes at the moment of conception. Sea turtles follow a very different rule. For them, sex is determined by temperature, specifically the temperature of the sand surrounding the eggs during the middle stage of their development. This unusual system has served sea turtles well for more than a hundred million years, but a warming climate is now putting it under pressure.\n\nThe rule itself is surprisingly simple. Eggs that develop in cooler sand tend to hatch as males, while those in warmer sand tend to become females. For many species, the \"pivotal temperature,\" at which roughly equal numbers of males and females are produced, is around 29 degrees Celsius. Just a degree or two above that, a nest may produce almost nothing but female hatchlings.\n\nIn 2018, researchers studying green turtles on Australia's Great Barrier Reef published a striking result. Among young turtles that had hatched on the warmer beaches of the northern reef, more than 99 percent were female. Older adults from the same population were also mostly female, but far less extremely so, which suggests that the imbalance has grown as temperatures have risen over recent decades. Similar trends have been reported on nesting beaches in other parts of the world.\n\nAt first, a population with more females might not seem like a crisis, since one male can mate with several females. In the short term, it may even increase the number of eggs laid. The danger lies further ahead. If males become too rare, many eggs may never be fertilized. At even higher temperatures, a more serious problem appears: the embryos may not survive at all, so that no turtles of either sex hatch.\n\nConservationists are experimenting with ways to cool things down. On some beaches, volunteers place shade cloth over nests or gently sprinkle them with water to lower the temperature of the sand. Elsewhere, eggs are moved to shaded hatcheries where conditions can be controlled. Taiwan has a part to play as well. Green turtles still come ashore to lay eggs on a few beaches here, most famously on Wang-an Island in Penghu, where nesting sites are protected during the summer breeding season.\n\nThese measures can buy time, but they treat the symptoms rather than the cause. In the past, sea turtles survived major climate shifts, partly by changing when and where they nested. The question is whether they can adapt quickly enough this time, when temperatures are rising unusually fast. Their future, in other words, may depend less on what happens on the beach than on what humans decide to do about greenhouse gas emissions.", "passage_zh": "對人類和大多數其他哺乳動物來說，寶寶是公是母，在受孕的那一刻就由基因決定了。海龜卻遵循一套非常不同的規則。對牠們而言，性別是由溫度決定的——確切地說，是蛋在發育中期時周圍沙子的溫度。這套不尋常的機制讓海龜順利生存了超過一億年，但如今氣候暖化正讓它承受壓力。\n\n這套規則本身出奇地簡單。在較涼的沙中發育的蛋，往往會孵出公海龜；在較溫暖的沙中發育的蛋，則往往會變成母海龜。對許多海龜物種來說，能孵出大約相同數量公、母海龜的「關鍵溫度」約為攝氏 29 度。只要比這個溫度高出一兩度，一窩蛋孵出的可能幾乎全是母海龜。\n\n2018 年，研究人員在澳洲大堡礁研究綠蠵龜，發表了一項驚人的結果。在大堡礁北部較溫暖的海灘上孵化的年輕海龜中，超過 99% 是母的。同一族群中年紀較大的成龜也以母龜居多，但比例遠沒有那麼極端，這顯示隨著近幾十年來氣溫上升，性別失衡的情況愈來愈嚴重。世界其他地區的產卵海灘也有類似趨勢的報告。\n\n乍看之下，一個母龜較多的族群似乎不算危機，因為一隻公龜可以和好幾隻母龜交配。短期內，這甚至可能增加產下的蛋的數量。危險在於更遙遠的未來。如果公龜變得太稀少，許多蛋可能永遠無法受精。而在更高的溫度下，會出現更嚴重的問題：胚胎可能根本無法存活，結果公龜和母龜都孵不出來。\n\n保育人士正在嘗試各種降溫的方法。在一些海灘上，志工會在巢上方架設遮陽網，或輕輕地灑水，以降低沙子的溫度。在其他地方，蛋會被移到有遮蔭、能控制環境條件的孵化場。臺灣也能盡一份心力。綠蠵龜仍會上岸到臺灣的幾處海灘產卵，其中最有名的是澎湖的望安島，那裡的產卵地在夏季繁殖期間會受到保護。\n\n這些措施可以爭取時間，但它們治標不治本。過去，海龜曾經熬過重大的氣候變化，部分原因是牠們改變了築巢的時間和地點。問題在於，這一次在氣溫上升得異常快速的情況下，牠們能否適應得夠快。換句話說，牠們的未來取決於人類決定如何處理溫室氣體排放，可能更甚於海灘上發生的事。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How sea turtles find their way back to the beaches where they were born", "How rising temperatures threaten the balance of male and female sea turtles", "Why green turtles prefer to lay their eggs in Taiwan", "How scientists count the eggs in a sea turtle nest"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一、二段說明海龜的性別由沙子溫度決定，第三段以大堡礁研究說明母龜比例大增，第四段說明其危險，第五、六段談保育措施與根本原因，故主旨為 (B)。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(C) 臺灣望安島只是第五段的一個例子，文中沒說綠蠵龜偏好在臺灣產卵。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, what usually happens to eggs that develop in warmer sand?", "options": ["They hatch more quickly as males.", "They produce larger and stronger turtles.", "They tend to hatch as females.", "They produce equal numbers of males and females."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：Eggs that develop in cooler sand tend to hatch as males, while those in warmer sand tend to become females. 較溫暖的沙中孵出的多半是母海龜，故選 (C)。(A) 與文意相反，較涼的沙才孵出公海龜；(D) 公母數量大致相等是在約攝氏 29 度的「關鍵溫度」時；(B) 文中未提及。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the 2018 study on the Great Barrier Reef suggest?", "options": ["The share of female turtles has increased as the beaches have warmed.", "Most of the older adult turtles in the population were male.", "Turtles on cooler beaches had stopped nesting altogether.", "Green turtles can no longer be found on the Great Barrier Reef."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：北部較暖海灘孵出的年輕海龜超過 99% 是母的，而年紀較大的成龜雖也以母龜居多，但 far less extremely so，這 suggests that the imbalance has grown as temperatures have risen，故選 (A)。(B) 文中說成龜也是 mostly female；(C)(D) 文中皆未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The phrase \"buy time\" in the last paragraph is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["spend a lot of money", "waste time and effort", "solve a problem forever", "delay a problem for a while"], "answer": 3, "explain": "末段：These measures can buy time, but they treat the symptoms rather than the cause. 遮陽、灑水等措施只能治標、不能治本，所以 buy time 指「爭取時間、暫時延緩問題」，故選 (D)。(C) 與「治標不治本」相反；(A) 是 buy 的字面意思，不合文意；(B) 作者並未認為這些措施是白費力氣。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why could a population with too few males eventually decline?", "options": ["The females would stop coming ashore to lay eggs.", "Many eggs might never be fertilized.", "The males would eat many of the eggs.", "The sand on the beaches would become too cool."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段：If males become too rare, many eggs may never be fertilized. 公龜太少時，許多蛋可能無法受精，族群就可能衰退，故選 (B)。(A)(C) 文中未提及；(D) 與全文「沙子愈來愈熱」的情況相反。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a110", "title": "Waiting for the Second Marshmallow: What the Famous Test Really Shows", "title_zh": "等待第二顆棉花糖：著名實驗真正告訴我們的事", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Education"], "passage": "In the late 1960s, the psychologist Walter Mischel and his team at Stanford University invited young children from the university's nursery school into a small room and placed a treat, such as a marshmallow, in front of them. The deal was simple: a child could eat the treat right away, or wait alone until the researcher returned, which could take up to twenty minutes, and then receive two. Some children covered their eyes, turned their chairs around or sang quietly to themselves, while others gave in within seconds.\n\nThe experiment became famous because of what happened years later. When Mischel followed up with some of the original participants as teenagers, he found that those who had waited longer tended to have higher SAT scores, cope better with stress and be described by their parents as more focused. The message seemed clear: self-control at the age of four could predict success later in life. Countless books and teachers repeated the idea, and \"Don't eat the marshmallow!\" became a popular motto for discipline.\n\nHowever, more recent research has complicated this neat story. In 2012, researchers at the University of Rochester added a twist. Before the marshmallow test, one group of children was promised better art supplies, and the adult kept the promise; another group was let down when the adult returned empty-handed. Children who had learned that the adult was reliable waited, on average, about four times longer than those who had been disappointed. For a child who has learned that promises are often broken, eating the treat immediately is not a lack of willpower but a reasonable choice.\n\nThen, in 2018, a larger study tested the original claim with more than 900 children from a much wider range of backgrounds. It did find a link between waiting and later school achievement, but the link was only about half as strong as earlier studies had suggested, and it became much smaller once the researchers took into account factors such as family income and the home environment. In other words, a child's ability to wait may partly reflect his or her circumstances rather than a fixed personality trait.\n\nNone of this means that self-control is unimportant. Rather, the newer findings suggest that it can be shaped by experience and environment, which is encouraging news. Mischel himself argued that the children who succeeded relied on strategies, such as distracting themselves, instead of pure willpower, and strategies can be taught. For students fighting distractions at their desks, the lesson may be not to stare at the marshmallow, but to put it somewhere out of sight.", "passage_zh": "1960 年代後期，心理學家沃爾特‧米歇爾和他在史丹佛大學的團隊，邀請該校附設幼兒園的孩子進入一間小房間，並在他們面前放一樣點心，例如一顆棉花糖。規則很簡單：孩子可以馬上吃掉點心，也可以獨自等待，直到研究人員回來——最久可能要等上二十分鐘——然後就能得到兩份。有些孩子遮住眼睛、把椅子轉過去，或小聲地對自己唱歌，另一些孩子則在幾秒鐘內就忍不住了。\n\n這個實驗之所以出名，是因為多年後發生的事。米歇爾追蹤部分當年的受試者到他們青少年時期，發現當初等得比較久的孩子，SAT 成績往往比較高、比較能應付壓力，父母也說他們比較專注。這個訊息似乎很明確：四歲時的自制力可以預測日後的成功。無數書籍和老師一再重複這個觀念，「別吃棉花糖！」也成了一句常用來勉勵自律的口號。\n\n然而，較新的研究讓這個簡潔的故事變得複雜了。2012 年，羅徹斯特大學的研究人員在實驗中加了一個轉折。在棉花糖測驗之前，大人答應其中一組孩子會給他們更好的美術用品，而且信守承諾；另一組孩子則失望了，因為大人空著手回來。已經體認到大人很可靠的孩子，平均等待的時間大約是失望那組孩子的四倍。對一個已經學到「承諾常常不會兌現」的孩子來說，立刻吃掉點心並不是缺乏意志力，而是合理的選擇。\n\n接著在 2018 年，一項規模更大的研究以九百多名來自更多元背景的孩子，重新檢驗原本的說法。研究確實發現等待能力和日後的學業成就有關，但關聯的強度只有早期研究所顯示的一半左右；而且在研究人員把家庭收入和家庭環境等因素納入考量之後，這個關聯變得小得多。換句話說，孩子能不能等待，可能有一部分反映的是他所處的環境，而不是固定不變的人格特質。\n\n這些都不代表自制力不重要。相反地，新的研究結果顯示，自制力可以受到經驗和環境的塑造，這是令人振奮的消息。米歇爾本人就認為，成功等到的孩子靠的是策略，例如轉移自己的注意力，而不是單純的意志力，而策略是可以教的。對於坐在書桌前和各種讓人分心的事物奮戰的學生來說，啟示或許是：不要盯著棉花糖看，而是把它放到看不見的地方。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How Mischel designed a perfect intelligence test for young children", "Why young children should not be given sweets at school", "How newer research has changed our understanding of a famous self-control study", "Why the University of Rochester proved that Mischel's study was a failure"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一、二段介紹米歇爾的棉花糖實驗及其著名結論，第三、四段說明 2012 年與 2018 年的新研究 complicated this neat story，第五段再說明新發現的意義，故主旨為 (C)。(A) 該實驗測的是自制力，並非智力測驗；(B) 文中未討論學校該不該給孩子甜食；(D) 羅徹斯特大學的研究只是補充新觀點，並未證明原研究失敗，第五段也說自制力仍然重要。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 3, what happened in the 2012 study?", "options": ["Children who had been let down by an adult waited for a much shorter time.", "Children who were promised art supplies refused to eat the marshmallow at all.", "Children who waited longer were given better art supplies as a reward.", "The researchers found that the adult's reliability made no difference."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段：Children who had learned that the adult was reliable waited, on average, about four times longer than those who had been disappointed，可知被大人失信的孩子等待時間短得多，故選 (A)。(B) 文中沒說他們完全不吃；(C) 美術用品是在測驗「之前」承諾的，並非等待後的獎勵；(D) 與研究結果相反，大人是否可靠影響很大。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"complicated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["proved completely false", "made more famous", "explained clearly", "made less simple"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段首句 more recent research has complicated this neat story，接著說明新研究讓「自制力決定成功」這個簡潔（neat）的結論多了其他因素，因此 complicated 是「使變得複雜、不再那麼單純」，與 made less simple 最接近，故選 (D)。(A) 新研究並未完全推翻原研究，2018 年研究仍發現有關聯；(B)(C) 都不符合句意。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from paragraph 4 about the earlier follow-up studies?", "options": ["They included more children than the 2018 study did.", "They may have given too much credit to self-control alone.", "They were carried out only with children from poor families.", "They proved that family income has no effect on school results."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第四段指出 2018 年的研究發現關聯 only about half as strong as earlier studies had suggested，而且考慮家庭收入與家庭環境後關聯變得更小，可推知早期研究可能高估了自制力本身的影響，故選 (B)。(A) 2018 年的研究才是 larger study；(C) 早期受試者來自史丹佛大學附設幼兒園，2018 年研究的背景才 much wider，文中並未說早期受試者來自貧困家庭；(D) 與文意相反，家庭收入會影響結果。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"not to stare at the marshmallow, but to put it somewhere out of sight\" in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Students should remove distractions instead of relying only on willpower.", "Students should not eat sweets while they are studying.", "Students should reward themselves with snacks after finishing their work.", "Students should hide their snacks so that classmates cannot take them."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段先說成功的孩子靠的是 strategies, such as distracting themselves, instead of pure willpower，再把這個道理應用到 students fighting distractions at their desks，可知「把棉花糖放到看不見的地方」是比喻：與其硬靠意志力，不如把會讓人分心的東西移開，故選 (A)。(B)(C)(D) 都把棉花糖當成真正的零食，誤解了這個比喻。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a111", "title": "Forget 21 Days: How Long Does It Really Take to Form a Habit?", "title_zh": "忘掉二十一天吧：養成一個習慣到底需要多久？", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Health", "Life"], "passage": "If you have ever decided to start a new habit, you have probably been told that it takes just 21 days. The number appears in countless self-help books, apps and social media posts, and it is attractive because it sounds achievable: three weeks of effort, and the new behavior will run on its own. Unfortunately, the evidence behind this popular claim is surprisingly weak.\n\nThe idea seems to come from Maxwell Maltz, an American plastic surgeon who wrote a best-selling book in 1960. He noticed that his patients needed a minimum of about 21 days to get used to their new faces, and he suggested that people generally need at least that long to adjust to a change. Over the years, his careful word \"minimum\" was quietly dropped, and \"at least 21 days\" turned into \"21 days,\" a neat rule that Maltz had never actually tested.\n\nIn 2009, a research team led by Phillippa Lally at University College London decided to measure habit formation directly. They asked 96 volunteers to choose one simple daily behavior, such as eating a piece of fruit with lunch or going for a fifteen-minute run before dinner, and to perform it in the same situation every day. Each day, the participants reported how automatic the behavior felt; in other words, whether they did it without having to think about it or push themselves.\n\nThe results were far less tidy than the 21-day rule. On average, it took 66 days for a behavior to reach its highest level of automaticity, but the range was enormous: one person needed only 18 days, while for others the researchers estimated that it would take as long as 254 days. Simple actions, like drinking a glass of water, generally became automatic sooner than demanding ones, like exercise. Interestingly, missing a single day did not seriously damage the process, so an occasional slip need not be a reason to give up.\n\nWhat does this mean for students who want to review vocabulary every evening or stop checking their phones during homework? First, expect the process to take months rather than weeks, and do not feel like a failure when day 22 still requires effort. Second, attach the new behavior to a fixed cue, such as \"after I finish dinner, I open my word list,\" because a consistent situation is what allows the brain to link the action to its trigger. Finally, start small. A habit that feels almost too easy at first is more likely to survive long enough to become automatic.", "passage_zh": "如果你曾經下定決心要養成一個新習慣，大概有人告訴過你：只要二十一天就夠了。這個數字出現在無數的勵志書、應用程式和社群媒體貼文裡。它很吸引人，因為聽起來做得到：努力三個星期，新的行為就會自動運作。可惜的是，這個流行說法背後的證據出奇地薄弱。\n\n這個說法似乎來自美國整形外科醫師麥斯威爾‧馬爾茲，他在 1960 年寫了一本暢銷書。他注意到，病人最少需要大約二十一天才能習慣自己的新面孔，並因此提出：人們通常至少需要這麼長的時間來適應一項改變。多年下來，他謹慎使用的「最少」一詞被悄悄拿掉，「至少二十一天」變成了「二十一天」，成了一條馬爾茲本人從未實際驗證過的簡潔規則。\n\n2009 年，倫敦大學學院由菲莉帕‧拉莉帶領的研究團隊，決定直接測量習慣是如何養成的。他們請 96 名志願者各自選擇一個簡單的日常行為，例如午餐時吃一份水果，或晚餐前跑步十五分鐘，並且每天在相同的情境下執行。參與者每天回報這個行為感覺有多「自動」，也就是說，他們是否不必多想、也不必勉強自己就會去做。\n\n研究結果遠不如「二十一天規則」那樣整齊。平均來說，一個行為要花 66 天才會達到最高的自動化程度，但個別差異非常大：有一個人只需要 18 天，而研究人員估計，有些人可能要長達 254 天。簡單的行動，例如喝一杯水，通常比費力的行動（例如運動）更快變得自動。有趣的是，偶爾漏掉一天並不會嚴重破壞這個過程，所以偶爾鬆懈一次，不必成為放棄的理由。\n\n這對想每天晚上複習單字，或想在寫作業時不再一直查看手機的學生來說，代表什麼呢？第一，要有心理準備：這個過程需要的是幾個月而不是幾個星期，到了第二十二天仍然需要努力，也不要覺得自己失敗了。第二，把新的行為和一個固定的提示連結起來，例如「吃完晚餐後，我就打開單字表」，因為一致的情境才能讓大腦把這個行動和它的觸發點連在一起。最後，從小處開始。一個剛開始簡單到幾乎太容易的習慣，比較有可能撐得夠久，直到變成自動自發。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the writer's main purpose in this passage?", "options": ["To prove that Maxwell Maltz was a dishonest doctor", "To correct a popular belief about habits and give more realistic advice", "To encourage students to try plastic surgery to change their habits", "To explain why exercise is the most important habit for students"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段說「二十一天養成習慣」的說法 the evidence behind this popular claim is surprisingly weak，第二到四段說明這個說法的由來與 2009 年研究的真實結果，第五段再提供學生實際可行的建議，故選 (B)。(A) 第二段只說他的「最少」被後人省略，並未指他不誠實；(C) 整形手術只是說法的來源背景；(D) 運動只是研究中的例子之一。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 2, where did the \"21 days\" idea come from?", "options": ["A study of 96 volunteers at University College London", "A popular app that rewarded users for keeping habits", "An experiment in which patients ate fruit with lunch every day", "A surgeon's observation of how long patients took to get used to their new appearance"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段：Maxwell Maltz, an American plastic surgeon... noticed that his patients needed a minimum of about 21 days to get used to their new faces，可知這個說法源自整形外科醫師觀察病人適應新面孔的時間，故選 (D)。(A)(C) 是第三段 2009 年拉莉的研究，其結果反而推翻了二十一天之說；(B) 文中只說應用程式會引用這個數字，並非其來源。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which of the following is TRUE about the results of the 2009 study?", "options": ["The average time was 66 days, but some people needed far longer.", "Every participant formed a habit within exactly 21 days.", "Exercise habits became automatic sooner than drinking water did.", "Missing one day forced participants to start the process again."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：On average, it took 66 days...but the range was enormous，有人只要 18 天，有人估計要 254 天，故選 (A)。(B) 與研究結果不符；(C) 同段說簡單的行動（喝水）比費力的行動（運動）更快變得自動，正好相反；(D) 同段說 missing a single day did not seriously damage the process。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"automatic\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["done with the help of a machine", "done only once in a while", "done without having to think about it", "done in order to please others"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段在 automatic 之後用 in other words 解釋：whether they did it without having to think about it or push themselves，即「不必思考、不必勉強就會去做」，故選 (C)。(A) 是 automatic 在機器方面的意思，不符本文語境；(B)(D) 與上下文無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Which student is following the writer's advice most closely?", "options": ["Ken plans to study for three hours every night and expects the habit to be fixed in three weeks.", "Lily reviews her notes whenever she happens to remember, at different times each day.", "Sam gives up his plan after he forgets to practice the guitar one evening.", "Mei practices five English words every night right after she brushes her teeth."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第五段建議：要有花上數月的心理準備、把新行為連結到固定提示（fixed cue），並且 start small。Mei 每晚刷牙後（固定提示）練習五個單字（從小處開始），最符合建議，故選 (D)。(A) 一開始就每晚三小時，又期待三週就養成，違反 start small 及「需要數月」的提醒；(B) 時間不固定，缺少一致的情境；(C) 第四段說漏掉一天不必放棄。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a112", "title": "The Waiter Who Never Forgot: Why Unfinished Tasks Stay on Our Minds", "title_zh": "從不忘記的服務生：為什麼未完成的事總是掛在心上", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Education"], "passage": "According to a well-known story, in the 1920s a group of psychologists in Berlin often met at a café near their university. Their teacher, Kurt Lewin, noticed something curious about one of the waiters. The man could remember long, complicated orders for a whole table without writing anything down, yet once the bill had been paid, the details seemed to vanish from his memory completely. One of Lewin's students, a young woman from Lithuania named Bluma Zeigarnik, decided to find out whether this was a general feature of the human mind.\n\nIn her experiments, Zeigarnik gave volunteers around twenty short tasks, such as solving puzzles, doing simple math problems and making small figures out of clay. She allowed them to finish some of the tasks but interrupted the others before they were done. Later, when the volunteers were asked to list the tasks they could remember, they recalled the interrupted ones about twice as often as the completed ones. Zeigarnik published her findings in 1927, and this tendency came to be known as the Zeigarnik effect.\n\nLewin explained the result in terms of tension. When we start a task, he suggested, the mind creates a kind of inner pressure that is released only when the task is complete. Until then, the unfinished task keeps knocking at the door of our thoughts. Later researchers have pointed out that the effect does not appear in every study and depends on how much a person cares about the task. Still, most of us recognize the feeling of a half-written essay that follows us to bed.\n\nTelevision writers have long taken advantage of this tendency. An episode that ends just before a secret is revealed leaves viewers with an unfinished story that is hard to forget, which is why they come back the following week. The same principle can help students. Many people put off a difficult assignment because getting started feels painful. However, if you work on it for just five minutes, the task becomes \"unfinished\" rather than \"not started,\" and that small amount of tension may pull you back to it later.\n\nThe effect also has a downside: too many open tasks can crowd the mind and make it hard to relax or sleep. Fortunately, you do not always have to finish a task to quiet it. In a 2011 study, people who were reminded of an unfinished goal were far less distracted by it once they had written down a specific plan for when and how they would complete it. So, on a busy night, try making a short list of tomorrow's tasks before going to bed. Your brain may then be willing to let them wait until morning.", "passage_zh": "根據一個廣為流傳的故事，1920 年代，柏林有一群心理學家常在大學附近的一家咖啡館聚會。他們的老師庫爾特‧勒溫注意到其中一位服務生有件奇特的事。這個人可以不用寫下任何東西，就記住整桌客人又長又複雜的點餐內容；可是一旦客人結完帳，這些細節似乎就從他的記憶中完全消失了。勒溫的一位學生，一位來自立陶宛、名叫布盧瑪‧蔡格尼克的年輕女子，決定要查明這是否是人類心智普遍具有的特性。\n\n在實驗中，蔡格尼克給志願者大約二十項簡短的任務，例如解謎題、做簡單的數學題，以及用黏土捏小人偶。她讓志願者完成其中一些任務，卻在其他任務完成之前打斷他們。之後，當志願者被要求列出自己記得的任務時，他們想起被打斷的任務的次數，大約是已完成任務的兩倍。蔡格尼克在 1927 年發表了她的研究結果，這種傾向後來就被稱為「蔡格尼克效應」。\n\n勒溫用「緊張」來解釋這個結果。他認為，當我們開始一項任務時，心裡會產生一種內在的壓力，只有在任務完成時才會釋放。在那之前，這項未完成的任務會一直敲著我們思緒的門。後來的研究人員指出，這個效應並不是在每項研究中都會出現，而且取決於一個人有多在乎這項任務。儘管如此，我們大多數人都熟悉那種感覺：一篇寫到一半的作文，會一路跟著你上床睡覺。\n\n電視編劇很早就懂得利用這種傾向。一集在祕密即將揭曉前結束的節目，會讓觀眾心裡留下一個難以忘懷、還沒結束的故事，這就是他們下週會再回來收看的原因。同樣的原理也能幫助學生。很多人會拖延困難的作業，因為開始動手的感覺很痛苦。然而，如果你只先做五分鐘，這項作業就從「還沒開始」變成「尚未完成」，而這一點點緊張感，可能會在之後把你拉回來繼續做。\n\n這個效應也有缺點：太多尚未完成的事會塞滿腦袋，讓人難以放鬆或入睡。幸好，你不一定要把事情做完才能讓它安靜下來。在一項 2011 年的研究中，被提醒有某個目標尚未完成的人，一旦寫下何時、如何完成它的具體計畫，受到它干擾的程度就大幅降低。所以，在忙碌的夜晚，睡前試著列一張明天要做的事的簡短清單吧。這樣一來，你的大腦或許就願意讓它們等到早上了。", "questions": [{"q": "Why does the writer mention the waiter in paragraph 1?", "options": ["To introduce the observation that led to Zeigarnik's research", "To show that waiters in Berlin had unusually good memories", "To complain about the poor service in cafés in the 1920s", "To explain why customers should always pay their bills quickly"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第一段描述服務生能記住未結帳的點餐，結帳後卻忘記，接著說蔡格尼克 decided to find out whether this was a general feature of the human mind，可知作者用這個故事引出蔡格尼克研究的起點，故選 (A)。(B) 重點不在服務生記憶力特別好，而在「未完成」與「已完成」的差別；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "In Zeigarnik's experiments, which tasks did the volunteers remember best?", "options": ["The tasks they found the easiest", "The tasks they had completed first", "The tasks that had been interrupted", "The tasks that involved making clay figures"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第二段：they recalled the interrupted ones about twice as often as the completed ones，被打斷的任務被記住的次數約為完成任務的兩倍，故選 (C)。(A)(B) 文中未提及任務難易或完成順序；(D) 捏黏土只是任務的例子之一，與記憶效果無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"vanish\" in paragraph 1 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["repeat", "disappear", "improve", "change"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段 yet once the bill had been paid, the details seemed to vanish from his memory completely，yet 表示與前面「記得又長又複雜的點餐」相反，所以結帳後細節從記憶中「消失」，vanish 與 disappear 意思最接近，故選 (B)。(A)(C)(D) 都無法和 completely 及前文的轉折語氣搭配。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, why might working on a hard assignment for just five minutes be helpful?", "options": ["Five minutes is enough time to finish most school assignments.", "Teachers give extra points to students who start early.", "It allows students to forget about the assignment completely.", "The assignment becomes unfinished, and that tension may draw the student back to it."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段：if you work on it for just five minutes, the task becomes \"unfinished\" rather than \"not started,\" and that small amount of tension may pull you back to it later，故選 (D)。(A) 文中沒說五分鐘就能完成作業；(B) 未提及老師加分；(C) 與蔡格尼克效應相反，未完成的事反而更不容易忘記。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "A student cannot fall asleep because she keeps thinking about everything she must do tomorrow. According to the passage, what is the best thing for her to do?", "options": ["Get up and try to finish all of the tasks before morning", "Watch one more episode of a TV drama to relax", "Write down a specific plan for when and how she will do the tasks", "Try hard not to think about any of the tasks at all"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第五段說不一定要把事情做完才能讓它安靜下來：2011 年的研究中，人們寫下 a specific plan for when and how they would complete it 後就比較不受干擾，並建議睡前列出明天的待辦清單，故選 (C)。(A) 文中說 you do not always have to finish a task；(B) 第四段反而說電視劇的懸念會讓人放不下；(D) 文中未建議硬逼自己不去想。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a113", "title": "25,000 Streets in Your Head: How London Taxi Drivers Changed Their Brains", "title_zh": "腦中裝著兩萬五千條街：倫敦計程車司機如何改變了自己的大腦", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Health"], "passage": "To drive one of London's famous black taxis, it is not enough to have a driver's license. Would-be drivers must pass a demanding exam known as \"the Knowledge,\" which requires them to memorize about 25,000 streets and thousands of landmarks, from hospitals to theaters, within six miles of Charing Cross in central London. Trainees typically spend three to four years riding around the city on scooters, learning routes by heart, and many of them give up before they ever qualify.\n\nFor the neuroscientist Eleanor Maguire of University College London, these drivers offered a rare natural experiment. In a study published in 2000, she and her colleagues used brain scans to compare licensed taxi drivers with men who did not drive taxis. They focused on the hippocampus, a seahorse-shaped structure deep inside the brain that plays a key role in memory and in finding our way around. The rear part of the hippocampus turned out to be larger in the taxi drivers, and the longer a driver had been doing the job, the larger it tended to be.\n\nThere was an obvious objection, however. Perhaps people who are born with a large hippocampus are simply more likely to become taxi drivers. To test this, Maguire and her colleague Katherine Woollett followed 79 trainees for several years, scanning their brains before and after their training, and compared them with a group of people who were not training. In 2011, they reported that only the trainees who passed the Knowledge showed growth in the rear hippocampus; those who failed or gave up, as well as the comparison group, showed no such change. This was strong evidence that intense learning can physically reshape the adult brain, an ability known as neuroplasticity.\n\nThe change, however, may come at a price. Taxi drivers in these studies tended to do worse than comparison groups on certain tests of new visual information, such as remembering a complex drawing. This suggests that the brain may balance a gain in one area against a cost in another, although researchers are still investigating exactly how this works.\n\nThese findings raise an interesting question for the smartphone age. When we follow turn-by-turn directions from a navigation app, we hardly need to build a mental map at all. A 2017 study from the same university found that activity in the hippocampus rose when volunteers planned their own routes through a simulation of London streets, but not when they simply followed instructions. Nobody is suggesting that we throw our phones away. Now and then, however, finding your own way might be good exercise for the brain.", "passage_zh": "想在倫敦開一輛著名的黑色計程車，光有駕照是不夠的。想當司機的人必須通過一項名為「倫敦知識大全」（the Knowledge）的嚴格考試，要背下倫敦市中心查令十字周圍六英里內大約兩萬五千條街道，以及從醫院到劇院等數千個地標。受訓者通常要花三到四年的時間騎著機車在城裡四處跑，把路線牢牢記住，而且很多人還沒取得資格就放棄了。\n\n對倫敦大學學院的神經科學家埃莉諾‧馬圭爾來說，這些司機提供了一個難得的天然實驗。在 2000 年發表的一項研究中，她和同事用腦部掃描比較了持有執照的計程車司機和不開計程車的男性。他們把焦點放在海馬迴上，那是位於大腦深處、形狀像海馬的構造，在記憶和認路方面扮演關鍵角色。結果發現，計程車司機海馬迴的後段比較大，而且司機做這份工作的時間愈久，這個部位往往就愈大。\n\n不過，有一個明顯的質疑。也許天生海馬迴就比較大的人，本來就比較可能成為計程車司機。為了驗證這一點，馬圭爾和她的同事凱瑟琳‧伍利特追蹤了 79 名受訓者好幾年，在他們受訓前後掃描大腦，並與一組沒有受訓的人做比較。2011 年，她們發表報告指出，只有通過考試的受訓者，海馬迴後段才出現增長；沒通過或中途放棄的人，以及對照組，都沒有這種變化。這有力地證明了密集的學習能在實質上重塑成人的大腦，這種能力稱為「神經可塑性」。\n\n然而，這種改變可能要付出代價。在這些研究中，計程車司機在某些關於新視覺資訊的測驗上，例如記住一幅複雜的圖畫，表現往往比對照組差。這顯示大腦可能會在某一方面的收穫和另一方面的損失之間取得平衡，不過研究人員仍在探究其中確切的運作方式。\n\n這些發現在智慧型手機時代引出了一個有趣的問題。當我們照著導航應用程式一個路口接一個路口的指示走，幾乎完全不需要在腦中建立地圖。同一所大學在 2017 年的一項研究發現，當志願者在模擬的倫敦街道中自己規劃路線時，海馬迴的活動會增加；但只是照著指示走的時候就不會。沒有人主張我們應該把手機丟掉。不過，偶爾自己找路，或許是讓大腦運動的好方法。", "questions": [{"q": "What does the passage mainly show?", "options": ["London taxi drivers are more intelligent than other drivers.", "Navigation apps have made the Knowledge exam unnecessary.", "People with a large hippocampus are born to be good drivers.", "Intense learning can change the structure of the adult brain."], "answer": 3, "explain": "全文以倫敦計程車司機為例：第二段發現司機的海馬迴後段較大，第三段的追蹤研究證明這是學習造成的，並點出 intense learning can physically reshape the adult brain，故選 (D)。(A) 文中未比較智力，第四段甚至提到司機某些測驗表現較差；(B) 文中未說考試已不必要；(C) 正是第三段被追蹤研究否定的說法。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 1, what is \"the Knowledge\"?", "options": ["A brain scan that all London drivers must take", "An exam that requires drivers to memorize London's streets and landmarks", "A navigation app designed for London's taxi drivers", "A course that teaches trainees how to ride scooters safely"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一段：a demanding exam known as \"the Knowledge,\" which requires them to memorize about 25,000 streets and thousands of landmarks，故選 (B)。(A) 腦部掃描是第二段研究人員使用的工具；(C) 導航應用程式出現在第五段，與考試無關；(D) 受訓者騎機車是為了熟記路線，不是學騎車。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "Why did Maguire and Woollett follow the trainees for several years?", "options": ["To help the trainees pass the Knowledge more quickly", "To find out how many trainees would give up their training", "To check whether the training itself caused the hippocampus to grow", "To prove that taxi drivers have worse memories than other people"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段先提出質疑：也許天生海馬迴大的人比較會成為計程車司機；To test this，她們追蹤受訓者受訓前後的大腦變化，目的是確認是否是訓練本身造成海馬迴增長，故選 (C)。(A) 研究並未協助受訓者；(B) 有人放棄只是研究結果的一部分，不是研究目的；(D) 記憶較差的部分是第四段提到的其他發現。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "The word \"objection\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["a reason to doubt something", "a goal to aim for", "an object in the brain", "a result of an exam"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第三段 There was an obvious objection, however. 下一句隨即提出「也許天生海馬迴大的人比較會當上計程車司機」，這是對第二段結論的質疑，因此 objection 指「反對意見、懷疑的理由」，故選 (A)。(B) 目標、(C) 腦中的物體、(D) 考試結果都不符合上下文。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer suggest about navigation apps in the last paragraph?", "options": ["They are dangerous and should be banned for taxi drivers.", "Relying on them all the time may give the hippocampus less work to do.", "They help people build better mental maps than paper maps do.", "They were invented by the scientists at University College London."], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一段說使用導航時 we hardly need to build a mental map at all，2017 年研究也發現只照指示走時海馬迴活動不會增加，作者因此建議偶爾自己找路，可見一直依賴導航可能讓海馬迴少了「運動」的機會，故選 (B)。(A) 作者明說 Nobody is suggesting that we throw our phones away，沒有主張禁止；(C) 與文意相反；(D) 文中未提及。故選 (B)。"}]}, {"id": "a114", "title": "Twenty-Four Jams: The Puzzle of Choice Overload", "title_zh": "二十四種果醬：「選擇過多」之謎", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Business", "Science"], "passage": "On two Saturdays in a California supermarket, shoppers came across a tasting booth offering free samples of jam. For some hours the table displayed 24 different flavors; at other times, it displayed only six. The psychologists behind the booth, Sheena Iyengar and Mark Lepper, wanted to know a simple thing: would having more options make people more likely to buy?\n\nThe large display certainly attracted more attention. About 60 percent of passing shoppers stopped to taste when 24 jams were on offer, compared with 40 percent for the smaller display. Yet when it came to actually buying, the pattern reversed dramatically. Only about 3 percent of the people who stopped at the large display went on to purchase a jar, while about 30 percent of those who saw just six flavors did. Published in 2000, the study suggested that too many choices can leave people confused and unable to decide, a problem that came to be called \"choice overload.\"\n\nThe jam study soon became one of the most famous experiments in psychology. In his 2004 book The Paradox of Choice, the psychologist Barry Schwartz argued that endless options in modern life, from breakfast cereals to phone plans, can make people anxious and less satisfied, because every option they reject becomes a possible regret. Businesses took note, and some companies reduced the number of products they offered in the hope of selling more.\n\nHowever, when other researchers tried to repeat the finding, the results were mixed. A 2010 review that combined the results of 50 experiments found that, on average, the choice overload effect was close to zero. In some studies, more options made people less likely to choose; in others, more options made people more likely to choose, or made no difference at all. This did not mean that the original study was wrong, but it did suggest that the effect appears only under certain conditions.\n\nLater research has helped to identify those conditions. Choice overload seems most likely when the options are hard to compare, when people do not already know what they want, and when they feel pressure to make the perfect choice. Someone who already loves strawberry jam will find it in a row of 24 within seconds; someone who has never tasted jam before may simply walk away. For students choosing a college department from a long list, the lesson is useful: deciding on your priorities before you look at the options can turn a frightening list into a manageable one.", "passage_zh": "在加州一家超市裡，連續兩個星期六，購物的人都會經過一個提供果醬免費試吃的攤位。有些時段桌上擺了 24 種不同口味，其他時段則只擺 6 種。設置這個攤位的心理學家希娜‧艾恩嘉和馬克‧萊珀，想知道一件簡單的事：選擇愈多，會不會讓人愈想買？\n\n大型的陳列確實比較吸引目光。擺出 24 種果醬時，約有 60% 經過的顧客停下來試吃；小型陳列則是 40%。然而，說到真正購買，情況卻大逆轉。在大型陳列前停下來的人當中，只有大約 3% 後來買了一罐；而只看到 6 種口味的人當中，約有 30% 買了。這項研究在 2000 年發表，顯示太多選擇可能讓人感到困惑、無法下決定，這個問題後來被稱為「選擇過多」。\n\n這項果醬研究很快就成為心理學界最著名的實驗之一。心理學家貝瑞‧史瓦茲在他 2004 年出版的《選擇的弔詭》（The Paradox of Choice）一書中主張，現代生活中無窮無盡的選項，從早餐穀片到手機資費方案，都可能讓人焦慮、比較不滿足，因為每一個被放棄的選項都可能成為遺憾。企業注意到了這一點，有些公司減少了所販售的產品種類，希望能賣得更多。\n\n然而，當其他研究人員嘗試重現這項結果時，結果卻不一致。一篇在 2010 年綜合了 50 項實驗結果的回顧研究發現，平均而言，「選擇過多」的效應幾乎是零。在某些研究中，選項愈多，人們愈不容易做出選擇；在另一些研究中，選項多反而讓人更願意選擇，或者根本沒有差別。這並不表示原本的研究是錯的，但確實顯示這個效應只會在某些條件下出現。\n\n後來的研究幫忙找出了這些條件。當選項難以互相比較、人們還不清楚自己想要什麼，以及覺得有壓力必須做出完美選擇的時候，似乎最容易發生選擇過多的情況。一個本來就愛吃草莓果醬的人，能在一排 24 種果醬中幾秒內就找到它；從沒嚐過果醬的人，則可能乾脆走開。對於要從一長串名單中選擇大學科系的學生來說，這個啟示很有用：在看選項之前先確定自己重視什麼，就能把一張令人害怕的清單，變成一張可以處理的清單。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the passage mainly about?", "options": ["How supermarkets can sell more jam by offering free samples", "Why Barry Schwartz's book became a best seller", "A famous finding about having too many options, and how later research has questioned it", "The reasons why strawberry jam is more popular than other flavors"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一、二段介紹果醬實驗與「選擇過多」的概念，第三段說明它的影響，第四、五段則指出後續研究結果不一致、效應只在某些條件下出現，故選 (C)。(A) 試吃只是實驗的方式，並非主旨；(B) 史瓦茲的書只在第三段提及，文中也沒說它是暢銷書；(D) 草莓果醬只是第五段的例子。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "Of the shoppers who stopped at the six-jam display, about how many bought a jar?", "options": ["3 percent", "30 percent", "40 percent", "60 percent"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段：about 30 percent of those who saw just six flavors did（買了一罐），故選 (B)。(A) 3% 是在 24 種果醬攤位停下的人的購買比例；(C) 40% 是經過小型陳列時停下試吃的比例；(D) 60% 是經過大型陳列時停下試吃的比例。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"reversed\" in paragraph 2 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["stayed the same", "became more popular", "was carefully checked", "turned the other way"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第二段先說大型陳列吸引較多人試吃，接著說 Yet when it came to actually buying, the pattern reversed dramatically，購買時反而是小型陳列的比例高出許多，可見情況「反過來了」，故選 (D)。(A) 與 Yet 表示的轉折相反；(B)(C) 都不符合上下文。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what did the 2010 review find?", "options": ["On average, having more options had almost no effect on choosing.", "The original jam study had used false numbers.", "More options always made people less likely to choose.", "Shoppers always prefer stores that offer fewer products."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段：A 2010 review... found that, on average, the choice overload effect was close to zero，故選 (A)。(B) 同段明說 This did not mean that the original study was wrong；(C) 同段指出有些研究中選項多反而讓人更願意選擇，並非 always；(D) 文中沒有這樣的結論。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Based on the last paragraph, who is MOST likely to experience choice overload?", "options": ["A boy who always orders the same drink at his favorite tea shop", "A girl who has already decided she wants a red bike under NT$5,000", "A student choosing between two pens that look almost the same", "A tourist who has never tried tea before and must pick one from 40 kinds"], "answer": 3, "explain": "最後一段指出，選擇過多最容易發生在選項難以比較、且 people do not already know what they want 的時候，就像從沒嚐過果醬的人可能乾脆走開。從沒喝過茶又要從 40 種中挑一種的觀光客最符合，故選 (D)。(A)(B) 都已經清楚自己要什麼；(C) 只有兩個選項，談不上選項過多。故選 (D)。"}]}, {"id": "a115", "title": "Is Anyone Going to Help? Rethinking the Bystander Effect", "title_zh": "會有人出手幫忙嗎？重新思考「旁觀者效應」", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Society", "Science"], "passage": "Late one night in March 1964, a young woman named Kitty Genovese was attacked and killed near her apartment building in New York City. Two weeks later, The New York Times reported that 38 of her neighbors had watched or listened to the attack and that none of them had called the police while it was happening. The story shocked the country and became a symbol of the coldness of modern city life.\n\nThe case inspired two psychologists, John Darley and Bibb Latané, to investigate why people fail to help. In a 1968 experiment, college students talked over an intercom with what they thought were other students in separate rooms. Suddenly, one of the voices seemed to have a seizure and called for help. When participants believed they were the only person who could hear, 85 percent went to help before the voice stopped. When they believed four other people were listening, only 31 percent did. The researchers called this a \"diffusion of responsibility\": the more people present, the more each one assumes that someone else will act.\n\nThis \"bystander effect\" became one of the best-known ideas in social psychology, and the Genovese story was told in textbooks for decades. Yet later investigations revealed that the newspaper's account had been seriously exaggerated. Few of the neighbors had actually seen the attack, and most heard only noises they did not understand. At least one person did call the police, and one neighbor rushed out and held Genovese in her arms as she was dying.\n\nMore recent research has also painted a more hopeful picture. In 2019, an international team studied security camera footage of more than 200 real public conflicts in the Netherlands, the United Kingdom and South Africa. In about nine out of ten cases, at least one bystander stepped in, whether by calming someone down or pulling people apart. What is more, the more bystanders there were, the more likely it was that somebody would intervene. Although each person in a crowd may feel less responsible, a larger crowd also contains more potential helpers.\n\nSo what should you do if you ever need help in public, or see someone else who does? Experts suggest breaking the diffusion of responsibility by making the request personal. Instead of shouting, \"Somebody call an ambulance!\", point to a specific person and say, \"You in the blue jacket, please call 119.\" Once a request is directed at one person, it becomes much harder for that person to assume that someone else will take care of it.", "passage_zh": "1964 年 3 月的一個深夜，一位名叫姬蒂‧吉諾維斯的年輕女子在紐約市自家公寓大樓附近遭到攻擊身亡。兩週後，《紐約時報》報導說，她有 38 位鄰居目睹或聽到了這場攻擊，但在事發過程中，沒有任何一個人報警。這則報導震驚全美，也成為現代都市生活冷漠無情的象徵。\n\n這個案件促使兩位心理學家約翰‧達利和比布‧拉塔內去研究人們為什麼不伸出援手。在 1968 年的一項實驗中，大學生透過對講機和他們以為是待在其他房間的學生交談。突然間，其中一個聲音似乎癲癇發作，並大聲求救。當參與者以為只有自己聽得到時，85% 的人在聲音停止之前就去幫忙；當他們以為還有另外四個人也在聽時，只有 31% 的人這麼做。研究人員稱之為「責任分散」：在場的人愈多，每個人就愈會認為別人會採取行動。\n\n這種「旁觀者效應」成為社會心理學中最廣為人知的概念之一，吉諾維斯的故事也在教科書中流傳了數十年。然而，後來的調查發現，那篇報導嚴重誇大了事實。實際目睹攻擊的鄰居很少，大部分人只聽到一些搞不清楚是什麼的聲音。至少有一個人確實報了警，還有一位鄰居衝出去，在吉諾維斯臨終時將她抱在懷裡。\n\n較新的研究也描繪出比較有希望的景象。2019 年，一個國際研究團隊分析了荷蘭、英國和南非兩百多起真實公共衝突的監視器影片。在大約十分之九的案例中，至少有一位旁觀者出手介入，不論是讓某人冷靜下來，還是把人拉開。不僅如此，旁觀者愈多，就愈有可能有人出面介入。雖然人群中的每個人可能覺得自己的責任比較小，但人群愈大，可能出手幫忙的人也愈多。\n\n那麼，如果你在公共場所需要幫助，或看到別人需要幫助時，該怎麼做呢？專家建議，把請求針對特定的人，以打破責任分散。與其大喊「誰來叫救護車！」，不如指著某個特定的人說：「穿藍色外套的那位，請幫忙打 119。」一旦請求是針對某一個人，那個人就很難再認為別人會處理了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["People living in big cities never help strangers in trouble.", "A famous idea about helping has been partly challenged, but it still offers useful lessons.", "The New York Times reported the Genovese case accurately.", "Security cameras have made it unnecessary for bystanders to help."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第一、二段介紹吉諾維斯案與旁觀者效應，第三、四段指出原報導被誇大、新研究顯示多數情況下有人出手，第五段再根據「責任分散」提出求助的實用方法，故選 (B)。(A) 正是第四段研究所反駁的觀念；(C) 第三段說報導 had been seriously exaggerated；(D) 監視器只是研究人員收集資料的工具。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "In Darley and Latané's experiment, what happened when participants believed four other people could hear the voice?", "options": ["Fewer than one third of them went to help.", "Almost all of them went to help immediately.", "They called the police instead of helping.", "They asked the other listeners what to do."], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：When they believed four other people were listening, only 31 percent did（去幫忙），31% 不到三分之一，故選 (A)。(B) 85% 去幫忙是以為只有自己聽到時的結果；(C)(D) 文中都沒有提到。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "The word \"exaggerated\" in paragraph 3 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["kept secret from the public", "written by many different people", "made to seem bigger or worse than it really was", "printed on the front page of the newspaper"], "answer": 2, "explain": "第三段說報導被 seriously exaggerated，接著列出事實：真正目睹的人很少、有人報警、有鄰居衝出去抱住她，可見原報導把鄰居的冷漠說得比實際嚴重，exaggerated 意為「誇大的」，故選 (C)。(A)(B)(D) 都與後文所舉的事實無關。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What can be inferred from the 2019 study described in paragraph 4?", "options": ["People in the Netherlands are kinder than people in other countries.", "Darley and Latané's experiment was completely wrong.", "Most public conflicts end without anyone getting involved.", "Being in a large group does not necessarily make help less likely."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第四段指出約十分之九的衝突中至少有一位旁觀者介入，而且 the more bystanders there were, the more likely it was that somebody would intervene，可推論身處人多的場合不一定就比較得不到幫助，故選 (D)。(A) 研究並未比較各國善良程度；(B) 同段承認人群中每個人可能覺得責任較小，並未全盤否定舊研究；(C) 與「十分之九有人介入」相反。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "A classmate suddenly faints on a crowded MRT platform. According to the passage, what is the best thing to do?", "options": ["Point to one particular person and ask him or her to call 119.", "Shout loudly, \"Somebody help us!\" to everyone on the platform.", "Wait quietly, because someone else will surely call for help.", "Take a video of the situation so that the police can see it later."], "answer": 0, "explain": "最後一段建議 point to a specific person，例如「穿藍色外套的那位，請幫忙打 119」，因為請求針對某一個人時，對方就很難推給別人，故選 (A)。(B) 正是文中說「與其……不如」要避免的喊法；(C) 這正是「責任分散」造成的錯誤想法；(D) 文中未建議。故選 (A)。"}]}, {"id": "a116", "title": "The Silent Thief: How a Phone You Don't Touch Can Steal Your Focus", "title_zh": "安靜的小偷：沒碰的手機如何偷走你的專注力", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Technology", "Education"], "passage": "Imagine you are about to start an important test. Your phone is switched to silent and lying face down on the desk. You have no intention of touching it, and it does not make a single sound. Surely it cannot affect how well you do? According to a much-discussed 2017 study from the University of Texas at Austin, it may do exactly that.\n\nIn the study, led by the researcher Adrian Ward, about 800 smartphone users completed tasks on a computer that measured how much information they could hold and process in their minds at once. Before starting, participants were randomly told to do one of three things: place their phone face down on the desk, keep it in a pocket or bag, or leave it in another room. In every case, the phones were silenced, so there were no alerts or vibrations to interrupt anyone.\n\nThe results were striking. Participants whose phones were in another room performed significantly better than those whose phones were on the desk, while those with phones in a pocket or bag scored somewhere in between. Remarkably, most participants said that they had not been thinking about their phones at all during the tasks. The effect was also strongest among people who said they depended most heavily on their phones in daily life.\n\nThe researchers called this effect \"brain drain.\" Their explanation is that our attention is a limited resource. Because a phone is so closely linked to messages, friends and entertainment, the brain treats it as something important. Even when we successfully resist the urge to check it, the effort of ignoring it quietly uses up some of the mental capacity we would otherwise devote to the task in front of us. In other words, the problem is not only that the phone interrupts us, but also that the brain has to work to avoid being interrupted.\n\nIt is worth noting that not every later study has found such a clear effect, and scientists are still debating how large it really is. Still, the advice that follows from it is simple and costs nothing. When you need to concentrate, whether on homework, an exam or a long piece of reading, putting your phone face down may not be enough. Leave it in another room, or at least put it away in your bag, out of sight. You may be surprised how much easier it becomes to focus when there is one less thing your brain has to ignore.", "passage_zh": "想像一下，你即將開始一場重要的考試。你的手機已經切換成靜音，正面朝下放在桌上。你完全不打算碰它，它也沒有發出任何聲音。它總不可能影響你的表現吧？根據德州大學奧斯汀分校在 2017 年一項備受討論的研究，它可能真的會。\n\n在這項由研究人員亞德里安‧華德主持的研究中，大約 800 名智慧型手機使用者在電腦上完成一些測驗，這些測驗可以測量他們能同時在腦中記住並處理多少資訊。開始之前，參與者被隨機指示做以下三件事之一：把手機正面朝下放在桌上、放在口袋或包包裡，或是留在另一個房間。在所有情況下，手機都設成靜音，所以不會有任何通知或震動打擾任何人。\n\n結果相當驚人。手機放在另一個房間的參與者，表現明顯優於手機放在桌上的人；而手機放在口袋或包包裡的人，分數則介於兩者之間。值得注意的是，大部分參與者都說，他們在測驗過程中完全沒有想到自己的手機。此外，在那些自認日常生活中最依賴手機的人身上，這個效應最強。\n\n研究人員把這種效應稱為「大腦流失」。他們的解釋是：我們的注意力是有限的資源。因為手機和訊息、朋友、娛樂關係如此密切，大腦會把它當成重要的東西。即使我們成功抗拒了查看手機的衝動，努力忽略它這件事，也會悄悄用掉一部分本來可以投入眼前任務的腦力。換句話說，問題不只是手機會打擾我們，還在於大腦必須費力避免被打擾。\n\n值得一提的是，並不是後來的每一項研究都發現這麼明顯的效應，科學家們仍在爭論它到底有多大。儘管如此，由此得出的建議既簡單又不花一毛錢。當你需要專心的時候，不論是寫作業、考試還是閱讀長篇文章，把手機正面朝下可能還不夠。把它留在另一個房間，或至少收進包包裡，讓自己看不到它。少了一樣大腦必須忽略的東西，你可能會驚訝地發現，專心變得容易多了。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main point of the passage?", "options": ["Phones should be banned from all schools and exam rooms.", "Silent phones are safe because they cannot interrupt us.", "Simply having a phone nearby may reduce our ability to focus.", "People who use their phones often are less intelligent than others."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第一段提出「靜音、正面朝下的手機也可能影響表現」，第二到四段說明研究結果與「大腦流失」的原因，第五段建議專心時把手機放到看不見的地方，故選 (C)。(A) 作者只建議把手機放遠，並未主張全面禁止；(B) 與全文論點相反；(D) 文中討論的是專注力與腦力的分配，並非智力高低。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "According to the study, which group of participants performed the best?", "options": ["Those who kept their phones in a pocket", "Those who placed their phones face down on the desk", "Those who held their phones in their hands", "Those who left their phones in another room"], "answer": 3, "explain": "第三段：Participants whose phones were in another room performed significantly better than those whose phones were on the desk，放在口袋或包包裡的人分數介於兩者之間，故選 (D)。(A) 分數居中；(B) 表現最差；(C) 研究中沒有這一組。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "Which finding does the writer describe as surprising in paragraph 3?", "options": ["Many participants checked their phones secretly during the tasks.", "Most participants said they had not been thinking about their phones.", "Participants with phones in bags did better than everyone else.", "People who seldom used phones were affected the most."], "answer": 1, "explain": "第三段用 Remarkably（值得注意、令人驚訝的是）帶出：most participants said that they had not been thinking about their phones at all，故選 (B)。(A) 第二段說手機都靜音，文中沒有偷看手機的描述；(C) 放在包包的人分數居中；(D) 與同段 strongest among people who... depended most heavily on their phones 相反。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "The word \"devote\" in paragraph 4 is closest in meaning to ______.", "options": ["give", "lose", "borrow", "refuse"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第四段 uses up some of the mental capacity we would otherwise devote to the task in front of us，意思是原本可以「投入、用在」眼前任務上的腦力，devote 與 give 最接近，故選 (A)。(B) lose 是忽略手機造成的結果，不是 devote 的意思；(C) borrow、(D) refuse 都不合句意。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "What is the writer's attitude toward the \"brain drain\" findings in the last paragraph?", "options": ["Completely doubtful, because later studies proved them wrong", "Angry, because phone companies have hidden the truth", "Careful but practical, since the effect is debated but the advice is still useful", "Uninterested, since the findings do not apply to students"], "answer": 2, "explain": "最後一段先坦承 not every later study has found such a clear effect，科學家仍在爭論效應大小（態度謹慎），接著用 Still 轉折，說這個建議 simple and costs nothing，並具體建議學生把手機放到別處（態度務實），故選 (C)。(A) 作者只說效應大小仍有爭議，並未說被證明是錯的；(B) 文中未提及手機公司；(D) 作者正是把研究應用在學生的作業與考試上。故選 (C)。"}]}, {"id": "a117", "title": "Nobody Is Staring at Your Shirt: The Spotlight Effect", "title_zh": "沒有人盯著你的上衣看：聚光燈效應", "level": "Reading · B2", "tags": ["Science", "Life"], "passage": "Have you ever spilled soy sauce on your shirt at lunch and spent the rest of the day convinced that everyone was staring at the stain? Or replayed an awkward comment you made in a group chat, certain that your friends were still thinking about it hours later? If so, you have experienced what psychologists call the spotlight effect: the tendency to believe that other people notice our appearance and behavior far more than they actually do.\n\nThe term was made famous by a clever experiment at Cornell University, published in 2000. The psychologist Thomas Gilovich and his colleagues asked students to put on a T-shirt printed with a large picture of Barry Manilow, a singer whom most students at the time considered extremely uncool. Each student then walked into a room where several other students were filling out questionnaires, stayed briefly and left. Afterwards, the shirt wearers were asked to guess how many people in the room had noticed who was on their shirt. They guessed about half. In reality, only about a quarter could say.\n\nWhy do we make this mistake so consistently? One explanation is that each of us is, quite naturally, at the center of our own experience. We know exactly how embarrassed we feel, so we assume our discomfort must be obvious to everyone else. We start from our own point of view and fail to adjust enough for the fact that other people are busy worrying about themselves. The student sitting next to you is probably far more concerned about his own hair than about the stain on your shirt.\n\nA closely related error is the \"illusion of transparency,\" the belief that our inner feelings, such as nervousness, can be clearly seen by others. In one study, people who gave a short speech believed they looked much more nervous than their audience actually thought they did. Encouragingly, when speakers were told about this illusion beforehand, they felt calmer, and their speeches were rated as better.\n\nUnderstanding the spotlight effect will not make embarrassing moments disappear, but it can shrink them to their real size. The next time you trip on the stairs or mispronounce a word during an English presentation, remember that most people will barely notice, and those who do will probably have forgotten about it by lunchtime. The spotlight we feel shining on us is, for the most part, a light we have switched on ourselves.", "passage_zh": "你有沒有在午餐時把醬油灑在上衣上，接下來一整天都認定每個人都在盯著那塊汙漬看？或是一再回想自己在群組聊天裡說過的一句尷尬的話，確信朋友們過了好幾個小時還在想著它？如果有，那你就體驗過心理學家所說的「聚光燈效應」：也就是傾向於認為別人對我們外表和行為的注意，遠比實際上多得多。\n\n這個名詞因康乃爾大學一項巧妙的實驗而出名，該研究於 2000 年發表。心理學家湯瑪斯‧季洛維奇和同事請學生穿上一件印著歌手貝瑞‧曼尼洛巨幅照片的 T 恤，當時大多數學生都認為這位歌手非常俗氣。接著，每位學生走進一間有好幾位學生正在填寫問卷的房間，短暫停留後離開。之後，研究人員請穿 T 恤的學生猜猜看，房間裡有多少人注意到他們衣服上印的是誰。他們猜大約一半。實際上，只有大約四分之一的人說得出來。\n\n為什麼我們總是一再犯這種錯誤？一種解釋是，我們每個人很自然地都是自己經驗的中心。我們清楚知道自己有多尷尬，所以就以為自己的不自在一定也很明顯，每個人都看得出來。我們從自己的觀點出發，卻沒有充分考慮到一個事實：別人正忙著為自己的事操心。坐在你旁邊的同學，在意自己頭髮的程度，大概遠遠超過在意你上衣的汙漬。\n\n一個密切相關的錯誤是「透明度錯覺」，也就是以為我們內心的感受，例如緊張，會被別人看得一清二楚。在一項研究中，進行簡短演講的人以為自己看起來非常緊張，但聽眾實際上感覺到的緊張程度，遠比他們想像的低。令人欣慰的是，當演講者事先得知這種錯覺之後，他們感覺比較平靜，演講也獲得比較好的評價。\n\n了解聚光燈效應並不會讓尷尬的時刻消失，但能把它們縮小到真正的大小。下次你在樓梯上絆了一下，或在英文報告時把一個字念錯，記得大多數人幾乎不會注意到，而注意到的人大概到午餐時間就已經忘了。我們感覺到照在自己身上的聚光燈，大部分其實是我們自己打開的。", "questions": [{"q": "What is the main idea of the passage?", "options": ["Students should avoid wearing T-shirts with pictures of singers.", "Giving a speech is the most stressful experience for teenagers.", "Most people are too busy to help others who are embarrassed.", "People tend to overestimate how much others notice them."], "answer": 3, "explain": "第一段定義聚光燈效應為 the tendency to believe that other people notice our appearance and behavior far more than they actually do，第二到四段以實驗與相關研究說明，第五段勸人不必太在意尷尬時刻，故選 (D)。(A) T 恤只是實驗工具；(B) 演講只是第四段的例子，文中未比較何者壓力最大；(C) 文中說別人忙著擔心自己，但並未談到幫助別人。故選 (D)。"}, {"q": "In the Cornell experiment, about how many people in the room actually noticed who was on the T-shirt?", "options": ["About a quarter", "About half", "Nearly everyone", "Not a single person"], "answer": 0, "explain": "第二段：They guessed about half. In reality, only about a quarter could say. 穿 T 恤的學生猜約一半，實際上只有約四分之一的人說得出來，故選 (A)。(B) 是穿衣者自己猜測的比例，並非實際結果；(C)(D) 文中未提及。故選 (A)。"}, {"q": "Why did the researchers most likely choose a Barry Manilow T-shirt for the experiment?", "options": ["Because Barry Manilow was a famous psychologist at Cornell", "Because wearing it would make the students feel embarrassed", "Because the students in the room were all his fans", "Because his picture was too small for anyone to recognize"], "answer": 1, "explain": "第二段說這位歌手是 a singer whom most students at the time considered extremely uncool（當時學生認為非常俗氣），穿著他的 T 恤容易讓學生覺得難為情，正好能測試人們是否高估別人對自己的注意，故選 (B)。(A) 他是歌手，不是心理學家；(C) 文中沒說房間裡的人是他的歌迷；(D) 第二段說照片是 a large picture。故選 (B)。"}, {"q": "According to paragraph 4, what happened when speakers learned about the illusion of transparency before their speeches?", "options": ["They refused to give their speeches.", "They looked more nervous to the audience.", "They felt calmer and gave better speeches.", "They spoke for a much longer time than before."], "answer": 2, "explain": "第四段：when speakers were told about this illusion beforehand, they felt calmer, and their speeches were rated as better，故選 (C)。(A)(D) 文中未提及；(B) 與文意相反。故選 (C)。"}, {"q": "What does the writer mean by \"a light we have switched on ourselves\" in the last sentence?", "options": ["We should turn off the lights to save energy during presentations.", "The attention we feel from others is mostly created by our own minds.", "Only famous people are really followed by spotlights wherever they go.", "We should try hard to become the center of everyone's attention."], "answer": 1, "explain": "最後一句是比喻：我們覺得照在自己身上的聚光燈（被別人注意的感覺），大部分是自己打開的，也就是說這種被注視的感覺多半來自自己的想像，呼應第三段「我們是自己經驗的中心」的解釋，故選 (B)。(A)(C) 把聚光燈當成真正的燈光，誤解比喻；(D) 與作者勸人不必在意別人眼光的用意不符。故選 (B)。"}]}];
